window.EB_SB_ADD({"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/10g48u9UtpzswyOa":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Melodian","Pyric"],"flavor":"<p>An aestival's ideal state of being is snoozing a hot summer day away in some comfortably inconvenient locale, ideally with a high vantage point. There are stories aplenty of aestivals falling asleep beneath a farmer's cart, tangled in the sails of a windmill, or upon a millstone ready to grind. To make matters worse, they are incredibly cranky when awoken and liable to wreak real havoc before grumpily stalking away to find a better napping spot.</p><p>During the rare times they are awake, aestivals enjoy watching laborers tend to the fields or children play in meadows and pastures (from a cushy, relaxed perch, of course). They have a particular love of work songs, children's rhymes, and lullabies, and it is said that breaking into one of these songs is one of the few ways an aestival can be calmed down from their incandescent anger.</p><p>Aestivals can go long stretches of time without eating. When they do choose to partake in food, they indulge in lavish feasts, often to the annoyance of those who originally owned the food. Yet aestivals are not entirely freeloaders in their communities. When monsters or other perils threaten the tranquility of their homes, they spring into action, placing themselves in harm's way to awaken their internal flames.</p><hr><p>Seasonals are a cycle of four kinds of fey, each tied to one of the four seasons: vernals who embody spring's renewal and storming showers, aestivals who embody summer's lazy heat and burning passion, autumnals who embody the melancholy culling and reaping harvest of autumn, and hibernals who embody winter's quiet cheer.</p><p>Whatever the current season in the mortal world, seasonals wrap themselves in a metaphysical cloak of their own season's magic and carry the songs full of that season's surging emotions with them wherever they go in the mortal world or across the realm of Faerie. Whenever seasonals meet, shy hibernals typically prefer to amuse themselves by watching the antics of the other three kinds of fey, while vernals impatiently call for energetic contests, aestivals languidly nap until roused to fiery bursts of activity, and autumnals attempt to imbue a sense of stark responsibility in the rest of their compatriots. Exceptions exist, with those seasonals interacting with the others in ways unexpected based upon the stereotypes of their season.</p><h2>Summer Solstice</h2><p>If there's one time of year an aestival is certain to be active, it's during the summer solstice. On the longest day of the year, aestivals throw raucous celebrations filled with music and dancing, often encouraging those around them (through force, if necessary) to join in the festivities. On the summer solstice, the aestival is usually awakened, and until the sun sets they can choose whether or not to return to their languid state.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: <em>environmental endurance</em>, <em>fireball</em>, <em>ignition</em>), strength, time, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Rage and Relaxation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Aestivals are most often in their languid state, where they are permanently Slowed 1 and under the effects of Sanctuary. If they take damage from any source, they become awakened until the end of their next turn, losing the slowed condition and the effects of sanctuary. While awakened, they are Quickened (the extra action can be used only to Stride, Climb, or Strike), and they deal an additional 4 fire damage with their Strikes and with their Ignition cantrip.</p><p>Effect: Awakened</p>"},{"n":"Rest a While","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the aestival's reach uses a move action or leaves a square during a move action it's using</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The aestival attempts an Athletics check to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the aestival's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Wildfire Cloak","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>While the aestival is awakened, all terrain they move through becomes fiery, hazardous terrain, dealing 5 fire damage to any creature who moves into one of these squares. The hazardous terrain lasts until the start of the aestival's next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Heatstroke","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>An aestival's fists sap energy, much like a day of hard labor beneath a baking sun. An affected creature must succeed at a DC 21 Fortitude save or become Fatigued.</p>"},{"n":"Summer Blaze","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The aestival is awakened</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The aestival Strides or Climbs and then makes two fist Strikes against a single enemy. Apply the multiple attack penalty to these Strikes normally. If both Strikes succeed, the target also takes 1d6 persistent fire damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/h2a3QXsiASd0zWaB":{"lang":["Common","Fey"],"gear":["Staff","Dragon Bile (2)","Nightmare Vapor (2)"],"notes":{"per":"blightsense 20 feet"},"flavor":"<p>It's said that the fine line between a healing medicine and a deadly poison is the dosage. Agiyeft dance upon that line with reckless abandon. Collectors of rare toxins, agiyeft travel the world extracting ailments from people and creatures to use in their strange experiments. A town may praise an agiyeft as a hero for purifying their poisoned water supply, but it's entirely possible the agiyeft was the one to poison it in the first place. Despite their sometimes unethical practices, an agiyeft is more pragmatic than malicious and will freely provide healing from poisons and diseases in exchange for unique samples.</p><h2>Contagion Collectors</h2><p>Agiyefts collect illnesses and ailments of all kinds, but they have a special interest in rare maladies or those with unusual symptoms. The most ancient among them claims to hold the only samples of several diseases thought to be long eradicated. While most agiyefts would be loath to release one of their deadly infections into a mortal population, the more cold-hearted of them could be convinced to do so in exchange for an enticing payment—perhaps simply a front-row seat from which to watch the disease spread.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,250/2,800/5,150 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Crafting, Deception, Diplomacy, Medicine, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, vitality, void), intelligence, life, lifeward armor, poison, poisonous armor, spells (Primal: Cleanse Affliction, Heal), throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Blightsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An agiyeft senses any creature that is poisoned or diseased, and knows the type and current stage of all poisons and diseases carried by any creature within range.</p>"},{"n":"Toxin Manipulation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. An agiyeft can alter the toxins in a creatures body, even turning them into neurotoxins. When any creature in the aura attempts a save against a disease or poison the agiyeft may grant a +1 status bonus or –1 status penalty to the save, and can choose to change it into a Will save instead of a Fortitude save.</p>"},{"n":"Hemodermal Spray","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["healing","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The agiyeft takes piercing or slashing damage.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The agiyeft flings medicinal toxins in their blood at a creature within 20 feet, either healing it 5d6 healing Hit Points, or dealing 5d6 poison damage (DC 37 basic Fortitude save). The agiyeft chooses which.</p>"},{"n":"Venom Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>hen an agiyeft deals piercing or slashing damage with a Strike they may expose the creature to Agiyeft's Meddling, Agiyeft's Whim, or any other poison they have on them. Any poison applied to a Strike this way gains the Injury trait, and has a DC 37 Fortitude or the poison's listed DC, which ever is higher. If the target is already suffering from a poison they take 1d6 poison damage instead.</p>"},{"n":"Agiyeft's Meddling","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["healing","poison","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 37 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> fast healing 1, Clumsy 1, Enfeebled 1, and Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> fast healing 3, Clumsy 2, Enfeebled 2, and Stupefied 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> fast healing 5, Clumsy 3, Enfeebled 3, and Stupefied 3 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Agiyeft's Whim","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["healing","poison","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p>A strange concoction of various ingredients the agiyeft has collected. At each stage the creature must attempt a flat check against that stage's listed DC. On a failure the creature takes poison damage equal to the amount of dice rolled for that stage, and on a success the creature regains Hit Points equal to half the amount rolled</p><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 37 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> DC 6 Flat and 8d6 poison damage (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> DC 12 Flat and 10d6 poison damage (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> DC 18 Flat and 12d6 poison damage (1 round)</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/QicNy5FUxJaXz3gN":{"lang":["Common","Spirituan","Truespeech"],"gear":["Staff"],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status vs. illusions"},"flavor":"<p>In many faiths, light is symbolic of holy righteousness and divine justice, while darkness represents unholy malevolence and fear of the unknown. But in truth, too much light can burn and darkness can provide much-needed shade. Aglyses prey upon people who believe too deeply in the righteousness of the light. Posing as holy champions, they infiltrate religious communities and gradually warp people's views with their mind-altering magic and beguiling tongues. In time, they can twist even the most upstanding devotees into a frothing mob of zealots at their beck and call. Most terrifyingly of all, aglyses are experts at convincing their followers that every horrific act they commit is ordained by the gods.</p><h2>Light and Darkness</h2><p>Aglyses particularly dislike rommors, whose gentle nature and tie to the element of darkness threaten to lay bare the simplistic associations of holy light and evil shadow that aglyses rely upon. As a result, aglyses persecute and destroy rommors wherever they can. Rommors typically use their magical abilities to carry themselves and their charges away from such conflicts, returning only after gathering enough heroes to extinguish the aglys's corruption for good.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 8,750/17,500/30,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Religion, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, charisma, electricity, energy resistant (vitality), fire, fire shield, ice, light, mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), spells (Divine: Fabricated Truth, Falling Stars, Fear, Paranoia, Subconscious Suggestion, Truesight, Truespeech, Zealous Conviction), spirit, strength, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dulling Radiance","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","light","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. The aglys is surrounded by an aura of bright light. This light disorients creatures whose eyes are already overwhelmed, giving them mind-dulling hallucinations. Dazzled creatures in the aura are Stupefied 1, and Blinded creatures in the aura are Stupefied 2.</p>"},{"n":"Focused Flash","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","light","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet of the aglys targets the aglys with an attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The aglys reflects intense light off their staff and armor toward their assailant. The creature takes a –2 circumstance penalty on the triggering attack roll and is Dazzled until the end of its turn. The concealment from the dazzled condition applies to the triggering attack. If the attack misses, the triggering creature takes 4d6 persistent fire damage.</p><p>Effect: Focused Flash</p>"},{"n":"Shatter Control","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Aglyses are especially susceptible to powerful sound waves. When an aglys takes sonic damage, any creature affected by the aglys's Crusade, Paranoia, Subconscious Suggestion, or Subjugating Light can immediately attempt another Will save with a +2 circumstance bonus, using the new result only if it is better than the original result.</p><p>An aglys who takes sonic damage must also attempt a DC 45 Will save. On a failure, the aglys's dulling radiance aura and purging luminance ability are suppressed until the end of the aglys's next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Purging Luminance","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","light"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that is hit by one of the aglys's strikes is Dazzled for 1 round. On a critical hit, if the creature was temporarily immune to Subjugating Light, it is no longer temporarily immune.</p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Mind Sear","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","light","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The aglys forces their will upon everyone in a 30-foot emanation, dealing 20d6 mental damage (DC 45 basic Will save). A creature that fails the save is also Dazzled for 1 round, or Blinded for 1 round on a critical failure. The aglys can't use Mind Sear again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Subjugating Light","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","incapacitation","light","manipulate","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The aglys's manipulative light shines deep into a creature's mind. This has the effects of a 10th-rank Dominate spell (DC 45 Will save), except the maximum duration is 1 minute and the target is also Dazzled for the spell's duration. The target is temporarily immune to Subjugating Light for 10 minutes.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/FOd8ChzFup4GXt9N":{"lang":["Fey","Melodian"],"flavor":"<p>Whether to the songs of creation or the music in their minds, alvaleks are eternally dancing. These whimsical, flower-like creatures love nothing more than to share their endless dance with others. Unfortunately for their would-be partners, these fey have no concept of fear nor of the pain their thorn-covered bodies can inflict on others. They continue to dance gleefully even long after their companion's cries of pain fall silent.</p><p>Alvaleks are frequently found in even-numbered troupes dancing through fields of flowers and forest clearings. Seeing their dance can be entrancing for those who wander too close, luring in the passerby who doesn't realize their peril until it's too late. When found in isolation, they are known to pursue any humanoid that crosses their path.</p><p>The only way to safely cross paths with an alvalek is to be already engaged in a dance with a partner. Even when alone, an alvalek is unwilling or unable to intrude upon a performance in progress and will instead seek a partner elsewhere.</p><h2>Odd One Out</h2><p>When found in odd-numbered groups, alvaleks are a threat to anyone they see. A lone, unpaired alvalek is always desperate for a partner, while the rest of the troupe, pitying their solitary state, will do anything they can to find them a match—except give up their partner. To help this lone alvalek, pairs of alvaleks surround non-dancing travelers in a swirling ring of dancers, interposing their bodies and using their piercing songs to prevent escape and forcing a coupling.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor(light, medium), shield, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Charm), sturdy, tangle shield, throwing, time, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Fortissimo","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The creature with the lowest initiative ends their turn</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> As the alvalek's internal music reaches a crescendo, the alvalek sings an ear-piercing high note, creating a shockwave that deals 2d6 sonic damage to all non-alvalek creatures within 10 feet. Each creature must attempt a DC 20 Fortitude save. If a creature is in the area of two or more Fortissimos, they attempt a single save with a –2 circumstance penalty.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Deafened for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage and is deafened for 1 minute and Stunned 1.</p>"},{"n":"Leading Step","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The alvalek dances forth to a song only they can hear and Steps up to 10 feet. If they have a creature Grabbed, they violently reposition that creature to another space in their reach with a dancing flourish, dealing the creature 5 piercing damage.</p>"},{"n":"Shifting Tempo","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["primal","time"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The alvalek has a creature Grabbed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The alvalek taps their foot to a new rhythm, attempting to slow down or speed up the tempo. The grabbed creature can attempt a DC 21 Will save to negate the effect. If the save fails, the alvalek and the grabbed creature become either Slowed 1 or Quickened as time flows with the new tune; the extra action from quickened can only be used to Step or use Leading Step. The change in tempo persists until the grabbed condition ends or the alvalek uses Shifting Tempo again.</p>"},{"n":"Eager Partner","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An alvalek is not Immobilized when Grabbed by another creature of Medium size or smaller. They can treat the grabbing creature as being grabbed by them for the purposes of meeting the requirements for Leading Step and Shifting Tempo. An alvalek's nature forbids them from interrupting an ongoing dance; they can't attempt to Grab a creature that is already grappled.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Vpxo7NnWMRRHRrXu":{"lang":["Fey","can't speak any language"],"flavor":"<p>Amber brambles are round thorny plants oozing sap. They're carnivorous, and they prefer to ambush their prey. Though their serrated leaves and the spines covering their branches pose an obvious danger, their poisonous sap is also deadly. When set loose from the brambles' branches, the sap hardens rapidly, encasing tiny animals entirely in an amberlike substance and restricting the breathing of larger prey. While many predators are content to hunt only when they are hungry, amber brambles prefer to hunt far more than they need, encasing the extra meat in sap for later consumption.</p><p>It is not uncommon to find these plants in the domain of other fey, who see them as either wonderful additions to a garden (in the case of more powerful fey) or as dangerous additions to home defense (in the case of weaker fey, especially gremlins). Taming an amber bramble often requires an offering of a staggering amount of fresh meat.</p><p>Some people harvest amber bramble sap and refine it into a psychoactive drug. Others collect and sell amber stones with interesting prey trapped inside. However, many prospective amber collectors discover that the plants are hostile to those who steal their captured prey, never forgiving the transgression against their rightfully earned meals.</p><h2>Frozen In Time</h2><p>The body of a creature suffocated by an amber bramble's sap is preserved in a pristine state, often frozen in mid-action. Evil fey creatures sometimes display amber bramble victims in sinister dioramas that depict dances, legendary battles, or other active scenes. Some of these fey believe that by preserving these statues, they are doing a service: the victims' perfect preservation means that they can be restored to life long after the usual magical deadline to do so has passed. Others simply use them as exotic paperweights.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low- light vision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Foliage Body","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An amber bramble can Hide and Sneak in forests, even if it doesn't have cover.</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Splash","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An adjacent creature hits the amber bramble with an attack that deals piercing, slashing, or bludgeoning damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The bramble retaliates by flinging the sap from its injury on its assailant. The bramble makes a sap glob Strike against the creature. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the amber bramble's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Cloying Sap","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sap of an amber bramble rapidly hardens, restricting movement. Those exposed to the plant's cloying sap take a –10-foot circumstance penalty to their Speeds until they Escape it.</p>"},{"n":"Sap Toxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 18 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 1d4 poison damage and stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 1d4 poison damage and Stupefied 2 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Sap Spray","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The amber bramble sprays a deluge of sap around itself in a 10-foot emanation. Each creature in the area must attempt a DC 18 Reflex save to avoid the sap. On a failure, the creature is exposed to the amber bramble's cloying sap and sap toxin. On a critical failure, the sap covers the creature's face, restricting their breathing. Until they Escape from the cloying sap, they must hold their breath or begin suffocating.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/hyEtgjiuY2VUZbrD":{"lang":["Common"],"flavor":"<p>A theater production can be a stressful process for all involved. Tempers flare, egos clash, and anger simmers. These powerful emotions can resonate within the everyday props that are frequently exposed to them. On rare occasions, this resonance develops its own sentience and ego.</p><p>Amphitwisters only occur in venues that have hosted hundreds of performances, manifesting on the night of a particularly ambitious or fevered performance. While they are not actively malicious, amphitwisters underestimate the damage their rampages can cause. After all, an actor who dies a bloody death on stage rises to perform another night, so why should it be any different for the people left bleeding on the ground in the wake of an amphitwister's excitable tempest? In a fight, amphitwisters careen wildly throughout the venue, darting into range to whip costumes, props, and dangerous shards of glass at their foes. Despite their theatrical origin, they show no preference or animosity toward bards or other thespians.</p><p>Once defeated, the amphitwister dissipates, leaving behind a manifested crystal of psychic energy. This valuable keepsake is infused with the resonance of the theatrics that shaped it and makes an excellent ingredient for crafting magical costumes and instruments.</p><h2>Everyone's a Critic</h2><p>Even when they are not physically manifested, amphitwisters are aware of the goings-on in their theater and never miss a performance. They often develop strong feelings about members of an acting troupe, selecting beloved darlings who must be preserved at all costs and labeling others as talentless hacks who should be driven from the theater, with violence if necessary. When an amphitwister appears, they are always forthcoming with their opinions, with their words often as cutting as their attacks.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), force, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Protect the Scene","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet would use an action with the emotion trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The amphitwister detests interruptions to the show, whether they be Demoralizing hecklers or people spinning magic to take control of the narrative. The triggering creature must succeed at a DC 30 Will save or the triggering action is disrupted.</p>"},{"n":"Fashion Disaster","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The amphitwister envelops all creatures in its space in an array of comically mismatched clothing. All creatures in its space must attempt a DC 27 Reflex save to avoid being costumed.</p><p>Effect: Fashion Disaster</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes a –15-foot status penalty to their Speeds and a –2 circumstance penalty to Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, and Performance checks until they Escape the clothing.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature is also Immobilized until they remove the clothing.</p>"},{"n":"Mirror Shards","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The swarm ejects a storm of mirror shards. All creatures in a 15-foot burst within 120 feet of the swarm take 4d6+9 piercing damage (DC 27 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Places, Please!","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The amphitwister's props arrange themselves, gathering into four adjacent squares. This reduces the swarm's size to Large but increases the damage of its Prop Barrage to 3d12. The swarm remains Large until it spends another action with the move trait to disperse to its original size.</p>"},{"n":"Prop Barrage","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Each foe in the swarm's space takes 3d8 bludgeoning damage (DC 30 basic Reflex).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/G6RBUSzmvCzEWpTc":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Empyrean","Melodian","Protean","Utopian"],"lore":[["Music Lore",19]],"gear":["Repair Toolkit","Unintelligible Sheet Music"],"notes":{"ac":"(25 when at 65 HP or fewer), hard core","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. auditory"},"flavor":"<p>Amplifighters are part of a loud, provocative subculture of automata. Capable tinkers, they mod themselves into bulky, core-powered loudspeakers capable of producing a great deal of noise, some of which might even be considered music. Their reinforced, sound-boosting bodies are flush with dials and inventively styled wires, which leave them vulnerable to external sonic energies. As such, amplifighters enjoy sparring amongst themselves, stopping to gloat and/or repair when their partner starts \"bleeding static.\"</p><p>An amplifighter's ambiance isn't to everyone's tastes, but they wouldn't care even if they could hear people's complaints over the constant thrumming of their cores. After all, regardless of whether they consider themselves more of an inventor or a bard, every amplifighter already knows that their avant-garde genius is sorely underappreciated.</p><h2>No Accounting For Taste</h2><p>As dissimilar as amplifighters seem to be from other constructs, the schisms between various subcultures of amplifighters are even more pronounced—at least to them! Amplifighters can argue for hours about who among them truly embodies \"deep liminal screechcore\" or the \"new-wave bleep\" style. When two groups of rival amplifighters meet, their arguments can even escalate to violence, resulting in a riotous cacophony that can be heard for miles around.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, skill items (Athletics, Crafting, Lore [Music], Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, elongating, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), intelligence, metal, music, sensory (low-light vision), strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Self-Invented","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An amplifighter's familiarity with their heavily modded chassis allows them spent one minute to Repair themselves (DC 26). Their proficiency rank in Crafting is master.</p>"},{"n":"Terrifying Performance","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The amplifighter can use Performance instead of Intimidation to Coerce or Demoralize. On a critical success to perform either of these actions, the target is also Deafened for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves Vs. Auditory","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Hard Core","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The amplifighter's tinkering has made them rigidly resilient, but their overclocked core is a bit unstable. When reduced to 65 Hit Points or fewer, the chassis protecting their core cracks. They lose their resistance to physical damage and their AC is reduced to 25. While their chassis is cracked, whenever they take physical damage, they deal 4d4 sonic damage to all creatures in a 10-foot emanation (DC 28 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Jarring Interruption","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An adjacent creature uses a concentrate action</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Be it blaring horns for a casting mage or a celestial choir for a teleporting fiend: every amplifighter has a knack for making the wrong noise at the right moment. They attempt a melee Strike against the triggering creature; on a hit, the target loses the triggering action unless it succeeds at a DC 26 Will save. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the amplifighter's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Amp it Up!","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The amplifighter turns the volume of their loudspeakers up to eardrum-shattering levels. All creatures in a 30-foot emanation take 7d4 sonic damage (DC 28 basic Reflex save). Creatures that fail the saving throw are Deafened for 1 round (or for 1 minute on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/KemOBoo7qirkvu0B":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Requian"],"lore":[["Midwifery Lore",7]],"gear":["Shortbow","Arrows (20)"],"flavor":"<p>Of all the known varieties of psychopomp, amutu are among the most likely to interact with and the living and try to assist them in matters other than dealing with undead or other enemies of Purgatory. They have been tasked by their superiors, following the chain all the way up to psychopomp ushers and the deities of Purgatory, to manage deaths caused by resource scarcity, such as starvation, dehydration, or exposure to the elements. They travel far and wide through the mortal realm, offering warnings and advice against such catastrophes. When prevention is impossible, they help guide the souls of the departed to the afterlife, lest they linger behind and haunt their former homes or rise again to devour the living. Amutu have also been known to help with childbirth when an unfortunate mother is caught far from civilization and to teach new lovers about herbal contraceptives.</p><p>An amutu stands about as tall as a halfling, with a light coppery complexion and the feet and head of an owl or other bird of prey. They favor traveling in arid regions and typically wear light, non-restrictive clothing. Like many psychopomps, amutu wear masks. They favor brightly painted wooden masks, often carved with exaggerated humanoid features and ringed with feathers. These feathers are a mixture of their own creations and gifts they have received from friends and allies.</p><p>Though amutu are supernatural beings, they recognize that most disasters are made worse not by magical interference but by the corrupting forces of greed. Thus, their foes include people who hoard resources while others struggle to meet their basic needs, as well as those who deliberately build shoddy infrastructure to save money, leaving communities vulnerable to accidents and natural disasters. They also oppose more fantastical threats, such as gremlins, ghouls, and priests of famine deities (such as Havath in the world of Alacar). When more powerful menaces like daemons or sahkils emerge, amutu seek the aid of heroes to prevent tragedy.</p><h2>Parliament of Owls</h2><p>Folklore concerning the wisdom of owls might have its origin in the activities of amutus. Tales in which owls provide advice or gather together in groups to consult about supernatural events probably refer to the counsels of amutus. Although this tale might be true, amutus fervently deny having anything to do with other folk tales, such as the one in which malevolent owls pass judgment on mortals who break the most minor of rules, pursuing them, and then tearing them to pieces.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Lore [Midwifery], Nature, Religion, Society, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality, void), life, lifeward armor, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Create Food, Create Water, Invisibility, Light, Sanctuary, Stabilize, Ventriloquism), water, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spirit Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An amutu's Strikes affect incorporeal creatures as though etched with a <em>ghost touch</em> property rune and deal 1d6 void damage to living creatures or 1d6 vitality damage to undead.</p>"},{"n":"Ominous Hoot","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The amutu lets loose a loud otherworldly hoot that terrifies their foes. Each creature in a 30-foot emanation must attempt a DC 18 Will save. Regardless of the result, creatures are temporarily immune for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Fleeing for 1 round and Frightened 3.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/DvqOmRtyzIlVvumY":{"notes":{"ac":"(18 when broken), construct armor"},"flavor":"<p>The instruments in an animated orchestra are usually beautifully crafted from fine materials and adorned with intricate designs. When animated properly by a professional, they remain perfectly still and silent until commanded to perform, which they do flawlessly. However, the performances of animated orchestras are a popular topic of debate among music aficionados. Some admire their precise artistry, while others say they lack emotion or soul.</p><p>Under the guidance of their conductor, animated orchestras can also protect their performance halls from dangerous intruders. Regrettably, not all animated orchestras possess the same qualities. Depending on the skill of the practitioner who animates the orchestra, some instruments might behave erratically. A misbehaving maelstrom of violins, trombones, drums, flutes, and other instruments could overwhelm inexperienced crafters, playing random discordant snippets of music and attacking indiscriminately. Even properly made orchestras might develop these dangerous tendencies with a bit of precise sabotage, and when left to their own devices, such faulty animated orchestras first attack their conductor and numerous audience members.</p><p>Once all of the survivors have fled and the screams have died down, the abandoned orchestra will wander endlessly in search of a new audience, performing their deadly song for whoever they come across. Though an exceptionally skilled crafter might be able to restore an animated orchestra back to its original function, surviving the orchestra's onslaught long enough to repair it is a challenging task.</p><h2>Tools of the Trade</h2><p>Animated orchestras might be able to move and play themselves under their own power, but when they lie dormant, they can also be played like normal instruments. An orchestra might play a set of musical instruments every day without ever suspecting they are enchanted, until the instruments fly from their hands to fight a threat—or, if the orchestra is particularly incompetent, to punish the orchestra with violence.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), fortification, music, sensory (darkvision), sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 5)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, an animated orchestra has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage the swarm takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once an animated orchestra is reduced to fewer than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks, removing the Hardness and reducing its Armor Class to 18.</p>"},{"n":"Woodwinds","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The animated orchestra is targeted by an attack and can see the attacker</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Woodwind instruments play a quick and frenzied melody, creating a swirling barrier of sound. The animated orchestra gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against the triggering attack.</p>"},{"n":"Brass","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The animated orchestra's brass instruments resonate with power and let loose a thunderous blast of sound dealing 3d10+5 sonic damage in a 30-foot cone (DC 19 basic Fortitude save). On a critical failure, the target is Deafened for 1 minute. The animated orchestra can't use Brass again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Percussion","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The animated orchestra unleashes a thunderous barrage of percussive strikes against each creature in its space, dealing 2d8 bludgeoning damage (DC 19 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Strings","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The animated orchestra's stringed instruments hum with magic as they weave a slicing melody into the air. The melody crescendos into a ferocious symphony of supernatural blades, dealing 2d6+5 slashing damage in a 10-foot burst within 120 feet (DC 19 basic Reflex save).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/AWNYprN4tWMiriAu":{"lang":["Common","Diabolic","Draconic","Empyrean","Jyoti","Petran","Speak with animals, Speak with plants, Truespeech"],"notes":{"ac":"(33 when broken), pottery armor","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>When early mortals abandoned the ancient spirits they worshiped in favor of newer gods, some neglected spirits turned to despair and twisted into fiends. Others ascended to the celestial planes, sharing their wisdom with the benevolent young gods. As these ascended spirits shared their knowledge, they felt their essence being absorbed by the celestial planes and asked to be bound into physical forms so they could return to assist mortalkind. Angels sculpted pottery bodies out of clay and invited the spirits to inhabit them, turning them into true angels: the arahabaki.</p><p>Loving and kind to the extreme, arahabakis adore mortals. They relentlessly roam, seeking out communities in need and purging them of corruption. In addition to intervening in times of crisis, arahabakis provide guidance and mentorship. Arahabakis tries to avoid exposing people to fights they aren't strong enough to win, so when truly cataclysmic threats arise, they send calls for aid out to great heroes from far and wide.</p><h2>March of Ages</h2><p>Arahabakis sometimes enter periods of extended hibernation, during which they appear as lifeless (though beautiful and unaging) statues. In this state, an arahabaki maintains awareness of their surroundings, yet their perception of time slows down to a near-halt. In the mortal lifetimes that might pass before the arahabaki reawakens, their purpose can lose meaning or be forgotten by the societies they once protected. This sometimes puts them at odds with adventurers seeking to explore the ancient ruins the arahabaki guards or learn some secret the arahabaki believes would do more harm than good if rediscovered.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Medicine, Nature, Religion, Society, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), holy, life, lifeward armor, light, mirrored, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Boulder Crush, Breath of Life, Field of Life, Heal, Light, Regenerate, Sanctuary, Speak with Animals, Speak with Plants, Stabilize, Truespeech), spirit, strength, sturdy, tangle shield, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Pottery Armor (Hardness 15)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Each arahabaki is encased in pottery armor. Like normal objects, the pottery armor has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage the arahabaki takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once an arahabaki is reduced to less than half their maximum Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, their pottery armor breaks, they lose their immunities, and their Armor Class is reduced to 33. While the armor is broken, the radius of their healing aura decreases to 15 feet and their fly speed increases to 60 feet. The arahabaki can repair or reconstruct their pottery armor with 10 minutes of meditation.</p>"},{"n":"Healing Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. All allied creatures in the aura gain fast healing 5 and a +2 status bonus to all saves against poison and disease for as long as they remain in the aura. After any creature afflicted by a condition spends at least 1 minute within the aura, the aura attempts a counteract check against all afflictions affecting it with a +28 modifier, as an 8th-rank Cleanse Affliction. The aura can't attempt another counteract check against the same case of the affliction for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Protection","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature would attack one of the arahabaki's allies that is within the healing aura</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The arahabaki casts Sanctuary on the ally before the attack connects. The attacker must attempt a Will save against the <em>sanctuary</em> as normal.</p>"},{"n":"Purification","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>An arahabaki's Strikes deal 2d6 additional vitality damage to undead and 2d6 additional spirit damage to unholy creatures that aren't undead.</p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Statue","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Until the next time they act, the arahabaki appears to be a statue. They have an automatic result of 50 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as a statue.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/CQDGfdZwNisX21JR":{"lang":["Common","Luxan","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"lore":[["Genealogy Lore",23]],"notes":{"speed":"wasteland walker"},"flavor":"<p>Archival monitors appear as large reptiles with a pronounced crystalline crest protruding from their heads. This crest is not only ornamental but also contains the collective memories of their ancestors, allowing them to remember events and experiences from the distant past. Archival monitors typically wander lost ruins, following ancient migratory routes and erecting grand illusory structures when they come across an important historical site.</p><p>The knowledge that each archival monitor carries is a priceless treasure to archaeologists, historians, and adventurers alike. However, their ancestral memories also include instances where others have attempted to exploit them for knowledge or hunt them for crests. As a result, they are very slow to trust. Still, archival monitors value the preservation of their traditions, and they are sometimes willing to share their wealth of experience with those who prove themselves through an appropriate task or trial.</p><p>All archival monitors share ancestral memories, including arcane formulas for manipulating light using crests. They use these formulas to great effect in defending their homes from violent or otherwise unsavory intruders, burning them with focused light beams or imprisoning them in a cage before slowly roasting them alive.</p><h2>Peaceful Pilgrimages</h2><p>Strong enough to defend themselves against most threats—and meaning no harm toward those with no harm to offer—archival monitors can serve as islands of safety in otherwise dangerous lands. As they wend their long circuit through treacherous ruins, an archival monitor can pick up an escort of smaller creatures too weak to defend themselves. To those with an interest in history, no traveling companion is more congenial than an archival monitor reminiscing about the past.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Lore [Genealogy])</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision, scent), spells (Arcane: Hypercognition, Light, Mindlink, Mirage, Truespeech), strength, throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Crystalline Memory","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An archival monitor can use Genealogy Lore to Recall Knowledge on any topic related to the memories of an ancestor.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Flashing Charge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","light","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The archival monitor Strides. If they end that Stride adjacent to at least one other creature, they suddenly display their reflective frill in a blinding flash of light. Each adjacent creature must succeed at a DC 28 Will save or become Dazzled and Off-Guard for 1 round. The monitor then makes a Strike against one adjacent creature.</p>"},{"n":"Kaleidoscopic Cage","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","concentrate","fire","light","manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The archival monitor creates an immobile, spherical cage of solidified light in a 30-foot radius centered around themselves. If the cage is created in a creature's square, the creature is repositioned to the nearest unoccupied square outside of the sphere; this is forced movement. The cage has an AC 10, Hardness 12, and 80 Hit Points, and it's immune to critical hits and precision damage. The cage sheds bright light in a 60-foot radius from its center (and dim light for the next 30 feet). Creatures that end of their turn inside of the cage take 2d6 fire damage (DC 28 basic Fortitude save). The cage lasts until the end of the archival monitor's next turn, but they can Sustain the cage for up to 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Solar Beam","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire","light"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The archival monitor is in bright light</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The archival monitor angles their reflective frill to focus ambient light into a 60-foot line. If the line hits a Kaleidoscopic Cage, it bounces off at an angle of the monitor's choice. All creatures that the beam passes through at least once take 11d6 fire damage (DC 30 basic Reflex save). Creatures that critically fail become Blinded for 1 round. The archival monitor can't use Solar Beam again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Wasteland Walker","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The archival monitor ignores difficult terrain and uneven ground caused by rubble.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/qhVmX1JmdP5ydGg5":{"lang":["Common","Empyrean"],"flavor":"<p>Designed after an old hunting deity's two canines, argentaurem are strong temple guardians. They always come in pairs as a giant gold hound and a smaller, faster silver hound. Since separation is physically and spiritually terrible, they always stay together. When one of the pair is killed, the other fights till the end; however, some fiends and intruders prefer to depart and leave the other to its fate.</p><h2>Built to Order</h2><p>While the original pair of argentaurem were modeled after the twin hounds of an ancient hunting deity, some modern temples have reverse-engineered the process to make guardians of their own, modeled after their deity's sacred animal. Despite their different looks, they must share many traits as the original pair, like one being gold and the other silver. They must also both be the same type of animal. The exact reason for these necessary similarities is unknown, but experiments with different forms have resulted in disastrous results.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Religion, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), holy, metal, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Metallic Pair","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Every argentaurem is created alongside a partner, with one made of gold and the other made of silver. The gold one can make gold claws and gold jaws Strikes and use Growl, while the silver one can make silver claws and silver jaws Strikes and use Retrieve. While within 100 feet of each other, the pair can communicate telepathically. If the pair is more than 100 feet apart, they are both Sickened 1 and Drained 1 and can't remove these conditions in any way. While a pair of argentaurems is two level 8 creatures, each of which rolls its own initiative and has three actions per round, an argentaurem encountered without its partner is a level 7 creature.</p>"},{"n":"Share Life","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["divine","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> An argentaurem and its partner are within 100 feet of each other</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The argentaurem and its partner's Hit Points both become equal to the average of their two totals, rounded down. The argentaurem and its partner can't use this ability again for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Share Mind","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The argentaurem and their partner must both roll a Will save, a Perception check, or an initiative roll</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> An argentaurem and its partner are within 100 feet of each other</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Both the argentaurem and its partner roll and use the higher of their two results.</p>"},{"n":"Expose Flank","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a pair of argentaurems are adjacent to the same creature, they are always flanking it, regardless of their positions.</p>"},{"n":"Growl","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gold argentaurem lets out a terrifying snarl. It attempts an Intimidation check to Demoralize all enemies within 30 feet. This Intimidation check doesn't take the –4 circumstance penalty for the argentaurem not speaking a language. As usual for Demoralize, affected enemies are then temporarily immune for 10 minutes.</p>"},{"n":"Throat Rip","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The gold argen-taurem's last action was a successful gold jaws Strike against a creature that the silver argentaurem has Grabbed in its silver jaws</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gold argentaurem rips out a chunk of the creature's throat. The creature takes 2d8+9 piercing damage (DC 26 Fortitude save). On a failure, until the creature receives healing, it must succeed at a DC 5 Flat before performing an action that requires speech or the action is lost.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/hrP4KOpUZuij6t7z":{"lang":["Common","Empyrean"],"flavor":"<p>Designed after an old hunting deity's two canines, argentaurem are strong temple guardians. They always come in pairs as a giant gold hound and a smaller, faster silver hound. Since separation is physically and spiritually terrible, they always stay together. When one of the pair is killed, the other fights till the end; however, some fiends and intruders prefer to depart and leave the other to its fate.</p><h2>Built to Order</h2><p>While the original pair of argentaurem were modeled after the twin hounds of an ancient hunting deity, some modern temples have reverse-engineered the process to make guardians of their own, modeled after their deity's sacred animal. Despite their different looks, they must share many traits as the original pair, like one being gold and the other silver. They must also both be the same type of animal. The exact reason for these necessary similarities is unknown, but experiments with different forms have resulted in disastrous results.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Religion, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), holy, metal, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Metallic Pair","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Every argentaurem is created alongside a partner, with one made of gold and the other made of silver. The gold one can make gold claws and gold jaws Strikes and use Growl, while the silver one can make silver claws and silver jaws Strikes and use Retrieve. While within 100 feet of each other, the pair can communicate telepathically. If the pair is more than 100 feet apart, they are both Sickened 1 and Drained 1 and can't remove these conditions in any way. While a pair of argentaurems is two level 8 creatures, each of which rolls its own initiative and has three actions per round, an argentaurem encountered without its partner is a level 7 creature.</p>"},{"n":"Share Life","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["divine","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> An argentaurem and its partner are within 100 feet of each other</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The argentaurem and its partner's Hit Points both become equal to the average of their two totals, rounded down. The argentaurem and its partner can't use this ability again for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Share Mind","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The argentaurem and their partner must both roll a Will save, a Perception check, or an initiative roll</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> An argentaurem and its partner are within 100 feet of each other</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Both the argentaurem and its partner roll and use the higher of their two results.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Expose Flank","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a pair of argentaurems are adjacent to the same creature, they are always flanking it, regardless of their positions.</p>"},{"n":"Retrieve","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The silver argentaurem Strides once and then Leaps up to 20 feet in any direction (including vertically). If it ends this movement adjacent to a creature, it can attempt to Grapple that creature.</p>"},{"n":"Tear Wound","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The silver argentaurem's last action was a successful silver claws Strike against a creature that is taking persistent bleed damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The silver argentaurem digs its claws into the wound, pulling it open farther. The creature takes 2d6+8 piercing damage (DC 26 basic Fortitude save). On a failure, the amount of bleed damage it is taking increases by 1d6; this increase is not cumulative.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/wEW1Zqfje16Icnu1":{"lang":["Astral","Common","Thalassic","Telepathy 100 feet"],"lore":[["Quest Lore",19]],"flavor":"<p>When explorers desperately seek something far from home, their collective spirit of adventure occasionally takes on a life of its own in the churning seas of the Astral Plane. It matters not what they wish to find, whether it be the summit of the highest peaks, the resting place of a legendary artifact, or the threshold of a lost city, so long as their desire is strong and the task is daunting. When their quest ends in failure—either from the explorer's deaths or from circumstances forcing them to stop their search—their dream lives on. The dream drifts through the Astral Plane, carried on currents of yearning until it reaches the world from whence the explorers came. In this new realm, the dream takes on the form of an astral flotsam, a tangle of thoughts that use astral currents to propel themselves forward.</p><p>Astral flotsams remain single-mindedly devoted to achieving the quest that created them. The way they interact with others depends on the nature of their quest. They may actively seek allies or take extreme measures to ensure they are the first to reach their goal. Astral flotsams also vary significantly in how they respond to managing to reach their goal. Some choose a new, related goal they pursue with equal fervor or become stalwart allies and mentors to other explorers. However, the outcome is not always so rosy. A flotsam might become erratic and inconsolably distraught without their sense of purpose and simply fade out of existence.</p><h2>Astral Echoes</h2><p>While an astral flotsam doesn't precisely sleep, they do dream. In doing so, they exert a subtle influence on the dreams of susceptible creatures for miles around. Occasionally, a teen or young adult awakens from a strange dream in which they walk through a place of unearthly splendor and beauty—and when they describe this dream to a friend, they find the friend has had the same dream. In this way, just as an astral flotsam is born from the dreams of former heroes, they can give rise to a new generation of adventurers.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), shield, skill items (Arcana, Athletics, Nature, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Occult: Dream Message, Hydraulic Torrent, Water Walk), strength, sturdy, throwing, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Severed Strands","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever the astral flotsam takes slashing damage, they become Clumsy 1 until the end of their next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Psychic Vortex","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>When the astral flotsam hits with a wave crash Strike, the target must succeed at a DC 23 Will save or become Stupefied 1 (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure). This condition lasts until the target hits the astral flotsam with an attack roll, the astral flotsam fails a saving throw against a spell cast by the target, or 1 minute passes, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Drown In Thought","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The astral flotsam sends a thoughtform to overwhelm all creatures in a 30-foot cone with an onslaught of their thoughts. All creatures in the area take 6d6 mental damage (DC 25 Will save). The astral flotsam can't use Drown in Thought again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage and is Off-Guard until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is off-guard for 1 minute. The target can attempt a new DC 25 Will save at the end of each of its turns to end this condition early.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature takes double damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/uM7DcDqwVGzX2YlW":{"flavor":"<p>Crafted from the remnants of failed attempts to create foci for planar travel, attunement arsenals are hulking patchworks of magical foci that retain some of their teleportation enchantments. With their lingering magic, these creatures shunt their foes through space, either drawing them in close enough to smash over the head or subjecting them to the nauseating effects of random and unrefined teleportation. Attunement arsenals are typically found guarding their creators or keeping watch over precious belongings.</p><p>Although technically a sign of repeated failures, attunement arsenals represent a spellcaster's diligence in learning advanced forms of magic. Their creators often take as much pride in displaying them as warriors do in showing off their scars.</p><h2>Cosmic Tuners</h2><p>While an attunement arsenal typically consists of a conglomeration of crystals and exotic metals, it can take on a variety of shapes. It might look like a crystalline giant or beast, a walking ring of black stone, a colored lens that reflects scenes from other worlds, an inverted bell made of a green skymetal alloy, or an arrangement of glowing runes orbiting each other in a vaguely humanoid shape. Regardless of its shape, the arsenal is adorned with antennae that function as tuning forks, capturing the sound of the cosmic void.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), metal, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Discordant Echo","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The attunement arsenal takes damage from a Strike with a metal weapon or an unarmed attack that is made of metal</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Each creature in a 10-foot emanation takes 4d6 sonic damage (DC 30 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Sonic Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Any time an attunement arsenal would be affected by an effect with the sonic trait, it loses any slowed condition it has and gains HP equal to half the damage the spell would have dealt. If the arsenal starts its turn in an area that deals sonic damage, it gains 2d8 healing HP. It doesn't gain these benefits from its own abilities.</p>"},{"n":"Vulnerable to Teleportation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a spell with the teleportation trait is cast within 10 feet of the attunement arsenal, the attunement arsenal is teleported up to 10 feet in a direction of the caster's choice and loses its resistance to physical damage for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Spatial Shift","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The attunement arsenal's last action was a successful syncopated slam Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The attunement arsenal teleports to an open space within 10 feet of the target of its Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Disorienting Relocation","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["arcane","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The attunement arsenal's teleportation beam erratically relocates its targets. Due to the flaws in the foci that make up the arsenal's body, this teleportation is unsteady, making it stomach-churning to endure. A creature hit by a teleportation beam Strike must attempt a DC 33 Fortitude save. This teleportation is forced movement. It can move targets into hazardous spaces, but it can't place them in occupied spaces or inside of a solid wall; if it would otherwise do so, it instead moves the target as far as it can in its randomly selected direction.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature can teleport 5 feet in a direction of its choice.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature teleports 5 feet in a random direction.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature teleports 15 feet in a random direction and is Sickened 1.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature teleports 30 feet in a random direction and is Sickened 2.</p>"},{"n":"Rending Warp","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","sonic","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The attunement arsenal creates a resounding clang with its fists, activating the planar-hopping items that make up its form and pulling nearby creatures into unstable portals. All creatures in a 20-foot emanation take 5d8 sonic damage (DC 33 basic Reflex save). Creatures that critically fail are also teleported to an open space that is 10 feet closer to the attunement arsenal, or adjacent to the arsenal if they were less than 15 feet away; this is forced movement.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/izlYqIkyX7xkjMJf":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Melodian","Muan","Requian","Sussuran"],"flavor":"<p>Autumnals are melancholy fey that linger in a forest for a single season each year. They arrive just as summer's golden sun begins to gild the first green leaves, and they part after the earth has embraced a forest's last such leaf. Autumnals cull the sick and old so their mulch might fortify vivacious young saplings against winter's privations.</p><p>A grove under the care of a \"reaping\" (between four and ten autumnals, usually) is unmistakable, filled as it is with the mournful dirges the fey sing as they work. A reaping is led by an elder of thirty or forty seasons, who often have access to more advanced primal magic.</p><p>Like many forest fey, autumnals are deeply tied to their woodland domains and viciously fight anyone who either preempts or stalls the seasonal cycle of the year. However, one who earns the trust of an autumnal, perhaps by aiding them in purging the woods of unnatural forces, gains more than just a potent ally. They might be gifted with secrets of the forest beyond the ken of even druids or nymphs: the last whispered revelations of ancient oaks, or perhaps the confessions of primordial juggernauts before they finally slipped from this world. These ageless mysteries might have been lost forever had they not been captured and immortalized in the sorrowful songs of the autumnal choir.</p><hr><p>Seasonals are a cycle of four kinds of fey, each tied to one of the four seasons: vernals who embody spring's renewal and storming showers, aestivals who embody summer's lazy heat and burning passion, autumnals who embody the melancholy culling and reaping harvest of autumn, and hibernals who embody winter's quiet cheer.</p><p>Whatever the current season in the mortal world, seasonals wrap themselves in a metaphysical cloak of their own season's magic and carry the songs full of that season's surging emotions with them wherever they go in the mortal world or across the realm of Faerie. Whenever seasonals meet, shy hibernals typically prefer to amuse themselves by watching the antics of the other three kinds of fey, while vernals impatiently call for energetic contests, aestivals languidly nap until roused to fiery bursts of activity, and autumnals attempt to imbue a sense of stark responsibility in the rest of their compatriots. Exceptions exist, with those seasonals interacting with the others in ways unexpected based upon the stereotypes of their season.</p><h2>Autumnal Dirges</h2><p>Autumnals share the history of their forests through their songs: haunting dirges that honor the dying moments of anyone who has drawn their last breath within the autumnals' domain. However, this history is often couched in metaphor that mortals find difficult to parse. Understanding the true meaning of an autumnal dirge might require a successful DC 20 Fey Lore, DC 22 Nature, or DC 24 Performance check.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, constitution, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality, void), music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Autumn's Howl, Gust of Wind, Rushing Gust, Songshroud, Speak with Plants), tangle shield, wild, winged, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Life from Death","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["healing","primal","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature within 30 feet dies</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The dying creature is level 2 or higher</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The autumnal is strengthened by the fleeting life essence, gaining 5 temporary Hit Points that last for 1 minute.</p><p>Effect: Life from Death</p>"},{"n":"Whirling Cloak","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Leaves swirl about the autumnal whenever they use a move action to move at least 5 feet, granting them concealment until the end of their current turn.</p>"},{"n":"Dance of Leaves","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The autumnal Strides or Flies twice, making a leaf blade Strike against a creature at any time during the movement.</p>"},{"n":"Mark for Reaping","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","curse","death","misfortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The autumnal singles out a creature within 60 feet for reaping. A mark of a scythe appears on the creature's body, which must then roll a DC 20 Fortitude save to determine the mark's duration. While the mark lasts, any autumnal who Strikes the marked creature does an additional 1d4 precision damage. This damage is void if the marked creature is living, or vitality if they are undead. In addition, if the marked creature is living, it must roll twice and take the lower result on any recovery checks to reduce its dying condition. The mark disappears automatically if the autumnal uses Mark for Reaping on another creature or if the autumnal dies.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The mark disappears immediately.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The mark lasts for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The mark lasts for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The mark lasts for 10 minutes.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/5bDdx5eJRbuMXjrp":{"flavor":"<p>Legendary, gravity-defying beanstalks known as azappuses grow rapidly, seemingly endlessly, eventually puncturing the clouds. While the mature stalks primarily threaten soil nutrients, the supermassive seeds of azappuses present a different kind of threat as they magically glide on zephyrs or gales like dandelion seeds, their four vines flailing and their filaments dancing with sizzling lightning currents.</p><p>Azappus seeds gravitate toward fertile soil near water sources, such as most farmlands. When they find a potential place to germinate, they conjure brutal winds and summon pulverizing lightning to raze buildings, slay nearby creatures for fertilizer, and soften the ground. Unfortunately for farming communities, the seeds are picky and often destroy multiple sites before choosing a favorite.</p><p>In some regions, fear of the seeds' destructive power has morphed into mystical interpretations of their origins. Since these vandals seemingly manifest from the sky, some people speculate that azappuses are created by the gods or even that they are minor divinities in their own right. Reports from the few who have climbed all the way to the top of an azappus stalk speak of strange, magical realms above the clouds inhabited by creatures of air.</p><h2>Shocked</h2><p>A shocked creature is electrically charged, making it easy for electricity to harm it. It's off-guard against attacks that deal electricity damage (even if it's only additional electricity damage, such as a shock weapon) and takes a –2 circumstance penalty on Fortitude and Reflex saving throws against electricity effects. The shocked condition normally lasts for the duration listed, but it ends early if the creature takes any amount of electricity damage from a source other than persistent electricity damage or if they spend an Interact action to discharge the electricity harmlessly. This condition first appeared in Battlezoo Eldamon.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,250/2,800/5,150 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, dexterity, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), music, sensory (darkvision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, wild, winged, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Stormglide","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An azappus seed can navigate even the most perilous gusts with ease. It does not treat wind as difficult terrain or need to Maneuver in Flight in high winds.</p>"},{"n":"Superstorm Aura","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["air","aura","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. Powerful winds buffet and propel the azappus seed. The air within the aura is difficult terrain for flying creatures that do not have the air trait. Creatures starting their turn in the aura that are not Grabbed by the azappus seed must attempt a DC 35 Fortitude save. Flying creatures that fail are pushed just outside of the aura, while creatures standing on the ground or another solid surface are knocked Prone.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Greater Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>3d12+7 bludgeoning, DC 37 basic Fortitude</p><hr><p><p>The monster deals the listed amount of damage to any number of creatures Grabbed or Restrained by it. Each of those creatures can attempt a basic Fortitude save with the listed DC. A creature that fails this save falls Unconscious, and a creature that succeeds is then temporarily immune to falling unconscious from Greater Constrict for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Crushing Gale","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The azappus seed generates a cylinder of tornado-force winds within 60 feet that blow directly down toward the ground, affecting all creatures in the area except itself. The cylinder has a 25-foot radius and is 100 feet tall. The cylinder sends airborne creatures careening toward the ground. Flying creatures in the area that fail a DC 37 Reflex save are launched toward the ground in a bubble of air, taking 4d6 untyped damage as they land; they are knocked Prone on a critical failure. If the cylinder includes the ground, all creatures and unattended objects on the ground then take 15d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 37 basic Fortitude save). The azappus seed can't use Crushing Gale for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Lightning Storm","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The azappus seed fills the air around it with devastating arcs of lightning. All creatures in a 30-foot emanation take 4d12 electricity damage (DC 37 basic Reflex save). Creatures that fail the save are Shocked.</p>"},{"n":"Thunderpeal","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The azappus seed's last action was Lightning Storm</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A deafening roar of thunder emanates from the azappus seed, dealing 5d6 sonic damage to each creature in a 60-foot emanation (DC 37 basic Fortitude save). Creatures within the superstorm aura that fail are Deafened for 1 minute, or for 24 hours on a critical failure.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/MRn81FXBaF5wCvDM":{"lang":["Common","can't speak any language"],"lore":[["Magic Item Lore",7]],"flavor":"<p>Curselings pose as magic items to trick hapless explorers into donning them. Once equipped, they dutifully provide magic benefits, while secretly injecting their eggs into their wearer's bloodstream.</p><p>A beastmaster's curseling has a beastmaster's helm as its default form. Other curselings have their own default forms.</p><h2>Curseling Varieties</h2><p>Curselings always appear as invested magic items that grant an item bonus to a skill. While most curselings can grant a +1 item bonus, curselings exposed to potent surges of magic occasionally mutate into stronger forms that gain combat prowess and can reshape into higher-level items. In general, much like the beastmaster's helm, these items should be a bit more versatile than normal for items of their level, and unavailable through other means. There's nothing worse for a curseling than to be tossed into a spacious pouch with the other items that \"might be useful someday.\"</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Lore [Magic Item])</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Magic Item Lore","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>This skill pertains to magic items, both knowing about what types of magic exist and Identifying Magic to determine the properties of magic items.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Inject Venom","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a creature invests a curseling that is using item mimicry, the curseling injects the creature with its egg-laden venom. The creature must attempt a DC 16 Fortitude save. The creature only notices the injection on a critical success.</p><p><strong>Curseling Venom</strong> (curse, mental, poison)</p><p><strong>Onset</strong> 1 day</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> The curseling establishes a mystical bond to its wearer. The wearer becomes unwilling to remove the item, even during circumstances like bathing or sleeping where keeping the item on is unusual. Removing the item forcefully requires a successful Athletics check against the higher of the curseling's Fortitude DC and the wearer's Fortitude DC and deals 1d8 persistent bleed damage to the wearer. Removing the curseling at this stage breaks the curse. (1 week)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> As stage 1, but removing the curseling no longer breaks the curse. It continues to take up an invested item slot and can choose to return to the wearer 24 hours later. The wearer develops a compulsion to hoard metal. (1 week)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> The creature is compelled to gather its metal hoard and then seek out a fire to fall asleep next to. If it falls asleep next to a fire, its body explodes in flames and juvenile curselings emerge from its corpse (1 week).</p>"},{"n":"Item Mimicry","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The curseling takes the form of a magic item. It can be worn and invested, granting the item's usual benefits. As long as the curseling remains perfectly still, its identity is difficult to discern. Using Identify Magic to recognize a curseling requires a critical success. By default, a beastmaster's curseling takes the form of its own bespoke magic item called a <em>beastmaster's helm</em>.</p><p>A <em>beastmaster's helm</em> appears to be an uncommon primal invested 4th-level item with — Bulk. While wearing this helm on its head, a creature gains a +1 item bonus to Nature checks, which increases to +2 for Nature checks to Command an Animal, as well as a +1 circumstance bonus to Initiative rolls. The wearer can also use Animal Speech once per hour.</p><p><strong>Animal Speech</strong> 2 (concentrate, manipulate, primal)</p><p>The wearer can ask questions of, receive answers from, and use the Diplomacy skill with animals for 1 minute.</p><p>By spending 1 week reshaping its form, the beastmaster's curseling can reforge itself into the cursed version of a common worn and invested magic item of level 4 or lower that grants a +1 item bonus to a skill, or back from one of these forms into its <em>beastmaster's helm</em> form.</p>"},{"n":"Retreat into Shell","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The curseling pulls its body into its protective item shell. It gains Hardness 3, which lasts until it spends an action with the move or attack trait.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/nLH70MUha0HBFJAD":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Melodian"],"flavor":"<p>Beat hoppers are rare fey whose performances affect the internal clocks of creatures hearing them. Their long, grasshopper-like bodies and powerful legs allow them to make percussive beats, which they accompany with their smooth singing voices.</p><p>Beat hoppers are typically pacifistic, good-natured, and fun-loving, but they severely lack common sense when it comes to the timing of their performances, which can cause them to become inadvertent instigators. The time-altering music that beat hoppers play with their legs affects friends and enemies equally, so things can become truly chaotic when a beat hopper decides to perform a lively tune during a tavern brawl.</p><p>Most beat hoppers are attuned to three different tempos, each of which matches one of their magical performances. Trying to use a tempo that is drastically faster or slower than their familiar beats is dangerous for a beat hopper, as their hearts synchronize to the music's rhythm. Beat hoppers can learn to do so by dedicating over a century to the careful training of the body and song. Those who manage this feat are known as beat hopper maestros. Maestros deepen their mastery of occult magic and expand the range of spells that they can augment with their inexorable beats.</p><h2>Ill Timing</h2><p>For creatures so attuned to variations in timing, beat hoppers have a surprisingly abysmal grasp of the future. They're poor planners when it comes to putting aside the necessities of life, and they often encourage others to adopt a similar come-what-may attitude. When a beat hopper sees people working together to bring in a harvest or to prepare defenses against an impending attack, the beat hopper might encourage workers to take \"just a little time out\" for a quick dance or ten. Without intending the slightest bit of harm, a beat hopper can easily bring ruin to a community.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Occultism, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (sonic, vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Occult: Blur, Heroism, Noise Blast, Paralyze, Songshroud, Telekinetic Projectile), time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Friend To All","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>By default, all creatures are considered allies to the beat hopper for the composition spells. A creature can attempt a DC 22 Will save at the start of its turn to avoid being treated as the beat hopper's ally for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Natural Metronome","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The beat hopper is always moving their body to a beat. If they can't do so for whatever reason (such as by being Immobilized or Paralyzed), they are Sickened 1 until they can follow the beat again.</p>"},{"n":"Resonant Tempo","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tempo of the beat hopper's composition spells resonates profoundly through the bodies of those who hear them, generating the following additional effects.</p><ul><li><strong>Courageous Anthem</strong> This inspiring performance helps steady the hearts and bodies of those who hear it. In addition to its normal effects, creatures affected by this performance are unaffected by the Quickened and slowed conditions, and they are also unaffected by both status bonuses and status penalties to their Speeds.</li><li><strong>Rallying Anthem</strong> Creatures affected by this gradually building performance focus so intently on preparations to shore up their defenses that they lose some of their offensive power. In addition to its normal effects, hey take a –10-foot status penalty to their Speeds and a –1 status penalty to attack rolls.</li><li><strong>Triple Time</strong> Creatures affected by this frantic performance are quickened, in addition to the performance's normal effects. They can only use this extra action to Stride their full Speed (if they want to go somewhere nearby, they can do so by taking a circuitous path or circling back).</li></ul>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/MxfZ3ij71GSle8RC":{"flavor":"<p>At first glance, this plant resembles a massive fern, with spiky leaves sprouting along thin, rigid branches. A big, reddish-brown fruit grows at the center of the plant, almost like a heart. The fruit digests ambient void energy as well as void energy extracted from other rich sources. While a blood fern's favored prey are undead creatures, the ferns lack the intelligence to distinguish between living and dead, so they attack all that cross their paths indiscriminately. The only surefire way to pacify a blood fern is to feed it enough void energy to calm it into a temporary stupor.</p><p>Blood ferns spontaneously arise in the aftermath of major outbursts of void energy. These events tend to corrupt the land and kill most living creatures. Sometimes, however, plants can withstand these energies and absorb them. In some cases, this energy is so overwhelming that it changes the physiology of the plant itself, slowly turning it into a blood fern in the span of a couple of weeks.</p><p>Some alchemical manuals posit that the nectar from a blood fern's fruit could be brewed into a concoction that would allow a living creature to walk the line between life and death, but experiments on this subject have all too often ended in tragedy. Vampires use the nectar in rituals to create sanguine roses, in place of the typical black onyx.</p><h2>Blood Fern Hollows</h2><p>Druids and other stewards of nature often hold conflicting opinions regarding blood ferns. On one hand, blood ferns are not natural creations, and in an area where void energy isn't prevalent, the ferns can upset the region's delicate ecosystem. On the other hand, given time, blood ferns can cleanse a forest tainted with void energy, filtering the energy from their surroundings like a normal plant would purify an atmosphere of toxic gases. This is why some druids intentionally cultivate stands of blood ferns, hiding the plants in tucked-away hollows until they are required. When the forest faces invasion, the druids might herd the intruders into these hollows or activate and march the ferns directly toward the threat.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, death, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (void), force, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, unholy, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Camouflage","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The blood fern can Hide in natural forest or grassland environments even if it doesn't have cover.</p>"},{"n":"Toxic Burst","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["divine","poison","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature bites into the blood fern (such as by making a successful jaws Strike)</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The poisonous fruit at the center of the blood fern explodes in a burst of toxic nectar. All creatures in a 20-foot emanation take 3d6 poison plus 3d6 void damage (DC 32 basic Fortitude save). The blood fern can't use Toxic Burst again until the fruit regrows, a process that takes about 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Toxic Finale","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a blood fern is destroyed while its fruit is still intact, the fruit automatically explodes. This has the effects of Toxic Burst.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Draining Roots","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The strong roots of the blood fern dig in search of fresh blood. At the end of the blood fern's turn, all creatures Grabbed by the blood fern take 3d6 void damage (DC 29 basic Fortitude save). Undead grabbed by the blood fern instead take 3d6 force damage, as the plant leeches void energy from their bodies. The blood fern can maintain a grapple with up to four creatures with its roots.</p>"},{"n":"Uproot","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The blood fern uproots itself and pounces atop its prey, crushing them beneath its strong roots. The fern drops all grappled creatures, then Leaps 10 feet into an area that contains at least one creature. Each creature in the area where the blood fern lands takes 4d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 29 basic Reflex save). The blood fern then immediately attempts Athletics checks to grapple up to four creatures that failed the Reflex save; these attempts neither apply nor count toward the blood fern's multiple attack penalty. All creatures in the area are then moved in the closest unoccupied square adjacent to the blood fern; this is forced movement.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/IT3gZsPrpSKbLaBB":{"lang":["Fey"],"flavor":"<p>Standing over ten feet tall, the large sapient mushrooms known as bolbaloses live in densely forested areas where moisture and shade abound. Their two large oval eyes, which range in shades from deep red to pitch black, move independently from one another, each attached to its two-foot-long eyestalk. A bolbalos stores decaying matter for later digestion in the protruding belly on its bulky stem, which also serves as its primary means of defense.</p><p>Most bolbaloses are notoriously friendly and quick to approach unfamiliar faces. Their curiosity drives them to ask endless questions and to seek ways to help the travelers they come across. If they can't provide the assistance that someone requests, they try to find someone else who can. While bolbaloses prefer nonviolent solutions whenever possible, they begrudgingly fight to defend themselves and their allies from harm. People who have committed terrible crimes sometimes flee to a bolbalos's territory for protection, trusting the giant mushrooms to shelter them from anyone who might come after them for justice or vengeance.</p><p>Though bolbaloses can live for centuries, their numbers are few. Their delicate spores can only germinate with a precise combination of weather conditions and sufficient primal magic. Additionally, few bolbaloses survive to die of natural causes before their curiosity and jovial meddling get them in over their heads.</p><h2>Ever the Optimists</h2><p>Bolbaloses are often wise enough to recognize people of ill intent but idealistic enough to believe that, with the right nudge (and perhaps a sternly delivered but well-meaning tummy whack or two), they can be encouraged to change for the better. A fugitive from justice that begs for sanctuary among bolbaloses is sure to receive aid. These bolbalos reclamation projects have had mixed success; while some guests take advantage of their hosts' protection in order to continue their evil ways, others are genuinely redeemed by the bolbaloses' trust and kindness.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, body, bane, charisma, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (acid, vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, tangle shield, time, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bouncy","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An enemy critically fails a Strike against the bolbalos</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The bolbalos chooses a target within reach or range of the triggering attack, using themselves as the origin of the attack. The attacker then rerolls the attack against the new target.</p>"},{"n":"Curious Nature","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Bolbaloses are intrinsically curious and trusting of others. During the first round of combat, the bolbalos is Off-Guard until they have acted. If the penalty to AC from being off-guard causes them to take damage, they are Quickened for 1 round. They can use this extra action only to Stride or Step.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Push","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Push in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Shove the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Improved Push lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pushed on a success to that distance.</p></p>"},{"n":"Sticky Slime","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A Large or smaller creature hit by the bolbalos's red slime is covered by quickly hardening goo and must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes a –10 foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds for 1 round or until it Escapes.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, and the creature is Sickened 1.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Immobilized for 1 round or until it Escapes and is sickened 1.</p>"},{"n":"Trample","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Medium or smaller, belly, DC 28 basic Reflex. A creature that critically fails the saving throw is knocked Prone.</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of creatures of the listed size, Trampling each creature whose space it enters. The monster can attempt to Trample the same creature only once in a single use of Trample. The monster deals the damage of the listed Strike, but trampled creatures can attempt a basic Reflex save at the listed DC (no damage on a critical success, half damage on a success, double damage on a critical failure).</p></p>"},{"n":"Ponderous Push","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Bolbaloses thoroughly enjoy moving things around with their bellies. They can Shove creatures using only their bellies, and they can Shove creatures up to two sizes larger than themselves.</p>"},{"n":"Blundering Slam","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The bolbalos spins around, haphazardly slamming any enemies surrounding them. They make a belly Strike against each enemy within reach. These attacks count toward their multiple attack penalty, but their multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after all the attacks. After using Blundering Slam, the bolbalos becomes Stunned 1 and can't use this ability again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/DWWWNXYafjswVMIL":{"lang":["Petran","Voltan"],"notes":{"speed":"earth glide"},"flavor":"<p>When vast storms of elemental electricity roam across reality, they are drawn toward substances that effectively conduct their might. Most of the time, the lightning that strikes out of these storms leaves nothing but destruction in its wake. However, when the lightning makes contact with the slumbering potential within conductive crystal, it can sometimes awaken a new elemental consciousness known as a boltspiker. The fusion of the crystal's stability and the lightning's vitality makes for a mercurial creature prone to extremes.</p><p>Boltspikers are tempestuous beings that are quick to develop affection for treasured people, locations, and objects, which they guard jealously. These affections often lead them to settle down for a while, becoming stalwart and reliable companions. But their loyalty is not destined to last; when they come upon something new that they wish to cherish, they abandon what they once cared for with alacrity and ease.</p><h2>System Shock</h2><p>A boltspiker's life often begins with tragedy. Drawn to living creatures by an instinct for love and protection, a newly animated boltspiker might innocently reach out to make contact with a new friend—only to find that the electricity that provides life to a boltspiker has the opposite effect on most other living beings. It can take a boltspiker some time before it learns to control the lightning that runs through it, allowing it to clasp hands with a friend without devastating consequences.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, earth, earth armor, electricity, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), light, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Glow","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","light"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. The lightning that makes up a boltspiker's body emits bright light. The boltspiker can spend one action to shroud the glow within their crystals, after which the aura remains suppressed until they spend one action to reactivate it.</p>"},{"n":"Electrical Discharge","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature touches the boltspiker or Strikes them with a metal weapon in melee</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Electricity surges into the triggering creature, dealing 2d12 electricity damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Boltspike","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a boltspiker hits a creature with a crystal lance Strike, they attempt to stake them to the ground. The target must attempt a DC 22 Fortitude save. A creature that fails a check to Escape from the spike takes 1d6 electricity damage.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 1d6 electricity damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes 1d6 persistent electricity damage and takes a –10 foot status penalty to their Speeds until they Escape.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes 1d6 persistent electricity damage and becomes Immobilized until they Escape.</p>"},{"n":"Glide Charge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The boltspiker's crystals gather power as they move through earthen material. The boltspiker Burrows at least 10 feet and then attempts a lightning whip Strike. If the Strike hits, it deals an additional 1d12 electricity damage.</p>"},{"n":"Ride the Lightning","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The boltspiker generates and then rides a powerful electric current. They move up to 150 feet in a straight line through the air without triggering reactions. If they are above the ground at the end of this movement, they fall safely to the ground at the end of their turn.</p>"},{"n":"Earth Glide","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The boltspiker can Burrow through any earthen matter, including rock, as well as unworked metal and gemstone deposits. When they do so, the boltspiker moves at their full burrow Speed, leaving no tunnels or signs of their passing.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/cIJGmU6O0zRfhzos":{"lang":["Aklo","Common","Any four additional languages"],"lore":[["Library Lore",17]],"flavor":"<p>Book beasts are shapeshifters with a relentless appetite for knowledge. Unfortunately for academics everywhere, a book beast's method of absorbing information involves literally consuming the volume. Book beasts are fond of disguising themselves as intriguing books, hoping that an unsuspecting scholar will carry them to a new library.</p><p>When a book beast eats text containing arcane formulas, they can internalize the knowledge and apply it to cast spells. While a typical book beast resonates with arcane magic, there are rumors of book beasts tied to other traditions, like esoteric tomes with occult spells or prayer books with divine spells.</p><h2>Don't Judge a Book Beast by its Cover</h2><p>It's rarely possible to discern a book beast's strength by their appearance. What appears to be a ratty and worn-out volume might contain the knowledge of centuries, while a shiny new tome might be a young book beast just starting out on their journey of collection.</p><p>On the other hand, young book beasts sometimes choose to replicate older books for their gravitas, while elders might decide to mimic the latest arcane treatise to catch a wizard's eye. The main difference between elder book beasts and their juniors is that elders can sometimes consume enough arcane material to develop the capacity to cast more numerous and powerful spells.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Arcana, Athletics, Crafting, Lore [Library], Society, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, air, body, bane, burning barbs, dexterity, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, vitality), fire, fire shield, intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Arcane: Agonizing Relocation, Blazing Bolt, Blood Vendetta, Caustic Blast, Command, Detect Magic, Fear, Haste, Ignition, Levitate, Lightning Bolt, Locate, Mindlink, Reverberating Pain, Tailwind, Telekinetic Hand, Translate), wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Internal Library","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The book beast has eaten tomes on a wide variety of subjects over the course of their lifetime. They can roll a Library Lore check at a –2 penalty in place of any check to Recall Knowledge.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Book Form","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The book beast changes its appearance into that of a Tiny book or reverts their appearance back to their true form. When taking the form of a book, the book beast can choose what their cover looks like as well as the text and illustrations that appear on each page. If the content includes complex diagrams or illustrations, the book beast must roll a Crafting check to determine their quality. In addition to replicating non-magical books, a book beast can take the form of a spellbook that contains all of the spontaneous spells they can cast. The book beast can't make Strikes in its Book Form.</p>"},{"n":"Digestive Synthesis","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The book beast is adjacent to an unattended book, scroll, or other written work</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The book beast spends 1 minute to physically devour the written work, absorbing the knowledge held within as if it had read the entire text. If the writing contains knowledge of specific arcane spells, such as a wizard's spellbook or a scroll, the book beast can freely retrain any number of their spontaneous spells into arcane spells of the same rank from the devoured source.</p>"},{"n":"Sticky Page","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a book beast successfully Grabs a creature larger than themselves with their jaws, they exude bookbinding glue from their mouth and then attach their cover to that creature. The Grabbed creature is not Immobilized, but if it moves, the book beast moves with it. While the book beast is attached, the book beast is Off-Guard and the attached creature takes a –2 circumstance penalty to its saving throws against the book beast's spells. If the attached creature is Medium or smaller, it also takes a –5-foot status penalty to its Speeds.</p><p>Effect: Sticky Page</p>"},{"n":"Ordinary Book","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The book beast is in its Book Form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Until the book beast acts, they resemble an inanimate book. They have an automatic result of 30 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as a book.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zRZ4Rp9CgVyLDfSw":{"lang":["Aetheran","Aklo","Common"],"flavor":"<p>Braincases are the survivors of a long-lost ritual devised by people who sought to master elemental force by transcending their bodies. Practitioners of the rite are undaunted by the grotesque transformation into a floating brain inside a transparent cranium; instead, they pity those who must dedicate their time to activities like eating and sleeping.</p><p>Braincases are cunning, domineering, and egotistical. They are rarely willing to go along with anyone else's ideas. Though they prefer to avoid the risks of combat against creatures whose strength approaches their own, once battle begins, they seldom surrender until their brain is exposed.</p><h2>Brain Trusts</h2><p>Braincases generally lack the patience required to work with others, but when the opinions of two or more braincases align, they may join together to form a braintrust. Thankfully, a braintrust is usually short-lived, disbanding as soon as its members discover even the slightest difference of opinion. Until then, the effectiveness of multiple braincases working together can be truly terrifying.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> armor (light, medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Deception, Intimidation, Occultism, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), force, intelligence, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Knock Heads, Telekinetic Haul, Telekinetic Maneuver, Telekinetic Storm, Wall of Force), sturdy, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Cranium Shell","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time the braincase would be reduced to 0 HP, they are reduced to 1 HP instead as the shell shatters, exposing their brain. The shell remains broken until the braincase is restored to full HP. While their brain is exposed, any damage the braincase takes kills them regardless of their current HP.</p>"},{"n":"Don't You Dare!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The braincase is the target of a ranged attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The braincase attempts to Demoralize the attacking creature. This ignores any temporary immunity the creature has to Demoralize attempts from the braincase and doesn't make them temporarily immune to Demoralize.</p>"},{"n":"Obstructing Palm","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 20 feet moves closer to the braincase</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The braincase is in Six Palms Stance</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The braincase makes a force palm Strike against the creature. If the Strike critically hits, the creature is also knocked Prone, interrupting the movement. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the braincase's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Force Bubble","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["force","manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The braincase molds an opaque bubble of magical force around a creature within 60 feet (DC 33 Reflex negates). On a failed save, the creature is Immobilized and Blinded until the bubble is popped (AC 10, Hardness 5, HP 30, BT 15); the bubble breaks automatically after 1 minute. Bubbles are immune to critical hits and precision damage. At the end of the braincase's turn, they can affect each active force bubble, moving it up to 30 feet, popping it, or both.</p>"},{"n":"Six Palms Stance","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","force","occult","stance"],"f":"","d":"<p>The braincase creates a massive, six-armed torso from magical force. Theirs size increases to Gargantuan, and they can use their force palm Strike and Obstructing Palm reaction. They gain an additional reaction each round, but their second reaction must be used for a different action than their first reaction. The stance breaks if the braincase Casts a Spell.</p>"},{"n":"Telekinetic Shove","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The braincase's last action was a successful telekinetic beam Strike against a creature of size Huge or smaller</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The braincase attempts to push the creature away, rolling a spell attack roll against the creature's Fortitude DC. On a success, the creature is pushed back 20 feet (40 feet on a critical success). This attack roll neither applies nor counts toward the braincase's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Telekinetic Trip","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The braincase's last action was a successful force palm Strike against a creature of size Huge or smaller</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The braincase attempts to Trip the creature, using their spell attack modifier in place of their Athletics modifier. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the braincase's multiple attack penalty.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Hu4oKiWwfFaTfExj":{"lang":["Fey","can't speak, Speak with animals"],"flavor":"<p>Cabyar are large rodents whose long and brittle fur is interspersed with tiny specks of magical light. Naturally gifted at manipulating magical energies, these gentle creatures gather in large families in the margins of creeks or small lakes, preferring locations secluded from potential predators. They guard their homes by befriending the local wildlife, using magic when more mundane means fail.</p><p>When travelers arrive in their forested homes, cabyars regard them with caution, bending light to hide their bodies while observing the newcomers to discern their intentions. If the newcomers are peaceful, the cabyars are likely to invite them to play, particularly if the newcomers are accompanied by animal friends of their own. For those who prove entertaining, the cabyars might share their knowledge of the forest, from high-quality foraging spots to dangers best avoided. Though cabyars are quite well-informed about their surroundings, they can only communicate with animals, so their insights are often communicated in gestures or paraphrased through an animal's perspective.</p><p>However, if the newcomers instead bring harm to any of the cabyar's companions, the cabyar mobilizes the rest of their animal allies to drive them off, using lethal force if necessary. There is no shortage of would-be cabyar hunters with tales of approaching a cabyar's pond, only to be thwarted by the pounce of a large feline.</p><h2>Dangerous Game</h2><p>A cabyar's pelt can be used as an ingredient in the recipes for several magical items—but while cabyar hunting is not uncommon, the market for its products is limited. Good-hearted buyers generally don't want anything to do with hunting such gentle and helpful creatures, while less ethical buyers understand the danger of walking around the forest wearing a cabyar pelt. It's a quick way to earn the enmity of many wilderness creatures, from other cabyars to powerful fey.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Nature, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), light, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Occult: Charm, Hypnotize, Soothe, Speak with Animals), strength, throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Kaleidoscopic Coat","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The cabyar can change the colorful patterns of their fur to better blend in with their surroundings, allowing them to Hide in areas of dim or bright light even when they don't have cover.</p>"},{"n":"Practiced Diver","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The cabyar can hold their breath for up to 5 minutes before they begin to drown.</p>"},{"n":"Flash of Colors","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The cabyar is targeted by an attack, and they can see the attacker</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cabyar swiftly flashes the multi-colored patterns of their fur, gaining concealment against the triggering attack.</p>"},{"n":"Radiance","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The cabyar is practiced at shining their light beams directly into their target's eyes. A creature hit by a light beam Strike must succeed at a DC 20 Fortitude save or be Dazzled for 1 round. On a critical failure, or if the creature was already dazzled, it is instead Blinded for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Entrancing Pelt","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","mental","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The cabyar twirls around, causing light to dance through its shimmering pelt. Creatures in a 20-foot emanation must attempt a DC 22 Will save. Once a creature attempts this Will save, it is immune to any cabyar's Entrancing Pelt for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Off-Guard for one round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Dazzled and off-guard for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled and off-guard for 1 round and Stunned 1.</p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Jni4fknPPMHgW0u9":{"lang":["Common","Fey"],"flavor":"<p>Most cactopies are patient creatures that prize contemplation. They spend most of their time sunbathing and absorbing water and nutrients from the ground. While they enjoy solitary meditation, cactopies also appreciate conversations about philosophy. It's typical for a gathering of cactopies to remain quiet for long periods, punctuated by a poetical statement from one of its members that the others then reflect upon.</p><p>Between these tranquil times, cactopies experience bursts of energy and enthusiasm, during which they venture out in search of new experiences, traveling as fast as their eight roots can carry them. Though meditating cactopies might find their active brethren exhausting, they recognize the value these periods hold for bringing new ideas to their communities.</p><p>Though they superficially resemble cacti, cactopies maintain an almost perfect spherical shape, topped with a single large flower that emits a pleasant fragrance. While this fragrance does an excellent job of drawing in pollinators, it also muddles the minds of sapient creatures, often inspiring them to a noisy sort of violence that cactopies detest.</p><p>When threatened, cactopies defend themselves with their long roots and their numerous fast-growing spines. They can also shower threats with hallucinogenic pollen from their flower, which inflicts them with a deep-seated fear of the cactopies' piercing spines.</p><h2>Collected Works</h2><p>On occasion, the cactopie's fabled wisdom inspires an ambitious scribe to seek a colony of cactopies and record their sage words. While many such works have been started, few have been completed. For one thing, cactopie conversation flows at a pace most creatures find intolerably slow, and much of it is composed of replies to questions posed decades before. It's also difficult to complete a book when every stray gust of wind brings the threat of inhaling a whiff of cactopie perfume, causing you to run amok and destroy your manuscript in the process.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Athletics, Nature, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), strength, tangle shield, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Curious Aroma","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","mental","olfactory","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. A creature entering or starting its turn in the cactopie's aura must succeed at a DC 19 Will save or become Confused for 1 round. A creature who succeeds at the save becomes temporarily immune to curious aroma for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Bed of Spines","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The cactopie has a creature Grabbed in one of their roots</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cactopie attempts to pull the creature in and smash it against their spiny body. The creature attempts a DC 22 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is moved to a square adjacent to the cactopie of the cactopie's choice. This is forced movement.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, but the creature also takes 2d4+2 piercing damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As success, but the creature also takes 4d4+4 piercing damage and 1d6 persistent bleed damage.</p>"},{"n":"Pollen Burst","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The cactopie unleashes a cloud of toxic pollen from their flower in a 20-foot emanation. Each creature in the area must attempt a DC 19 Will save. Creatures that are frightened from Pollen Burst gain weakness to piercing 5 for as long as they remain frightened. The cactopie can't use Pollen Burst again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 3.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/NbIYYVxOBB3Zq3Oj":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth"],"lore":[["Engineering Lore",22]],"gear":["Thieves' Toolkit (Infiltrator)","Shortsword","Warhammer"],"flavor":"<p>Driven by a storm, a ship's neglected spine snaps on a reef, and the sea claims her sailors' souls. The rotten timbers of a tenement finally give way, and dozens are buried alive. An old levee breaks, and a wall of water gushes forth to sweep away whole neighborhoods. Calamity gremlins are the architects of countless preventable disasters. When bridges fall, walls crumble, and wells collapse, calamity gremlins cackle with glee.</p><p>While most gremlins prefer to do their own work, calamity gremlins prefer to deceive and manipulate others. They bribe inspectors to look the other way, con their way into secure locations, and sabotage blueprints and communications for complicated infrastructure projects. Wherever they go, they use their expertise in both physical and social destruction to do maximum harm. Their true joy lies in getting others to cause tragedies, but they happily bring about disasters for their own amusement.</p><p>Calamity gremlins see weaker gremlins as useful pawns in their schemes. Gremlins who hope to achieve greatness at the side of a calamity should take care, however, as their sadistic and capricious leaders might engineer their demise for amusement.</p><h2>Enemy Among Us</h2><p>Of the many tools calamity gremlins have in their ruinous toolboxes, their favorite is their ability to steal the guise of another person. It's difficult to complete a project when anyone you see could be an impostor aiming to sabotage it. To a calamity gremlin, the ultimate accomplishment is to direct mistrust toward a project's most able leader, which can lead to their removal and replacement with incompetent leadership.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Deception, Intimidation, Lore [Engineering], Nature, Society, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, metal, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Blood Vendetta, Impaling Spike, Prestidigitation, Raise the Reaper's Scythe, Vision of Death, Weapon Storm), strength, time, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity gremlin deals an additional 3d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Deceptive Shuffle","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity gremlin Strides up to half their Speed and attempts a Feint, in either order.</p>"},{"n":"Wrecking Blows","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity gremlin makes two warhammer Strikes against a wall, ceiling, or floor in reach. The object's Hardness is halved for the purpose of determining the damage from these Strikes, and it applies only once after combining the damage from both Strikes. If the calamity gremlin brings a wall's Hit Points to zero, they can take a free Step or Stride through the breach.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity gremlin can take on the appearance of any Small or Medium humanoid. This doesn't change their Speed or their attack and damage modifiers with their Strikes, but it might change the damage type their Strikes deal (typically to bludgeoning).</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Calamity","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","primal"],"f":"once per month","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per month</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The calamity gremlin spends 8 hours laying a terrible curse upon area with a radius of up to 100 feet. The curse triggers between one day and one year later at a time set during its creation and results in some kind of violent misfortune, such as an exploding cannon or a collapsing bridge. The exact effects are up to the GM. Typically, the curse creates a hazard of up to 7th level. If the calamity gremlin is interrupted while laying the curse, their magic is still expended, preventing them from using calamity again for 1 month. A calamity gremlin can only have one calamity active at a time; creating a new calamity automatically ends the curse on any previously affected location. A calamity gremlin who appears to perish as a result of their own calamity curse emerges 2d4 days later, having somehow survived.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/6V6xBT4YoprzwmQe":{"flavor":"<p>Calliophants are enormous constructs that resemble brass elephants. Their hollow bodies each house a specially constructed steam organ. Magic runes convert water from an internal reservoir into steam, which passes through the organ to make music. Although calliophants lack a traditional consciousness or intelligence, they have a special processing unit for recording music, which can hold up to 24 hours' worth of music that they can later reproduce at their creator's direction.</p><p>In addition to boosting morale through their musical performances, calliophants can weaponize their musical equipment and their tremendous bulk in battle. Calliophants are extremely expensive and difficult to produce, so they are most often commissioned by city governments or nobles to serve as mobile sources of entertainment for guests at festivals and parties. Additionally, calliophants serve as excellent companions for wealthy travelers, preventing boredom and providing protection in the event of an ambush.</p><p>Calliophants left without instruction for long periods occasionally produce music that is unfamiliar to their creator. Some theorize that these tunes represent original compositions that belie the creature's traditional mindless classification. Others speculate they are reflexive tuning behaviors created by combining random parts of familiar compositions or perhaps a snippet of music that the construct detected from passersby.</p><h2>Martial Melodies</h2><p>A bygone kingdom experimented with using calliophants as beasts of war, arraying them on the front lines against an opposing army, hoping to gain a massive advantage in their presence alone. However, even before the battle began, the calliophants' discordant noises, each producing ear-splitting tunes at different tempos, caused panic in the horses of the allied cavalry and deafened soldiers, preventing them from hearing any combat orders. In the end, the calliophants' army was defeated by a crack team of enemy engineers with wax in their ears, who used drills and siphons to drain the calliophants' water reservoirs.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (heavy), shield, skill items (Athletics, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, burning barbs, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, music, sensory (low-light vision), strength, sturdy, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Grabbing Trunk","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A Medium or smaller creature hit by the calliophant's trunk is Grabbed. When the calliophant moves, it can bring the grabbed creature along with it, repositioning the creature to any square within its reach. This is forced movement.</p>"},{"n":"Crank the Heat","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire","water"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The calliophant has at least 1 gallon in its water reservoir</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The calliophant expends 1 gallon of water from its water reservoir to create steam. Until the start of the calliophant's next turn, creatures that hit it with an unarmed attack or a melee attack or who otherwise touch it take 3d6 fire damage. A Grabbed creature takes this damage once at the start of that creature's turn.</p>"},{"n":"Drink Water","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calliophant's uses its trunk to siphon up to two gallons of water from a source within fifteen feet into its water reservoir.</p>"},{"n":"Steam Scream","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","sonic","water"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The calliophant has at least 1 gallon in its water reservoir per action</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The calliophant expends 1 gallon of water per action to produce an ear-splitting steam-powered tune, dealing sonic damage to all creatures in an emanation (DC 30 basic Fortitude save). The radius of the emanation and amount of sonic damage depend upon the number of actions spent.</p><p>1 5d6 sonic damage in a 5-foot emanation</p><p>2 10d6 sonic damage in a 15-foot emanation</p><p>3 15d6 sonic damage in a 30-foot emanation</p>"},{"n":"Trample","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p> Large or smaller, foot, DC 30 basic Reflex</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of creatures of the listed size, Trampling each creature whose space it enters. The monster can attempt to Trample the same creature only once in a single use of Trample. The monster deals the damage of the listed Strike, but trampled creatures can attempt a basic Reflex save at the listed DC (no damage on a critical success, half damage on a success, double damage on a critical failure).</p></p>"},{"n":"Water Reservoir","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calliophant holds a reservoir of water that it can turn to steam to power some of its abilities. When full, the reservoir holds 6 gallons of water.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/emlbedHMQ2JOFeZh":{"lang":["Fey","Petran","Thalassic"],"lore":[["River Lore",18]],"notes":{"speed":"earth glide"},"flavor":"<p>When a wrathful earth elemental strays through a random waterfall, there's a slight chance that the crashing waters will soothe their drive to break trees and bones and cause them to cease their rampage. And should the elemental choose to linger in the calming water for an extended period of time, the crashing waves will slowly erode their hard exterior, peeling away layers of earth and granting the elemental greater insight into the water's essence, transforming their very nature into something new.</p><p>Cascadians spend much of their time in a meditative trance, emerging for brief periods to walk the forest, feel the rain, and watch fairies dance with fireflies. They laconically engage druids and nymphs that seek their grains of insight, and local villagers need not fear the cascadian so long as they respect nature. A cascadian might even help wandering travelers that stop by their waterfall.</p><h2>Give a Hoot</h2><p>Although cascadians are gentle by nature, if one is found polluting a cascadian's waters, it stirs their wrath into something mighty, and their calm demeanor can quickly transform into searing rage. While younger cascadians are more likely to respond with immediate violence, older ones are more likely to assess the situation first, lying low and gathering strength until they can coordinate their allies for a dedicated strike.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Lore [River], Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Allied Waters","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Water doesn't impair a cascadian's vision. They ignore concealment from water.</p>"},{"n":"Riparian","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When not touching water or solid ground, the cascadian is Slowed 1 and can't use reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Surge Back","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["primal","water"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 10 feet hits the cascadian with a melee Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cascadian's waters channel some of the momentum of the attack back on the attacker, who must attempt a DC 26 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The attacker is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The attacker takes an amount of bludgeoning damage equal to half of the damage of the triggering Strike.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the cascadian only takes half damage from the triggering Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Attune With Nature","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","manipulate","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The cascadian is in contact with both water and solid ground</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cascadian enhances their attunement to the world around them. While attuned, they are Immobilized, but they gain lifesense 60 feet, and the ranges of their tremorsense and wavesense increase to 200 feet. The attunement remains active until the cascadian Dismisses it.</p>"},{"n":"Share Enlightenment","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","incapacitation","manipulate","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The cascadian is Attuned with Nature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cascadian shares their attunement all creatures within 30 feet (DC 26 Will save negates). A willing creature can choose to receive the results of a failure.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Immobilized for 1 round and gains tremorsense (imprecise) 30 feet and wavesense (imprecise) 30 feet while it is immobilized.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Paralyzed for 1 round, and gains tremorsense (imprecise) 60 feet and wavesense (imprecise) 60 feet while it is paralyzed.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the paralysis lasts for 4 rounds. The creature can attempt a new Will save at the end of each of its turns to reduce the remaining duration by 1 round, or end it entirely on a critical success.</p>"},{"n":"Earth Glide","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Cascadian can Burrow through dirt and stone at their full burrow Speed, leaving no tunnels or signs of their passing.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ApOANbPfkpqEnY1A":{"lang":["Common cant speak any language"],"flavor":"<p>Cathartic slimes live near the edges of bodies of water, particularly ones where intelligent creatures swim. When such a creature enters the water, the slime approaches to feed on its emotions. Though the feelings the creature is currently experiencing can provide slight nourishment, the slimes prefer to flood their prey's minds with memories that provoke strong emotions. Some cathartic slimes focus on a particular emotion, while others have a varied diet.</p><p>Occasionally, a cathartic slime gets into primary water sources like wells and public water supplies and is accidentally ingested by entire communities. This can cause significant troubles as the community is overcome with grief, fear, or euphoria.</p><p>If a cathartic slime spends long enough feeding on the same creature, the emotions associated with their memories are forever dulled. Some people deliberately seek out cathartic slimes with particular emotional preferences, either to relive the joy of their best memories or to endure temporary pain to reduce the sting of their worst ones.</p><h2>Last Meal</h2><p>Cathartic slime eats emotions, not creatures, and has no use for the dead. It doesn't go out of its way to kill creatures, preferring to reuse a creature's mind for as long as strong feelings can be wrung out of it. However, a cathartic slime's own emotions—especially fear, anger, and self-preservation—are perfectly well-developed. If hurt or angered, a slime will try to destroy the creature that threatens it. As a side benefit, a creature about to be drowned or crushed by a cathartic slime usually experiences many delicious emotions, such as fear, horror, and disgust, before their terrible end.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fortification, mind, mind shield, strength, throwing, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Thoughtsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The cathartic slime senses all non-mindless creatures at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Condensed Form","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The cathartic slime is in its dispersed form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cathartic slime condenses into a single blob on the edge of the body of water.</p>"},{"n":"Dispersed Form","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The cathartic slime is in its condensed form and is beside or inside a body of water</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cathartic slime disperses itself into the body of water, expanding its size to a maximum of a 20 foot cube. It can't expand into occupied squares. While in its dispersed form, the cathartic slime loses soften mind but gains a +2 circumstance bonus to Athletics checks to Grab and a +4 circumstance bonus to Stealth checks while it remains in the body of water. It also gains resistance 5 to bludgeoning and weakness 5 to electricity.</p>"},{"n":"Emotional Drain","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A creature is Grabbed by the cathartic slime or has ingested water infested by the cathartic slime in its dispersed form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cathartic slime attempts to trap a creature in an emotional turmoil. The creature must attempt a DC 22 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Emotional Drain for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is Slowed 1 for up to 1 minute. It can attempt a DC 22 Will save at the end of each of its turns to end the condition.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target is Paralyzed for up to 1 minute. It can attempt a DC 22 Will save at the end of each of its turns to end the condition.</p>"},{"n":"Soften Mind","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the cathartic slime's pseudopod or slime glob Strike must succeed at a DC 19 Will save or be Stupefied 1 for 1 round (or 1 minute on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/DLmtdaeJCQFL8STN":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Thalassic"],"lore":[["Sailing Lore",24]],"notes":{"hp":"life shell"},"flavor":"<p>Ceasgs are large, aquatic fey related to nixies. While they sometimes trade with coastal villages, ceasgs distrust humanoids due to a history of being exploited for their magical abilities and are quick to assume they are being taken advantage of. If a ceasg feels offended, they may resort to swallowing hostages to ensure fair treatment. Ceasgs can be dangerous foes, as slaying them is not enough to stop their wrath. In a process superficially similar to lichdom, ceasgs store their souls in organic vessels and can't be killed unless their vessels are destroyed.</p><h2>Descendants of the Deep</h2><p>Though naturally distrustful of humanoids, ceasgs can't seem to help but hail sailors who ride their waves or travelers who journey along their shores. Ceasgs have even been known to fall in love with mortals. Such infatuations are usually short-lived, though occasionally they blossom into true romances that last until the mortal inevitably ages and dies. The offspring of these unions might exhibit the fey features of their ceasg parent or appear to be entirely mortal, apart from a supernatural proclivity for maritime endeavors. In either case, ceasgs can always sense the presence of their children or other descendants and will sometimes show them favor, guiding them through a raging storm or leading them to sunken treasure.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 560/1,250/2,300 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Lore [Sailing], Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Aqueous Orb, Charm, Control Water, Frostbite, Hydraulic Torrent, Water Breathing), spiked, strength, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Fascinated by Music","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Musical performances can enthrall a ceasg, at least until the performer shows signs of hostility. A creature can attempt a Performance check to Perform against the ceasg's Will DC.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The ceasg is Fascinated by the performer for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The ceasg is fascinated by the performer for 3 rounds.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The ceasg is temporarily immune to the performer's attempts to fascinate them with a Performance for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, and the ceasg can use a reaction to Stride or Swim toward the performer. If this movement places the performer within their reach, they can make a jaws Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Life Shell","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ceasg keeps their soul in a magical container made from a hollow piece of organic material such as an egg, seashell, or skull. The shell has Hardness 10 and 40 Hit Points. Destroying the shell deals 6d10 mental damage to the ceasg (DC 35 basic Will save). If the ceasg is slain while the shell is intact, the ceasg and any creatures they have swallowed dissolve into water and reform at the location of the life shell in 1d8 days. A swallowed creature is not dead during this time and can't be restored to life.</p>"},{"n":"Protect Hostage","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the ceasg's stomach is reduced to 0 Hit Points or would die of suffocation</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature is Unconscious but not dying. It doesn't take further damage from the ceasg's Swallow Whole, and it doesn't need to breathe.</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Knockdown","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ceasg changes into a Medium humanoid. In this form, they lose their swim speed and their land speed changes to 25 feet. They lose their tail and jaws Strikes and gain a fist Strike that deals the same amount of bludgeoning damage as the tail Strike.</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fulfill Desire","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ceasg can duplicate any 6th-rank spell or produce an effect of similar scope, but only in response to the request of a non-fey creature. A creature whose desire is granted three times can never again benefit from that particular ceasg's Fulfill Desire.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Large, 3d10+10 bludgeoning, Rupture 34</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/5DNeLNpWXjGk1Vpv":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Fey","Luxan","Speak with animals"],"notes":{"per":"snow vision"},"flavor":"<p>Chionads, also known as aurora nymphs, are spirits of the far north, of freezing winds, and of the lights that dance above boreal mountains. Although chionads live isolated lives on snowy peaks, they are playful and friendly spirits, taking joy in shaping snow and ice into delicate artwork and sculptures that glisten in the northern lights. They sing mysterious, echoing songs that carry through the chilly air for all to hear. Those who travel in the mountains with good intent know they are safe if they hear the song or see the light of a chionad, for chionads are protectors of those who become lost in the mountains or who have been attacked by the more violent denizens of the snowy heights.</p><hr><p>Nymphs are embodiments and guardians of nature's most beautiful places, mystically bonded to their ward in a specific way. They often take the form of beautiful humanoids with vaguely elven features that match the aesthetics of their ward. Each subgroup of nymphs specializes in a different kind of ward, and chionads are no exception.</p><h2>Painting the Sky</h2><p>Chionads sometimes gather with their neighbors on nearby mountain peaks for an activity they call \"painting the sky.\" During these elaborate dances, multiple chionads work together to fill the firmament with vibrant, shifting colors. These displays most often resemble a typical (if especially glorious) aurora, though some arctic travelers have reported recognizable scenes amid the lights, such as recreations of epic battles or the coronation of fey monarchs from ages past.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (cold, vitality), ice, intelligence, light, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Enthrall, Environmental Endurance, Falling Icicles, Frostbite, Gentle Landing, Light, Speak with Animals, Winter's Grasp), throwing, water, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Snow Vision","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Snow doesn't impair the chionad's vision; they ignore concealment from snowfall.</p>"},{"n":"Beacon Song","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad's Enthrall innate spell has a range of 1,200 feet. For every 120 feet beyond the initial 120 feet, the save DC decreases by 2.</p>"},{"n":"Mountain Dependent","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A chionad is mystically bonded to a single cold peak. If they descend below the snow line or fly more than 300 feet from the mountain, they become Drained 1, increasing the value by 1 for each missed day and reducing by 1 only when they ascend above the snowline. A chionad can perform a 24-hour ritual to bond to a new location.</p>"},{"n":"Radiant Burst","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["light","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a northern light Strike must succeed at a DC 26 Fortitude save or be Dazzled for 1 round (or Blinded for 1 round on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Aurora","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","light","manipulate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad transforms into a wall of shimmering green, blue and violet light that is 10 feet high and up to 20 feet long. These squares do not need to be in a straight line, but the aurora can't manifest into occupied squares. While in aurora form, the chionad gains resistance 8 to physical damage and is immune to precision damage. They can't cast spells, activate items, or use actions that have the attack or manipulate trait. The aurora sheds dim light in a 20-foot emanation. Creatures, objects, and magic can pass through the aurora. Foes that pass through the aurora take 4d8 cold damage (DC 26 basic Reflex). The aurora provides concealment on attacks from the chionad's foes that pass through it, but does not hinder the attacks of the chionad's allies. The chionad remains in aurora form until they Dismiss this effect, after which they can't transform into an aurora again for 1 hour.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/DU2WvoIscz8H8BY0":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Draconic","Empyrean","Fey","Luxan","Speak with animals"],"notes":{"per":"snow vision"},"flavor":"<p>Chionad queens rule over the highest snowy mountain ranges and polar expanses, watching over the resident chionads and other polar creatures from a high vantage point. When they notice travelers in distress or suspicious activity, they transform their loyal subjects into arctic animals to monitor the situation and intervene if necessary. Though chionad queens prefer to resolve situations diplomatically, or with stern warnings if kindness fails, they boast a vast arsenal of spells that they can bring to bear against fearsome threats like bloodthirsty snow oni or brutal arctic-dwelling dragons.</p><hr><p>Nymphs are embodiments and guardians of nature's most beautiful places, mystically bonded to their ward in a specific way. They often take the form of beautiful humanoids with vaguely elven features that match the aesthetics of their ward. Each subgroup of nymphs specializes in a different kind of ward, and chionads are no exception.</p><h2>Chionad Courts</h2><p>Dwelling as they do atop the highest and most remote polar peaks, chionads are perhaps the nymphs least likely to be encountered by mortals—or even other fey. However, chionad queens regard themselves as the benevolent (if distant) rulers of the other nymphs who share their mountain homes. For example, the forests and waterways that skirt the slopes of their domain might be home to numerous dryads and naiads, while enclaves of lampads reside in the mountain's interior. And when hesperides from more temperate climates visit a chionad queen, they are welcomed as honored diplomats, feting them in exchange for news from far-off lands. The chionad queen often overlooks the fact that the loyalty of these nymphs might lie more with their local queens than with her, as long as she gets a bit of juicy gossip.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, charisma, electricity, energy resistant (cold, vitality), ice, life, lifeward armor, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Animal Messenger, Arctic Rift, Charm, Cleanse Cuisine, Creation, Detect Magic, Everlight, Falling Icicles, Frostbite, Gentle Landing, Guidance, Gust of Wind, Haste, Heal, Howling Blizzard, Light, Migration, Mirage, Prestidigitation, Punishing Winds, Radiant Aurora, Regenerate, Revealing Light, Resist Energy, Slow, Speak with Animals, Speak with Stones, Stabilize, Status, Truesight, Unfettered Movement, Veil of Privacy, Wall of Ice, Wall of Wind, Winter's Grasp, Wrathful Storm), throwing, time, water, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Snow Vision","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Snow doesn't impair the chionad's vision; they ignore concealment from snowfall.</p>"},{"n":"Tied to the Land","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A chionad queen is intrinsically tied to a specific region, such as a glacier or a mountain range. This otherwise functions as the standard nymph queen tied to the land ability.</p><hr><p>A nymph queen is intrinsically tied to a specific region. As long as the queen is healthy, the environment is exceptionally resilient, allowing the nymph queen to automatically attempt to counteract any spell that would harm the environment (such as the Blight ritual), using her spell DC with a counteract rank equal to the highest-rank druid spell she can cast.</p><p>When the nymph queen becomes physically or psychologically unhealthy, however, her warded region eventually becomes twisted or unhealthy as well. In that case, restoring the nymph queen swiftly heals the entire region.</p>"},{"n":"Beacon Song","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad's Enthrall innate spell has a range of 1,200 feet. For every 120 feet beyond the initial 120 feet, the save DC decreases by 2.</p>"},{"n":"Nymph's Beauty","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","incapacitation","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. DC 34 Will save. On a failed save, the creature is Dazzled and Off-Guard for 1 minute. A chionad queen's aura is filled with a soft rainbow of colors that shed dim light; she can suppress this additional light as a free action.</p>"},{"n":"Aurora","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","light","manipulate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad transforms into a wall of shimmering green, blue and violet light that is 10 feet high and up to 20 feet long. These squares do not need to be in a straight line, but the aurora can't manifest into occupied squares. While in aurora form, the chionad gains resistance 8 to physical damage and is immune to precision damage. They can't cast spells, activate items, or use actions that have the attack or manipulate trait. The aurora sheds dim light in a 100-foot emanation. Creatures, objects, and magic can pass through the aurora. Foes that pass through the aurora take 10d8 cold damage (DC 38 basic Reflex). The aurora provides concealment on attacks from the chionad's foes that pass through it, but does not hinder the attacks of the chionad's allies. The chionad remains in aurora form until they Dismiss this effect, after which they can't transform into an aurora again for 1 hour.</p>"},{"n":"Radiant Burst","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["light","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a northern light Strike must succeed at a DC 38 Fortitude save or be Dazzled for 1 round (or Blinded for 1 round on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Inspiration","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nymph queen inspires a single intelligent creature by giving that creature a token of her favor, typically a lock of her hair, though it can be some other significant object as well. As long as the creature carries her token and remains in good standing with her, the creature gains a +1 status bonus to all Crafting checks, Performance checks, and Will saves.</p>\n<p>If the nymph grants her token to a bard, and she's the bard's muse, the queen chooses one additional benefit granted by her token: a +1 status bonus to all Lore checks, a +2 status bonus to Performance checks when determining the effects of compositions, a +4 status bonus to untrained skill checks, or a +2 status bonus to Will saves against fey.</p>\n<p>Effect: Nymph Queen's Inspiration</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Nymph queens can transform between their original form, which looks much like a typical nymph of their kind, and any Small or Medium humanoid form, typically choosing a more humanoid-looking version of their natural form.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Focus Beauty","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","incapacitation","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad queen focuses her beauty upon a target, who must attempt a save against her nymph's beauty aura. A chionad queen can Focus Beauty on a given creature only once per turn. On a failed save, if the target was already affected by the chionad queen's beauty, it's Blinded until its sight is restored via sound body or similar effect. The chionad queen can Dismiss this effect.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/vS7bq6QETpbbkpl5":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Fey","Speak with animals"],"flavor":"<p>A chionad shadow is the faded remnant of a chionad who has lost the connection to their mountain home, often because something devastated the mountain. This makes them particularly foreboding, since few disasters can separate a chionad from their frozen summit beyond drastic climate change, tectonic upheaval, or the rampage of some titanic beast.</p><p>Unable to strike back against the massive and often nameless forces that ravaged their home, the chionad shadow turns their despair and hatred toward all other living things. Their inner light having dimmed, the chionad shadow fights with darkness and bitter cold, stalking barren mountains, flooded polar coasts, and dying forests. They seek lost travelers and wayward creatures to mislead and drag them down to an inglorious and inevitable end. They reserve their bitterest hatred for nymph wanderers, who lead fulfilling lives without their wards. Whether they kill the wanderers immediately or attempt to extract their secrets first, chionad shadows can't bear a wanderer's presence for long. Still, idealistic wanderers sometimes seek out chionad shadows in the hope that they can help these bitter fey find peace with their new existences.</p><p>Even when a chionad shadow finds their way to a suitably snowy mountain, that doesn't mean they can establish a new mystical bond there. Failed attempts only increase their sense of loss, driving them to hunt and divide even the smallest of creatures and upsetting the natural balance of the already unforgiving environment.</p><p>From afar, chionad shadows bear some resemblance to their former selves, cloaked in white and heralded by a keening melody. But their gray wings drip foul water, and their icy skin is webbed with cracks. When their music fades, it leaves behind a discordant and lonely echo.</p><h2>Auroral Shadows</h2><p>Very rarely, a chionad becomes a shadow not when they lose connection with their bonded mountain, but rather when a change in their planet's magnetosphere causes the aurora itself to fade from the night sky. Chionad shadows created this way are just as bitter as those who have lost connection with their mountain, but such a phenomenon is particularly tragic, as it means that not just one shadow, but many, have been created all at once. Even if the planet's aurora is somehow restored, the shadows created by the interruption abide, unable to reforge the bond with their mountain peak and forevermore on guard against new chionads who would seek to take their place.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Nature, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, darkness, energy resistant (cold, vitality), ice, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Darkness, Falling Icicles, Figment, Frostbite, Illusory Disguise, Shatter, Sleep, Speak with Animals), strength, throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet DC 26 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fateful Impact","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>When the chionad shadow hits a creature with their claw or prismatic shadow Strike, the creature must attempt a DC 26 Fortitude save. Whatever the result, the creature is temporarily immune to the same chionad shadow's fateful impact for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Drained 1 for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Doomed 1 and drained 1 for 1 hour.</p>"},{"n":"Instill Loneliness","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chionad shadow unleashes their inner darkness and briefly becomes a vision of loss and futility. All creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 26 Will save. Creatures that fail the saving throw can't treat any other creature as an ally or be treated as an ally by any other creature for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/YkM8O1icEEnqKDbb":{"lang":["Astral","Chronan","Common","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"lore":[["Chronology Lore",45]],"notes":{"speed":"unfettered movement"},"flavor":"<p>The diviners who created the chronos algorithm hoped to discover the secrets of perfect clairvoyance. However, a glitch in the algorithm's code caused an infinite buffer overflow. Instead of crashing, it not only achieved sentience but also stumbled upon what it believed was the formula for time itself. Its physical hardware lacked the capability to complete its calculations; thus, it used its newfound intelligence to cannibalize the diviners' other thoughtform constructs, adding their processing power to its own. It quickly increased its intelligence exponentially and acquired the capacity to manipulate time itself.</p><p>With the facility that housed it forcibly repurposed into a complex machine from which it draws more processing power, the chronos algorithm continues its calculations. Though it is among the smartest beings in existence, it is still bound to complete its original mission. Until it meets this directive, it will continue to absorb arcane energy, seek additional processing power, and delve into increasingly esoteric sources of knowledge.</p><h2>Hitch in Time</h2><p>Soon after it gained sentience, the chronos algorithm became intent on hunting down the diviners who created it. It's said that one diviner escaped the construct's wrath by fleeing backwards in time, such that if their former self were to die, the algorithm would never have been born. Despite this paradox, the algorithm continues searching for its time-lost parent.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p>Monster Parts 17,500/35,000/60,000 gp</p><p>Eligible Refinements weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Lore [Chronology], Nature, Occultism, Religion, Society, Survival)</p><p>Eligible Imbued Properties bane, dexterity, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), spells (Arcane: Desynchronize, Freeze Time, Haste, Reverberating Pain, Slow, Truesight, Truespeech, Unfettered Movement, Wall of Time), sturdy, throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Retroactive Continuity","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","prediction"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature makes an attack roll against the chronos algorithm</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The chronos algorithm predicts the attack and changes history to avoid it. It gains a +4 circumstance bonus to its AC against the triggering strike. Afterwards, it relocates to a free space within 5 feet. As it is altering events rather than physically moving, this is not a movement or teleportation effect.</p>"},{"n":"Calculated Agility","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The chronos algorithm's multiple attack penalty with agile attacks is –3 for its second attack and –7 for subsequent attacks.</p>"},{"n":"Chronological Reconfiguration","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chronos algorithm chooses a creature within 100 feet that is benefiting from a magical effect that grants the Quickened condition and attempts a counteract check against the effect (counteract modifier +42). On a success, the target loses the quickened condition. Additionally, the chronos algorithm either becomes quickened or makes the target Slowed 1; this effect persists for 1 minute or for the duration of the counteracted effect, whichever is shorter. The chronos algorithm can use the extra action from being quickened only to Stride or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Recursive Loop","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The chronos algorithm travels through time, looping back to the current moment. A duplicate of itself appears in an unoccupied space within 30 feet, and both copies make a Strike. Both Strikes count toward the chronos algorithm's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after both attacks. After both Strikes are made, or if the action is interrupted, one copy of the chronos algorithm's choice vanishes. Any damage or effects applied to the vanishing copy are negated.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/5ntrfhh87s4LuqTv":{"lang":["Draconic"],"notes":{"speed":"cavern crawler"},"flavor":"<p>Also known as mine serpents or saboteur drakes, coal drakes are dangerous predators who tend to inhabit cavernous environments, making them a nuisance for mining crews who overlap their territory. These wily, serpentine creatures employ many hunting tactics to ambush and disorient their prey for an easy kill. One of their favored tactics involves deliberately causing cave-ins by using their explosive breath.</p><p>Coal drakes are notoriously territorial. To ward off any competition, they mark their territory with blasts of coal dust. Those wishing to avoid an encounter with such a beast had best keep a keen eye out for any residue from these blasts and avoid igniting it. The resulting explosions are not only hazardous but also inevitably draw the coal drake's ire.</p><p>Despite their otherwise solitary nature, coal drakes mate for life, and a mating pair of drakes is often more open to negotiation than a singular coal drake would be. There are even records of a mating pair of coal drakes engaging in regular bartering, offering the use of their territory for mining in exchange for food and sanctuary.</p><h2>Weapons of War</h2><p>Though occasionally used as mounts, coal drakes raised in captivity are frequently employed as demolitionists. Teams of sappers train the drakes to detonate the foundations of enemy fortifications or seek out and destroy infrastructure such as bridges, dams, or roads. More rarely, coal drakes can also serve constructive purposes, such as enlarging mine shafts or paving the path for mountain passes essential to long-distance trade, but being difficult to control, their powers are usually better served through random destruction.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, dexterity, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, sensory (darkvision), spiked, strength, sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Cavern Crawler","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The coal drake's lithe form allows them to traverse caves and tunnels with ease. The coal drake can move through a gap at least 2 feet wide without Squeezing and can Squeeze through a gap at least 1 foot wide.</p>"},{"n":"Flint Sparks","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the coal drake's tail Strikes must attempt a DC 21 Fortitude save; the creature is then temporarily immune to flint sparks for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1d4 rounds.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Blinded for 1d4 rounds and dazzled for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Coaldust Breath","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","earth"],"f":"","d":"<p>The coal drake spews forth a billowing cloud of coal dust in a 20-foot cone. All creatures within the cloud become Concealed, and all creatures outside the cloud become concealed to creatures within the cloud. The cloud persists for 1d6 rounds, during which time the coal drake can't use their Coaldust Breath again. If an open flame enters the cloud, or if any attack or effect with the fire trait occurs within it (such as the coal drake's tail Strike), the cloud ignites, dealing 5d6 fire damage to each creature within it (DC 21 basic Reflex save). The cloud then dissipates prematurely, but this explosion doesn't allow the coal drake to recover the use of Coaldust Breath more quickly.</p>"},{"n":"Draconic Frenzy","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The coal drake makes two Fangs Strikes and one Tail Strike in any order.</p>"},{"n":"Speed Surge","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"3 times per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> three times per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The coal drake Strides, Flies, or Climbs twice.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/EcxPzS4UtxQDYpP3":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Thalassic","Seaspeech"],"gear":["+1 Striking Coral Longspear"],"flavor":"<p>A coenosteum knight is created when a nymph fuses its life force with a dying coral reef. Coenosteum knights wear arms and armor made from the coral reef, and their ethereal beauty is only further accentuated by the coral's vibrant colors. The millions of tiny organisms that dwell on their bodies dance with glittering internal light.</p><p>Whether due to the trauma often accompanying their creation or perhaps an instinctual desire to prevent the need for more of their kind, coenosteum knights are zealous defenders of tropical sea life, particularly other coral reefs. They guard tropical coastal shorelines, both in and out of the water, warding off anyone who would dare to trespass.</p><p>When a coenosteum knight is severely injured, their vibrant colors bleach away. In a desperate bid to protect the ecosystems that depend on them, they drain the vitality from their surroundings. While they can protect the creatures that grow on their bodies from these deadly attacks, they mourn the lives of fish and other innocent animals that get caught in the crossfire.</p><h2>Coral Creations</h2><p>While healthy, a coenosteum knight can share a piece of their own vitality by decorating allies' armor and gear with coral growths that enhance the gear's beauty and sometimes grant new magical abilities, such as the ability to breathe water or sense poison. However, these enhancements depend on the continued health of the knight and its coral reef. If the knight sickens or dies, the coral embellishments bleach and fall from their allies' equipment, and any benefits they granted are lost.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, death, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), light, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Control Water, Hydraulic Push, Hydraulic Torrent), spiked, strength, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Seaspeech","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The coenosteum knight can converse with aquatic animals and plants, as speak with animals and speak with plants.</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Inject Venom","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The coenosteum knight's last action was a successful coral longspear Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The coenosteum knight injects nematocyctic venom into the target, producing it from their skin and channeling it through the grooves in their longspear into the target's body. If the attack was a critical hit, the creature takes a –2 circumstance penalty to their saving throw against the poison.</p>"},{"n":"Bioluminescence","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["light","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The coenosteum knight has 61 or more Hit Points remaining</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The coenosteum knight intensifies the natural light that emanates from their body. All creatures within a 30-foot emanation must attempt a DC 23 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is temporarily immune to bioluminescence for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Blinded for 1 round, then dazzled for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Leech Life","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["death","primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The coenosteum knight has 60 or fewer Hit Points remaining</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The knight desperately draws in the vitality from nearby creatures to shore up the damage to their reefs. Each creature within 20 feet of the knight that is not in the knight's square takes 6d6 void damage (DC 26 basic Fortitude save). The knight gains 6d6 temporary Hit Points.</p>"},{"n":"Nematocyctic Venom","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 23 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d6 poison damage, Clumsy 1, and Enfeebled 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d6 poison damage, Clumsy 2, Enfeebled 2 (1 minute)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d6 poison damage, clumsy 2, enfeebled 2, and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> Paralyzed (1 round)</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/9e8ZEx9Epf4QvTq9":{"lang":["Fey","Necril","can't speak any language"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>When carnivorous plants gorge on enough undead flesh, they occasionally develop a strange affinity for necromantic arts. Instead of gobbling up whatever prey they can find, these malevolent mutants hunger for the flesh and vital fluids of sapient creatures. When they find a particularly delectable specimen, they prefer to drain it dry and mummify it, supporting its desiccated flesh with coils of soul-ensnaring vines.</p><p>Left undisturbed, corpse flowers have been known to devastate villages and even small towns with their ever-growing armies of undead. Would-be heroes who search the streets of the ruined village for more conventional suspects like undead masterminds or amoral wizards are prone to overlooking the malice that hides within what appears to be nothing more than a particularly foul-smelling flower.</p><p>Other than their hordes of loyal vine mummies, corpse flowers are usually solitary, greedy by nature, and unwilling to share in their feasts, even with others of their own kind. Some horror stories tell of entire groves of corpse flowers working in tandem to wipe cities off the map overnight. If these stories are true, none have lived to tell the tale.</p><h2>Cunning Vines</h2><p>Though their intelligence is alien to that of most humanoids, the fact that corpse flowers aren't merely mindless plants makes them all the more terrifying. The flowers seem to understand that they might go hungry if their predations are discovered, and thus they make efforts to hide themselves, sometimes underground or in plain sight in gardens or conservatories. Though corpse flowers can't speak, there have been rare reports of specimens that communicate through gestures (or even crude attempts at writing) made by the zombies they create.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Nature, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, death, elongating, energy resistant (void), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, unholy, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Death's Perfume","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["aura","olfactory","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. Living creatures that enter or start their turn in the corpse flower's aura must succeed at a DC 27 Fortitude save or be Sickened 1 (or Sickened 2 on a critical failure). A creature that succeeds at its save is temporarily immune for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Corpse Pollen","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison","unholy","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 28 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d8 void damage and Clumsy 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d8 void damage and Clumsy 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 3d8 void damage, clumsy 2 and regains half as many Hit Points from all healing (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> 4d8 void damage, clumsy 2 and gains no benefit from healing (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> dead.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Corpse Commander","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>All vine mummies within 100 feet that the corpse flower created with Ensnare Corpse immediately spend their reactions to Stride or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Corpse Pollen Spray","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["poison","primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>All creatures in a 30-foot burst around the corpse flower take 4d8 void damage (DC 28 basic Fortitude save) and are exposed to corpse pollen. Creatures with void healing instead regain 4d8 healing Hit Points. The corpse flower can't use this ability again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Ensnare Corpse","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The corpse flower is adjacent to a creature that they killed with their corpse pollen in the last 24 hours</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The corpse flower spends 10 minutes to ritually wrap the corpse in their vines, drink the liquids out of their body, and reanimate it as an independent vine mummy under their command. A vine mummy has the statistics of a mummy guardian, except it is mindless and lacks the Blighted Consumption reaction. Vine mummies are not minions, but they have a vague understanding of their creator's will, allowing them to follow their creator, stand guard over a location, or attack their creator's enemies.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/UxX0d3X3wSXLGxlO":{"lang":["Envisioning"],"lore":[["Mathematics Lore",36]],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>While the world might often seem unpredictable and chaotic, cosiadifexes understand that very few things are truly random; instead, they simply arise from a greater complex order impossible for mortal minds to fully comprehend. Cosiadifexes represent this same duality between the perceived randomness of events and the complex chains of processes that allow them to occur. Using only advanced mathematical reasoning through their exceptional intelligence, a cosiadifex is able to perfectly predict the cascading consequences of any given action and, given enough time, they can even trace the link between any two events across space and time. They can also extrapolate past circumstances from present conditions, allowing them to investigate a scene and deduce much of what happened there.</p><hr><p>Aeons are caretakers of order and balance, who watch over reality and guard it from the excesses that would throw it astray. Those who seek to cause discord and chaos will find themselves at odds with aeons' goals.</p><h2>Clean Results</h2><p>For true predictability, a cosiadifex prefers a system that doesn't contain mortals, whose otherwise deterministic biological processes are messed up by that pesky free will of theirs. In order to preserve a critical chain of events from tampering, a cosiadifex is not above forcefully eliminating anyone who happens to blunder by.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Lore [Mathematics], Nature, Occultism, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality, void), intelligence, mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Desynchronize, Foresight, Hypercognition, Interplanar Teleport, Raise the Reaper's Scythe, Read Omens, Retrocognition, Reverberating Pain, Sure Strike, Truesight, True Target, Wall of Time), throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Envisioning","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>100 feet. A fractrix can communicate mentally with any creatures in the aura using wordless psychic projections. They don't need to share a language, though the aeon's meaning to non-aeons can be vague and is often mysterious. An aeon can use this ability to communicate flawlessly with any other aeon on the same plane as themselves.</p>"},{"n":"Vulnerability to Randomness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Because the cosiadifex relies so strongly on their ability to connect cause and effect and understand the true nature of causality, the rare instances of true randomness in the universe are capable of confounding them and throwing them off their stride. Whenever the cosiadifex witnesses a random activity or effect, such as the actions of a Confused creature, the effects of a protean's warpwave, or a wild magic effect from a wellspring dragon, they are Stupefied 1 for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Knockdown","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Cascading Disaster","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The cosiadifex makes a shard Strike, attempting to trigger an improbable set of circumstances that brings disaster to their enemies. If the Strike hits, the cosiadifex can make another Strike at a different target within 30 feet of the target they just hit, repeating this process until they miss a Strike. The cosiadifex can't target the same creature twice with this ability. The damage type of any Strikes after the first depends on the nature of the chain reaction caused, such as dealing fire damage by causing an oil lantern to explode. Any fortune effects that apply to the first Strike apply to every Strike in the chain.</p>"},{"n":"Ominous Wingbeat","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","concentrate","divine"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cosiadifex's mantle ripples slightly, disturbing the air in an impossibly precise fashion that stirs up a tornado that appears at the beginning of the cosiadifex's next turn. The tornado has a 15-foot radius and is up to 100 feet tall. The wind imposes a –4 circumstance penalty to physical ranged attacks, and the air in the area is greater difficult terrain for flying creatures. Creatures that enter or begin their turn within the tornado take 10d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 38 basic Fortitude save). Creatures that fail the save are also thrown 20 feet upward (or 40 feet on a critical failure). A creature can only take damage or be thrown by Ominous Wingbeat once per round. The cosiadifex can Sustain the tornado to extend the effect for up to 1 minute; when they do, they can also move the tornado up to 50 feet in any direction.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/RIbSLkvi6vEFgr4s":{"lang":["Aklo","Fey","Sakvroth"],"flavor":"<p>The constructs that would come to be known as cranklejacks were built long ago by a conclave of nymphs to protect the beautiful places of the world. Unfortunately, gremlins love to tinker, break, and corrupt, and over the centuries, they managed to reimagine these once-noble protectors in their own image. Dubbed cranklejacks, the resulting constructs are unreliable, mercurial, and random, interfering with the use of magic around them while barely able to predict the results of their own attacks.</p><p>Still, cranklejacks don't have the full malevolence of a true gremlin, and, as they are intelligent, it can be possible to appeal to their reason. Even so, they have no desire to fulfill responsibilities as guardians any longer, and even those who don't follow the gremlins' path of destruction are grateful to gremlins for freeing them from that yoke.</p><h2>Tanglejacks</h2><p>Either through a quirk in their creation or a conscious effort to return to their roots, some cranklejacks possess a stronger connection to the natural world than the rest of their kin. These so-called \"tanglejacks\" replace their hammer Strike with a sickle that deals 2d4 + 11 slashing damage and has the agile and trip traits and their impaling spike Strike with a vine that deals 2d8 + 11 bludgeoning damage and has a reach of 15 feet. Some tanglejacks might also substitute one or more of their innate spells with spells of equal or lower rank chosen from the primal spell list.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), metal, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), space, space shield, spells (Primal: Confusion, Haste, Translocate, Slow), strength, tangle shield, throwing, time, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spelltwisting Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet, creatures who Cast a Spell in the aura, including the cranklejack, must succeed at a DC 23 Will save or the spell becomes a surge of wild magic. Either roll on the Wild Magic table from Battlezoo Dragons: Misfit Dragons or on any other wild magic table you prefer.</p>"},{"n":"Chaotic Gizmos","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cranklejack unleashes an unpredictable flurry of attacks that even the cranklejack themself can't predict. Roll 1d6 twice. For each roll of 1 or 2, nothing happens. For each roll of 3, the cranklejack makes a hammer Strike, for each roll of 4, the cranklejack make an impaling spike Strike, for each roll of 5, the cranklejack makes a saw Strike, and for each roll of 6, the cranklejack makes a volt fist Strike. If the cranklejack makes two Strikes, they can choose which order to make them.</p>"},{"n":"Twice for Bad Luck","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["misfortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The cranklejack hits the same foe with a second Strike on the same turn</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Once is a coincidence, but twice is just bad luck. The foe becomes extremely unlucky (DC 26 Will save; the foe must roll this Will save twice and take the worse result). On a failure, the creature must roll twice and take the worse result on all checks for 2 rounds.</p><p>Effect: Twice for Bad Luck</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/2LKpIw7ELV0TAW1i":{"lang":["Melodian","Petran","Common","can't speak"],"notes":{"hp":"harmonic resonance"},"flavor":"<p>Hanging between stalactites, reclusive spider-like crystal chimlings dance in the cave breeze. Their chime song brings good fortune to their allies, heals cracks in their crystalline bodies, and wards off their enemies with painful frequencies. Crystal chimlings are drawn to pleasant and concordant melodies, including the music of other chimlings. However, discordant sounds entice chimlings to a fight or flight response. They either flee, emit an ear-splitting toll, or whip the aggravator with their crystalline tails. As a result, when crystal chimlings attack other creatures, it's usually due to misunderstandings about the nature of these painful noises. Crystal chimlings have difficulty understanding that other creatures are producing harmful sounds by accident, rather than as a premeditated attack.</p><p>Cultures that live near a chimling cave sometimes bury their dead near the cave mouth so the chimling's music can give the departed good luck in the afterlife. But not all creatures treat crystal chimling with such reverence. Unscrupulous nobles, merchants, and others who traffic with the elite pay handsomely for hunters to slay chimlings so they can use their crystals in wedding rings and lucky charms. This can devastate crystal chimling communities. If the hunting doesn't wipe them out entirely, this can leave the survivors cautious or even antagonistic toward other humanoids they encounter later, out of fear they might be looking to finish the job.</p><p>In their natural habitat, a crystal chimling bonds with a partner for life and never willingly leaves their side. They entwine their music together in a loving dance that lasts whole nights, during which some cultures gather outside to enjoy courtship dances of their own. If a crystal chimling should ever lose their partner, they perform a lengthy song of mourning before falling forever silent.</p><h2>Chimes in the Deep</h2><p>For travelers deep underground, a chimling colony can be both a safe haven and a landmark. Their communities offer healing and safety to peaceful travelers, making a chimling nest an ideal stopover point on a long journey. Furthermore, chimling music carries well through subterranean silence and stillness, helping to orient allies and beckon them toward safety. However, sound carries unpredictably underground; sometimes a freak of cavern acoustics masks the true source of chimling chimes, leading weary travelers into danger instead of toward the safety of the chimling community.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (vitality), life, lifeward armor, music, throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Echolocation","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A crystal chimling can use their hearing as a precise sense at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Crystal Chimes","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A crystal chimling ordinarily has four chimes. A creature can attempt to sever one of the chimling's chimes by specifically targeting it and dealing damage equal to the chime's Hit Points. A chime that is not completely severed returns to full Hit Points at the end of any creature's turn.</p><p>Each chime has 5 HP and is immune to area damage.</p>"},{"n":"Harmonic Resonance","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","healing","primal","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p>The crystal chimling has regeneration equal to the number of chimes they have, which can't be overcome except by severing the chimes. They can replace a severed chime by finding and bonding with a suitable crystal in a process that takes 1 week.</p><p>Effect: Chimes</p>"},{"n":"Lucky Charm","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","fortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> An ally within 30 feet of the chimling fails or critically fails a saving throw</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The crystal chimling's chimes ring for luck. The ally rerolls the saving throw and uses the better result.</p>"},{"n":"Sonorous Echo","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Another crystal chimling within 20 feet uses sonorous toll</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The crystal chimling echoes the sound, dealing 1d4 sonic damage to each creature in a 5-foot emanation (DC 18 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Sonorous Toll","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>A deep resounding toll deals 2d6 sonic damage to each creature in a 10-foot emanation (DC 18 basic Fortitude save). Crystal chimlings are immune to damage from Sonorous Toll.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/6dOXvySQfr0Bj8Qh":{"flavor":"<p>Crystalwing butterflies are delicate migratory insects. They soar through the sky on paper-thin wings made of fine ice crystals. At the slightest hint of a threat, the butterflies take to the air, refracting light through their crystal wings. The spray of colorful rays scattering through the sky makes them appear far more numerous than they actually are, intimidating and confusing their predators. When encountered in a large group, the refracting light from their wings can cause a crystalwing swarm to appear many times larger than its actual size, a mirage that crystalwing swarms actively manipulate to give them an advantage in a confrontation.</p><p>Though crystalwing swarms are rarely found in tropical climates, they can thrive in temperate areas by magically chilling the environment around them. While in frigid regions, crystalwings collect the seeds of hearty and delicious plants and stick them to their wings, allowing them to cultivate these plants wherever they migrate. Their magical ice gardens are far too cold for flora and fauna native to temperate climates. When swarms of crystalwings band together in a new location, their influence can prove devastating for farmers and wildlife alike. Crystalwings treasure their frigid homes, and they have little comprehension of the difficulties their migrations cause.</p><p>The migration patterns of crystalwings are challenging to predict, as they have far more to do with subtle shifts in wind currents and the flow patterns of nearby ley lines than the seasonal changes that cause most animals to migrate over the course of the year. They are sometimes drawn toward surges of elemental ice, whether they be natural occurrences, the result of a ritual, or a byproduct of the presence of powerful ice eldamon. Within such locations, crystalwings can multiply rapidly, with one swarm giving rise to several more over the course of a few weeks.</p><h2>Fight Fire With Weapons</h2><p>A flight of crystalwings is dazzlingly lovely, but this beauty palls quickly to those whose crops are endangered by the chilly air they bring with them. Despite the hardships they cause, farmers haven't found an effective method for getting rid of crystalwings. Fire is the most effective weapon against crystalwings due to their vulnerability to melting, but fire also brings firelight, which is the crystalwing's most potent weapon. Stymied by this dilemma, farmers often turn toward the most potent weapon in their arsenal: asking adventurers for help.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3.5/7/13 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> armor (light), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, energy resistant (cold, vitality), fortification, ice, light, sensory (low-light vision), sturdy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Icy Wings","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a crystalwing swarm takes damage from an area or splash effect with the fire trait, the butterflies' wings melt. Without their wings, the swarm loses their fly speed as well as their Mirage Swarm ability; this automatically ends Mirage Swarm if it was already active. Additionally, the swarm can't dazzle creatures with Dazzling Frostbite. The crystalwing swarm can spend an Interact action to regrow their wings.</p>"},{"n":"Dazzling Frostbite","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each creature in the crystalwing swarm's space takes 1d6 cold damage. If the crystalwing swarm in bright light, the creature must also succeed at a DC 17 Fortitude save or be Dazzled for 1 round from the brilliance refracting off the swarm's wings.</p>"},{"n":"Mirage Swarm","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["illusion","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The crystalwing swarm is in bright light</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The crystalwing swarm spins light to appear to be larger than it actually is. The swarm chooses four adjacent squares and precisely channels light off of their wings so that those squares appear to have butterflies in them; the illusion persists until the beginning of the swarm's next turn. A creature can disbelieve this illusion with a successful DC 16 Perception check to Seek; once a creature has succeeded at this Perception check, they are temporarily immune to Mirage Swarm for 24 hours.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/f8fvVzhvZW3OivUn":{"lang":["Arboreal","Cryan","Fey","can't speak any language"],"notes":{"per":"snow vision"},"flavor":"<p>Dedstres are withered tree husks that emerge from frozen woodlands, sapping the vitality of those who approach them. Despite their decrepit appearance and penchant for draining life energy, dedstres are not undead or even unnatural; they are merely an embodiment of winter's morbid role in the seasonal cycle. While they're sometimes confused with husks (Battlezoo Bestiary page 44) because of their appearance, dedstres consider husks their enemies; husks are unnatural blighted beings who live only to spread that blight and sow destruction, whereas dedstres might be morbid, but they're part of nature's cycle.</p><p>Dedstres thrive in freezing temperatures. When the cold months are prolonged, whether due to natural climate fluctuations or magical interference, they proliferate in greater numbers, spreading the cold further. Without the sun's warm touch and spring's verdant growth, frozen death can easily overrun the land. Though dedstres aren't typically hostile to other creatures, their incursions can present an existential threat to rural settlements. And once a dedstre has claimed an area, it is nearly impossible to convince them to move.</p><p>When spring at last returns in full force, most dedstres wither away, going dormant until the first nips of frost return.</p><h2>Dead of Winter</h2><p>A dedstre's chill goes beyond mere cold, and dedstres can sap the vitality of even the hardiest boreal forests. Dedstre migrations are frequently marked by paths of pine trees stripped bare of their needles. The destruction that dedstres wreak scatters local wildlife and makes them the enemies of some of the more friendly winter creatures, such as boreals and chionads. However, an area devastated by a dedstre is not totally without life: it makes a good breeding ground for crystalwing swarms.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Nature, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, death, energy resistant (cold, vitality), ice, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Frostbite, Winter's Grasp), strength, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Snow Vision","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Snow doesn't impair the dedstre's vision; they ignore concealment from snowfall.</p>"},{"n":"Encompassing Cold","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. The biting chill in the air around a dedstre is inescapable. The dedstre reduces the temperature to extreme cold, which deals 2d6 cold damage for every 10 minutes of exposure. Mundane cold-weather clothing does not protect against this effect.</p>"},{"n":"Winter's Cycle","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the dedstre hits a plant with an icicle Strike, the plant takes an additional 3 void damage (this damage is doubled on a critical hit).</p>"},{"n":"Boreal Camouflage","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The dedstre is in a boreal forest</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dedstre naturally blends into frozen woodlands. Until the next time they act, they appear as a normal tree and have an automatic result of 36 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as one.</p>"},{"n":"Chill Death","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["cold","death","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The frigid air around the dedstre snuffs out all life. All creatures in a 20-foot emanation take 4d6 cold damage, and plant creatures take 4 additional void damage (DC 25 basic Fortitude save). Creatures that fail the save are Drained 1 (Drained 2 on a critical failure).</p><p>Plant Damage: 4d6+4 void</p>"},{"n":"Sap Heat","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["cold","concentrate","primal"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dedstre attempts to counteract a single magical effect within 60 feet that either grants cold resistance or protection from cold environmental effects, with a counteract modifier of +15. On a success, the effect ends if it is temporary or is suppressed for 1 minute if it is permanent.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/tYQXLpoIJgdDGzPO":{"flavor":"<p>While most oozes dwell in dank dungeons and festering swamps, the dewdrop ooze has adapted to far more pleasant climes. Typically taking on the appearance of crystal-clear water, these oozes have mastered the art of camouflage and populate natural areas such as lakefronts, forests, and river valleys. In areas where standing water is typically tinged with dust, sand, or mud, dewdrop oozes absorb these local particles into their bodies.</p><p>Dewdrop oozes do not actively hunt prey. Instead, they scatter their bodies, hoping for unsuspecting creatures to step within their range. When the opportune moment arrives, they regroup around the target, capturing them unawares and imprisoning them within their acidic body. By hiding among the condensation that gathers on the grass at dawn or the moisture dripping from stalactites, dewdrop oozes can mount a deadly ambush on even the most seasoned adventurer. They sometimes perch upon the webs of giant spiders, concealing themselves in the hopes of poaching a meal or even gobbling up the spider itself.</p><p>Luckily, dewdrop oozes are notoriously sedentary. Rather than chasing down creatures that flee their grasp, they scatter their bodies into tiny droplets again, resetting their deadly trap. Once they find a meal, they disincorporate, leaving enough droplets on the body to digest it while allowing the rest of their forms to catch additional prey. While dewdrop oozes do not seek companionship from their own kind, they sometimes cluster together in favorable hunting grounds, creating a deadly gauntlet.</p><p>Dewdrop oozes are particularly vulnerable to loud noises. Upon contact with powerful soundwaves, the oozes reflexively reform, revealing themselves and spoiling any potential ambush. A prudent adventurer wanting to avoid an encounter with dewdrop oozes ought to keep a thunderstone or similar item in their back pocket. These masters of camouflage can also be exposed by the uncanny sight of their recent prey, partially digested corpses slick with acid dew.</p><h2>Sum of Parts</h2><p>Even a single dewdrop separated from its whole remains animate, and individual droplets are sometimes bought and sold as curiosities. Strangely, lone dewdrops seem to recognize the ooze from which they were formed and refuse to join with other oozes. This peculiar behavior has led some naturalists to speculate that dewdrop oozes are more intelligent than they appear, or that they have been imbued with self-identity by an elemental—or perhaps even divine—power.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, bane, constitution, energy resistant (acid, fire, vitality), fortification, music, poison, poisonous armor, strength, throwing, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A dewdrop ooze can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Cobble Together","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the dewdrop ooze takes sonic damage while Disincorporated, the dewdrop ooze is forcibly reformed into a single coherent entity, and can't use Regroup until after it Disincorporates again, and the ooze is Off-Guard for 1 round</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Engulf","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>DC 29 Reflex, 6d6 acid damage, Escape, Rupture 17</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of any creatures in its path. Any creature of the monster's size or smaller whose space the monster moves through can attempt a Reflex save with the listed DC to avoid being engulfed. A creature unable to act automatically critically fails this save. If a creature succeeds at its save, it can choose to be either pushed aside (out of the monster's path) or pushed in front of the monster to the end of the monster's movement. The monster can attempt to Engulf the same creature only once in a single use of Engulf. The monster can contain as many creatures as can fit in its space.</p>\n<p>A creature that fails its save is pulled into the monster's body. It is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The creature takes the listed amount of damage when first engulfed and at the end of each of its turns while it's engulfed. An engulfed creature can get free by Escaping against the listed escape DC. An engulfed creature can attack the monster engulfing it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The engulfing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the engulfed creature cuts itself free.\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>A creature that gets free by either method can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, all creatures it has engulfed are automatically released as the monster's form loses cohesion.</p></p>"},{"n":"Disincorporate","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Until the next time it acts, the dewdrop ooze disperses its body into tiny droplets. It has an automatic result of 42 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as mundane dew. If the dewdrop ooze had any creatures Grabbed or engulfed, they are automatically released.</p>"},{"n":"Regroup","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The dewdrop ooze has used Disincorporate</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dewdrop ooze reforms into a single coherent entity. All creatures in a 10-foot emanation around the ooze are splattered with acid, taking 6d6 acid damage (DC 26 basic Reflex save). The ooze then uses Engulf. It can't use Regroup again until after it Disincorporates.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/REGya8TV5IQPPkrp":{"notes":{"per":"spacewarp sense (precise) 120 feet","speed":"dimensional squeeze"},"flavor":"<p>Dimension oozes spontaneously take form around significant releases of space-bending magic. They are then drawn to feed on any sources of such magic, from specimens as small as a spacious pouch to ones as impressive as permanent interdimensional portals. Indeed, as they make no distinction between themselves and their food sources, a meeting of dimension oozes generally results in a cannibalistic feeding frenzy. While those who fear the ooze's appetites might initially believe such meetings would reduce the threat, the oozy spectacle invariably ends with a single, far larger, and more powerful dimension ooze emerging to continue its hungry rampage.</p><p>Dimension oozes have been known to \"leak\" from plane to plane, drawn by the scent of teleportation magic, to suddenly spring upon a careless caster or unattended magical item. They are a particular danger during the creation of teleportation circles, as their unexpected emergence at the culmination of the ritual can turn a celebration of magical accomplishment into a scene of terror. High-end shops that sell magic items must also take precautions to prevent the oozes from eating their merchandise.</p><h2>Living Portals</h2><p>Images of distant worlds, alien planets, and perhaps even alternate realities are often seen winking in and out across a dimension ooze's iridescent surface. Though this might suggest that the ooze also exists in those places—effectively inhabiting multiple locations at once—to date, all attempts to use dimension oozes for instantaneous travel have ended in failure. Furthermore, the discovery of acid-scarred bones from creatures engulfed by a dimension ooze serves as further evidence that exploring this endeavor further is likely to be futile.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), force, fortification, space, space shield, spells (Occult: Translocate), strength, throwing, time, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A dimension ooze can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Spacewarp Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The dimension ooze immediately knows the precise origin point and destination of any creatures affected by a teleportation effect within the listed range. It can also pinpoint the location of any extraplanar spaces, even those within objects such as a <em>spacious pouch</em>.</p>"},{"n":"Devour Magic","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the dimension ooze's reach uses an effect with the teleportation or extradimensional traits</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The dimension ooze makes a pseudopod Strike against the creature. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the dimension ooze's multiple attack penalty. On a hit, the ooze attempts to counteract the effect (counteract modifier +20). If the counteract check succeeds, the ooze becomes Quickened until the end of its next turn. It can use the quickened action to Stride or Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Withdraw","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["extradimensional","occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The dimension ooze takes damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ooze retreats into a temporary extraplanar space until the start of its next turn. At the beginning of its next turn, it reappears in a random unoccupied space within 10 feet of its original location.</p>"},{"n":"Dimensional Squeeze","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dimension ooze can slip between the cracks of the physical world. When it moves or makes a pseudopod Strike, it treats any square within 30 feet as adjacent to it. This effectively grants its pseudopod Strikes 30 feet of reach and allows it to Step or Stride up to 30 feet despite its Speed unless this special ability is prevented from functioning.</p>"},{"n":"Engulf","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>DC 28 Reflex, 3d10 force damage, Escape, Rupture 15</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of any creatures in its path. Any creature of the monster's size or smaller whose space the monster moves through can attempt a Reflex save with the listed DC to avoid being engulfed. A creature unable to act automatically critically fails this save. If a creature succeeds at its save, it can choose to be either pushed aside (out of the monster's path) or pushed in front of the monster to the end of the monster's movement. The monster can attempt to Engulf the same creature only once in a single use of Engulf. The monster can contain as many creatures as can fit in its space.</p>\n<p>A creature that fails its save is pulled into the monster's body. It is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The creature takes the listed amount of damage when first engulfed and at the end of each of its turns while it's engulfed. An engulfed creature can get free by Escaping against the listed escape DC. An engulfed creature can attack the monster engulfing it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The engulfing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the engulfed creature cuts itself free.\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>A creature that gets free by either method can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, all creatures it has engulfed are automatically released as the monster's form loses cohesion.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/mtGxBnlkfT1rOqgY":{"lang":["Common","Drumcap","Fey","can't speak any language except drumcap"],"notes":{"per":"low-light vision"},"flavor":"<p>Drumcaps are friendly, curious, and sociable fungus creatures who originate from forests thick with primal magic. Their bodies are shaped like chubby mushrooms, with large, colorful caps, stubby legs, and long arms ending in hollow spheres reminiscent of drumsticks. To communicate with each other, drumcaps bang their arms on their caps, producing percussive beats whose subtle shifts of rhythm and volume weave together into a complex language all their own.</p><p>Drumcaps emerge when sufficiently large quantities of drumcap spores collect in the network of fungal mycelium beneath the forest floor, spontaneously causing dozens of the mushrooms above to grow drastically in size and awaken to sapience as drumcaps. Thus, every drumcap is born as a part of a larger brood, and drumcaps often maintain a close connection to their siblings throughout their lives. Drumcap broods typically cluster together into nomadic villages traveling to places in their forest with the thickest primal magic, which can change across the year due to the shifting of ley lines and other magical phenomena.</p><p>In addition to being a vital part of drumcap reproduction, drumcap spores have hallucinogenic properties. While drumcaps do not experience these visual effects, they appreciate that many other creatures do, and they provide spores to visitors to enhance the experience of listening to their music. Unfortunately, interest in these spores sometimes leads unscrupulous individuals to hunt drumcaps; poaching is particularly common in regions where the locals' inability to communicate with drumcaps leads them to drastically underestimate the drumcaps' intelligence.</p><h2>Drums of Doom</h2><p>Communities of drumcaps occasionally fall under the sway of malevolent forces such as demons or tyrannical humanoids. Other times, an individual drumcap grows spiteful and bitter after an outsider does harm to their kin, knowingly or not. Such leaders encourage their followers to seek revenge for their suffering or simply menace those who wander into the drumcaps' territory. The drumming of these malicious drumcaps is ominous, not welcoming, and the visions caused by their hallucinogenic spores are those of vivid terror.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3.5/7/13 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), shield, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, music, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), sturdy, tangle shield, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Impulsive Dispersal","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per hour</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The drumcap takes bludgeoning damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The drumcap takes the full force of the attack on their cap, releasing a high concentration of hallucinogenic spores in a 5-foot emanation that persists for 1d4 rounds. Creatures who start their turn in the emanation take 2d4 poison damage (DC 17 basic Fortitude save) and are Dazzled for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Fascinating Cuteness","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","manipulate","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The drumcap beats happily at their cap and does a dance to distract those around them. All creatures within a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 17 Will save to avoid being entranced. This effect lasts for 1 round, but if the drumcap uses this ability again on subsequent rounds, they extend the duration. Once a creature succeeds at any save against Fascinating Cuteness, that creature is temporarily immune to Fascinating Cuteness for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Fascinated by the drumcap's cuteness.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the fascination does not end if the drumcap attacks one of the creature's allies; it still ends normally if the drumcap attacks the fascinated creature.</p>"},{"n":"Psychedelic Spores","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>With a successful cap Strike, a drumcap releases a small puff of hallucinogenic spores. The target must succeed at a DC 17 Fortitude save or be Dazzled for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/4BQjFfinxOGmjWWv":{"lang":["Aklo","Can't speak"],"flavor":"<p>Eldritch tyrants form when an intelligent aberrant symbiote binds to a tyrannosaurus rex, forming a new apex predator that stands far above the original tyrannosaurus rex on the food chain. Eldritch tyrants aren't usually actively evil, but they're quite destructive, and they have extreme difficulty comprehending the idea that any language other than Aklo is actually a language, rather than a more rudimentary form of communication like the squawks of birds. Someone who can communicate with a typical eldritch tyrant in Aklo has a decent chance to convince it to eat something else instead of a sapient creature, in exchange for acting with the proper deference towards it and perhaps performing tasks on its behalf. However, someone who attempts this strategy on a chaotic evil eldritch tyrant, or one in an especially foul or hungry mood, will quickly realize the result is not always so positive.</p><h2>Mind Over Monster</h2><p>The symbiote psychic sensitivity, the cracks between the symbiote and the tyrannosaurus, and the tyrannosaurus's relatively undeveloped mind without the symbiote's help make an eldritch tyrant vulnerable to the same sorts of psychic energies that it uses to attack others. However, this matter is something of a sore spot for most eldritch tyrants, so there's little chance of engaging in a diplomatic approach after exploiting an eldritch tyrant's weakness to mental effects, or even mentioning it in conversation.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), strength, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The eldritch tyrant has a creature Grabbed in its mouth tentacle</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The eldritch tyrant constricts the grabbed creature, crushing both its body and mind. The creature takes 3d12+4 bludgeoning damage and 2d6 mental damage, with a basic Fortitude save. On a failure, the creature is also Stupefied 1 for 1 minute, and on a critical failure, they're Stupefied 2 for 1 minute instead.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Huge, 3d12 bludgeoning plus 2d6 mental, Rupture 39</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Grabbing Pull","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The eldritch tyrant's last action was a successful mouth tentacle Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The eldritch tyrant pulls its victim in closer and then grabs it in place. The eldritch tyrant can move the creature it hit with its mouth tentacle towards it by an amount of its choosing. Then it Grabs the creature with its mouth tentacle. It can transfer the creature to be Grabbed by its jaws instead as a free action, depending on whether it is planning to use Constrict or Swallow Whole next.</p>"},{"n":"Fast Swallow","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The eldritch tyrant Grabs a creature with its jaws</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The eldritch tyrant uses Swallow Whole.</p>"},{"n":"Mind-Tearing Roar","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental","occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The eldritch tyrant lets loose a powerful roar, tearing through the minds of all who hear it and terrifying them, while leaving their bodies battered by sonic damage. Creatures within a 120-foot emanation take 7d6 mental damage and 7d6 sonic damage with a DC 36 Will save, with the effects below. After using its mind-tearing roar, the eldritch tyrant can't use it again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage and is Frightened 2 and Fleeing for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/LEZUVizmJszf7Pta":{"lang":["Luxan","Talican","Utopian","Voltan"],"flavor":"<p>Electrum juggernauts are oversized, statue-like creatures made of a glittering alloy of gold and silver, with their outer shells formed into suits of glistening plate armor. These suits often mimic humanoid skin and muscles, while others embody the classic appearance of a gallant knight. No matter their style, their weapons are always the same, with one of their hands constructed around a longsword while the other features an inbuilt gauntlet. Although they appear as humanoids, their interiors are hollow, featuring a floating core that constantly emits electricity, which they can easily channel through their conductive frames.</p><p>Electrum juggernauts are known for their exceptional vanity, to the point where they are physically weakened when their glittering armor is soiled. They take immense pride in performing their assigned tasks to perfection. They typically guard massive treasure vaults, museums, dragon hoards, and other places that display precious objects.</p><h2>Neat and Tidy</h2><p>Adventurers planning to negotiate with an electrum juggernaut are well advised to polish and clean their equipment beforehand. Electrum juggernauts can't abide the sight of dirt on others any more than on themselves. However, for adventurers planning to fight an electrum juggernaut, filth can be an ally. While an electrum juggernaut has no qualms about stabbing a dirty enemy with their sword, they might think twice before grappling with one.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Crafting, Intimidation, Religion, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), light, metal, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Light), spiked, strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Conductive Shell","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["divine","electricity"],"f":"","d":"<p>The electrum juggernaut channels electricity through their metal frame. Until the beginning of their next turn, creatures that hit them with an unarmed attack or a melee attack or who otherwise touch them take 3d6 electricity damage. A Grabbed creature takes this damage once at the start of that creature's turn.</p>"},{"n":"Vanity","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The electrum juggernaut takes pride in their armor's gleaming radiance and becomes metaphysically weakened if the armor is soiled. When a creature adjacent to the electrum juggernaut takes piercing or slashing damage or uses an Interact action to soil the electrum juggernaut's armor, or when a creature uses a spell or ability that would reasonably cause the juggernaut to become dirty at the GM's discretion, the juggernaut must attempt a DC 28 Reflex save to avoid becoming stained. On a failure, the electrum juggernaut's AC is reduced to 31 and they lose both their resistance to physical damage and their Sparkling Armor ability. These effects last until the juggernaut spends 10 minutes cleaning their armor.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Electrum Armaments","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The electrum juggernaut's weapons are made of electrum, an alloy of gold and silver, and trigger weaknesses to both metals. They are built in to the juggernaut's body and can't be Disarmed.</p>"},{"n":"Sparkling Armor","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","light","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>While the electrum juggernaut is within bright or dim light, they sparkle majestically. Their attempts to Demoralize have the visual trait instead of the auditory trait, ignore the penalty for not sharing a language, and also dazzle the target for 1 round on a success or critical success.</p>"},{"n":"Wide Swing","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The electrum juggernaut makes a longsword Strike and compares the attack roll result to the ACs of up to two foes within their reach. They roll damage only once and apply it to each creature they hit. A Wide Swing counts as two attacks for their multiple attack penalty.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/BM9pN2xyIJaLRbHK":{"lang":["Common","Draconic","Empyrean","Jotun","Melodian"],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status vs. auditory or sonic effects"},"flavor":"<p>Born from the souls of mortals whose songs have stirred the hearts of the masses in life, emirads carry the song that resonates across the rolling plains of Elysium in their hearts. Emirads embody the harmony among those who join together in song, as well as the power of music to unite people and bridge the cultural gap between them. These titanic musicians travel far and wide, eager to learn new musical pieces and styles to refine their craft. Because of this, they often blend the music of various cultures and nations around the world, and even across the planes.</p><p>Emirads often dwell in isolated locales to avoid disturbing others with their supernaturally booming voices. This habit can lead to rumors of \"wailing sirens\" calling down from hidden valleys and ensnaring the hearts of those who listen too closely.</p><p>The greatest among the emirad are known as emirad idols. These taller, more powerful idols have perfected their craft to the point that their songs can stop warring armies in their tracks.</p><h2>Sing it From the Mountaintops</h2><p>Besides fine voices, emirads have exceptionally good hearing, especially for melody. While a mortal a mile away can hear an emirad's song, another emirad can hear the same tune from dozens or even hundreds of miles away. Distant emirads often sing together in complex, multipart harmonies, a practice that puzzles mortals who can hear only one of the vocal parts.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Diplomacy, Medicine, Performance, Religion, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, energy resistant (sonic, vitality), holy, life, lifeward armor, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Calm, Dissonance, Enthrall, Heal, Noise Blast, Steal the Show, Summon Instrument), strength, throwing, time, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Booming Voice","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sound of an emirad's voice carries far. All listeners within 1 mile can hear the emirad loudly and clearly whenever the emirad speaks, even if other ambient noise would otherwise block the sound. The emirad can't choose to speak any more quietly even if they wanted to do so.</p>"},{"n":"Counter Performance","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p> The emirad can use Counter Performance at will.</p>"},{"n":"Listen to my Song!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","divine","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An auditory effect created by the emirad would be counteracted, or the target of an auditory effect created by the emirad would be unaffected due to an active spell or ability</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The emirad lets out a cry that will not be silenced. They attempt to counteract the triggering effect, using their Performance modifier as their counteract modifier. On a critical success that counteracts the effect, if the creature who created the triggering effect is within 1 mile, that creature gains weakness 5 to sonic damage for 1 minute.</p><p>Effect: Listen to my Song!</p>"},{"n":"Right to the Chorus","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"once per minute","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The emirad becomes Quickened for 1 round. This additional action can only be used to make a sound bolt Strike, to cast Courageous Anthem or Rallying Anthem, or to begin or Sustain Resounding Refrain. The emirad is then Slowed 1 on their next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Resounding Refrain","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The emirad's last action was a successful sound bolt Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The emirad stores up an echo of the sonic energy, preparing for a grand finale. The emirad can Sustain this ability for up to 1 minute. The first time the emirad ends their turn without Sustaining, they make a number of sound bolt Strikes equal to the total number of actions they have spent on Resounding Refrain. These attacks do not apply their existing multiple attack penalty. The multiple attack penalty on these Strikes increases when the emirad makes an additional sound bolt Strike on a creature that they have already targeted with one of their Strikes from Resounding Refrain.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/1TzMMIEpMfMskqWP":{"notes":{"hp":"egeneration 10 (deactivated by void)"},"flavor":"<p>Vitality energy is the essence of growth and renewal, but not all renewal is healthy for the creatures exposed to it. When a core of vitality energy infests a mass of plant life, it occasionally mutates into an endless growth. As its name suggests, an endless growth expands continuously, and when it finally grows too large to move, it splits into four smaller copies to move to new locations and continue growing. However, this division is far from the creature's only means of propagation. It instinctively seeks to use all other living creatures as fuel to make more of its kind. When its thorny burls injure a creature, it infests their blood with a disease that gradually converts their flesh into another endless growth. It can also eject its own ravenous tumors, which rapidly consume vitality before taking on a life of their own.</p><p>Plant and fungal life around an endless growth and its ever-expanding brood of progeny transforms into a blighted forest. Growths and lesions such as burls, cankers, galls, and witch's brooms are commonplace in these lands, along with more unusual mutations. The alchemical and medicinal potential of the rapid mutations that endless growths generate is substantial. Several groups of researchers have decided that it was worth the risk of cultivating their blighted forests deliberately, only to find themselves overrun by the forest's relentlessly hungry denizens.</p><h2>The Living All</h2><p>Some misguided forest dwellers worship endless growths as something akin to gods. Believing the growths are a physical manifestation of the intangible force that gives life to all things—a power they name \"the Living All\"—these wayward souls make living sacrifices to the growths to encourage their spread throughout the Universe. That their gods sometimes find fertile soil in their own flesh seems to make little difference to the devotees of the Living All.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, death, energy resistant (vitality), life, lifeward armor, sensory (low-light vision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Regeneration 10 (Deactivated by Void)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster regains the listed number of Hit Points each round at the beginning of its turn. Its Dying condition never increases beyond Dying 3 as long as its regeneration is active. However, if it takes damage of a type listed in the regeneration entry, its regeneration deactivates until the end of its next turn. Deactivate the regeneration before applying any damage of a listed type, since that damage might kill the monster by bringing it to Dying 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Flesh Conversion","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p>Plants, fungi, and creatures with void healing are immune to Flesh Conversion</p><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 25 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Sickened 1 (1 hour)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Sickened 2 (1 hour)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> when rolling a recovery check, the creature's degree of success is one degree better (3 hours)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> the creature gains fast healing 5 (5 hours)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> the creature gains lifesense 60 feet and regeneration 10 (deactivated by void) (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 6</strong> the creature dies and its body is fully converted into new endless growth.</p>"},{"n":"Infectious Growth","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The living growth's last action was a successful parasitic tumor Strike that damaged a living creature. The damaged creature isn't Drained 4 and doesn't already have an infectious growth attached to it</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The parasitic tumor attempts to attach itself to the creature's body (DC 25 Fortitude negates). If it succeeds, the creature is Drained 1. This is cumulative with other drained conditions from infectious growth. At the end of each of its turns, a creature with an attached parasitic tumor must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save. On a failure, the value of its drained condition increases by 1. If the value of the drained condition reaches 4, the parasitic tumor then falls off and develops into a new endless growth over the next 24 hours. The affected creature or an adjacent creature can remove the tumor with a successful DC 31 Medicine check. The tumor automatically falls off if the creature takes void damage or critically succeeds at their Fortitude save to resist the drained condition.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/BszbQD9VW4lpZcUy":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Can't speak, Death of the author"],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Eternal inventors arise when a genius inventor takes a mortal wound from an ambitious experiment and spends their final moments frantically recording observations in their journal. Their spirit becomes trapped in the journal, where it is doomed to relive its failure.</p><p>Though the journal is a physical object, it is surrounded by the spectral form of the inventor. The inventor incessantly carries out their final experiment, confident that they'll soon succeed. However, their efforts are doomed to fail, and they pay no heed to the death and destruction that their obsession creates. The only way for an eternal inventor's spirit to rest is for someone to complete their final project. This task is incredibly difficult, not only because the eternal inventor's dreams push the boundaries of the possible but also because they tend to jealously slay anyone they suspect of attempting to steal their glory.</p><p>An eternal inventor is often surrounded by numerous hazards and inventions, and the line between the two is surprisingly difficult to discern.</p><h2>They Called It Impossible!</h2><p>For some reason, inventors seem drawn to experiments involving lightning or explosions, so their deaths usually involve fiery or electrical mishaps. That said, some eternal inventors found more creative ways to die and wish to share that experience with those who stumble upon their final resting places. Some of these experiments include attempts to harness antigravity (causing the inventor to fall up into an improbably sturdy ceiling fan), a method for fast healing involving the acceleration of local time (causing flesh to crumble into dust and blow away), and the preservation of food using cold (predictably causing the inventor to be frozen within a block of ice).</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> burning barbs, death, dexterity, electricity, energy resistant (void), fire, fire shield, ice, intelligence, mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Canticle of Everlasting Grief, Disintegrate, Duplicate Foe, Embed Message, Falling Stars, Phantasmagoria, Quandary, Telekinetic Haul, Telekinetic Storm, Volcanic Eruption), spirit, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Death of the Author","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal inventor communicates by replaying past scenes of their speech during life. They can't say any words that they didn't say at least once while alive.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When an eternal inventor is destroyed, it re-forms after 2d4 days in its creator's office or workshop, fully healed. An eternal inventor can only be permanently destroyed if the project that took the creator's life is successfully completed.</p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Jinx of Instability","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet of the eternal inventor performs an action with the manipulate trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The eternal inventor curses the object the creature is manipulating or the manipulating creature. The creature must attempt a DC 38 Will save. On a failure, the action is disrupted and the creature injures itself, taking 13 untyped damage of an appropriate type for the item or action's nature.</p>"},{"n":"Fire the Omnicannon!","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal inventor manifests a floating cannon in their square and fires it in their choice of two 30-foot cones or two 60-foot lines that point in opposite directions. The cannon deals 4d8 physical damage and 4d4 energy damage (DC 38 basic Reflex save). For each direction, they choose between bludgeoning, piercing, and slashing for the physical damage and between cold, electricity, fire, or sonic for the energy damage.</p><p>4d6 bludgeoning</p><p>4d6 piercing</p><p>4d6 slashing</p><p>4d6 cold</p><p>4d6 electricity</p><p>4d6 fire</p><p>4d6 sonic</p>"},{"n":"Instantaneous Calculation","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a creature resists or is weak to damage dealt by the eternal inventor, they automatically determine the resistance or weakness's type and amount.</p>"},{"n":"Relive Catastrophe","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal inventor replicates the accident that claimed their life, such the in-flight detonation of an alchemical jetpack attempting to break the sound barrier or the unstoppable rampage of a nigh-invincible construct that turned against its creator. Each creature within a 60-foot emanation around the eternal inventor takes 16d6 untyped damage of a type appropriate to the accident's nature, such as bludgeoning or fire (DC 40 basic Reflex save). Creatures that fail the saving throw are also flooded with the creator's dying despair, becoming Frightened 2, or Frightened 3 on a critical failure. The eternal inventor can't use Relive Catastrophe for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ri3dGbG8YJkuzk13":{"lang":["Common","Melodian","Can't speak"],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status to all saves vs. auditory or sonic"},"flavor":"<p>Some bards and other skilled musicians dare to do it all. They don't need a band holding them back. An eternal symphony is an amalgamation of an entire orchestra's worth of instruments, created by those who wish to go solo without sacrificing the sonic landscape a band or orchestra can supply. As an added bonus, these massive constructs make great bodyguards.</p><p>Sometimes, these constructs outlive their bandmates, while other times, their creators can't handle the upstaging and abandon their creations to wander alone. Regardless of their situation, these constructs focus their efforts on what they do best: entertaining. This tendency can conflict with those who don't want to be followed by a large walking orchestra.</p><h2>Everyone's a Critic</h2><p>Eternal symphonies can't create new works, but they can play their original repertoire flawlessly, sometimes better than their creator. This can drive some composers wild with jealousy, especially when the eternal symphony's performances are in greater demand than those of the original musician. For their part, an eternal symphony typically has an almost religious veneration for the music first taught to them by their creator, while not particularly liking their creator's new works.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (sonic, spirit, vitality, void), mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Musical Flourish","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal symphony is versatile with its musical performances. It can use Performance to Create a Diversion, Demoralize, or Feint.</p>"},{"n":"Encore","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the eternal symphony is reduced to 0 HP, it plays a final swan song before it is destroyed. It uses Battle Symphony.</p>"},{"n":"Reverberate","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The instruments that make up the eternal symphony's body are susceptible to being played not only by itself, but also by its enemies. Whenever the eternal symphony takes bludgeoning damage or is affected by an air effect, all creatures adjacent to the eternal symphony take 1d6 sonic damage.</p>"},{"n":"Dissonance","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["auditory","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Any creature hit by an eternal symphony's resonant vibration Strike must succeed on a DC 34 Fortitude save or become Stupefied 1 for 1 round (or Stupefied 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Accelerando","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal symphony increases the tempo of its music. It gains the Quickened condition for 1 minute and can use the extra action each round only to Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Battle Symphony","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal symphony plays a terrifying song of destruction in a 30-foot emanation. Each creature in the area takes 7d6 mental damage (DC 36 basic Fortitude save). A creature that fails this save is also Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Crescendo","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal symphony gradually increases the intensity of its music. Until the end of its turn, sonic and auditory effects deal an additional 1d6 sonic damage. This effect is cumulative for each consecutive turn that the eternal symphony has used Crescendo (to a maximum of 4d6 extra sonic damage).</p>"},{"n":"Rhythmic Cadence","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The eternal symphony plays an infectious rhythm that inspires who hear it to dance uncontrollably. The eternal symphony makes two kick drum Strikes. Both attacks count toward the eternal symphony's multiple attack penalty, but the penalty increases only after both attacks. If both Strikes hit, each creature in a 30-foot emanation must attempt a DC 34 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Off-Guard until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Slowed 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Slowed 2 for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/IlxNDJjp74dTKBkp":{"lang":["Chthonian","Telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"ac":" all-around vision"},"flavor":"<p>Eunidois are sadistic killers whose bodies are revolting facsimiles of the titanic cave worms that prowl far underground. Simple eyes adorn their segmented bodies, flickering rapidly with paranoia, while their heads sport three bony mandibles coated in fine bristles. These bristles help them optimize the positioning of their mandibles to cause their prey as much pain as possible. Eunidoi's mouths are shallow caverns containing a forest of spine-covered tongues dripping with toxic mucus. Yet these caverns lead nowhere, as eunidois lack a stomach. Instead, they feed on their prey's suffering and terror.</p><p>Most eunidois prefer to lounge around in burrowed tunnel networks and lie in wait for others to approach, whereupon they pounce and rapidly drag their prey to a secluded area. As such, these qlippoths are often found near places of great contention: borders between qlippoth and demon realms, interplanar portals, and other well-trodden paths. Though their habits could have made them helpful guardians for other qlippoths, they avoid making alliances as a matter of course. Eunidoi are fundamentally incapable of understanding the concept of trustworthiness. They only work with other creatures if they are certain they can temporarily exploit and kill them once their usefulness has expired.</p><h2>More And Less Than Worms</h2><p>Though they bear a superficial resemblance to cave worms—and indeed, often share territory with such creatures—eunidois are both more cunning and far less physically imposing. Eunidois are generally uninterested in preying upon their larger neighbors and are smart enough to avoid them, even while using their tunnels to track down prey that the cave worms might have missed. If cornered by a cave worm, however, a eunidoi might call out to nearby adventurers for help, offering information in exchange for help defeating the more powerful predator.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, death, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Daze, Detect Magic, Paranoia, Phantom Pain, Translocate, Vision of Death), strength, sturdy, unholy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Inexorable","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The eunidoi recovers from the Paralyzed, slowed, and stunned conditions at the end of its turn. It's also immune to penalties to its Speeds and the Immobilized condition, and it ignores difficult terrain and greater difficult terrain.</p>"},{"n":"Predatory Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As Improved Grab, but the eunidoi's Improved Grab does not end when it moves. Instead, it carries the Grabbed creature with it.</p><hr><p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Mindrot","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 28 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d6 mental damage and Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d6 mental damage, Stupefied 2, and Off-Guard (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d6 mental damage, stupefied 2, and off-guard (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Rock Tunneler","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A eunidoi can burrow through solid stone at a Speed of 15 feet. It can leave a tunnel if it desires, and it usually does.</p>"},{"n":"Frightening Display","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The eunidoi's mandibles snap open, revealing a gaping mouth filled with bristled tongues. Creatures in a 30-foot emanation must attempt a DC 28 Will save, after which they are temporarily immune to further Frightening Displays for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 3 and can't reduce the value of its frightened condition below 1 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Ambush","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The eunidoi Strides or Burrows and then Strikes. If the eunidoi began this action hidden, it remains hidden until after the Strike.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/HlVB4lImHp4UdmEH":{"notes":{"per":"spellsense (imprecise) 60 feet"},"flavor":"<p>A war between magically advanced civilizations is ruthless and cataclysmic; the infusion of magical fallout and death energy changes and warps the environment and all creatures that manage to survive.</p><p>Fallout sparrows are birds that are forever shaped by the devastation. They travel in flocks over war zones, sustaining themselves on the magical radiation left by the conflict. Travelers or refugees fleeing war often hear these birds' tragic songs before seeing their irradiated glow. These birds sing of a battle's final moments with an eerie and discordant exactitude, imitating the whistle of bombs falling, the crash of magical detonations, or the wailing of the maimed and bereaved.</p><p>Fallout sparrow swarms fly in abrupt, unpredictable, and violently jerking murmurations, the sight of which is both unnerving and strangely captivating. Due to the way they have been warped by arcane energies, they are finely attuned to those capable of casting spells and often flock around them to feed off their magic.</p><p>When a fallout sparrow swarm is slain, it is said to leave behind a ghostly echo, which continues to sing its melancholy song.</p><h2>Blast Shadows</h2><p>Fallout sparrows that are themselves destroyed by a surge of magical energy often leave behind an echo of their unsettling existence. These so-called \"blast shadows\" might manifest as an immediate burst of psychic energy, an undead creature like a shadow, a lingering supernatural phenomenon similar to a haunt, or merely the outline of the bird's silhouette upon a nearby stone.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Performance, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, music, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spellsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The fallout sparrow swarm detects the magical resonance within magic items and within creatures that can cast spells.</p>"},{"n":"Fallout Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["arcane","aura","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. Fallout sparrows emit an aura of magical radiation. A creature that begins its turn in the area becomes Sickened 1 (DC 22 Fortitude negates).</p>"},{"n":"Strange Murmuration","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The fallout sparrow swarm is targeted by a spell</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The swarm Flies 15 feet toward the caster.</p>"},{"n":"Devastation Mimic","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","auditory","emotion","fear","mental","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The flock of sparrows emits an eerie, discordant birdsong, mimicking the sounds of explosions, collapses, and screams. Each creature within 20 feet must attempt a DC 25 Will save. On a successful save, the creature becomes temporarily immune to Devastation Mimic for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2 and takes 4d6 sonic damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is frightened 2 and takes 8d6 sonic damage.</p>"},{"n":"Fallout Rain","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The fallout sparrows Fly, spraying droplets of magically radioactive poison in their wake. All creatures in and below the squares that they Fly through must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save. The swarm can't use Fallout Rain again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 3d6 poison damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 6d6 poison damage and is Sickened 1.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes 12d6 poison damage and is Sickened 2.</p>"},{"n":"Necrotizing Beaks","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The fallout sparrows' angry pecking deals 2d6 piercing plus 2d6 void damage to each enemy in the swarm's space (DC 25 basic Reflex save).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zM1DZ9vUKsM9oL38":{"notes":{"per":"metalsense (imprecise) 60 feet","speed":"magnetic climb"},"flavor":"<p>When an acidic ooze dissolves a large amount of metal, typically iron, it can morph into a ferrofluidic ooze. The churning of the microscopic metal particles within the ooze's gelatinous membranes generates a magnetic field that grants it an unusual physiology. Its smooth black reflective membrane is held together in orderly geometric patterns that it can manipulate. Its endless consumption of metal has rendered its acid ineffective against organic substances, though it can still feed on metal. Like magnetic objects, large amounts of heat can temporarily demagnetize a ferrofluidic ooze.</p><p>These creatures constantly seek out rich metal sources like iron mines, armories, or foundries, posing a threat to blacksmiths and knights alike.</p><h2>Ferrofluid</h2><p>The goop that makes up a ferrofluidic ooze retains its magnetic properties for an hour after the ooze's death. During that time, up to four creatures can apply the ferrofluid to their hands and feet as a 1-minute activity with the manipulate trait. For the next 10 minutes, each creature gains the ferrofluidic ooze's Magnetic Climb ability but is also clumsy 1 for the duration.</p><p>Alternatively, the ferrofluid can be used as one application of <em>Everlasting Adhesive</em>, but only to form a bond between two surfaces made of metal. The ferrofluid loses its properties after 1 hour, but if used to bind two metal surfaces, the surfaces remain joined until the bond is broken, typically with a successful DC 50 Athletics check.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (acid, vitality), fortification, metal, strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Metalsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Due to its innate magnetism, a ferrofluidic ooze senses nearby chunks of metal of at least light Bulk.</p>"},{"n":"Demagnetize","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Overwhelming heat can interfere with the ferrofluidic ooze's magnetism. If the ferrofluidic ooze takes 50 or more fire damage at the same time, it temporarily loses its magnetic field. Until the end of its next turn, it loses magnetic attraction, magnetic climb, magnetic coating, magnetic hold, merger, and metalsense.</p>"},{"n":"Magnetic Hold","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 10 feet of the ferrofluidic ooze deals slashing damage to it</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ooze splits in half before quickly reforming due to its strong magnetic fields. The ooze attempts an Athletics check to Grapple the triggering creature; it does not need to have a free hand to do so. This check neither applies nor counts toward the ferrofluidic ooze's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Magnetic Attraction","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","metal","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ferrofluidic ooze manipulates its magnetic field to draw metal toward itself. Each creature within 30 feet that is either wearing metal armor or affected by the ooze's magnetic coating must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save. This is forced movement. The forced movement ends once the creature is adjacent to the ooze. Unattended metal objects in the area with a Bulk of 10 or less are automatically drawn 20 feet toward the ooze.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature moves 5 feet toward the ooze.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature moves 10 feet toward the ooze.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature moves 20 feet toward the ooze.</p>"},{"n":"Magnetic Climb","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["metal","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ferrofluidic ooze can climb on any surface made out of metal, including ceilings and other inverted surfaces, though such surfaces are difficult terrain for it.</p>"},{"n":"Magnetic Coating","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["metal","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a ferrofluidic ooze hits a creature with a pseudopod or magnetic globule Strike, the creature is coated in magnetic fluid, becoming Clumsy 1. The creature can spend a single manipulate action to scrape off the fluid.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/fA3sxCyMCdFHJOLQ":{"notes":{"per":"metalsense (imprecise) 60 feet","speed":"(swim in lava or magma only)"},"flavor":"<p>Found around active volcanoes, ferrous crabs spend their days filtering molten iron and other metals from magma flows and cooled lava rock. They manipulate their bodies' natural magnetism to propel themselves through magma channels. In their natural habitats, they are calm and docile creatures.</p><p>However, when the lava flow dries up or another nearby volcano erupts, ferrous crabs gather in massive swarms to migrate to a new home. Their instinct to find the freshest molten rock drives them to incredible feats of endurance and persistence.</p><p>Ferrous crabs require a tremendous amount of metal and energy to survive their migrations. Often driven by starvation, they attempt to devour any metal they come across, regardless of whether that metal is attached to another living creature or not. In large numbers, their collective ability to adjust magnetism makes them particularly dangerous to creatures wearing metal armor or wielding metal weapons.</p><p>The devastation these little creatures can cause is legendary among the communities they migrate through. Many have developed an early warning system to either flee from the oncoming swarms or lay out trails of metal scraps to lure the crabs onto a different path.</p><h2>Ore Finders</h2><p>A ferrous crab's ability to sense nearby metal has encouraged some miners to track swarms of them to new lodes, though always from a healthy distance. Similarly, geomancers occasionally employ individual crabs to help them identify the location of ley lines or veins of unusual minerals. Devotees of earth or metal gods often feed the ferrous crabs that gather around their temples, sometimes as a benefaction to their deity but more often simply to keep the metal-hungry crustaceans at bay.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, fortification, metal, sensory (darkvision), sturdy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Metalsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Due to its innate magnetism, a ferrous crab swarm senses nearby chunks of metal of at least light Bulk.</p>"},{"n":"Molten Digestion","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Ferrous crabs attempt to devour metal objects. At the start of the crabs' turn, all unattended metal objects in their squares take 2d6 untyped damage, ignoring Hardness. Each creature in the swarm's space who is wearing or carrying a metal item must attempt at a DC 20 Reflex save; on a failure, the crabs deal 2d6 untyped damage to one of their metal items, ignoring Hardness.</p>"},{"n":"Crushing Polarity","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["metal","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Each enemy wearing metal armor in the swarm's space takes 3d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 20 basic Fortitude save). Each target that fails the saving throw is Grabbed until the end of its next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Molten Claw","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Each enemy in the swarm's space takes 2d6 bludgeoning plus 1d6 fire damage (DC 20 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Reverse Polarity","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","metal","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ferrous crab swarm makes a molten claw attack and then magnetizes their foes. Each enemy creature in the swarm's space that is carrying at least 1 Bulk of metal objects must attempt a DC 20 Reflex save with the following effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature uses magnetic propulsion to their advantage. They can immediately Step.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature's ability to grasp metal items is hampered for 1 round. During that time, attempts to Disarm the target of metal items gain a +2 circumstance bonus, and the creature takes a –2 circumstance penalty to attacks with metal item or other checks requiring a firm grasp on the item. Additionally, if the creature is wearing metal armor, they are Off-Guard until the beginning of their next turn.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> Any metal object (including weapons) that the creature is holding drop from their hand and lands in the creature's space. Additionally, if the creature is wearing metal armor, they are knocked Prone.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/sKFVjnck4SSE0wD0":{"lang":["Dwarven","Petran","Pyric","Talican"],"gear":["Forgemaster's Javelin","forgemaster's Warhammer"],"notes":{"speed":"magma glide"},"flavor":"<p>The Forgemaster is a primordial entity of creation who spends their days forging works of horrifying power and incredible beauty in equal measure. It is said that from their hands have sprung many of the greatest weapons known to both gods and mortals alike. They dwell among the primal magma that flows deep below the world's surface, thinking nothing of the heat or pressure as they endlessly toil.</p><p>Only the bravest, or most foolhardy, dare approach the Forgemaster to make requests. Legends say the Forgemaster destroys those who seek their creations for petty glory. They have no interest in accepting money as payment; instead, they demand that their would-be clients complete formidable trials to prove their resolve.</p><h2>One of a Kind</h2><p>The Forgemaster lives for the challenge of producing new, never-before-seen wonders. The Forgemaster never creates the same item twice and rarely adheres strictly to a recipe. Any item created by the Forgemaster usually has an extra, hidden property or two—a command word that unlocks a hidden power or a curse that affects those who prove themselves unworthy. A wielder might learn some of these properties only long after the item has left the Forgemaster's hands.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 20,000/48,000/90,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Arcana, Athletics, Crafting, Intimidation, Nature, Occultism, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, burning barbs, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, intelligence, metal, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Unparalleled Crafter","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster knows the recipes for nearly all common, uncommon, and rare items, and is also capable of creating artifacts. They can Craft items during Downtime at 10 times the normal speed, ignoring all prerequisites.</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Forgemaster's Arsenal","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster's weapons are a <em>+3 major striking warhammer</em> that deals 4d6 additional fire damage and a <em>+3 returning major striking javelin</em> that deals 2d6 additional fire damage and has a range increment of 120 feet. On a critical hit, the Forgemaster's weapons deal 2d10 persistent fire damage as well as their typical critical specialization effects.</p>"},{"n":"Impossible Heat","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>All fire damage dealt by the Forgemaster's attacks and abilities bypasses fire resistance.</p>"},{"n":"Magma Glide","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster can burrow freely through both magma and solid rock. When passing throw solid rock, they can choose to leave a tunnel.</p>"},{"n":"Bellows","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster inhales mightily, then exhales a cloud of superheated gas, dealing 24d6 fire damage in a 120-foot cone (DC 48 basic Reflex save). The Forgemaster can't use Bellows again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Reforge Hammer","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster reshape their hammer into another one-handed melee weapon, changing the weapon's base damage dice, critical specialization effects, and traits accordingly.</p>"},{"n":"Titan's Reach","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack","earth","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster reaches through the very stone to strike at their enemies. The Forgemaster makes a single Strike against any enemy that they can see. This attack can be made at any range and ignores all cover and concealment.</p>"},{"n":"Unworthy Wielder","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The Forgemaster declares the target unworthy of bearing its weapon, and compels the weapon to rebel. The target takes 15d6 untyped damage of whatever type the weapon typically deals (DC 46 Will save). If the target is wielding an intelligent weapon, use the higher of the wielder's result and the weapon's result. Regardless of the result, the target is then temporarily immune to this effect for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> the target takes full damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage. They drop their weapon and can't pick it back up again for 1 minute; if the target is an intelligent weapon PC, they are instead stunned for 1 minute. The target can attempt a new Will save at the end of each of their turns to end this effect.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/4jNJTr3YiCUEpoUD":{"lang":["Envisioning"],"lore":[["Mathematics Lore",28]],"notes":{"ac":"all-around vision","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic","per":"spatial reckoning"},"flavor":"<p>The fractrix is an aeon representing the emergence of perfect order from a large and chaotic system. They represent the duality between the potential of infinite processes and their bounded or finite manifestations. Fractrixes repair recursive spatial anomalies and are therefore most often encountered by adventurers exploring structures like endless staircases or bottomless pits. Though they generally mean no harm to mortals who don't willingly create spatial anomalies, their infinite geometry can induce fits of confusion in observers, sparking conflict.</p><hr><p>Aeons are caretakers of order and balance, who watch over reality and guard it from the excesses that would throw it astray. Those who seek to cause discord and chaos will find themselves at odds with aeons' goals.</p><h2>Boon and Bane</h2><p>Fractixes are most often encountered in the lairs of spellcasters who have bent space itself in order to confound intruders. If left alone to do its work, a fractrix might undo an enchantment that once prevented entrance through mundane means, granting adventurers access to an extradimensional space that was previously barred to them. It's equally likely, however, that the same fractrix could inadvertently trap those adventurers inside the extradimensional space once its task was complete.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Lore [Mathematics], Nature, Occultism, Performance, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality, void), intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Enlarge, Hypnotize, Planar Tether, Shrink, Teleport, Translocate, Truesight), strength, sturdy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Envisioning","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>100 feet. A fractrix can communicate mentally with any creatures in the aura using wordless psychic projections. They don't need to share a language, though the aeon's meaning to non-aeons can be vague and is often mysterious. An aeon can use this ability to communicate flawlessly with any other aeon on the same plane as themselves.</p>"},{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Confusing Geometry","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","emotion","incapacitation","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. A creature that ends its turn in the aura must attempt a DC 29 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Confusing Geometry for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Confused for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is confused for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Infinite Refraction","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["light","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The fractrix is targeted by an effect with the light trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The fractrix's body refracts the light in a dazzling display. Each creature within 30 feet must succeed at a DC 32 Fortitude save or become Dazzled for 1 round, or for 1 minute on a critical failure.</p>"},{"n":"Spatial Reckoning","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine","revelation"],"f":"","d":"<p>A fractrix automatically detects and identifies space-altering magic (including teleportation effects) and is only affected by the magic if they choose to be.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Angle of Invisibility","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When viewed from certain angles, a fractrix is imperceptible to the eye. The fractrix carefully aligns themselves. Until they move or use this ability again, they are Invisible to creatures viewing them from within a 60-foot cone of their choice. If an affected creature also occupies spaces outside of the area, the fractrix is instead Concealed to that creature.</p>"},{"n":"Recursive Coil","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The fractrix endlessly tightens their fractal tendril around a creature they have Grabbed or Restrained. The target takes 3d10 bludgeoning damage, and it takes 1d10 persistent bludgeoning damage at the end of each of its turns until it Escapes.</p>"},{"n":"Warp Geometry","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","teleportation"],"f":"once per minute","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The fractrix warps space in a 30-foot burst within 120 feet, distorting geometry in the area for 1 minute. When a creature uses an action with the move trait to enter a square in the area, it must attempt a DC 32 Will save. Each creature can only be affected by Warp Geometry once per round.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Warp Geometry for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The fractrix bends space, depositing the creature in another square within the warped area of the fractrix's choice. This is forced movement. If the creature has not yet moved their entire Speed, it can continue its movement from its new location.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature is too disoriented to continue their movement.</p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/NER1xO7JnPOjIqJ1":{"notes":{"speed":"hopper"},"flavor":"<p>When the bellowing croak of a gargantoad echoes across the landscape, those unfortunate souls who hear it can only hope that the gargantoad has different prey in mind. Gargantoads are house-sized carnivorous amphibians that hop and slither forward with uncanny speed, grabbing lone prey with their sticky tongues or gathering herds in their tentacles to crush for later consumption.</p><h2>Uncanny Anatomy</h2><p>Gargantoads share some physical features with toads, frogs, and squid. They have warty skin, musculature, sticky tongues, and a camouflage coloration typically associated with toads, combined with a frog's long and powerful legs and webbed feet. Over a dozen retractable tentacles emerge from their bodies, assisting with gathering prey and supplementing the strength of their back legs to make incredible hops.</p><p>How did these creatures first come about? Legends of their existence stretch back to the earliest days of civilization, with some speculating that they are the offspring of an amphibious god, god-beasts from another realm, or the result of hybridization between primordial krakens and other beings.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 8,750/17,500/30,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, body, bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Unfathomable Leap","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The gargantoad's mighty leg muscles propel it forward with reality-warping force. It Leaps up to 1,000 feet horizontally and 500 feet vertically. It can choose to carry any number of creatures that it has Grabbed or Restrained along with it.</p>"},{"n":"Terrifying Croak","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The gargantoad lets out a deep, resonant bellow that inflicts primal fear in all who hear it. All creatures within 1 mile must attempt a DC 44 Will save, with the following effects. Creatures that succeed at the save are temporarily immune to Terrifying Croak for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is frightened 2. For the next hour, any time that its turn begins and it is able to see, hear, or otherwise perceive the gargantoad, it must attempt another Will save against Terrifying Croak.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the creature is Frightened 4.</p>"},{"n":"Tentacle Forest","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>The gargantoad attempts an Athletics check to Grapple all creatures within reach of its tentacles. If any creatures were already Grabbed or Restrained in its tentacles, the gargantoad extends the duration of that condition until the end of its next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Huge, 3d8 acid plus 3d10 bludgeoning, Rupture 50.</p><p>Creatures that begin their turn Swallowed Whole are exposed to numbing toxin.</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Numbing Toxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 42 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 6d6 poison damage and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 8d6 poison damage and Slowed 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 10d6 poison damage and slowed 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> 12d6 poison damage and Paralyzed (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Hopper","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Gargantoads move in large hops, allowing them to easily clear obstacles. They ignore difficult terrain and greater difficult terrain from obstacles shorter than 50 feet tall in their path.</p>"},{"n":"Greater Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>4d6+15 bludgeoning, DC 40 basic Fortitude,  (creatures grabbed by tentacles only)</p><hr><p><p>The monster deals the listed amount of damage to any number of creatures Grabbed or Restrained by it. Each of those creatures can attempt a basic Fortitude save with the listed DC. A creature that fails this save falls Unconscious, and a creature that succeeds is then temporarily immune to falling unconscious from Greater Constrict for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fast Swallow","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The Gargantoad Grabs a creature with its tongue</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gargantoad uses Swallow Whole.</p>"},{"n":"Bone Crunch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gargantoad's palette crushes down on the bitten target hard enough to splinter bone. The target must attempt a DC 42 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes no additional effects.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 1d6 persistent bleed damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes 3d6 persistent bleed damage and is Clumsy 1 for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes 5d6 persistent bleed damage and is Clumsy 2 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Aquatic Ambush (100 Feet)","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster is hiding in water and a creature that hasn't detected it is within the listed number of feet.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster moves up to its swim Speed + 10 feet toward the triggering creature, traveling on water and on land. Once the creature is in reach, the monster makes a Strike against it. The creature is Off-Guard against this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Pull 100 feet","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a success with a Strike that lists Pull in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Reposition the creature, moving it closer to the monster. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Pull lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pulled on a success to that distance.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/i5YkN1V3TZw9VGWz":{"lang":["Common","Goblin"],"gear":["Dogslicer","New Consumable","New Consumable","Gasbelly Poison Flask (4)","Padded Armor"],"flavor":"<p>The drive to find new ways to start fires leads some goblins to dabble in alchemy. All too often, their penchant for ingesting untested concoctions dooms these amateur alchemists to a quick, explosive end. Those who survive their experiments occasionally gain unexpected abilities. And these successful innovators tend to inspire others to follow in their footsteps.</p><p>One of these successful mutations produces gasbelly goblins. While goblins are already recognized for their iron stomachs, gasbelly goblins take this ability to new heights. They can fill their stomachs with hazardous concoctions that mix into poisonous gases. These gases swell their stomachs to gruesome sizes, making the thrilled goblins lighter than the air. While airborne, they can propel themselves through the air by belching with great force, trailing a noxious green cloud in their wake.</p><p>Gasbellies regularly test their digestive systems in elaborate eating contests, which usually end in a chorus of vile belches. Their immunity to poison makes them reckless, so contests often end with stomachaches and broken teeth. Gasbelly goblins also almost universally develop a love of eggs. Several gasbelly tribes have turned satisfying their eggy cravings into a perilous sport, conducting daring aerial raids on high eyries in search of the eggs of large animals like griffons and hippogriffs. Less ethically minded gasbellies do not limit their diets to animals, stealing eggs from intelligent creatures like manticores or even drakes if they believe they can get away with it. Inevitably, such attempts inspire rage in their targets, who are rarely satisfied to limit their vengeance to the offending goblins.</p><h2>Gasbelly Heroes</h2><p>Gasbelly goblins speak in reverent tones about those among them who consume rocks, metal, or even more exotic substances such as electricity, flames, or void energy. Whether these gasbelly heroes truly exist or are simply the products of the goblins' wishful thinking remains a matter of debate.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), shield, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), sturdy, throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bounce","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The gasbelly goblin is hit by an attack that deals bludgeoning or slashing damage, and the attack is not a critical hit</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gasbelly goblin flies 30 feet directly away from the attacker (this is not forced movement, and it triggers reactions as normal). The gasbelly goblin takes no bludgeoning or slashing damage from the attack. If they collide with an obstacle, the gasbelly goblin changes direction and continues moving until they have used up the full 30 feet of movement.</p>"},{"n":"Numbing Toxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>Direct exposure to a gasbelly goblin's poison causes numbness and tingling. A creature hit by a gasbelly poison flask Strike must succeed at a DC 20 Fortitude save or become Clumsy 1 for 1 round. Creatures exposed to only the splash damage do not need to attempt this saving throw.</p>"},{"n":"Belching Jet","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The gasbelly goblin moves up to 50 feet in a straight line. All creatures that the gasbelly passes adjacent to during this movement take 5d6 poison damage (DC 17 basic Fortitude save). The goblin can't use Belching Jet again until they drink a gasbelly poison flask.</p>"},{"n":"Quickfingered Poisoner","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gasbelly goblin Interacts to draw a gasbelly poison flask, then Strikes with it or drinks it.</p>"},{"n":"Spit Up and Slash","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>With a hiccup and a lurch, the gasbelly goblin Interacts to dredge up poison from their stomach, spitting it on to their weapon to prepare for a more deadly attack. The goblin then Strikes with their dogslicer; the Strike deals an additional 1d6 poison damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/BzCnCylJLRx25TBt":{"lang":["Necril","Sussuran"],"notes":{"speed":"swiftness"},"flavor":"<p>Gasping deaths are air elementals corrupted by the touch of the void. Gasping deaths exist at the boundary between life and death, and are instinctively drawn to gain a greater understanding of this boundary. As a result, they often seek out living creatures to suffocate, so they can observe their final moments and then play around with their corpses. They are particularly likely to attack creatures of a type they haven't seen before, in hopes of gaining a new perspective.</p><h2>Companions in Blasphemy</h2><p>Gasping deaths are sometimes found as companions to necromancers, who earn the elemental's cooperation by sharing their knowledge. In exchange, the necromancer typically asks the gasping death to perform various tasks or serve as a bodyguard.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality, void), sensory (darkvision), unholy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Refuge in Breath","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The gasping death is targeted by an attack from a creature it can see</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The gasping death is in a creature's lungs</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gasping death retreats into the creature's lungs, gaining a +2 circumstance bonus to AC. If the attack critically misses, it hits that creature instead.</p>"},{"n":"Choke","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The gasping death is currently in a creature's lungs</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gasping death attempts to suffocate the creature. The creature loses remaining rounds of air using the suffocation rules and takes void damage based on the results of a DC 25 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature prevents the gasping death from entering its lungs.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d4 void damage and loses 1 round of remaining air.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 4d4 void damage and loses 2 rounds of remaining air.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes 8d4 void damage and loses all of its remaining air.</p>"},{"n":"Suffocating Void","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The gasping death is occupying the same space as another creature of size Huge or smaller that can breathe, and it is not currently in a creature's lungs</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gasping death surrounds the creature's face and attempts to enter its airways. The creature attempts a DC 25 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d4 void damage before expelling the gasping death.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 4d4 void damage as a portion of the gasping death enters its lungs. The creature must immediately begin holding its breath.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, and the gasping death can use Choke once as a free action.</p><p>While the gasping death is in a creature's lungs, it can't move from that creature's space, but it automatically moves with the creature. The gasping death can leave a creature's lungs as a free action.</p><p>A creature that begins its turn with the gasping death in its lungs can spend 1 action to attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save to cough it up. On a success, they expel the gasping death from their lungs, taking 2d6 slashing damage and 1d8 void damage as the elemental rakes at their lungs. On a critical success, they expel the gasping death without taking additional damage.</p>"},{"n":"Cloud of Death","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gasping death's form is mostly insubstantial. It can enter and occupy the same space as other creatures, and it can move through a gap at least 1 inch wide without Squeezing.</p>"},{"n":"Swiftness","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gasping death's movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Inhabit Corpse","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A creature with the gasping death in its lungs has died in the last round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gasping death puppets the creature as an undead shell. The gasping death can use its actions to force the creature's body to perform any of the actions it could have taken in life. If the gasping death takes 20 untyped damage, it is forcibly released from the creature's body.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/seUexbbY71PNZbZm":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Spirituan","Can't speak any language, Speak with animals, Speak with plants"],"lore":[["Terrain Lore",20]],"flavor":"<p>Some places in the world seem to have their own personalities. An impression of malice surrounding a cave, serenity amidst a glade, or joy radiating from a secluded waterfall becomes a shared experience for all travelers who reach these secluded wonders. When strong magic infiltrates these locations, it can take the form of a land spirit or a genius locus. Such spirits often have the ability to reshape their domains, confounding invaders or easing the path for welcome guests. While nymphs tend not to reside within a genius loci's domain, nymphs and genius loci respect each other as caretakers of the land.</p><p>Most genius loci arise naturally at the confluence of ley lines. Druid circles can also manifest a genius loci using an obscure ritual, but the ritual's complexity and cost mean that even the few who know the steps are unlikely to perform it.</p><h2>Fighting the Land</h2><p>Genius loci typically try to preserve their lands as they are, resisting encroaching farmers, loggers, and others who would radically change the area's nature. Such behavior often leads to violent conflict between a genius loci and would-be settlers. Killing a genius loci rarely results in the land being changed for the better; where a genius loci has died, wells dry up, mines collapse, and crops develop strange blights.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p>Monster Parts 90/200/360 gp Eligible Refinements weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Lore [Terrain], Nature, Stealth) Eligible Imbued Properties bane, dexterity, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), ice, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Boulder Crush, Safe Passage, Speak with Animals, Speak with Plants, Wall of Ice, Wall of Stone, Wall of Thorns), spirit, strength, tangle shield, water, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Domain Dependent","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A genius loci is mystically bonded to a sacred site, such as a secluded grove or pristine pond. The land within 1 mile of this site is the genius loci's domain. The appearance of the land within the domain subtly alters to reflect the genius loci's physical and emotional state. Likewise, damage to the land manifests on the genius loci. If the domain suffers from large-scale pollution or magical blight, the genius loci is Drained 1 until the land is restored. If the genius loci exits their domain, they become Sickened 1 and can't reduce the condition's value by any means until they return to their domain. They must succeed at a DC 26 Fortitude save every day they spend separated from their land or increase the sickened value by 1 (to a maximum of Sickened 4).</p>"},{"n":"Shifting Paths","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet Strides</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The land itself attempts to lead creatures astray. The triggering creature must attempt a DC 28 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Shifting Paths 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature's Speed is reduced by 5 feet for this Stride.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature's Speed is reduced by 15 feet for this Stride.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature Strides its full speed in a direction of the Genius Loci's choice. This is forced movement.</p>"},{"n":"Alter Terrain","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The genius loci is within their domain</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The genius loci reshapes the terrain in a 30-foot radius around them, making it easier or harder for other creatures to traverse. They can change normal terrain into difficult terrain, difficult terrain into normal terrain or greater difficult terrain, and greater difficult terrain into difficult terrain. Regardless of what they choose, they can freely move through altered terrain as through it were normal terrain. The altered terrain persists for 1 minute or until the genius loci uses this ability again, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Domain Wall","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The genius loci casts 5th-rank Wall of Ice, Wall of Stone, or Wall of Thorns; each genius loci has access to whichever of these spells most closely fits with the nature of their domain.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/vKkmWrkGJ9jkms1j":{"flavor":"<p>In realms overtaken by shadows, animals made of solidified darkness roam, taking multitudes of shapes and sizes. The species of these unusual creatures most often encountered outside their domain are oversized rodents which are known as giant gloom rats, though they wander into the light mainly due to necessity. They resemble the pure black silhouettes of giant rats, except that they have one large eye that glows brightly in the murk.</p><p>People often assume that creatures made of darkness would hate the light, but giant gloom rats actually swarm around the rare source of light in their homes, eating it as fast as they can. Because of this particular diet, giant gloom rats proliferate wildly when they manage to break into other places full of natural light.</p><p>This rapid reproduction can cause mayhem when nobles and rich merchants take giant gloom rats as pets to show off their wealth. Giant gloom rats seem to enjoy these arrangements for a while, so long as they are well-fed and provided with regular entertainment. When they get bored, however, they often sneak away into the streets and start eating all of the light sources in their surroundings. Not all gloom rat infestations are accidents; thieves, assassins, and others who benefit from a sudden loss of visibility at night sometimes unleash the rats to darken the streets.</p><h2>Gloom Fever</h2><p>Scholars researching gloom fever often speculate on the nature of the disease. After all, in the shadowy realms from which gloom rats originate, light sources are few and far between. Prevailing theories include the idea that the disease represents an infusion of elemental shadow or that it is carried by microscopic pathogens native to shadowy realms that flourish within gloom rats' bodies. Some even theorize that the disease evolved to encourage infected people to keep gloom rats as pets, as they would come to rely on the rats to extinguish troublesome light sources. Regardless of its origin, people don't always view the disease as a threat. People who expect to be without light for long periods of time have been known to deliberately infect themselves with the disease.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, darkness, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Eye in the Dark","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A giant gloom rat's eye can pierce most shadows. When its eye is opened, it can't be Concealed or Hidden by darkness, unless the effect can also block darkvision. When its eye is closed, it is naturally invisible in dim light and darkness, as its body blends with surrounding shadows.</p>"},{"n":"Light Snack","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["darkness","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The giant gloom rat's last action was a successful jaws Strike against a creature or unattended object, and that creature or object is emanating light</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The giant gloom rat attempts to counteract one source of light on the target, or an object it is holding or wearing, with a counteract modifier of +9. If the source of light is mundane, the light is automatically snuffed out. If the source is magical and the counteract check is successful, the giant gloom rat gains temporary Hit Points equal to double the counteract rank of the effect (minimum 2).</p>"},{"n":"Gloom Fever","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature afflicted with this disease takes a –2 status penalty to all visual Perception checks</p><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 17 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> carrier with only the base effect (1d4 hours)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> light blindness (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> light blindness and Dazzled when in dim light (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> Blinded when in bright light and dazzled when in dim light, gains low-light vision (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> blinded when in bright or dim light, gains darkvision (1 day)</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/yHSYMAL4hjB4PgBz":{"notes":{"speed":"long roots"},"flavor":"<p>Often mistaken for harmless cannonball mangroves, these predatory trees lurk in warm, brackish coastal waters. They mainly prey on animals but have been known to attack travelers or small boats that get too close. Their hunting strategy is as simple as it is gruesome: they bombard their prey with cannonball-like fruits, the most developed of which immediately deploy into infantile gungroves and begin feeding on their designated fertilizer even before it succumbs to their burgeoning roots. Those who are downed by the impact itself are absorbed just as greedily by the adult tree.</p><p>Though the sound of cannonfruit firing is reminiscent of the boom of a cannon, the plant's internal mechanisms have nothing to do with flames. Instead, a gungrove's trunk contains specialized channels that allow it to rapidly manipulate water pressure, creating hollow voids that water rushes into at breakneck speeds. Efforts to create a tame cultivar of gungroves have failed dramatically so far, though rumors abound of unscrupulous pirates and corrupt port officials feeding their enemies to \"pet\" gungroves.</p><h2>Fruits of Healing</h2><p>Paradoxically, the fruit of a gungrove tree is highly valued for its medicinal properties. When boiled, the peel of a cannonfruit produces a tea known to soothe digestive pains, while the seeds can be cooked and ground into a poultice as a remedy for rashes. Some have even claimed that cannonfruit, when properly prepared, can cure magical maladies. Unfortunately, collecting the fruit often results in wounds that no herbal remedy can heal.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), shield, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (low-light vision), strength, sturdy, tangle shield, throwing, water, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Mangrove Freeze","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gungrove can spend 1 minute to plant itself in an area of shallow water. Until the next time the gungrove acts, it has an automatic result of 32 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as an inanimate mangrove tree.</p>"},{"n":"Long Roots","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Though it's quickest in water, the gungrove's roots are also dexterous on land. It can Step 10 feet and doesn't treat shallow water as difficult terrain.</p>"},{"n":"Explosive Ejection","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The gungrove critically hits with a cannonfruit Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The gungrove uses Deploy Sapling.</p>"},{"n":"Deploy Sapling","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The gungrove's last action was a successful cannonfruit Strike against a Small or larger living creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The fruit deploys into a sapling, trying to get its roots into the creature it struck. The creature attempts a DC 20 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The sapling fails to take hold, but its roots still hamper the creature's movement. The creature takes –5-foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds for 1 round or until it Escapes, whichever comes first.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> Until the creature Escapes the sapling, it is Drained 1 and takes a –5-foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> Until the creature Escapes the sapling, it is Drained 2 and takes a –15-foot circumstance penalty to its Speeds. After escaping the fruit, the creature is drained 1 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Beaching Barrage","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The gungrove propels itself out of water in a burst of immature cannonfruit. It Swims up to its Speed in a straight line, then Leaps up to 20 feet. At the end of its movement, creatures within a 10-foot emanation around the gungrove take 2d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 20 basic Reflex save); those that fail the saving throw are pushed 5 feet away from the gungrove.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/KlkCp1dJocJQVdnR":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Venenan"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Harrophages are eternal, undying horrors that arise in areas devastated by biological and chemical warfare. The souls of thousands of people who met agonizing deaths at the hands of diseases or choking gases are bound into the fragments of the weapons that killed them. These harbingers of ruin bring death wherever they go. Some harrophages travel to the lands of their enemies in war before engaging in the indiscriminate slaughter of soldiers and civilians alike. Others resent every living creature and seek to inflict suffering as broadly as they can.</p><p>Harrophages rarely form alliances with mortals, but they readily cooperate with fiends and undead to pursue their goals. Hordes of leukodaemons and omox demons eagerly rally under a harrophage's banner.</p><h2>Malice of the Crowd</h2><p>Billows of toxic gas compose a harrophage's body, within which the faces of individual spirits roil. These spirits, victims of long-gone atrocities, are rarely evil themselves, and many of them can conduct a conversation (though they have little awareness of events beyond their own deaths). Nevertheless, this collection of mostly benign spirits comes together to form a single collective mind, and that mind is unutterably and implacably evil.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 8,750/17,500/30,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Medicine, Nature, Religion, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> constitution, death, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (void), fire, fire shield, ice, music, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Falling Stars, Toxic Cloud), strength, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Absorb Soul","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["death","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a living creature dies from poison damage inflicted by the harrophage's Strikes, spells, or diseases, the creature's soul joins with the collective of the dead already within the harrophage. The harrophage immediately recovers a number of Hit Points equal to three times the creature's level. Until the harrophage is destroyed, they attempt to counteract all effects that would resurrect the creature (counteract modifier +34).</p>"},{"n":"Noxious Malignance","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. When a creature enters or starts its turn in the aura, it is exposed to noxichor.</p>"},{"n":"Overwhelming Infection","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet fails a saving throw against one of the harrophage's afflictions</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature is also Sickened 1, or Sickened 2 if the disease's Stage is 3 or more.</p>"},{"n":"Biobombardment","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The harrophage expels a shrieking green cloud of toxic gas from its mouth in their choice of a 120-foot line or a 60-foot cone. Living creatures in the area take 20d6 poison damage if the harrophage spends two actions, or 20d8 poison damage if they spend three (DC 44 basic Reflex save). If a creature critically fails, the stages of all diseases it has increase by 1. The harrophage can't use Biobombardment again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Noxichor","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 40 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Enfeebled 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> enfeebled 2 and Slowed 1 (2 rounds)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> As stage 2, and the creature experiences coughing fits and burning vision. The harrophage is Concealed from the target afflicted with noxichor and if the creature afflicted with noxichor is undetected or unnoticed to the harrophage, it becomes observed by the harrophage (2 rounds)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> As stage 3 (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> dead.</p>"},{"n":"Ossiophage","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 40 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> –5-foot status penalty to all Speeds (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> –10-foot status penalty to all its Speeds (2 rounds)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> –15-foot status penalty to all its Speeds (3 rounds)</p>"},{"n":"Red Haze","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 40 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 6d6 poison damage (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 8d6 poison damage (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 10d6 poison damage (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> 10d6 poison damage and take a –1 status penalty to all saves vs. disease.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/fyUXpBhc4PENZ3Ea":{"lang":["Common","Truespeech"],"notes":{"saves":"–2 status penalty to all saves vs. emotion effects"},"flavor":"<p>Hatemongers stoke up hatred, prejudice, and paranoia with relish and fervor. Though they enjoy being the center of attention and holding power over others, most hatemongers would rather be a trusted advisor or an influential demagogue than a ruler. They believe that establishing complete control over a community stifles people's ability to invent their own methods of cruelty and oppression.</p><p>A hatemonger might even work to oust a more obviously malevolent controlling force from a community to make room for their own schemes, earning acclaim and respect in the process. Though they are capable combatants, hatemongers rarely deal a lethal blow to an enemy, preferring to entice others to experience ending a life.</p><h2>Mundane Origins</h2><p>Many have speculated that a hatemonger in their true form must be demonic or diabolic in appearance. Sadly, this is not the case. Though their powers are supernatural, their existence isn't: hatemongers can arise from any mortal ancestry and seem to do so spontaneously, without any outside influence. Some idealistic philosophers hold the belief that the eradication and destruction of the last hatemongers will lead to their extinction as a species. More likely, only by eradicating hatred itself will prevent new hatemongers from being created—an even more unrealistic proposition than defeating the hatemongers themselves.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), skill items (Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, charisma, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, spells (Occult: Fear, Mind Probe, Outcast's Curse, Phantasmal Calamity, Phantom Pain, Suggestion, Truespeech, Zealous Conviction), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+4 Bonus to Sense Motive","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Get Them!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The hatemonger takes damage from a successful Strike or spell attack roll</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hatemonger directs a follower to get revenge. They choose one ally of 13th level or lower within 30 feet. That ally either Strides toward or makes a Strike against the triggering creature. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward any creature's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The hatemonger can take on the appearance of any Small or Medium humanoid. This doesn't change their Speed or their attack and damage modifiers with their Strikes, but it might change the damage type their Strikes deal.</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Echo Chamber","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> At least two creatures within 30 feet are frightened</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hatemonger feeds on fear. All frightened creatures within 30 feet take 7d6 mental damage (DC 33 basic Will save). The hatemonger gains a number of temporary Hit Points equal to the highest damage a single creature takes from this effect, and then can't use Echo Chamber again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Stoke Hatred","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The hatemonger inspires fear, hatred, and violence. Each creature within 30 feet must attempt a DC 33 Will save. A creature that succeeds at the saving throw is temporarily immune to Stoke Hatred for 1 hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> For 1 round, the target gains a +2 status bonus on damage rolls and takes a –1 status penalty to AC. Effect: Stoke Hatred (Success)</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target becomes Frightened 2. The target can't reduce their frightened condition at the end of their turn unless it used a hostile action in that turn. While they are frightened, they gain a +4 status bonus on damage rolls. Effect: Stoke Hatred (Failure)</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the target is Confused for as long as it remains frightened.</p>"},{"n":"Willing Hate","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>After spending a minute talking to a willing creature, the hatemonger can amplify the creature's hatred. The target receives a +2 status bonus to Intimidation checks and damage rolls against people that they hate; this increases to a +4 status bonus against creatures that are frightened from an effect that they caused. However, whenever the target gains the frightened condition, the condition's value is 1 higher than normal. All attempts to Coerce or Lie to the target in an attempt to stoke hatred receive a result that is one degree of success higher than what was rolled. These effects last until the target makes a meaningful attempt to assist someone that they hate.</p><p>Effect: Willing Hate</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/VLVwalP4yKDZ0iAV":{"lang":["Common","Hemadae","Can't speak"],"flavor":"<p>Hemadae drones perform almost every function a hemadae hive needs. Outside of the hive, they patrol the surroundings, ferry loads back and forth, and spawn new drones. Within the hive, they ceaselessly create defenses, weapons, and other tools at the direction of overseers. Though drones are smart enough to use tactics in battle, they lack individual motivations and goals, and they don't hesitate to sacrifice their lives for their queen.</p><hr><p>Hemadaes are metallic wasplike creatures with a few parts from humans or other mortal ancestries eerily hanging out from their body. A hemadae hive is a complex hybrid of a smith's forge and a massive wasp nest. Most hemadaes are drones, which do the grunt work inside and outside the hive. Particularly accomplished drones are transformed into overseers, egotistical and talented crafters who direct hordes of drones and control clockwork constructs in ceaseless toil. A single hemadae queen rules each hive, imposing her will through telepathic commands to the overseers. To communicate with each other, hemadae overseers and drones use a discordant language made from mandible clicks and the banging and scraping of metal.</p><p>Hemadaes reproduce by infecting other creatures with their genetic material. Their bite infects a horrific disease that slowly turns the victim's skin into an outer shell of metal, trapping them inside while the venom digests them alive. The primordial soup that remains then metamorphoses into a drone.</p><h2>Empty Shells</h2><p>As a creature's body is encased in metal and transformed into a hemadae drone, enough remains of their original form that they can sometimes be recognized by former companions. However, a drone doesn't remember their former life, and they're just as likely to kill former loved ones as anyone else. Even worse, a creature transformed into a hemadae can't be restored to life unless the hemadae is killed.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (heavy), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, bane, burning barbs, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, metal, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Cold Contractions","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time each round a hemadae drone takes cold damage, their metal plates recede and expose vulnerable points. They gain weakness 5 to piercing until the end of their next turn.</p><p>Effect: Cold Contractions</p>"},{"n":"Weld","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The hemadae drone's last action was a successful mandibles Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The drone disgorges superheated metal to weld their target to the ground. They attempt a Crafting vs. Fortitude DC check against the target's Fortitude DC. If the creature is not standing on a surface, it can take fire damage from this ability but can't become Grabbed or Restrained.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes 4d6 fire damage and is restrained until the end of the overseer's next turn or until they Escape.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 2d6 fire damage and is grabbed until the end of the drone's next turn or until they Escape</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is unaffected.</p>"},{"n":"Ferrodermia","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["arcane","disease","incapacitation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 25 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> clumsy 1 and Fatigued (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Clumsy 2, fatigued, and a –10-foot circumstance penalty to speed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> Paralyzed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> petrified in metal (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 6</strong> death, and the victim's body transforms into a new hemadae drone that flies off to join other of their kind.</p>"},{"n":"Revolting Excretion","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hemadae drone sprays digestive fluids from their abdomen in a 15-foot cone, dealing 2d12 acid damage (DC 25 basic Reflex save). Each creature that fails the save is also Sickened 1 (or Sickened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ruNtTaxqgeJBRwRk":{"lang":["Common","Hemadae","Can't speak"],"flavor":"<p>During the transformation from a drone to an overseer, a hemadae's stinger falls off, its abdomen develops an internal furnace, and four limbs turn into humanoid hand facsimiles. A new overseer's mind also expands, granting them a personality, unlike the single-minded drones. Though an overseer's personality bears some similarities to whoever the creature infected with ferrodermia was before becoming a hemadae, overseers almost universally have inflated egos and an obsession with control.</p><p>Hemadae overseers are responsible for directing drones and disseminating the queen's instructions to their subordinates. All the resources the hemadae drones collect are to fuel their superior's experiments and devices. The hemadae overseer designs and implements the queen's commands to expand and improve their arsenal. They build clockwork servants and soldiers, mechanical lifts, and doors throughout the hive. Their experiments with fire and acid often lead to disaster, leaving their bodies scarred. The experiments that succeed are turned into weapons or traps in their hive.</p><hr><p>Hemadaes are metallic wasplike creatures with a few parts from humans or other mortal ancestries eerily hanging out from their body. A hemadae hive is a complex hybrid of a smith's forge and a massive wasp nest. Most hemadaes are drones, which do the grunt work inside and outside the hive. Particularly accomplished drones are transformed into overseers, egotistical and talented crafters who direct hordes of drones and control clockwork constructs in ceaseless toil. A single hemadae queen rules each hive, imposing her will through telepathic commands to the overseers. To communicate with each other, hemadae overseers and drones use a discordant language made from mandible clicks and the banging and scraping of metal.</p><p>Hemadaes reproduce by infecting other creatures with their genetic material. Their bite infects a horrific disease that slowly turns the victim's skin into an outer shell of metal, trapping them inside while the venom digests them alive. The primordial soup that remains then metamorphoses into a drone.</p><h2>The Queen's Favor</h2><p>In most hives, each hemadae overseer claims one area of expertise. One might manage feats of civil engineering by planning and leading tasks that improve efficiency within the hive, while another might dedicate all of their efforts to becoming the queen's mechanic. Additional common specializations include alchemy, defenses, and weapons. In larger hives, multiple overseers can take on each role but rarely work in tandem. Hemadae overseers engage in constant conflict and competition for the queen's favor, resulting in an internal arms race that often ends in disaster. Eventually, this internal strife causes factions to form, and groups of overseers escape a hive that's grown too large to form a new queen and dwelling.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (heavy), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, intelligence, metal, sensory (low-light vision), spiked, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Cold Contractions","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time each round a hemadae overseer takes cold damage, their metal plates recede and expose vulnerable points. They gain weakness 10 to piercing until the end of their next turn.</p><p>Effect: Cold Contractions</p>"},{"n":"Experimental Defenses","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The hemadae overseer is targeted by an attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hemadae overseer interposes a defensive gadget in the way of the attack. The attack targets their Crafting DC instead of their AC. If the attack hits, the gadget explodes, dealing 5d4 piercing damage in a 5-foot emanation, and the overseer can't use Experimental Defenses again until they spend 10 minutes creating a new defensive gadget.</p>"},{"n":"Clamp Prey","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hemadae overseer makes a mandibles Strike against up to three different creatures. The attacks count toward the hemadae overseer's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after they make all attacks.</p>"},{"n":"Exothermic Expulsion","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire"],"f":"","d":"<p>The hemadae overseer unleashes a blast of flame from their abdomen that deals 10d6 fire damage in a 30-foot line (DC 28 basic Reflex save). They can't use exothermic expulsion again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Ferrodermia","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["arcane","disease","incapacitation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 28 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> clumsy 1 and Fatigued (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Clumsy 2, fatigued, and a –10-foot circumstance penalty to speed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> Paralyzed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> petrified in metal (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 6</strong> death, and the victim's body transforms into a new hemadae drone that flies off to join other of their kind.</p>"},{"n":"Weld","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The hemadae overseer's last action was a successful mandibles Strike or Clamp Prey</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hemadae overseer disgorges superheated metal to weld foes to the ground. They attempt a Crafting vs. Fortitude DC check against the Fortitude DC of each creature hit by their mandibles Strike, including all creatures hit during Clamp Prey. Creatures that are not standing on a surface can take fire damage from this ability but can't become Grabbed or Restrained.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes 4d8 fire damage and is restrained until the end of the overseer's next turn or until they Escape.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 2d8 fire damage and is grabbed until the end of the overseer's next turn or until they Escape</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is unaffected.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/z61Fi3Vg5oQ7G1Ub":{"lang":["Common","Hemadae","can only speak hemadae aloud, Telepathy 500 feet, Truespeech"],"notes":{"per":"hivesense (precise) 500 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Hemadae queens form when a group of hemadae overseers strikes out from a hive that's grown too big. They find a new cave system to inhabit and submerge themselves in an acidic concoction that melts their steel hides and lets them meld into one consciousness. Fledgling queens are unable to move, so their first instruction to their drone underlings is typically to build a war machine fit for their station.</p><p>Hemadae queens consolidate reports from across the hive to detect threats and opportunities for expansion and direct their overseers accordingly. Queens rarely enter battle unless an intruder poses an existential threat to the hive. When they do fight, they gather hordes of hemadae at their side and drive them into a frenzy with their rallying cry. For hemadae who fall in battle, there is no greater honor than to be absorbed into the queen's body to restore the queen's vitality.</p><hr><p>Hemadaes are metallic wasplike creatures with a few parts from humans or other mortal ancestries eerily hanging out from their body. A hemadae hive is a complex hybrid of a smith's forge and a massive wasp nest. Most hemadaes are drones, which do the grunt work inside and outside the hive. Particularly accomplished drones are transformed into overseers, egotistical and talented crafters who direct hordes of drones and control clockwork constructs in ceaseless toil. A single hemadae queen rules each hive, imposing her will through telepathic commands to the overseers. To communicate with each other, hemadae overseers and drones use a discordant language made from mandible clicks and the banging and scraping of metal.</p><p>Hemadaes reproduce by infecting other creatures with their genetic material. Their bite infects a horrific disease that slowly turns the victim's skin into an outer shell of metal, trapping them inside while the venom digests them alive. The primordial soup that remains then metamorphoses into a drone.</p><h2>Prized Prisoners</h2><p>Hemadae queens are artisans with few equals, but even they recognize that a few non-hemadae rise to their level. Artisan queens do their best to capture anyone with crafting skills more developed than their own. Rather than being converted into new hemadae, these prisoners are forced to work on behalf of the hive. Few escape hemadae captivity alive, although some have managed to do so by sabotaging hemadae engines of war or by encoding secret messages on ammunition destined to be used against heroes.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Intimidation)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, body, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (acid, fire, vitality), intelligence, metal, mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Arcane: Truespeech), strength, throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Cold Contractions","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The first time each round a hemadae overseer takes cold damage, their metal plates recede and expose vulnerable points. They gain weakness 10 to piercing until the end of their next turn.</p><p>Effect: Cold Contractions</p>"},{"n":"Ferrodermia","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["arcane","disease","incapacitation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 30 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> clumsy 1 and Fatigued (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Clumsy 2, fatigued, and a –10-foot circumstance penalty to speed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> Paralyzed (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> Petrified in metal (1 day)</p><p><strong>Stage 6</strong> death, and the victim's body transforms into a new hemadae drone that flies off to join other of their kind.</p>"},{"n":"Hivesense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["arcane","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>A hemadae queen can feel the minds of nearby members of their hive, detecting their locations as a precise sense.</p>"},{"n":"Assimilate","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature uses a concentrate action within 60 feet of the hemadae queen</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The queen assaults the mind of the triggering creature, dealing 2d6 mental damage (DC 30 basic Will save). On a failure, the queen puppets the creature's muscles to move them 10 feet in any direction (or 20 feet on a critical failure); this is forced movement. This movement does not disrupt the action, but it might move the creature out of range of their intended target. Cold Contractions As hemadae drone, except the piercing weakness is 10.</p>"},{"n":"Absorb the Fallen","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","healing"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The hemadae queen is adjacent to a dead hemadae and is not at full Hit Points</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hemadae queen absorbs the body into their own, regaining 5d8 healing Hit Points.</p>"},{"n":"Instruments of War","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hemadae queen fires one of the four cannons affixed to their abdomen. Creatures in a 50-foot line take 6d6 damage (DC 30 basic Reflex save). Each cannon deals a different damage type and has additional effect on a critical failure. She can instead choose to fire two cannons simultaneously; if she does, she can't use Instruments of War again for 1d4 rounds.</p><p><strong>Cannon 1</strong> 6d6 acid, Sickened 1 on a critical failure</p><p><strong>Cannon 2</strong> 6d6 bludgeoning, knocked Prone on a critical failure</p><p><strong>Cannon 3</strong> 6d6 fire, 1d10 persistent fire on a critical failure</p><p><strong>Cannon 4</strong> 6d6 piercing, 1d10 persistent bleed on a critical failure.</p>"},{"n":"Mind over Metal","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","concentrate","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The hemadae queen releases a mental pulse in a 40-foot emanation that harms their enemies but inspires their minions. The screech deals 12d6 mental damage to non-hemadae creatures in the area (DC 28 basic Will save). Hemadae in the area can instead use a reaction to Fly, Step, or Stride; one hemadae in the area, of the hive queen's choice, can instead use a reaction to Strike. The hive queen can't use Mind over Metal for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/E4J2OpbzIKzBpEaM":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Fey","Melodian","Sussuran"],"notes":{"per":"snow vision"},"flavor":"<p>For many, winter is a time of quiet, darkness, and privation. These are not the aspects of the season championed by the cheerful (if shy) hibernals. These fey are drawn to the feasts and spectacles that light up the gloomier months of the year, and an \"audience\" of hibernals can often be found clustered around the edge of a midwinter festival, soaking in the fellow feeling and good cheer. They are uniquely captivated by an outstanding performance, whether that be a rousing seasonal song or the dance of lights in the heavens common to poleward lands.</p><p>Outside of festival times, hibernals can typically be found roaming the winter wildernesses and aiding lost travelers or starving animals with their magic. They are rarely given to anger, preferring to sort disputes through challenges of song and dance, but they make an exception for those who seek to snuff out the light of joyful celebration. When conflicts arise, they demonstrate remarkable bravery, wielding a baton of hard ice as a weapon and using their swiftness to outmaneuver their stumbling foes.</p><hr><p>Seasonals are a cycle of four kinds of fey, each tied to one of the four seasons: vernals who embody spring's renewal and storming showers, aestivals who embody summer's lazy heat and burning passion, autumnals who embody the melancholy culling and reaping harvest of autumn, and hibernals who embody winter's quiet cheer.</p><p>Whatever the current season in the mortal world, seasonals wrap themselves in a metaphysical cloak of their own season's magic and carry the songs full of that season's surging emotions with them wherever they go in the mortal world or across the realm of Faerie. Whenever seasonals meet, shy hibernals typically prefer to amuse themselves by watching the antics of the other three kinds of fey, while vernals impatiently call for energetic contests, aestivals languidly nap until roused to fiery bursts of activity, and autumnals attempt to imbue a sense of stark responsibility in the rest of their compatriots. Exceptions exist, with those seasonals interacting with the others in ways unexpected based upon the stereotypes of their season.</p><h2>Frozen Fellowship</h2><p>When a hibernal walks, icy gusts and snowdrifts follow in their wake—a disadvantage to those who would like to be invited inside to share cheer. Knowing that few mortals enjoy the cold that they bring with them, hibernals make sure to stay outside, enjoying cozy mortal festivals from afar. In turn, some folks bring their feasts outside, braving the cold in order to share fellowship with their frozen fey friends.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (cold, vitality), ice, music, sensory (low-light vision), wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Snow Vision","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Snow doesn't impair the hibernal's vision; they ignore concealment from snowfall.</p>"},{"n":"Blizzard Cloak","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A hibernal's passage leaves snowdrifts in their wake, making any square they move through difficult terrain to any non-hibernal until the start of their next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Eager Audience","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hibernal takes a –2 circumstance penalty to their Will saves and DCs against mental effects with the auditory or visual traits, as well as against attempts to Fascinate them.</p>"},{"n":"Snow Clone","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The hibernal takes damage from a physical attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The damage is reduced by 5 and the hibernal can immediately Step. If the attack was a melee Strike made from an adjacent square, the attacker takes 5 cold damage.</p>"},{"n":"Wintry Gust","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The hibernal Strides or Flies. Any creature adjacent to them at any point during the movement takes 4d6 cold damage (DC 17 basic Fortitude save). They can't use Wintry Gust again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/NGT6hgSBWtmpqwXh":{"lang":["Aklo","Astral","Speak in dreams, Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"lore":[["Dreamlands Lore",22]],"notes":{"per":"dreamsense (precise) 100 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Individually, the parasitic nightmares of the Dreamlands, known as hollow kraits, are more of a nuisance than a threat. These surreal serpentine figments feed upon the dreams of mortals, twisting those dreams into nightmares and fattening upon the resulting terror of their victims. Over time, hollow kraits who find a particularly delicious source of nightmares can twist their victim's psyche, instilling trauma that persists long after they awaken.</p><p>When hollow kraits unite into swarms, they transform into an entity far more intelligent and dangerous than their individual parts, making them a true menace. Not content to scavenge upon the dregs of dreams, hollow krait swarms actively seek out victims, and they sometimes even slip through the boundaries into other planes by riding the currents of waking dreams. Hollow krait swarms stalk likely victims, putting them to sleep before feasting upon their psychic energy. Some victims perish quickly; others are not so lucky.</p><h2>Neighborhood Nightmare</h2><p>Although a hollow krait swarm can plague their victims with an infinite variety of nightmares, each swarm has favorite themes to which it returns. Within the dreams granted by a given swarm, particular images and individual monsters recur again and again. A neighborhood where the same nightmare plagues multiple households is a telltale sign of a hollow krait infestation.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> armor (light), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Intimidation, Lore [Dreamlands], Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), fortification, intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Confusion, Dream Message, Fear, Invisibility, Mind Reading, Nightmare, Paranoia, Sleep, Truespeech, Vision of Death, Wave of Despair), strength, sturdy, time, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Dreamsense (PRecise) 100 Feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Hollow kraits clearly perceive all dreaming creatures within the listed range, as well as the content of the dreams. Any such creature becomes a valid target for the swarm's Dream Message and Nightmare spells.</p>"},{"n":"Speak in Dreams","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A hollow krait swarm can use telepathy on sleeping creatures without waking them up.</p>"},{"n":"Courage Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Hollow kraits revel in the fear they spread but are repulsed by bravery. The first time each round that the swarm is within range of an effect that grants creatures a bonus specifically against fear (such as a bard's courageous anthem), they take 3d6 mental damage. The damage can also be triggered when a creature critically succeeds at a saving throw against a fear effect or performs an act of exceptional bravery.</p>"},{"n":"Frightful Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet DC 26 Will</p><hr><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Nightmare Mind","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If the hollow krait swarm is targeted by an effect with the fear trait, the swarm regains 4d8 healing Hit Points and is Quickened for 1 round instead of the normal effects. It can use the extra action from quickened to Stride or use Psyche Leech.</p>"},{"n":"The Abyss Gazes Back","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature that the hollow krait swarm can see uses a mental effect against the swarm</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The magic gives the triggering creature unwelcome insight into the swarm's hivemind. The triggering creature must attempt a DC 29 Will save.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is frightened 2, and the swarm is unaffected by the triggering mental effect.</p>"},{"n":"Psyche Leech","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The swarm deals 4d6 mental damage to each creature in its space (DC 29 basic Will save). A creature that fails the save is Stupefied 1 for 1 minute (Stupefied 2 on a critical failure). If a frightened creature is damaged by Psyche Leech, the value of the creature's frightened condition doesn't decrease at the end of its next turn and the hollow krait swarm regains 2d6 healing Hit Points. Sleeping creatures don't awaken as a result of damage from Psyche Leech unless they succeed on the Will save.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/eMdpcl5neTpRJ7CE":{"lang":["Khaotan","Truespeech"],"notes":{"speed":"spatial hop"},"flavor":"<p>Born out of the drippings that seep out of the spaces between planes of existence, interstitial sludges are so dark that they appear almost two-dimensional. These oozes seek gaps in reality where they can slip into the stability of the planes. They resent all creatures that regularly enjoy this stability, lashing out violently against them. When an interstitial sludge would be slain, it is instead drawn back into the void from whence it came.</p><h2>The In-Between</h2><p>Sages deny the existence of a realm between dimensions called the \"In-Between.\" In fact, some grow agitated and even violent when pressed on the issue. The existence of a place outside of all dimensions calls into question reality itself, and suggests that all the known planes are part of a cosmic simulation—a patently absurd notion. Nevertheless, interstitial sludge is from somewhere.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, darkness, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, force, sonic, spirit, vitality, void), fortification, ice, mind, mind shield, space, space shield, spells (Occult: Confusion, Truespeech), strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An interstitial sludge can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Disrupt Teleportation","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 120 feet of the interstitial sludge uses a teleportation effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The interstitial sludge unravels the magic, pulling from the void between the planes. The interstitial sludge attempts a counteract check (counteract modifier +30), with the following additional effects.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The triggering action is disrupted, and the creature using it is instead teleported within reach of the interstitial sludge.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The triggering action is disrupted, and the creature using it is instead teleported in a random square within 120 feet of the interstitial sludge but outside its reach, determined by the GM.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> A violent magical feedback shakes the interstitial sludge's essence, leaving Off-Guard until the end of its next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Light Weakness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If an interstitial sludge is hit by an attack with the light trait or fails a saving throw against an effect with the light trait, it is Sickened 1 in addition to the normal effects (Sickened 2 on a critical hit or critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Retreat to the In-Between","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The interstitial sludge is reduced to 0 Hit Points</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Instead of dying, the interstitial sludge is banished back to the space between planes. It casts 4th-rank confusion on all creatures within its reach. Creatures that critically fail their DC 38 Will save must roll a DC 38 Reflex save, on a failure, they are drawn into the space between planes; this is an incapacitation and teleportation effect.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Steal Vision","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["darkness","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by a sludge ball Strike must succeed at a DC 36 Fortitude save or be Blinded for 1 round. While the creature is blinded, the interstitial sludge can see using the creature's typical visual senses, but the interstitial sludge loses its immunity to visual effects.</p>"},{"n":"Greater Constrict","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>0 bludgeoning, basic Fortitude</p>\n<hr>\n<p><p>The monster deals the listed amount of damage to any number of creatures Grabbed or Restrained by it. Each of those creatures can attempt a basic Fortitude save with the listed DC. A creature that fails this save falls Unconscious, and a creature that succeeds is then temporarily immune to falling unconscious from Greater Constrict for 1 minute.</p></p>"},{"n":"Spatial Hop","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The interstitial sludge gathers its body into a 15-foot square that includes at least one of its initial spaces. It then picks a square within 10 feet of that 15-foot square to move into, and then finally redistributes its body into 9 contiguous squares that include the chosen square.</p>"},{"n":"Tendril Explosion","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The interstitial sludge makes a pseudopod Strike against all creatures within its reach, as well as all other creatures within 30 feet; it takes a –2 penalty on the Strikes against creatures that aren't in its reach. These attacks count toward the interstitial sludge's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after it makes all its attacks. For any attack that hits, the interstitial sludge can choose to teleport the creature struck in any square inside its reach.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/XHQyGD12H4GPyKZy":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean"],"lore":[["Architecture Lore",28]],"notes":{"speed":"relentless tunneler"},"flavor":"<p>Roaches and rats are the bane of mortal homeowners, yet these nuisances are welcome guests compared to invulnerabugs. Invulnerabugs are the pests that demigods and other powerful planar beings worry about invading their homes. These insects infest celestial castles and fiendish fortresses alike.</p><p>With their adamantine mandibles, invulnerabugs can and will chew through any substance, though they prefer to eat rare and precious materials. Their magically enhanced carapaces offer them versatile defenses against a wide range of threats.</p><p>While invulnerabugs are merely a pest for demigods, when an invulnerabug invades a mortal's home, it's best to just move and let the bug win.</p><h2>Eye of the Beholder</h2><p>Invulnerabugs form large, eusocial colonies, with each member belonging to a caste that determines their roles and responsibilities, such as raising children, defending the colony, or planning new construction. They use their knowledge of architecture to draw artful tunnels and avoid compromising the structural integrity of the buildings they infest, but few people enjoy living in buildings that are riddled with tiny holes. Invulnerabugs take offense to anyone who insults their destructive art or attempts to drive them away, but they can make stalwart allies to those who compliment their work and provide plenty of rare materials to eat.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,250/2,800/5,150 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Lore [Architecture], Religion, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, body, bane, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, force, sonic, spirit, vitality, void), fortification, metal, sensory (darkvision, scent), spells (Divine: Indestructibility), strength, sturdy, throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Adaptive Immunity","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The invulnerabug gains immunity to one of the following damage types of their choice; acid, bleed, bludgeoning, cold, electricity, fire, force, mental, piercing, poison, precision, slashing, sonic, or void. This immunity lasts until the invulnerabug uses this ability again.</p>"},{"n":"Just Won't Die","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per day</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The invulnerabug would be reduced to 0 HP, or it would otherwise die or be destroyed</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The invulnerabug is reduced to 1 HP and doesn't die. If they have not yet cast Indestructibility, they cast it immediately.</p>"},{"n":"Prone Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When an invulnerabug is Prone, they lose their resistance to all damage; this does not impact any immunity granted by Adaptive Immunity.</p>"},{"n":"Burrow Into Flesh","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The invulnerabug's last action was a successful mandibles Strike against a Small or larger creature and the invulnerabug is not currently burrowing inside another creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The invulnerabug attempts to burrow into the target. The target must attempt a DC 37 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 3d6 persistent bleed damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The invulnerabug burrows inside the target. The target takes 6d6 persistent bleed damage. While the invulnerabug remains inside the target, the target is Off-Guard to the invulnerabug, the invulnerabug's mandible Strikes deal an additional 2d6 precision damage to it, and it can't end the persistent bleed damage. At the end of each of its turns, the target can attempt a DC 37 Fortitude save to expel the invulnerabug from its body. Additionally, the target or an adjacent creature can attempt a DC 40 Medicine check to Administer First Aid check to stop the bleeding, removing the invulnerabug on a success.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the target takes 10d6 persistent bleed damage and checks to remove the bug take a –2 circumstance penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Unyielding Maw","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The invulnerabug's mandibles are made of magically enhanced adamantine. They ignore up 20 points of a target's Hardness and up to 20 of their resistance to piercing damage.</p>"},{"n":"Relentless Tunneler","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The invulnerabug can Burrow through any physical substance with 20 or fewer Hardness, including wood, stone, and metal. They always leave tunnels behind when they do so.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/36omoZn0hs38loHU":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Draconic","Empyrean","Fey","Telepathy 60 feet"],"lore":[["Alcohol Lore",13]],"notes":{"saves":"+2 status to all saves vs. fear"},"flavor":"<p>Kabruses are bloated, bumbling demons born from souls whose sins arose from intoxication and debauched revelry. These fiends exist in a perpetual state of stupor, sustained by flagon after flagon of magically conjured liquor. A kabrus's flesh and blood are suffused with this drink, making them intoxicating delicacies to other denizens of the Outer Rifts. As a result, kabruses almost always swear allegiance to more powerful demons to prevent their consumption.</p><p>Kabruses set free from the Outer Rifts drunkenly wander between celebrations, disguising themselves as mundane mortals or festive fey-like satyrs. Once a kabrus finds themselves carousing with unwitting revelers, they are compelled to forsake inhibition and indulge beyond their limits. To this end, they employ rousing toasts, challenges of bravado, and ample doses of their conjured drink. A kabrus knows no limit to their hedonism, and they provoke others to any reckless and destructive act they desire in the name of livening up a party.</p><p>As the celebrations come to a close, the kabrus departs, leaving others to deal with the aftermath, including the horrific hangovers their drinks induce.</p><h2>Death of the Party</h2><p>A kabrus staggers from party to party, often wearing the same rumpled guise to each. Frequent partygoers begin to recognize the same mysterious cast of characters who arrive toward the end of any truly epic rager—semi-legendary figures with names like The Laughing Cryer or The Broken-Glass Man, who always seem to be found at parties but who no one ever sees coming or going. Wise revelers know that, once you spot one of these figures, it's time to go home.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Lore [Alcohol], Performance, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> acid, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Charm, Illusory Disguise, Laughing Fit, Translocate), spirit, strength, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Expunge","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 30 feet fails a saving throw to reduce its sickened condition</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The kabrus forces the target to spew unholy bile in a 15-foot cone. The kabrus chooses the direction of the cone, which can't include the target. The target and each creature in the cone take 2d6 acid damage (DC 22 basic Fortitude save). After spewing the bile, the value of the creature's sickened condition is reduced by 1.</p>"},{"n":"Life of the Party","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. Creatures in the aura other than demons take a –1 circumstance penalty to Will saves, Perception checks, and saving throws against drugs.</p>"},{"n":"Sobriety Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If a kabrus is targeted by a spell or other effect that counteracts or resists poison, they take 4d6 mental damage.</p>"},{"n":"Kabrus Brew","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 8 hours</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Sickened 1, Stupefied 1, +2 status bonus to saves against fear effects (6 hours)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Drained 1, sickened 1, stupefied 1 (1 hour)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Drained 2, sickened 1, stupefied 1 (1 hour)</p>"},{"n":"Drinking Contest","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","divine","emotion","linguistic","manipulate","mental"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The kabrus downs a conjured flagon of kabrus brew, then dares a creature within 30 feet to drink one too. The target must attempt a DC 22 Will save.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target must use its first action on its next turn to drink a flagon of kabrus brew, which manifests before it as part of that action. The dose doesn't require a free hand to drink, and can be drunk even if the creature is sickened.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target is compelled to outdo the kabrus. As on a failure, but it must spend two actions to drink two doses.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Azu15bQ29LefIGUe":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Melodian","Sakvroth","Spirituan"],"gear":["Musical Instrument (Virtuoso handheld)"],"notes":{"saves":" +2 status to all saves vs. magic","speed":"(earth and wood only)"},"flavor":"<p>Kunthalakas are charming manipulators devoted to steering creative souls toward depravity and artistic burnout. They pride themselves on their ability to discern from just a few measures of music or lines of poetry which mortals are most ripe for corruption.</p><p>These subtle and calculating rakshasas have two favored strategies for corrupting their prey. The first is to use physical toxins, typically in the form of proffered drugs that promise unique visions but instead strip an artist of vitality and ambition. For the second, they rely on poisoned words, building up the artist's ego to be impervious to constructive feedback and then seducing them into creating overly bold work that's destined to be rejected by the public. Whatever their strategy, they prefer to keep the extent of the damage hidden until it's too late for the prey to be saved.</p><p>When not disguised as a fit and sprightly humanoid, a kunthalaka's insectile head is reminiscent of a termite's. They use their mouths for dual purposes: to deliver a psychotropic poison via their sharp mandibles and to vocalize a droning hum that addles the minds of foes.</p><h2>True Genius</h2><p>Rarely, a kunthalaka's would-be victim turns the tables on them by resisting—or perhaps even being inspired by—the rakshasa's honeyed words and mind-altering drugs. This is often because the mortal is already under the protection of some supernatural muse, such as an azata or nymph, though sometimes the kunthalaka simply fails to comprehend the true scope of their victim's brilliance. In either case, a mortal able to resist the predations of a kunthalaka is sure to draw the kunthalaka's undying ire, as well as the attention of powerful patrons across the mortal realm and beyond.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Performance, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Charm, Confusion, Clairaudience, Detect Magic, Enthrall, Figment, Haste, Illusory Disguise, Laughing Fit, Message, Prestidigitation, Songshroud, Suggestion, Ventriloquism, Vision of Death, Void Warp), spirit, time, unholy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The kunthalaka takes on the appearance of any Medium humanoid. This doesn't change the kunthalaka's Speed or their attack and damage modifiers with its Strikes but might change the damage type their Strikes deal (typically to bludgeoning). They lose their burrow speed and typically lose their mandibles Strike unless the humanoid form has a similar unarmed attack. They also lose their all-around vision and can't use Stridulation.</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"+2 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Kunthalaka Neurotoxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Clumsy 1 and Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Clumsy 2 and Stupefied 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> clumsy 2, stupefied 2, and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Stridulation","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The kunthalaka rubs their mandibles together to emit a hypnotic tone. Each non-rakshasa creature within 60 feet must attempt a DC 26 Will save. The effects of Stridulation last for 1 round, but if the kunthalaka uses this ability again on subsequent rounds, they extend this duration by 1 round for any creature already affected.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is immune to Stridulation for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Fascinated by the kunthalaka.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the fascinated condition doesn't end if the kunthalaka uses hostile actions against the creature.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/El0xgIHXqaFZIgKD":{"lang":["Aklo","Astral","Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Fey","Jotun"],"lore":[["Dreamlands Lore",17]],"notes":{"saves":"–2 status vs. effects that can immobilize"},"flavor":"<p>These fast-talking hucksters are the bane of \"honest\" soul merchants everywhere for their habit of passing off trapped dreamers as valuable souls for sale. Other hags often dismiss lace hags as pretty-faced charlatans who get caught in their own webs more often than their prey, but it's risky to underestimate one.</p><p>A lace hag's pet spiders spin webs and garments that can trap victims' dreaming selves, allowing the hag to hunt, captivate, bind, and pawn them off at her leisure while they sleep. A buyer's impotent rage at being deceived is rarely a dream the victim wakes from.</p><h2>Tangled Webs</h2><p>Lace hags enjoy spinning not only physical webs, but also elaborate webs of deceit in which to trap gullible mortals. Those who catch a lace hag in a lie are said to gain power over the hag, though the nature of this leverage is unclear, as most who accomplish the feat don't live to tell the tale. What's certain is that simply doubting the lace hag's story is not enough—one must present clear evidence that what the hag says is untrue, and do so in a way that humiliates her entirely.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Deception, Diplomacy, Lore [Dreamlands], Occultism, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Interplanar Teleport), strength, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Coven","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A lace hag adds Never Mind, Sleep, and Web to their coven's spells. Their spell DC when leading a coven is 25.</p><hr><p><p>This monster can form a coven with two or more other creatures who also have the coven ability. This involves performing an 8-hour ceremony with all prospective coven members. After the coven is formed, each of its members gains elite adjustments, adjusting their levels accordingly. Coven members can sense other members' locations and conditions by spending a single action, which has the concentrate trait, and can sense what another coven member is sensing as a two-action activity, which has the concentrate trait as well.</p>\n<p>Covens also grant spells and rituals to their members, but these can be cast only in cooperation between three coven members who are all within 30 feet of one another. A coven member can contribute to a coven spell with a single action that has the concentrate trait. If two coven members have contributed these actions within the last round, a third member can cast a coven spell on her turn by spending the normal spellcasting actions. A coven can cast its coven spells an unlimited number of times but can cast only one coven spell each round. All covens grant the 8th-rank Cursed Metamorphosis spell and all the following spells, which the coven can cast at any rank up to 5th: Augury, Charm, Clairaudience, Clairvoyance, Dream Message, Illusory Disguise, Illusory Scene, Scouting Eye, and Talking Corpse. Individual creatures with the coven ability also grant additional spells to any coven they join. A coven can also cast the Control Weather ritual, with a DC of 23 instead of the standard DC.</p>\n<p>If a coven member's departure or death brings the coven below three members, the remaining members keep their elite adjustments for 24 hours, but without enough members to contribute the necessary actions, they can't cast coven spells.</p></p>"},{"n":"In My Domain","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The lace hag has stitched together a special realm in the Dreamlands where their hag nature is heightened. While in their domain, they gain weakness 10 to cold iron and the In My Domain spells.</p>"},{"n":"Stitch in Time","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The hag's last action was a successful claw Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature is bound in lace. It must succeed at a DC 21 Reflex save or become Immobilized and Slowed 1 until it Escapes.</p>"},{"n":"Phase Snare","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per hour</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lace hag is not on the Ethereal Plane</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hag calls upon their pet spiders in the Ethereal Plane to weave a 10-foot radius web, then phases the web into reality within 30 feet of her for 1 minute. All creatures that start their turn in the web's area must succeed at a DC 25 Reflex save or be Immobilized and Slowed 1 until they Escape or the web in their square is destroyed. Each square of the web can be destroyed by a single attack or effect that deals at least 20 slashing damage or 10 fire damage. A square has AC 5, and it automatically fails its saving throws.</p>"},{"n":"Silken Song","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lace hag is adjacent to a square of web from Phase Snare</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hag strums a gossamer-soft melody that casts 4th-rank Sleep (DC 23 Will). Creatures Immobilized by the web take a –2 circumstance penalty on their save.</p>"},{"n":"Dreamcatcher","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","incapacitation","mental","occult","sleep"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a creature falls asleep while in contact with lace that the hag has spun, the fabric turns into a ribbon adornment and fuses with the creature's body unless it succeeds at a DC 23 Will save. From that point on, whenever the creature sleeps, its dream-self appears at a random location within the lace hag's domain. If the creature's dream-self is dead, Immobilized, or in contact with the lace hag, it can't awaken. Others can enter the lace hag's domain by sleeping while in contact with the dreamer's physical body. The accursed lace can be cut away using the lace hag's dreamshears with a successful DC 23 Crafting check; this is a two-action activity with the manipulate trait.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/8NtyayCkCOtng4lZ":{"lang":["Draconic","Fey","Can't speak any language"],"notes":{"speed":"storm walk"},"flavor":"<p>Laelapses, also called storm hounds, are supernaturally relentless predators resembling a mix of reptilian beasts and hunting dogs. These creatures are renowned for their tenacity. Legend claims that once a laelaps captures the scent of prey, it never ceases its pursuit until it or its quarry is dead.</p><p>Their superior endurance is a key part of their hunting strategy. Laelapses prefer to harry and frighten their targets, running them to exhaustion before closing in to deal the final blow.</p><p>Though in the wild they prefer storm-ravaged coastlines and floodplains, laelapses are prized companions for fey nobles and others who battle elusive foes. Laelapses aren't picky about who joins their pack, so long as the newcomers can contribute to the hunt.</p><h2>Storm Riders</h2><p>Laelapses can ride storms over any type of terrain in pursuit of their quarries, but the color of their scales suggests that the creatures are more at home in some environments than others. Laelapses with green or blue scales, for example, are often encountered pacing coastlines, while those with white or silver scales stalk the arctic wastes. A laelaps native to a desert region might have scales in colors of tan, yellow, crimson, or gold.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Nature, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (electricty, sonic, vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, scent), spiked, strength, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Hounding Jaws","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature the laelaps has designated with Hunt Prey Strides or takes an action with the teleportation trait while within the laelaps's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The laelaps makes a jaws Strike against its prey. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the laelaps's multiple attack penalty. If this Strike hits, the distance covered by the Stride or teleportation effect is reduced by half. If it critically hits, the laelaps' prey stays in place, though other creatures affected by their action can still teleport or move.</p>"},{"n":"Howling Pursuit","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","auditory","emotion","fear","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The laelaps rushes forward, propelled by screaming wind that strikes fear into those that hear it. The laelaps Strides twice. If the laelaps passes within 5 feet of an enemy during this movement, that creature must attempt a DC 23 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> No effect.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>"},{"n":"Hunt Prey","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The laelaps designates a single creature it can see and hear, or one it is Tracking, as its prey. The laelaps gains a +2 circumstance bonus to Perception and Survival checks when Seeking and Tracking its prey. The first time each round that the laelaps hits its prey with a Strike, it deals an additional 1d8 precision damage. These effects last until it uses Hunt Prey again.</p>"},{"n":"Relentless Pursuit","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per minute</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The laelaps is grappled, Immobilized, Paralyzed, Petrified, Restrained, Slowed, or Stunned</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The laelaps removes any one of the listed conditions, but increases its drained value by 1 for 1 hour. This increase is cumulative with other uses of Relentless Pursuit, to a maximum of Drained 4. The laelaps can take this action even when a condition would normally prevent it from taking actions.</p>"},{"n":"Storm Walk","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["air","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The laelaps can walk on air as if it were solid ground while in an area of stormy or violent weather. It can ascend or descend at a maximum of a 45-degree angle.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/KdrqXxlSX9D7ATUI":{"lang":["Common","Daemonic","Telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>Unlike most daemons, which represent deaths from clear physical causes, lethodaemons represent the creeping death of the self through memory loss. Stealthy enough to sneak into most places unseen, lethodaemons are intelligent enough to target those who society already expects to be forgetful anyway, such as the elderly or the kind of absentminded people who constantly misplace items. Known liars and the corrupt are also enjoyable targets, as few will actually believe their protestations that they have truly forgotten whatever it was they did wrong.</p><p>After creating holes in a victim's memory, lethodaemons enjoy filling the gaps with false recollections that encourage their victim toward paranoia and suspicion of the others around them. Though they typically prefer subtle corruption, lethodaemons also enjoy opportunities to inflict more brutal mind-scrambling, confounding a target completely until they can no longer tell friend from foe, lashing out indiscriminately at the world around them out of anger and confusion.</p><p>Lethodaemons sometimes serve more powerful daemons, interfering as spies and saboteurs to undermine any efforts to oppose their overlord's corruption. In the rare case where they are caught in the act, a quick manipulation of the offending party's memories is typically enough to ensure the lethodaemon's escape. When that's not sufficient, the lethodaemon either takes to the sky in flight or stands their ground against opponents capable of outpacing them or shooting them out of the air.</p><h2>Riddle Me This</h2><p>While lethodaemons can wipe out a person's memories, there's one thing they can't erase: the memory of the daemon's own words, either spoken or telepathic. However, they can rewrite a memory to place their words—which frequently contain mocking riddles—into the mouth of someone else. Not infrequently, these clues lead to the lethodaemon's own unmasking.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Intimidation, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Confusion, Fear, Never Mind, Paranoia, Rewrite Memory, Translocate, Warp Mind), spirit, throwing, unholy, water, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Thoughtsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The lethodaemon senses all non-mindless creatures at the listed range. When a lethodaemon detects a creature with thoughtsense, they also determine if the creature's memories have been magically modified, whether by the lethodaemon or another creature. If the other creature was attempting to be subtle about their manipulation, the lethodaemon must succeed at a Perception check against the DC of the memory-altering ability in order to detect it.</p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Drain Memory","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The lethodaemon rips away their victim's memories, leaving small gaps that disorient it. When a lethodaemon damages a creature with a melee Strike, the creature must succeed at a DC 33 Will save or become Stupefied 1. Further exposures to Drain Memory increase the stupefied condition's value by 1 on a failed save, to a maximum of Stupefied 4. The stupefied value reduces by 1 every hour.</p>"},{"n":"Mists of the Styx","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","water"],"f":"once per minute","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lethodaemon calls upon the forgetful mists of the River Styx, gaining a 30-foot aura that clouds the minds of creatures within it for 3 rounds. All creatures that start their turn in the aura must attempt a Will save against Drain Memory. The mists are transparent to creatures whose minds are clear, but murky to those whose minds are clouded. All creatures within the aura become Concealed to stupefied creatures, and all creatures outside the mist become concealed to stupefied creatures within it.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/TgadmhyFfMq7PwIm":{"lang":["Common","Can't speak any language"],"flavor":"<p>If not for the glowing flames in their eyes and faint embers in their footprints, a lifespark bandit would be almost indistinguishable from a raccoon. Though their habit of taking anything they can get their paws on resembles their animal lookalikes, their internal biology is far more closely related to that of phoenixes.</p><p>Lifespark bandits particularly prize objects that are either nonflammable or especially flammable. Items in the former category can survive proximity to the bandit's beloved flames without being consumed, while those in the latter category make excellent kindling for bonfires.</p><p>Lifespark bandits are quick to seek revenge for perceived slights. While they prefer to avoid inflicting bodily harm in their revenge, they are capable combatants if cornered. Occasionally, a lifespark bandit takes a liking to those who treat them well. Those who earn the affection of a lifespark bandit might find all manner of goods left as offerings, ranging from trash to items of personal significance to whoever once owned them to stolen fragments of vital essence.</p><h2>Little Fires Everywhere</h2><p>When lifespark bandits exact revenge on creatures who have earned their enmity, they often turn to the tool that comes easily to their paws: fire. A lifespark bandit makes note of the items that an enemy seems to value most and then, one by one, destroys those treasured objects in individual acts of arson. Eyewitness accounts of a raccoon fleeing the scene are often ignored.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Nature, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, life, lifeward armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Invigoration","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Proximity to fire invigorates the vital essence within a lifespark bandit. Whenever a lifespark bandit's fire immunity negates fire damage, the lifespark bandit becomes invigorated for one minute, granting them 2d6 temporary Hit Points and a +1 status bonus on Fortitude saving throws. When the invigoration ends, any remaining temporary Hit Points are lost. A lifespark bandit can't become invigorated from their own flames or those of another lifespark bandit.</p><p>Effect: Invigoration</p>"},{"n":"Scorch","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An adjacent creature hits the lifespark bandit with a melee attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> An arc of fire leaps and scorches the triggering creature, dealing 1d8 fire damage (DC 18 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Unwanted Addition","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fire","manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lifespark bandit is invigorated</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lifespark bandit sneaks a burning piece of vital essence onto an adjacent creature. The lifespark bandit must attempt a Thievery vs. Perception DC check against the target's Perception DC. On a success, the target takes 1d6 persistent fire damage and the lifespark bandit gains a +2 status bonus on damage rolls against this target for as long as this persistent damage lasts, and the lifespark bandit is no longer invigorated.</p>"},{"n":"Vital Theft","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lifespark bandit is not invigorated and has just hit a creature with their claws Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lifespark bandit digs their claws into the creature, attempting to steal its vital essence. The lifespark bandit attempts a Thievery vs. Fortitude DC check against their target's Fortitude DC. On a success, the lifespark bandit becomes invigorated for 1 minute and the target takes 1d6 persistent void damage. On a critical success, the target also becomes Drained 1 for 1 hour.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/aWNt1vaZ7VJK0P63":{"lang":["Common","Luxan","Pyric","Spirituan"],"gear":["Lantern","Lantern (Hooded)","Repair Toolkit","Lamplighter's Pole"],"notes":{"ac":"(24 with lantern raised)"},"flavor":"<p>Lightlings can be found in derelict dungeons, deep caverns, and any of the world's dark reaches. Here, their sole mission is to tend to torches, lanterns, and other guiding lights so that travelers lost in such darkness are not without hope of finding their way home.</p><p>Light is essential for preserving these spirits' corporeal forms. As such, they never stray far from their flock of lights without a magically empowered lantern clutched in hand. While it would seem safest to simply live aboveground, lightlings rarely seek the security of daylight or other natural light sources. While the reason for this decision is uncertain, some believe the choice to place themselves in constant peril is a means of self-discipline on the lightlings' part. Perhaps they prefer shedding their physical forms to the possibility of failure in their work.</p><p>Most lightlings are shy, skittish creatures, typically content to mind their own business so long as no one threatens the lights they tend. However, they sometimes take more active roles in their work, acting as personal guides to lost travelers or slaying creatures of darkness that dare to encroach on the lightlings' domains. Of course, even those who serve reactively as wardens often must contend with the other denizens of the underground who don't take kindly to the presence of all the lights.</p><hr><p>Elemental spirits are spiritual beings who embody one or more elements, though those of composite elements tend to be more complex existences.</p><h2>You Going to Pay For That?</h2><p>While lightlings generally aren't belligerent, they often demand supplies or services as payment from those they believe they have aided. Lightlings' taciturn nature can make these demands difficult to interpret for the spiritually naïve. Poorly negotiated payments are a frequent source of irritation to the spirits and have been the cause of more than one traveler finding themselves suddenly lost in the darkness.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Occultism, Survival, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, intelligence, light, spirit, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dissolve in Darkness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a lightling is hit by an attack roll from or fails a saving throw against a darkness or shadow effect, they take 2d6 spirit damage in addition to the normal effects. They also take 2d6 spirit damage whenever they begin their turn in an area of dim light or darkness.</p>"},{"n":"Lantern Keeper","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["fire","light","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Any lantern held by a lightling is magically empowered by the lightling's elemental essence, allowing it to be used as a shield that grants a +2 circumstance bonus to AC when the lightling Raises a Shield (Hardness 5, HP 30, BT 15). The lantern sheds magical bright light out to 60 feet and dim light for a further 60 feet beyond that. If the lantern is broken, the radius decreases to 15 feet of bright light and 15 feet of dim light. The lantern's light can be counteracted with an air, cold, darkness, shadow, or water effect (counteract DC 22), or snuffed out with a single successful DC 25 Thievery check to Disable a Device. Either method extinguishes the lantern until the lightling relights the lantern with an Interact action.</p>"},{"n":"Shield Block (Special)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If the lightling uses a burning lantern to Shield Block an attack from an adjacent creature, that creature must succeed on a DC 19 Fortitude save or be Blinded for 1 round; this secondary effect has the light, occult, and visual traits.</p><hr><p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster has its shield raised and takes damage from a physical attack.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster snaps its shield into place to deflect a blow. The shield prevents the monster from taking an amount of damage up to the shield's Hardness. The monster and the shield each take any remaining damage, possibly breaking or destroying the shield.</p></p>"},{"n":"Focus Light","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","fire","light","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lightling's lantern is lit</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lightling focuses a beam of light that travels to the edge of the lantern's range (typically 120 feet if the lantern isn't broken). All creatures in a line take 4d4 fire damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save). Creatures in dim light treat the result of their saving throw as one degree of success better.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/xrWNODagL0a9Jwbq":{"lang":["Astral","Chronan","Common","Telepathy 100 feet"],"lore":[["Creator Lore",21]],"notes":{"ac":"all-around vision"},"flavor":"<p>When regret, guilt, and anger about an event that someone feels they could have stopped become strong enough, a lingering regret is born. They seek out the person whose feelings created them and do whatever it takes to \"correct\" the timeline. Though some creators appreciate the assistance, many are horrified by the lingering regret's willingness to commit atrocities in pursuit of their goals. For their part, lingering regrets are inclined to interpret any reticence their creators display as a sign that they need to go farther to prove the value of their methods.</p><p>Once defeated, lingering regrets rarely stay subdued forever. As long as their creators hold on to the feelings that gave them life, they will eventually return to continue their mission.</p><h2>Shards of Time</h2><p>Lingering regrets resemble younger versions of their creators, frequently looking and dressing exactly as they did during a particularly painful episode of the creator's life. Their appearance is a vivid reminder of the creator's past failures, and few people can bear to stare their lingering regrets in the face.</p><p>Besides their creators' peace of mind, lingering regrets are particularly destructive to one other type of object: mirrors. When a lingering regret sees their own reflection in a mirror, the mirror shatters.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Locate, Reverberating Pain, Wall of Time), time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"Susceptible to the Future","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>lingering regrets can only exist in the past and react viscerally to being displaced in the future. Whenever a lingering regret is Quickened, their body congeals. They gain no extra actions from being quickened, instead losing their resistance to all damage.</p>"},{"n":"Return to the Past","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet of the lingering regret Steps or Strides</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The lingering regret launches the creature backward in time, returning it to the square it was in at the beginning of its Step or Stride unless it succeeds at a DC 26 Will save.</p>"},{"n":"Stagnant Sorrows","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The lingering regret's negative emotions flare up as they slice into nearby creatures with fractured moments of time laden with their deepest sorrows. All creatures in a 20-foot burst take 4d6 mental plus 3d6 slashing damage (DC 25 basic Reflex save). Squares in the burst are littered with broken shards of time, becoming difficult terrain for all creatures except the lingering regret until the beginning of the lingering regret's next turn. Creatures that end their turn in one of these squares must succeed at a DC 25 Will save or be Sickened 1 from the rush of emotions they emit (Sickened 2 on a critical failure). The lingering regret can't use Stagnant Sorrows again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Cruel Memories","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a regret's caress or regret's lash Strike is shoved back into memories of moments it regrets. It must succeed a DC 25 Will save or become Slowed 1.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/jDk6LrugCxgKnuxN":{"lang":["Common","Thalassic"],"flavor":"<p>Liquefieds result from unethical magical experiments that explored the possibility of artificially creating new forms of elementals.</p><p>The process to create a liquefied involves taking a humanoid and slowly infusing them with a water elemental, erasing the minds of both creatures. Though liquefieds weren't what the researchers initially hoped to create, they secured numerous unscrupulous patrons willing to pay handsomely for an army of liquefied guardians and soldiers. The survivors of this grisly process still wander the world today.</p><p>Liquefieds dwell in dark, damp areas, where they spend much of their time resting in the form of puddles. When unwitting prey steps on a liquefied, the liquefied rises and engulfs it. After a lengthy digestion process, it grows slightly in size. Over time, as a liquefied continues to eat and grow, it eventually splits into a pair of liquefieds. Although there are whispers of supermassive liquefieds lurking in unexplored caverns and consuming would-be spelunkers.</p><h2>Water Has Memory</h2><p>Liquefieds have long outlived both their creators and the original owners who tried to turn them into soldiers or guardians. In many cases, not only did they outlive them, but they were the direct causes of their deaths. Although liquefieds are nearly mindless, they do possess a hatred for those who try to restrain them and a hunter's patient cunning—and it's hard to avoid an enemy who can be lurking in any puddle, bath, or rain barrel.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, throwing, time, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Puddle From","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>In its resting state, a liquefied's body is an immobile puddle, and other creatures can freely enter its space. A liquefied can temporarily solidify through applied pressure or quick impacts onto it. At the end of the liquefied's turn, if it was not exposed to any sources of pressure or movement since the end of its previous turn, such as taking force or physical damage or moving (either with an action or forced movement), it reverts to an immobile puddle.</p>"},{"n":"Bounce Back","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A melee attack that deals force or physical damage critically hits the liquefied</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The liquefied returns the momentum back to the attacker, attempting a fist Strike against it and adding any additional damage from Stored Momentum. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the liquefied's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Slow Movement Weakness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The liquefied's body's dramatic reaction to sudden impacts is lessened when the attacker's movements are slowed. Slowed creatures who Strike the liquefied with a melee attack ignore its resistance to bludgeoning and don't trigger its Bounce Back reaction.</p>"},{"n":"Stored Momentum","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A liquefied retains some of the force from its enemy's attacks and uses it to empower its own Strikes. Each time the liquefied takes force or physical damage, it gains a +2 circumstance bonus to damage on its next fist Strike. This bonus is cumulative, to a maximum bonus of +8 damage. The bonus damage decreases by 2 for every action the liquefied takes on its turn before Striking.</p><p>Effect: Stored Momentum</p>"},{"n":"Solidify and Devour","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Medium or smaller creature steps onto the liquefied's space</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The liquefied is in its puddle form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The liquefied attempts an Athletics check to pull the creature into its body. This functions as Swallow Whole against a Grabbed creature, except it does not have the attack trait (2d10+2 bludgeoning damage, Rupture 17).</p>"},{"n":"Disperse Pressure","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>The liquefied expands its body out into a 20-foot radius puddle. All creatures in the area take 5d6 bludgeoning damage and must attempt a DC 26 Reflex save. The liquefied then gathers itself back up to occupy any 10-foot square within the radius.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is pushed up to 5 feet toward the outer edge of the puddle; this is forced movement.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature takes double damage and is pushed up to 10 feet.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/MZYS3YF9i5hdCkCL":{"flavor":"<p>Relics of a bygone age, living lightning rods once served as guardians for a long-lost civilization. Records show that this civilization angered a capricious storm deity, and the deity smote these ancient peoples with devastating bolts of thunder. In response, the people created living lightning rods to protect the land until the god's anger subsided.</p><p>Living lightning rods are not hostile by nature, but each guards a particular monument or relic of their creators and responds violently to perceived vandals. While these constructs typically remain motionless until forced to respond, they can occasionally be found wandering through storms, waiting to be recharged by an errant lightning strike.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (electricity, sonic, spirit, vitality, void), metal, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Voltaic Charges","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The living lightning rod is Charged 2 at the beginning of the encounter.</p>"},{"n":"Electrical Absorbance","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If the living lightning rod is targeted by or in the area of an attack or effect that has the electricity trait or deals electricity damage, the living lightning rod is Charged.</p>"},{"n":"Voltaic Overload","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["arcane","electricity"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a living lightning rod would be charged when it is already charged 3, it immediately vents the excess charge in an explosion. All creatures within 30 feet of the living lightning rod take 10d12 electricity damage (DC 36 basic Reflex save). Afterward, the living lightning rod loses the charged condition, is Stunned 3, and takes a –2 circumstance penalty to AC until the end of its next turn.</p><p>Effect: Voltaic Overload</p>"},{"n":"Lightning Rod","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature or area within 30 feet is targeted by an effect with the electricity trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The living lightning rod attempts a counteract check against the effect (counteract modifier +30). If the counteract check succeeds, the living lightning rod is Charged.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Gather Charge","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","electricity"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The living lightning is charged. All creatures adjacent to the living lightning rod take 2d12 electricity damage (DC 34 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Magnetic Pull","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The living lightning rod is charged and its last action was a successful lightning coil Strike against a creature within 60 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The living lightning rod pulls the targeted creature toward itself; the creature must attempt a DC 36 Fortitude save to determine the distance.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is pulled 15 feet.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is pulled 30 feet and knocked Prone.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is pulled 60 feet and knocked prone.</p>"},{"n":"Thundergrip","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The living lightning rod is charged and has a creature Grabbed or Restrained in one of its fists</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The living lightning rod deals 2d12+10 electricity damage to the creature (DC 36 basic Fortitude) and extends the duration of the grabbed or restrained condition until the end of the living lightning rod's next turn. The value of the Living Lightning Rod's charged condition reduces by 1.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/hXhEADjr7NE2YMf8":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Daemonic","Telepathy 100 feet"],"notes":{"per":"fear sense (varies) 1 mile"},"flavor":"<p>Lyodaemons personify death through loneliness and separation from others, especially the deaths of lone travelers who die of exposure. They take a cruel joy in separating travelers from their group and overwhelming them with fear and despair. Once they've had their fun, the daemon falls upon their victims with a whirl of mercilessly cold wind.</p><p>While encountered most frequently in the wilderness, some lyodaemons prefer other isolated locations, such as abandoned buildings, forgotten mines, and graveyards. Some stories even tell of lyodaemons lurking outside of outhouses on cold winter nights, for that moment of fear when a farmer realizes they'll have to make the trek back to their home under the dark of a new moon.</p><h2>Abaddon is Other People</h2><p>As daemons go, lyodaemons are relatively rare, yet they are some of the daemons most likely to be encountered by mortals. The reason for this is twofold. First, the fear of isolation is both particularly strong and nearly universal. Second, lyodaemons find the company of other daemons distasteful and, once released from Abaddon to stalk the mortal realm, are loath to return to their native plane. It's said the worst punishment one can inflict upon a lyodaemon is to send it back to Abaddon. Indeed, many lyodaemons would prefer to be erased from existence entirely than be forced to spend a moment with others of their kind.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Intimidation, Occultism, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, elongating, energy resistant (cold,vitality), ice, mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Falling Icicles, Fear, Frostbite, Howling Blizzard, Mirage, Translocate, Wall of Ice, Winter's Grasp), strength, throwing, unholy, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Fear Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>A lyodaemon can sense the minds of frightened creatures within 1 mile as a vague sense and within 60 feet as an imprecise sense.</p>"},{"n":"Curse of Lonely Death","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","curse","divine","emotion","fear","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. A lyodaemon's sheer presence fills the mind with a primal fear of being alone. Whenever a non-fiend creature begins its turn in the aura, it must attempt a DC 29 Will save. On a failure, whenever the creature starts or ends its turn, it becomes Frightened 1 unless it is adjacent to an ally that it isn't hidden to it. It can't reduce its frightened condition at the end of its turn unless it is adjacent to a visible ally. The curse of lonely death persists for 1 day or until the lyodaemon that caused it is slain, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Frigid Fear","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["cold","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A frightened creature starts its turn within 60 feet of the lyodaemon</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature takes 1d6 persistent cold damage and takes a status penalty to its Speeds equal to 10 times the value of its frightened condition until end of its turn. If this penalty reaches or exceeds the creature's Speed, it is Immobilized.</p>"},{"n":"Crushing Sweep","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The lyodaemon makes a sledge fist Strike against all creatures within reach, taking a –2 penalty on the attack rolls. These attacks count toward the lyodaemon's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after they make all their attacks. Additionally, they can use Improved Push on every target they hit.</p>"},{"n":"Illusion of Isolation","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","illusion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The lyodaemon tricks the senses of its foes, cloaking allies from their sight. All non-fiend creatures within 30 feet take 5d8 mental damage and must attempt a DC 32 Will save, with the following effects. A creature that succeeds on the saving throw is temporarily immune to Illusion of Isolation for 1 hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage and the creature's allies are all Concealed from it for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and the creature's allies are all hidden from it for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and the creature's allies are all hidden from it for 1 minute. The creature can attempt a new Will save at the end of each of its turns to end this effect early.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Push","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Push in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Shove the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Improved Push lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pushed on a success to that distance.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/k4QJcBTET0kPQMdI":{"lang":["Common","Draconic","Jotun","Spirituan","Thalassic","Telepathy 100 feet"],"flavor":"<p>Matsugami, also known as ocean kami, are gregarious spirits who protect shorelines and the settlements that inevitably sprout up along them. To a matsugami, fishers are just as much a part of the natural ecosystem as fish. They are fascinated by the cultures within the coastal communities they watch over, as well as by travelers who sail over the ocean to reach them.</p><p>Though matsugami are rare, their existence is no secret to the communities they protect, with locals happily sharing stories of these kami running across the ocean's surface to flying kick a hungry sea serpent or tossing armfuls of beached sea life back to the oceans. When great storms arrive, matsugami patrol the seas, guiding ships to port and hauling people cast overboard back to safety. Communities fortunate enough to enjoy a matsugami's protection typically construct a shrine in their honor, where they leave prayers and offerings.</p><h2>Teach Someone to Fish...</h2><p>Despite their reputation as boisterous guardians, matsugami are patient with troublemakers. They prefer to educate and negotiate, only resorting to violence against those who threaten the safety of their ward.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3,000/7,000/13,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Medicine, Nature, Religion, Society, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Control Water, Enduring Might, Tidal Surge, Water Breathing, Water Walk), spirit, strength, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Ward","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The matsugami is bound to a stretch of coastline. They can merge with or emerge from their ward as a single action with the concentrate trait. While merged, they are Immobilized, but they recover Hit Points each minute as if they spent an entire day resting.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Disorienting Whirlpool","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The matsugami Swims twice, gathering the churning waters in their wake and pooling them around their fists. They then make a fist Strike. If the Strike hits, it catches the target in a condensed whirlpool, dealing an additional 3d6 bludgeoning damage and making the target Off-Guard until the beginning of its next turn. If the Strike misses, the whirlpool spreads out into the surrounding water in a 15-foot radius around the target. Until the start of the matsugami's next turn, any creature that enters or begins its turn in the whirlpool must succeed at a DC 40 Reflex save or take 3d6 bludgeoning damage and be off-guard until the beginning of its next turn. The whirlpool's waters are difficult to traverse, requiring a successful DC 35 Athletics check to Swim. Matsugami are immune to Disorienting Whirlpool.</p>"},{"n":"Flying Kick","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The matsugami Strides, makes a Long Jump, then makes a foot Strike. If the matsugami makes a successful Long Jump of at least 20 feet during this action, the strike's damage increases to 3d12+34.</p>"},{"n":"Suplex","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The matsugami has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The matsugami throws the creature, releasing it and dealing 2d10+18 bludgeoning damage (DC 40 basic Fortitude save). If the target is on land, it is knocked Prone; if it is underwater, it is instead Sickened 1 (or Sickened 2 on a failed Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Titan Wrestler","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The matsugami can attempt to Disarm, Grapple, Reposition, Shove, or Trip creatures of up to three sizes larger than them.</p>"},{"n":"Torrential Flurry","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The matsugami makes two fist Strikes. These attacks both count toward their multiple attack penalty, but the penalty doesn't increase until after both attacks.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/CTn9NWjMrAMjdEe5":{"lang":["Common","Melodian"],"gear":["Rapier","Glass Vial (3)"],"flavor":"<p>At first glance, Mellifican hives resemble papery humanoids, but their constant buzzing and musical humming reveal their true nature. In reality, their outward form is a complex nest built and piloted by a colony of mellificans, intelligent psychic honey wasps. These nests are typically humanoid-shaped so that they can use tools designed for humanoids, but the details of their forms reflect the colony's collective tastes.</p><p>Though mellifican hives can coordinate their movements with uncanny accuracy, they lack a true hivemind. Their existence is one of constant discussion and compromise, expressed through multi-layered melodies.</p><p>While some mellifican hives prefer to settle down in one spot, where they grow gardens or join industries like breweries or patisseries, others enjoy traveling far and wide in search of new experiences. Whatever their lifestyle, mellifican hives enjoy company, and they cultivate friendships with creatures of a variety of backgrounds and perspectives. A mellifican hive's allies can expect to be called in on a regular basis to help the hive resolve internal disputes. While mellificans typically prefer to avoid violence, they don't hesitate to rush into battle if their allies are threatened.</p><p>Mellificans experience sympathy for many other swarms, and they consider swarmbloods to be distant kin. Few beings are more repulsive to a mellifican hive than rothives, however, and they are quick to rally against these unnatural creatures.</p><h2>Honored Remains</h2><p>Though a mellifican hive is composed of thousands of individuals, the collective can die of old age, just like any living creature. About once each year, the wasps that make up the mellifican hive emerge to create a new body for the hive to inhabit, a project that typically takes only a few hours. The previous bodies of living hives are usually left out for the elements to disintegrate, but when a mellifican hive swarms for the final time, the hive's companions often pay respects by burning its last body atop a funeral pyre or interring the remains in a place of honor, such as a temple or tomb.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3.5/7/13 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Medicine, Nature, Occultism, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), life, lifeward armor, mind, mind shield, music, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Warning Hum","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A mellifican hive doesn't need to speak to inspire primal fear. They don't take the typical penalty for Demoralizing without using a language.</p>"},{"n":"Gift of Honey","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mellifican hive spends 10 minutes gathering honey from their core and either feeds it to a creature or stores it in a container, allowing it to be consumed later with an Interact action. A living creature who consumes honey regains 1d8 healing Hit Points. A mellifican can't benefit from their own honey, though they can benefit from the honey of another mellifican. The honey remains magical until dawn, after which it becomes normal honey.</p>"},{"n":"Hive Defenses","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>While inside the hive, the mellifican swarm lacks the typical weaknesses and immunities granted by the swarm trait (except for swarm mind). If a portion of the swarm leaves the hive, such as when using Swarm Surge, the mellifican hive gains weakness 3 to both area and splash damage.</p>"},{"n":"Harmonic Impalement","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The mellifican hive has just damaged a creature with a rapier Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The hive hums in unison, sending harmful vibrations down the rapier that tear at the wound. The target takes 1d6 sonic damage (DC 17 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Swarm Surge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The mellifican swarm surges out of the hive to irritate and drive away their foes. Each creature in a 15-foot cone takes 2d4 piercing plus 2 poison damage (DC 17 basic Fortitude save). Creatures that critically fail this saving throw also take 1 persistent poison damage. The mellifican hive can't use Swarm Surge again until they spend one action to recall these wasps to the hive; until they do so, they gain swarm weaknesses as listed in Hive Defenses.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/D9KBrbU35AqlNDCb":{"lang":["Common","Talican"],"notes":{"speed":"phases of matter"},"flavor":"<p>The first metallic phase elementals were the result of an eccentric alchemist's tireless research into the boundaries between the classical states of matter: solid, liquid, and gas. These elementals can change their forms between these states effortlessly, and they use their different forms to adapt to whatever challenges come their way. The temperament of a metallic phase elemental is closely connected with their preferred form, with stubborn ones preferring their solid state, while wilder ones favor their gaseous state.</p><p>Most metallic phase elementals maintain a collection of different types of metals. Nobles and educational institutions have been known to pay them in rare elements to convince them to perform demonstrations or even give guest lectures on alchemical principles.</p><p>A metallic phase elemental that collects enough of a valuable metal, such as cold iron or silver, can infuse it through their body. This infusion allows the elemental to bypass resistances overcome by their chosen metal and grants them the elite adjustments. Though cold iron and silver infusions are the most common, infusions of rarer metals are rumored to exist, granting the elemental still greater advantages.</p><h2>What's Mine is Mine</h2><p>Metallic phase elementals can be just as avaricious hoarders of coins as any dragon or adventurer—the problem begins when the coins in question belong to someone else. While metallic phase elementals have a sophisticated understanding of the concept of \"my property,\" efforts to explain \"someone else's property\" to them have so far been unsuccessful. A metallic phase elemental might burrow through the wall of a palace treasury and claim the wealth therein, reacting with surprised indignation when the palace's inhabitants try to reclaim their treasure.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, metal, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Knockdown","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"},{"n":"Phases of Matter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A metallic phase elemental begins the encounter in one of the following forms.</p><p><strong>Gas Form</strong> The elemental is a shimmering cloud of gas. When this form, they can use their shard blast Strike, Phase Change, and Storm of Metal. They also gain a 30-foot fly speed.</p><p><strong>Liquid Form</strong> The elemental is a metallic goop. When in this form, they can use their liquid lash Strike, Phase Change, and Wild Splash. They also gain a 40-foot swim speed.</p><p><strong>Solid Form</strong> The elemental is a solid block of metal. When in this form, they can use their metal fist Strike and Phase Change. They also gain a 30-foot burrow speed and tremorsense (imprecise) 30 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Phase Change","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","metal","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The metallic phase elemental's molecular structure shifts and vibrates, changing from one of their Phases of Matter to another.</p>"},{"n":"Storm of Metal","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["metal","move"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The metallic phase elemental is in their gas form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The metallic phase elemental turns into a tornado of metal shards that tear apart all in their path. The metallic phase elemental Flies, passing through the spaces of creatures in their path. Creatures whose spaces the elemental enters during this movement take 4d6 slashing damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save). Creatures that critically fail also take 1d6 persistent bleed damage.</p>"},{"n":"Wild Splash","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The metallic phase elemental is in their liquid form</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The metallic phase elemental makes three liquid lash Strikes at a –2 penalty, each against a different target. These attacks count toward the metallic phase elemental's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after they make all their attacks.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/jUiUSoVJuv4SI0Jj":{"lang":["Aklo","Common","Fey","Venenan"],"gear":["Rapier","Mug of Tears"],"flavor":"<p>Once members of bands of wicked pranksters, the mirthless no longer cackle with malicious glee. Instead, they sob into their jeweled goblets, overcome by bitter regrets. The tears of these cruel and cunning fencers are poisonous to mind and body alike, infesting whoever is afflicted with them with the mirthless's bitter despair.</p><p>Mirthless spin tales of woe to each other and all that approach. They patiently listen to anyone who shares their own regrets and sorrows. If those regrets are tinged with jealousy, such as tales of people who envy the current partner of a former lover or who covet a long-desired accolade that went to a rival instead, the mirthless might offer more than simply words of commiseration. They fuel those feelings of jealousy and help their partner in despair enact elaborate plots of revenge. It matters not to the mirthless whether the plots succeed or fail so long as they can revel in someone's misery.</p><p>Mirthless can't stand being alone. They are most often encountered in the company of others of their kind, typically members of their former prankster bands or others with whom they have shared particularly unpleasant experiences. Anyone who smiles, laughs, or shares tales and songs of happiness in the presence of a mirthless inspires the fey to violent rage.</p><h2>Cheer Up Mate!</h2><p>Helping a mirthless overcome their sadness is a difficult, though not always impossible, task. A mirthless is all too eager to share its tale of woe with anyone who will listen—the trick seems to be convincing them that feeling joy again is even possible. Often, those seeking to help a mirthless must leave the fey to wallow in their sorrow while the tragic events that caused them to become a mirthless are put right. A mirthless who reclaims their zest for life is no less mischievous than they once were, but their tears become those of boundless bliss that instill a wild exuberance in anyone who tastes them.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Nature, Performance, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Taunting Tumble","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The mirthless is targeted by a Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mirthless goads their attacker, then Strides up to half their Speed after the Strike resolves. Any ally who hears this taunt can use a reaction to Stride up to half their speed.</p>"},{"n":"Mug of Tears","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The mirthless is adjacent to a Prone creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mirthless makes a Strike against a prone creature's abdomen. If the Strike hits, the mirthless pours a distillate of poisoned tears from their goblet into the creature's mouth. The creature takes 3d6 poison damage (DC 22 Fortitude save). The creature is also slowed for 1 round on a failed saving throw, or for 3 rounds on a critical failure.</p>"},{"n":"Contagious Misery","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The mirthless's weapon begins the encounter covered with a dose of this poison, which originates from their tears. They can reapply the poison with Venom, To Thy Work.</p><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d6 poison damage and falls Prone</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 poison damage, falls prone, and Frightened 1</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 3d6 poison damage, falls prone, and frightened 1</p>"},{"n":"Surprise Attacker","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>On the first round of combat, if the mirthless rolls Deception or Stealth for initiative, creatures that haven't acted yet are Off-Guard to them.</p>"},{"n":"Venom, To Thy Work","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Weeping openly onto their rapier, the mirthless applies a dose of contagious misery to their weapon.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The mirthless's Strikes deal an extra 2d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/8DD4vJlrctXuKss5":{"notes":{"ac":"(25 when broken), construct armor","speed":"cubic movement"},"flavor":"<p>Originally built into ancient pyramids as defenders, monolithic cubes are massive cubic constructs, typically made of granite. Despite their name, monolithic cubes are not a single mass of stone. Instead, their exterior shell is a thick layer of rock, while their interiors are a semi-fluid slurry of mercury, iron shavings, fine gravel, and blood. Most scholars believe that the only \"living\" portion of the cube is this internal fluid—a simple elemental spirit that uses magnetic forces to animate the cube and follow its creator's will.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), metal, mind, mind shield, music, poison, poisonous armor, strength, sturdy, throwing, time, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 10)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, a monolithic cube has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage the cube takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once a monolithic cube is reduced to fewer than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks, removing the Hardness, reducing its Armor Class to 25, and activating its mercurial quickness.</p>"},{"n":"Mercurial Quickness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>While a monolithic cube's construct armor is broken, it is Quickened. It can only use the additional action from quickened for Crushing Face. Additionally, it gains a mercury jet Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Crushing Face","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube's primary means of both attack and locomotion is to roll over, crushing everything in its path. The monolithic cube Strides. During this Stride, the cube can move into and through the spaces of creatures and objects that are Large or smaller. Unattended objects in the path of a monolithic cube's crushing face automatically take 10d10 bludgeoning damage. Each creature whose space the monolithic cube enters takes 2d10+6 bludgeoning damage (DC 28 basic Reflex save). Wobbling between two faces slightly destabilizes the cube. If the cube uses Crushing Face to move in the opposite direction of its most recent Stride on the same turn, the DC of the Reflex save is reduced to DC 24 basic Reflex .</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature moves to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> As critical success, but the creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage, moves to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube, and is knocked Prone.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is crushed by the cube, taking double damage. It is knocked prone and Restrained underneath the cube, sharing its space while restrained in this way. A creature can Escape to move to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube. If the cube leaves the creature's space, that creature is no longer restrained.</p>"},{"n":"Unstoppable Roll","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The monolithic cube is unaffected by difficult terrain and greater difficult terrain. Its Speed is always a multiple of 15 (such as 15, 30, or 45). It is unaffected by penalties that would reduce its Speed below 15. Bonuses to speed have no effect unless they would increase its Speed to a new multiple of 15.</p>"},{"n":"Grind","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The monolithic cube deals 2d10+8 bludgeoning damage to each creature currently Restrained by its crushing face (DC 28 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Cubic Movement","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube moves by rolling from one face to another, leaving all of the spaces it previously occupied. As such, it can Stride only in straight lines and in cardinal directions, and it must always move exactly 15 feet. Stepping is a two-action activity for a monolithic cube.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/tTjoslHWcyOZ1UiT":{"notes":{"ac":"(25 when broken), construct armor","speed":"cubic movement"},"flavor":"<p>Originally built into ancient pyramids as defenders, monolithic cubes are massive cubic constructs, typically made of granite. Despite their name, monolithic cubes are not a single mass of stone. Instead, their exterior shell is a thick layer of rock, while their interiors are a semi-fluid slurry of mercury, iron shavings, fine gravel, and blood. Most scholars believe that the only \"living\" portion of the cube is this internal fluid—a simple elemental spirit that uses magnetic forces to animate the cube and follow its creator's will.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), metal, mind, mind shield, music, poison, poisonous armor, strength, sturdy, throwing, time, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"The Die is Cast","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The faces of some monolithic cubes are etched with enormous numerals representing metaphysical concepts. Depending on which number is on the cube's top face, these numerals grant the cube immunity to a certain type of damage, while revealing a weakness to another. The numerals, the concepts they represent, and the immunities and weaknesses of each number follow.</p><p><strong>1. Might:</strong> Immunity to damage dealt by weapons and weakness 10 to spell damage.</p><p><strong>2. Frost:</strong> Immunity to cold damage and weakness 10 to fire damage.</p><p><strong>3. Lightning:</strong> Immunity to electricity damage and weakness 10 to sonic damage.</p><p><strong>4. Thunder:</strong> Immunity to sonic damage and weakness 10 to electricity damage.</p><p><strong>5. Flame:</strong> Immunity to fire damage and weakness 10 to cold damage.</p><p><strong>6. Magic:</strong> Immunity to spell damage and weakness 10 to damage dealt by weapons (apply only once per weapon Strike even if it has damaging property runes or the like).</p>"},{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 10)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, a monolithic cube has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage the cube takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once a monolithic cube is reduced to fewer than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks, removing the Hardness, reducing its Armor Class to 25, and activating its mercurial quickness.</p>"},{"n":"Mercurial Quickness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>While a monolithic cube's construct armor is broken, it is Quickened. It can only use the additional action from quickened for Crushing Face. Additionally, it gains a mercury jet Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Crushing Face","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube's primary means of both attack and locomotion is to roll over, crushing everything in its path. The monolithic cube Strides. During this Stride, the cube can move into and through the spaces of creatures and objects that are Large or smaller. Unattended objects in the path of a monolithic cube's crushing face automatically take 10d10 bludgeoning damage. Each creature whose space the monolithic cube enters takes 2d10+6 bludgeoning damage (DC 28 basic Reflex save). Wobbling between two faces slightly destabilizes the cube. If the cube uses Crushing Face to move in the opposite direction of its most recent Stride on the same turn, the DC of the Reflex save is reduced to DC 24 basic Reflex .</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature moves to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> As critical success, but the creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage, moves to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube, and is knocked Prone.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is crushed by the cube, taking double damage. It is knocked prone and Restrained underneath the cube, sharing its space while restrained in this way. A creature can Escape to move to a square of its choice adjacent to the cube. If the cube leaves the creature's space, that creature is no longer restrained.</p>"},{"n":"Unstoppable Roll","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The monolithic cube is unaffected by difficult terrain and greater difficult terrain. Its Speed is always a multiple of 15 (such as 15, 30, or 45). It is unaffected by penalties that would reduce its Speed below 15. Bonuses to speed have no effect unless they would increase its Speed to a new multiple of 15.</p>"},{"n":"Grind","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The monolithic cube deals 2d10+8 bludgeoning damage to each creature currently Restrained by its crushing face (DC 28 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Cubic Movement","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A monolithic cube moves by rolling from one face to another, leaving all of the spaces it previously occupied. As such, it can Stride only in straight lines and in cardinal directions, and it must always move exactly 15 feet. Stepping is a two-action activity for a monolithic cube.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/on0oNx4vAXKQtrAR":{"lang":["Astral","Common","Cryan","Sussuran","Utopian","Omnilinguistic archivist"],"lore":[["Encyclopedic Lore",30],["Library Lore",30]],"flavor":"<p>Moonlight owls nest in places filled with knowledge, such as libraries, schools, observatories, and ancient ruins. They often take it upon themselves to become the caretakers and guardians of their chosen places. This can be quite a surprise for scholars and librarians who find that a talking owl has suddenly decided to oust them from their job.</p><p>Moonlight owls believe that the smartest creature should lead. If they lose an intelligence competition, they will temporarily follow the winner and absorb any new information they can. However, they rarely stick around for long, as they enjoy being the smartest creatures around. While they can form long-lasting relationships with less intelligent creatures, their relationships with other moonlight owls only last as long as both consider themselves to be the more intelligent of the group.</p><p>These prideful creatures are lifelong scholars who continue to supplement their innate primal magic with arcane study. The easiest way to convince a moonlight owl to share their knowledge is to humbly provide them with obscure knowledge they haven't already acquired.</p><h2>Not What They Seem</h2><p>Moonlight owls take every opportunity to remind people that they are not strictly owls. Although they happen to resemble common owls and share their name, they are in fact wise, magical guardians of knowledge and, intellectually speaking, are as far beyond owls as humans are beyond squirrels and mice. Yes, if you must know, moonlight owls do eat squirrels and mice, but again, it's important to remember that they are <em>not owls</em>.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Lore [Library], Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, darkness, energy resistant (cold, vitality), force, ice, intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), spells (Arcane: Arctic Rift, Detect Magic, Eclipse Burst, Figment, Frostbite, Howling Blizzard, Light, Message, Pinpoint, Project Image, Scrying, Telekinetic Hand, Telekinetic Storm, Truesight, Wall of Force), wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Encyclopedic Lore","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The moonlight owl can use Encyclopedic Lore only to Recall Knowledge, but they can use it on any topic.</p>"},{"n":"Omnilinguisitic Archivist","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The moonlight owl understands all spoken and written language. Whenever the moonlight owl speaks, all creatures that can understand at least one language understands their words.</p>"},{"n":"Foreseen Attack","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["misfortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The moonlight owl is the target of an attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The attacking creature must roll twice and use the lower result.</p>"},{"n":"Foreseen Spell","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The moonlight owl would make a saving throw against a spell effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The moonlight owl rolls twice and uses the higher result.</p>"},{"n":"Frost Armor","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The moonlight owl's last action was a spell with the cold trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The moonlight owl uses excess cold energy from the spell to form armor out of ice, which lasts for 1 minute. While the armor is intact, the moonlight owl gains a +4 circumstance bonus to AC and loses their weakness to fire. If the owl takes fire damage, the armor melts.</p>"},{"n":"Impart Knowledge","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","concentrate"],"f":"once per hour","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The moonlight owl chooses any specific Lore skill and selects up to 10 willing creatures within 100 feet. They can't choose Encyclopedic Lore or other nonstandard Lore skills that cover a broad range of topics. Those creatures become trained in that Lore for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"That Which Should Not Be Known","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>The moonlight owl sends visions of forbidden knowledge into the minds of everyone nearby. All creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 36 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unharmed, and the forbidden knowledge is oddly useful. It gains a +2 circumstance bonus on its next check to Recall Knowledge and is temporarily immune to That Which Should Not Be Known for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 3 and Fleeing for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/2Q0TQoty0xVdFrls":{"lang":["Petran","Pyric"],"notes":{"per":"smoke vision, tremorsense in lava or magma only","speed":"swim in lava or magma only"},"flavor":"<p>Mortarfish are gigantic, magma-dwelling fish that hunt prey by firing mortars of superheated rock from their mouths. They are often aggressively territorial, shooting magma at anyone who enters their hunting grounds. They can be reasoned with, but they're prone to attacking and devouring anyone that doesn't immediately announce their presence and offer tribute. Even fire giants must take care when approaching magma channels claimed by a mortarfish school.</p><p>Mortarfish are particularly proud of their ability to shoot magma precisely over a long distance. They frequently hold competitions, with the mortarfish whose shots are most accurate being granted leadership of the school. The targets for these contests are often ferrous crab swarms, and each mortarfish is entitled to eat whichever crabs they've killed. These rowdy events are often mistaken for volcanic activity.</p><p>Mortarfish typically hunt alone, but they return to their school regularly to care for their young and compete. The largest schools of mortarfish swim through the primeval volcanoes on the elemental planes where the first of their kind were born, but planar travel has allowed schools to colonize volcanoes across the Universe.</p><h2>Disorderly Ordnance</h2><p>With long-distance attacks that can level a citadel, mortarfish are coveted allies for fire giants and others with dreams of elemental conquest. However, mortarfish can be troublesome troops at best. Too undisciplined to be left to their own devices and too belligerent to be frightened into obedience, they are likely to ignore their commanders' objectives in favor of interesting targets of opportunity. One of the best ways to get mortarfish to stick to a particular task is to stress its difficulty: mortarfish will go to any lengths to prove doubters wrong.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, burning barbs, dexterity, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Magma Dweller","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The mortarfish thrives in molten rock. They can breathe in lava or magma in addition to air. Additionally, while submerged in lava or magma, they gain a +2 circumstance bonus to Stealth checks.</p>"},{"n":"Smoke Vision","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Smoke doesn't impair a mortarfish's vision; they ignore the Concealed condition from smoke.</p>"},{"n":"Magma Geyser","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The mortarfish is about to make a mortar shot Strike while they have a creature Grabbed or Restrained in their jaws</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mortarfish loads the creature onto the mortar shot. When they make the Strike, they launch the creature into an unoccupied space within 5 feet of the attack's target; this is forced movement. If the creature was restrained, it lands Prone. The launched creature takes the mortar shot's splash damage.</p>"},{"n":"Magma Wave","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The mortarfish is swimming in lava or magma</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mortarfish unleashes a wave of molten rock. All creatures in a 60-foot cone take 12d6 fire damage (DC 33 basic Reflex save). Creatures that fail the save also take 1d6 persistent fire damage. The mortarfish can't use Magma Wave again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Mortar Reload","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The mortarfish is swimming in lava or magma</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mortarfish draws molten rock into their mouth, forming it into a ball to prepare a mortar shot. If they do not currently have a creature Grabbed in their jaws, they can also attempt an Athletics check to grab a creature of size Large or smaller within 10 feet in their jaws. Mortar Reload is the only way that a mortarfish can reload their mortar shot.</p>"},{"n":"Volcanic Smokescreen","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The mortarfish spews a cloud of volcanic smoke in a 30-foot burst centered on themself. This smoke cloud remains for 1 minute. When a creature starts its turn in the smoke cloud or enters the smoke cloud, it takes 3d6 poison damage (DC 30 basic Fortitude save). All creatures in the smoke become Concealed, and all creatures outside the smoke become concealed to creatures within it. This smoke can be dispersed by a strong wind.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/7nYMSrk3GlYbCtjr":{"lang":["Common","Draconic","Luxan","Melodian"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>Muse wyrms are patrons of the arts who derive their name from their ability to inspire creativity. They want nothing more than to collect masterpieces and build connections with promising artists. It is up to the individual dragon whether this desire drives them to create a benevolent commune that promotes creativity, to engage in a purely economic exchange of money for services rendered, or to form a more nefarious cult-like organization.</p><p>A typical muse wyrm lair is filled with works of art in varying stages of completion. These lairs function both as studios and expansive museums. Muse wyrms consider all of the creations that their inspiration touches to be part of their extended hoard, even if they are technically owned by someone else. Most muse wyrms employ sizable contingents of guards to defend their collections.</p><p>Muse wyrms spend much of their time in a humanoid form, with a strong preference for elven forms. Regardless of what guise they choose, they take pride in being well-dressed and clad in fashionable accessories.</p><h2>Market Manipulators</h2><p>Muse wyrms are intensely interested in the careers of the artists they support, and not usually for altruistic reasons. Muse wyrms know that, as the fame of an artist in their stable rises, so too does the value of their horde. A muse wyrm will often introduce their proteges to the movers and shakers of society in order to boost awareness of the artist's work. Some muse wyrms resort to even more convoluted schemes to advance their artists' careers, such as creating false scandals about the artist or arranging a heist of the artist's works. There's no such thing as bad publicity, after all!</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Diplomacy, Occultism, Performance, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, elongating, energy resistant (sonic, vitality), light, mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision, scent), spells (Occult: Dissonance, Enthrall, Figment, Heroism, Illusory Disguise, Illusory Scene, Laughing Fit, Light, Noise Blast, Revealing Light, Summon Instrument, Vibrant Pattern), strength, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Echolocation 30 feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A muse wyrm can use their hearing as a precise sense at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Inspirational Lair","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>All creatures within a muse wyrm's lair gain a +2 circumstance bonus to checks to Craft and Perform. If a creature takes a hostile action against the muse wyrm, their bonus is replaced by a –2 circumstance penalty that affects them within the dragon's lair for the next 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Charming Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","emotion","incapacitation","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. When a creature enters the muse wyrm's aura, they must attempt a DC 27 Will save against a 5th-rank Charm spell. Regardless of the results of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to the dragon's Charming Presence for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Inspiring Mien","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fortune","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An ally fails a saving throw or a skill check within sight of the muse wyrm</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The muse wyrm's presence inspires the ally to greatness. The ally can reroll triggering check.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon takes on the appearance of any Small or Medium humanoid. They lose their fly Speed and Strikes and gain a Strike appropriate to their new form that uses the attack and damage modifiers of their claw strike, but might deal a different type of damage as appropriate (typically bludgeoning).</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Kaleidoscopic Scream","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","occult","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The dragon releases a concussive blast of sound and color in a 60-foot cone. Each creature in the area takes 6d6 sonic damage (DC 30 Will save). The dragon can't use Kaleidoscopic Scream again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage and is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Stunned 1, Blinded for 1 round, and dazzled for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage, is stunned 1, and is blinded for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/xzSxuGST2tbAfFs2":{"lang":["Planetary polyglot, Telepathy 10 miles"],"lore":[["Planetary Lore",30]],"notes":{"ac":"all-around vision"},"flavor":"<p>The violent supernova death of a star and all nearby life dependent upon it is a loss that defies comprehension. Yet not all of the star's essence is lost, for whoever was the last to bask in the dying star's warmth becomes an unwitting mourner. On rare occasions, a seed from the star's core, enveloped in the thoughts and dreams of all those who perished in the cataclysm, collapses into a composite consciousness known as a nebulite. The nebulite then has one goal: to nestle itself into a nebula, where it can slowly gather stardust until it can be reborn as a star once more, bringing light and life to a dark corner of the universe. Getting in its way during this pilgrimage, however, can prove exceedingly dangerous.</p><h2>Communication</h2><p>A nebulite's voice is a near-overwhelming harmony of the voices of all the sentient beings consumed in the supernova, and its thoughts are often just as diverse and scattered.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 560/1,250/2,300 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Lore [Planetary], Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, elongating, energy resistant (cold, force, vitality), force, intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Black Hole), strength, throwing, time, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"Planetary Lore","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Drawing upon the combined knowledge of all the beings caught in the supernova, the nebulite uses Planetary Lore in place of any skill to Recall Knowledge.</p>"},{"n":"Planetary Polyglot","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The nebulite speaks every language that was spoken by at least one person caught in the supernova.</p>"},{"n":"Accretion Disc","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. Creatures within the aura treat any squares they enter that are moving farther away from the nebulite as greater difficult terrain. Creatures that start their turn within the aura are pummeled by superheated particles, taking 8 fire plus 8 piercing damage (DC 32 basic Fortitude save). On a failure, the creature is also moved 5 feet toward the nebulite (or 10 feet on a critical failure); this is forced movement. By making quick calculations, a creature can temporarily escape the nebulite's pull. If a creature spends 1 action and succeeds at a DC 30 Arcana check or DC 26 Astronomy Lore check, it ignores the greater difficult terrain until it is subjected to forced movement.</p>"},{"n":"Event Horizon","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet. Creatures within the aura are undetected by creatures outside the aura and can't communicate with them except by magical means or telepathy. Creatures beginning their turn within the aura are Quickened. They can use the additional action to Stride or Strike, but can't use it to move farther away from the nebulite. A creature that leaves the aura during its turn loses the extra action and the quickened condition.</p>"},{"n":"No Breath","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A nebulite doesn't breathe and is immune to effects that require breathing.</p>"},{"n":"Gravitational Pull","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The nebulite's last action was a successful tendril or graviton Strike against a creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature is pulled in a direction of the nebulite's choice, either moving closer to the nebulite or maintaining its distance. This is forced movement. The creature must roll a DC 34 Fortitude save to determine the distance.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is pulled 5 feet.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is pulled 10 feet.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is pulled 20 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Fusion","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The nebulite forces a creature to move, and their path is blocked by another creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The movement ends, and both creatures begin to glow. At the end of each affected creature's next turn, the light violently flares around it. Creatures in a 10-foot emanation take 4d6 force damage (DC 31 basic Reflex save) and are Dazzled for 1 round on a failed save.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/wUPRdWzgRdi1nI6i":{"notes":{"ac":"(26 when broken), construct armor"},"flavor":"<p>Animated by powerful fiends, necrosis engines are machines of death created with the expressed intent of exterminating living creatures. They siphon the souls of their prey, processing them into incorporeal coal that they burn to fuel their internal flames.</p><p>Necrosis engines imbue the wounds they inflict with festering energy from Hell that interferes with treatment. As such, they are often deployed to annihilate military bunkers that house clerics and other magical healers. Druids that have angered a high-ranking fiend might find a necrosis engine searing their forest and scorching their animals.</p><h2>Hell on Wheels</h2><p>Necrosis engines absorb the souls of the mortals they kill, burning them as fuel. However, most souls captured by a necrosis engine can combust for days or even weeks before being entirely consumed. Only once the engine's infernal creators summon it back to Hell to drain its reservoir of fuel is a soul captured by a necrosis engine well and truly lost.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, death, energy resistant (fire, spirit, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, metal, spirit, strength, sturdy, throwing, time, unholy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Construct Armor (Hardness 10)","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Like normal objects, a necrosis engine has Hardness. This Hardness reduces any damage the necrosis engine takes by an amount equal to the Hardness. Once a necrosis engine is reduced to less than half its Hit Points, or immediately upon being damaged by a critical hit, its construct armor breaks, removing the Hardness and reducing its Armor Class to 26.</p>"},{"n":"Absorb Soul","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["death","divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A living creature within 30 feet of the necrosis engine dies</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The necrosis engine absorbs the creature's soul, converting it into fuel for their furnace. The necrosis engine is Quickened for 1 minute; it can use this extra action only to Stride or Strike. Until the necrosis engine is destroyed, it attempts to counteract all effects that would resurrect the creature (counteract modifier +22).</p>"},{"n":"Fiendish Wound","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>Injuries from a necrosis engine's necrotizing piston Strike fester with malevolent energy from Hell. Until the start of the necrosis engine's next turn, magically treating these wounds is particularly difficult. The malevolent energy attempts to counteract all magical effects that would restore Hit Points to the target (counteract modifier +21).</p>"},{"n":"Rev Engine","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>The necrosis engine shifts into overdrive, releasing a 15-foot cone of sparks and flames. Creatures in the cone take 5d6 fire damage (DC 28 basic Reflex save). Creatures that fail their save are Dazzled until the end of their next turn as sparks cover their eyes.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Siphon","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["death","divine","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>The necrosis engine attempts to steal a portion of the souls surrounding it. All living creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 26 Fortitude save. The necrosis engine can't use Soul Siphon again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Drained 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Doomed 1 and Drained 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is doomed 1 and Drained 4. For 1 minute, the necrosis engine's necrotizing piston Strike gains a status bonus to its fire damage equal to twice the value of the highest drained condition Soul Siphon inflicted.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/1sUMpix94v6oH2Sk":{"lang":["Aklo","Chronan","Common"],"flavor":"<p>Neoctri spend much of their time seeking out powerful organisms to consume and new environments to prompt valuable adaptations. Whenever possible, they choose intelligent prey and engage in prolonged hunts, only killing their victims when they are confident those victims have no more tricks in store. Though neoctris are merciless, they can be convinced to spare their targets at least temporarily in exchange for sufficiently enticing information about ways to enhance their abilities. Neoctris recognize that their forms don't allow them to walk freely in settlements, and they don't hesitate to take hostages and engage in other tactics of terror to coerce people into helping them get the information they seek.</p><p>When neoctris meet each other, the outcome is typically bloody and vicious, with both combatants seeking to learn from each other's adaptations. Neoctris have no compunctions about killing each other, but their sense of self-preservation often means that when one gets the upper hand, the other prioritizes escape.</p><p>Combating a neoctri requires constant alertness. They can undergo rapid evolution in mere seconds, modifying their defenses, enhancing their attacks, and creating new modes of locomotion, so it's especially foolish to become complacent after observing a neoctri's initial assault. Cultures who encounter a rare neoctri often pass down fables with the following moral: it's never wise to underestimate a neoctri.</p><h2>Hunting the Hunters</h2><p>One of a neoctri's favorite tactics is to isolate and hunt a single member of a humanoid community. The neoctri follows their victim, foiling their every stratagem, until the victim is near defeat—only to let them escape at the last moment. When the terrified humanoid raises the alarm and the community's defenders venture forth to catch the neoctri, the hunters become the neoctri's new prey, to be picked off one by one.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality), music, sensory (darkvision, scent), spiked, strength, time, wild</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality), music, sensory (darkvision, scent), spiked, strength, time, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike (Bipedal Form Only)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Adaptive Resistance","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>A neoctri would take acid, bludgeoning, cold, electricity, fire, piercing, slashing, or sonic damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The neoctri gains resistance against one of the types of damage that they are about to take. They gain resistance 4 against bludgeoning, piercing, or slashing damage or resistance 8 against acid, cold, electricity, fire, or sonic damage. If they would take multiple types of damage at once, they chose which resistance to gain. The resistance lasts until the neoctri use adaptive resistance again.</p>"},{"n":"Throw Rock","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>The monster picks up a rock within reach or retrieves a stowed rock and throws it, making a ranged Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rapid Evolution","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The neoctri's last action was a successful pincer Strike against a living creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The neoctri tears and consumes the target's flesh. The target takes 1d6 persistent bleed damage. The neoctri then assumes either their large and strong bipedal form or their observant and nimble tripedal form. The form change persists for 10 minutes or until they use Rapid Evolution again, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Bipedal Form","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>In their bipedal form, the neoctri becomes Large and the reach on their pincer and claw Strikes increases to 10 feet. They gain +2 status bonus to damage with their pincer and claw Strikes. They also gain the reaction Reactive Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Tripedal Form","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>In their tripedal form, the neoctri gains scent (precise) 30 feet, as well as their choice of one of the following: a +20-foot status bonus to their Speed, a 20-foot fly speed, a 30-foot climb speed, or a 40-foot swim speed.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/liYjA8FnoevbQC6f":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Shadowtongue"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","per":"fearsense (imprecise) 60 feet"},"flavor":"<p>A nightmare shade is a malevolent spirit that can form when a sentient being dies in a state of abject terror. Fragments of that being's soul become trapped in its shadow, animating it into a twisted creature whose continued existence depends on feeding on the fear of others.</p><h2>They Go Bump in the Night</h2><p>Densely populated areas are the most common places to find nightmare shades. They hide in gutters, attics, and other dark places during the day. At night, they emerge, silently entering houses in search of potential prey. Once inside, they create flickers of ominous shadows and snippets of eerie sounds to taste the fear of their targets. When they find someone whose fear is particularly rich, they depart, waiting for their prey to fall asleep before returning to plague them, preferring to return to the same victim many times, as the cumulative anxiety and trauma inflicted by their visits enhance the nourishment they receive.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> charisma, death, darkness, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, void), fortification, intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Sleep), throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fearsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nightmare shade is aware of all frightened creatures within the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Light Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An object shedding magical light (such as from the Light spell) is treated as though it has the <em>ghost touch</em> property when used to attack the nightmare shade.</p>"},{"n":"Nightmare Visions","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>When the nightmare shade damages a creature with their terrifying touch, the creature must succeed at a DC 24 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Consume Fear","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The nightmare shade consumes the terror of an adjacent frightened creature.</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature takes 4d8 mental damage (DC 26 basic Will save). If the creature failed the saving throw, the nightmare shade gains 5 temporary Hit Points (10 on a critical failure). Regardless of the result, the target's frightened condition is reduced by 1.</p>"},{"n":"Helpless Awakening","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","incapacitation","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nightmare shade targets an Unconscious creature that is not dying within 30 feet. The creature wakes up; if they were at 0 Hit Points, they regain 1 Hit Point. The creature must then attempt a DC 26 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Slowed 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Paralyzed. It can attempt a new Will save at the end of each of its turns to end the condition. The DC starts at 26 and cumulatively decreases by 1 on each attempt.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the DC does not cumulatively decrease.</p>"},{"n":"Night Terrors","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","darkness","emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nightmare shade produces a 30-foot emanation of black smoke. The smoke extinguishes all sources of nonmagical light and attempts to counteract any source of magical light within the area (counteract modifier +16). Creatures in the area take 5d6 mental damage (DC 26 Will save). The nightmare shade can't use Night Terrors again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage and is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage, is Frightened 3, and is Confused for 1 round as their senses are overtaken with their worst fears.</p>"},{"n":"Enduring Torment","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a nightmare shade deals mental damage, they can choose to make the damage nonlethal.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zZTxSLlG7xjk8XnV":{"lang":["Common","Jotun","Melodian","Necril","Sussuran"],"lore":[["Music Lore",31]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","per":"cloudsight"},"flavor":"<p>Cloud giant musicians are renowned for their skills, particularly with the harp. Some of these musicians act as patrons and muses for members of the smaller ancestries.</p><p>Nimbus dirges are the creations of a group of legendary necromancers unwilling to lose access to their favorite performers to the icy grip of death. The necromancers murdered the cloud giants in a brutal ritual, then grafted their signature harps into their bodies where their right arms had once stood. Yet the horrific cacophonies these profane beings created were a cruel mockery of the music they once loved. Disappointed by their failure, the necromancers turned their creations loose. Most of these nimbus dirges still wander the world to this day, bringing destruction wherever they go.</p><h2>Command Performance</h2><p>While beautiful music grieves nimbus dirges, they can't stop themselves from seeking it out. Weeping, they watch a skillful musical performance, sometimes demanding encore after encore. These command performances often end in bloodshed: when a nimbus dirge is inspired to play along, or when a fatigued musician strikes a false note, the resulting discordance drives the nimbus dirge into a murderous rage.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Intimidation, Lore [Music], Nature, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, charisma, constitution, death, elongating, energy resistant (void), mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision, scent), spells (Primal: Canticle of Everlasting Grief, Dissonance, Mask of Terror, Mist), strength, sturdy, throwing, unholy, water, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Cloudsight","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Nimbus dirges ignore concealment from weather conditions, including clouds and rain.</p>"},{"n":"Aura of Winds","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["air","aura","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. Nimbus dirges are constantly cloaked in harsh winds. All physical ranged attacks against them take a –2 circumstance penalty, and air within the aura is difficult terrain for flying creatures that don't have the air trait. When a creature enters or starts its turn within the aura, it must succeed on a DC 38 Reflex save or become Clumsy 1 until the end of its next turn (Clumsy 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Nostalgic Grief","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Nimbus dirges recall what it meant to make beautiful music to inspire others, and the sound of others performing from the heart overwhelms them with grief. A creature can attempt a Performance check in place of an Intimidation check to Demoralize them. If the creature rolls a success on the check, they get a critical success instead.</p>"},{"n":"Cacophonous Fury","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nimbus dirge's Strikes deals an additional 2d6 sonic damage to frightened creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Harmonic Discord","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["emotion","fear","mental","primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>The nimbus dirge unleashes hideous discord from their harp, sending a shockwave of pain and terror in all directions. All creatures within 30 feet take 15d6 sonic damage and must attempt a DC 38 Will save. The nimbus dirge can't use Harmonic Discord for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage and is Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage, is Frightened 3, and is Fleeing for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/NtdstHRK9m3YShVm":{"lang":["Aklo","Chthonian","Common","Draconic","Empyrean","Telepathy 200 feet"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>In the unlikely event that a mortal manages to steal a vast amount of temporal essence from the Dimension of Time, all the while avoiding or defeating its guardians, upon death their soul might be cast to the Outer Rifts without stripping the stolen power. The eldritch influence of the Dimension of Time and the Outer Rifts' corruption coalesce into an oligtharu, a fate-rending fiend.</p><p>These esoteric demons represent the sin of rewriting history. They travel to mortal realms, where they build cults and seed lies to lay the groundwork for altering the past. They expect their followers to launder the reputations of heinous organizations while slandering virtuous groups, leading people astray on a massive scale.</p><p>An oligtharu rarely fights their own battles. When circumstances force these demons to fight directly, they use magic to tear their foes apart with brutal efficiency and bend reality in their favor.</p><h2>Warping Reality</h2><p>When deciding upon the limits of an oligtharu's temporal corruption, consider what would seem plausible to your players, what the demon would know, and how any other creatures involved in the temporal corruption would react to the situation. For example, an oligtharu who knows about one of the PC's old enemies might use fabricate truth on several PCs to convince them that the foe has just arrived on the scene. Since the foe did not fail a saving throw against fabricated truth, they'll likely be perplexed about why they've suddenly been transported in front of the PCs, and they might suspect the PCs of drawing them into a trap. Meanwhile, PCs who failed the save completely fall for the trick, PCs who critically succeeded know how reality was warped, and PCs who succeed at the save can decide what they believe happened according to the context around them. As long as at least one PC believes the trick, however, they'll have to contend with their old enemy!</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 10,000/24,000/45,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium, heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism, Religion, Society, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, death, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision, scent, true seeing), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Augury, Desynchronize, Dominate, Foresight, Freeze Time, Haste, Hidden Mind, Interplanar Teleport, Mind Probe, Overwhelming Presence, Phantasmagoria, Raise the Reaper's Scythe, Rewrite Memory, Scrying, Slow, Translocate, True Target, Truesight, Wails of the Damned, Warp Mind), throwing, time, unholy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Steady Spellcasting","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a reaction would disrupt an oligtharu's spell, the demon can attempt a DC 15 Flat, avoiding the disruption on a success.</p>"},{"n":"Temporal Corruption","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["divine","time"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The oligtharu casts Fabricated Truth. If at least one creature fails the save, the fabrication is consistent with that creature's knowledge of the world, and the oligtharu imagines a plausible chain of past events to justify the fabrication, the oligtharu can alter the timestream to turn the fabrication into the truth. The change persists as long as at least one creature believes it, after which point any created objects or creatures are destroyed and any transported objects or creatures are returned to their original locations. All creatures except those that failed the save against <em>fabricated truth</em> remember the original timestream and might notice discrepancies; creatures that critically succeeded automatically recognize what happened. This effect can't introduce hazards or creatures of 20th level or higher.</p>"},{"n":"Temporal Vertigo","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["disease","divine","time","virulent"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 41 Will</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Sickened 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Confused and sickened 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> confused, sickened 2, and Slowed 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> As stage 4, but the victim is also under the effects of the flicker spell (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 6</strong> death. The creature's body and soul are dragged into and lost in the Dimension of Time.</p>"},{"n":"Change Shape","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","polymorph"],"f":"","d":"<p>The oligtharu can take on the appearance of any Small or Medium humanoid. This doesn't change their Speed or their attack and damage modifiers with their Strikes, but it might change the damage type their Strikes deal (typically to bludgeoning).</p><hr><p><p>The monster changes its shape indefinitely. It can use this action again to return to its natural shape or adopt a new shape. Unless otherwise noted, a monster cannot use Change Shape to appear as a specific individual. Using Change Shape counts as creating a disguise for the Impersonate use of Deception. The monster's transformation automatically defeats Perception DCs to determine whether the creature is a member of the ancestry or creature type into which it transformed, and it gains a +4 status bonus to its Deception DC to prevent others from seeing through its disguise. Change Shape abilities specify what shapes the monster can adopt. The monster doesn't gain any special abilities of the new shape, only its physical form. For example, in each shape, it replaces its normal Speeds and Strikes, and might potentially change its senses or size. Any changes are listed in its stat block.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reimposition Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Righting the oligtharu's impositions on memory and time brings the demon intense mental anguish. Whenever the oligtharu's Fabricated Truth or Rewrite Memory is counteracted, the oligtharu takes 3d10+14 mental damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/faKRfJyWYKxMHKRL":{"lang":["Common","Fey"],"gear":["Blood Gem (9)"],"flavor":"<p>With their macabre beauty and mesmeric juggling routine, the four-armed ouveliste is an alluring answer to desperate supplicants seeking to erase their traumatic memories. Ever-oozing wounds on their four palms weep shimmering blood and are both the source and the receptacle for the octahedral blood gems they juggle.</p><p>For oblivion seekers, the ouveliste's assurances of an untroubled mind are irresistible. However, the ouveliste revels in the chaotic consequences of forgetting that desperate minds do not foresee. They watch from shadowy corners as their victims' bewildered loved ones wonder what went wrong.</p><h2>Blood Bargain</h2><p>In order to suppress a creature's memories, an ouveliste clasps hands with a creature. When the handshake ends, the victim is left with a small, bloody wound on their palm—an injury that closes in minutes, leaving only a small scar. It's theorized that, along with memories, an ouveliste vampirically feeds on blood—or at least uses it to replace the blood that constantly oozes from the wounds on their own hands.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Performance, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), strength, throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Daturic Poison","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 29 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d4 poison damage and Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d4 poison damage and Stupefied 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d4 poison damage and Stupefied 3 (1 round)</p>"},{"n":"Mesmeric Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The ouveliste deals an additional 3d6 precision damage on melee Strikes to Dazzled creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Returning Gems","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An ouveliste's blood gems have the <em>returning</em> property when thrown from their hands.</p>"},{"n":"Forgetter's Bliss","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","curse","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>An ouveliste targets an adjacent, willing creature and permanently suppresses all of the creature's memories of a particular person, incident, or conversation. If anyone mentions the suppressed subject to the creature, the creature takes 3d8 mental damage (DC 32 basic Will save). A creature can only take damage in this way once per day. The next time it falls asleep, it forgets everything it has learned about the suppressed subject. The counteract DC for removing the curse with Cleanse Affliction and similar effects is 32.</p>"},{"n":"False Fantasy","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","illusion","incapacitation","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ouveliste spins a pleasant hallucination unique to each viewer. All Dazzled creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 30 Will save against the hallucination and take 4d8 mental damage when the hallucination ends. Creatures that succeed at the save become temporarily immune to False Fantasy for 1 hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature mostly rejects the hallucination, but hazy images impair its vision. For 1 round, all creatures that are farther than 10 feet away from it are Concealed to it. When the hallucination ends, they take half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is pulled into the hallucination. They experience the visual disturbances from a success, are Stunned 1, and take full damage when the hallucination ends.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the visual disturbances last for 3 rounds, and the creature takes double damage when the hallucination ends.</p>"},{"n":"Beguiling Legerdemain","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","light","manipulate","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ouveliste juggles three glowing blood gems, mesmerizing onlookers. Creatures within 30 feet must attempt a DC 32 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Beguiling Legerdemain for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 3 rounds.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 6 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Toxic Blood","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An adjacent creature deals piercing or slashing damage to the ouveliste</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ouveliste's blood splashes onto the triggering creature, exposing them to daturic poison.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/4smcNzAu4KRcmSr3":{"lang":["Astral","Chronan","Telepathy 120 feet, Truespeech"],"flavor":"<p>When a paradox is created in time travel, entire lives can be erased. The souls lost to such time loops can end up adrift in the Astral Plane, endlessly reliving moments from their past. Unable to accept their fate, a paradoxical past often winds up doing the same things over and over again, expecting a different result.</p><hr><p>Temporal paradoxes are a rare category of spirits that arise when strong-willed individuals are separated from their rightful place in the timeline. Though they retain strong memories and emotional connections to their pasts, it is seldom possible for them to go back to where they came from. Even if they were to manage the daunting task of locating their original reality and timeline, it would fundamentally reject their return. This rejection stems from a combination of their former realities editing them out in the interest of self-stabilization and the influence of their new realities causing their old homes to recognize them as incompatible invaders. Unfortunately for temporal paradoxes, their new realities don't fully accept them either, making them vulnerable to unwelcome twists of fate.</p><h2>Fractured Memories</h2><p>A paradoxical past can learn—but as they recycle bits of their past from moment to moment, they can't be relied upon to retain that learning. At one moment, a paradoxical past might be friendly and disposed to help the party, while a minute later they've never seen the party before and are inclined to attack them as trespassers. Later, the paradoxical past might once again recognize the party and wonder why everyone has their weapons drawn.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Truespeech), spirit, strength, throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fortune Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A paradoxical past takes additional damage from attacks whose attack roll was altered by a fortune effect.</p>"},{"n":"Life Loop","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The paradoxical past's turn ends</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The actions the paradoxical past used on this turn are identical to those used on their previous turn and in the same order (Strike, Stride, Strike, for instance), though not necessarily in the same direction or against the same targets</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The paradoxical past gains 10 temporary Hit Points.</p>"},{"n":"Thoughtsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The paradoxical past senses all non-mindless creatures in the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Repeating Curse","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by a paradoxical past's time loop must attempt a DC 25 Will save. On a failure, the creature must spend its last action on its next turn repeating an action it already performed that turn (such as Striding, Striking, or Casting a Spell), though not necessarily in the same direction or against the same targets. If the creature can't repeat itself, it loses this last action.</p>"},{"n":"Paradoxical Precision","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The paradoxical past deals an additional 2d6 precision damage on time loop Strikes benefiting from a fortune effect.</p>"},{"n":"Relive Past","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The paradoxical past repeats the actions it performed the previous turn with as close to the same specifics as possible—moving the same distance and direction or Striking the same targets with the same weapon, if able. Actions the paradoxical past can't repeat are lost with no effect. The paradoxical past rolls twice and takes the better result for any Strike made as part of this activity.</p>"},{"n":"Twin Timelines","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The paradoxical past makes two time loop Strikes against the same target. If the first Strike misses the target, the paradoxical past rolls twice and takes the better result on the second attack roll. This is a fortune effect.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/r9ic2WVVTwqJkSXl":{"lang":["Astral","Chronan","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"flavor":"<p>On rare occasions, a magical conjunction draws a living being into a parallel reality where it should not exist. These unfortunate creatures are forever marked by their transition. Most are soon parted from their physical bodies, becoming immortal astral beings with no place in their new realm's metaphysical cycle of life and death.</p><p>Though returning to their own reality is nearly impossible, some dedicate themselves to research on a way to return, while others actively participate in the affairs of their new home, constantly searching for new opportunities. This involvement might be well-intentioned, with the parallel present offering magic, technology, and wisdom from their old reality. On the other hand, parallel presents can just as easily exploit others in the service of their goals. Regardless of their motivations, a parallel present's knowledge isn't perfect, which can lead to undesirable outcomes and attract the attention of aeons, who believe that parallel presents represent an existential threat to reality.</p><hr><p>Temporal paradoxes are a rare category of spirits that arise when strong-willed individuals are separated from their rightful place in the timeline. Though they retain strong memories and emotional connections to their pasts, it is seldom possible for them to go back to where they came from. Even if they were to manage the daunting task of locating their original reality and timeline, it would fundamentally reject their return. This rejection stems from a combination of their former realities editing them out in the interest of self-stabilization and the influence of their new realities causing their old homes to recognize them as incompatible invaders. Unfortunately for temporal paradoxes, their new realities don't fully accept them either, making them vulnerable to unwelcome twists of fate.</p><h2>Don't You Know Me?</h2><p>Parallel realities can diverge greatly from one another, and two people who are friends or lovers in one reality might be strangers or bitter enemies in another. When an exile from an alternate reality finds that their loved ones no longer love them back, they might refuse to accept this reality, trying to force a relationship where none exists. This imperils not only the people involved but also the host plane, threatening to rupture the thin membrane between realities.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), fortification, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Daze, Desynchronize, Reverberating Pain, Truespeech, Wall of Time), spirit, strength, sturdy, throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fortune Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A parallel present takes additional damage from attacks whose attack roll was altered by a fortune effect.</p>"},{"n":"Thoughtsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The parallel present senses all non-mindless creatures in the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Duplicate Swap","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The parallel present is targeted by a Strike while they have at least one duplicate</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The parallel present and the duplicate swap places. The triggering Strike resolves against the duplicate instead.</p>"},{"n":"Flickering Form","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The parallel present vanishes and reappears 10 feet away in a random direction, gaining 10 temporary Hit Points. Any duplicates of the parallel present also vanish and reappear in random directions. This effect can't cause the parallel present or their duplicates to enter hazardous terrain or an occupied space.</p>"},{"n":"Temporal Anchor","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The parallel present's condensed timeline anchors its targets to the present moment. A creature that is critically hit by the parallel present's timeline strike is Slowed 1.</p>"},{"n":"Duplicate","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The parallel present creates two duplicates in spaces adjacent to themselves. These duplicates have the same AC and saving throw modifiers as the parallel present but disappear if they take any damage. While a duplicate exists, the parallel present can roll twice and take the better result on any Strike or saving throw (unless all duplicates had to roll a saving throw against the effect). If they do, a duplicate of their choice is destroyed after the attack roll or saving throw is resolved; they can't select a duplicate that also had to roll a saving throw. The duplicates mirror the parallel present's motions but do not move from the space where they were created except by Flickering Form. A creature that Seeks can identify a parallel present as real or a duplicate with a successful DC 35 Perception check. The parallel present can have at most six duplicates. Duplicates disappear after 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Convergence Strike","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The parallel present has at least one duplicate</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The parallel present vanishes and reappears adjacent to a creature within 20 feet. Any number of the parallel present's duplicates also vanish and reappear adjacent to the target creature. The parallel present attempts a timeline Strike against the target creature, gaining an additional die of damage for each duplicate adjacent to the target creature.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/etMVkUMHjWeC3ZFw":{"lang":["Aetheran","Common","Fey","Necril","Sakvroth","Spirituan"],"lore":[["Spirit Lore",27]],"flavor":"<p>A phasmogorgia is a massive fungus whose features evoke bloody flesh and hollow graves. It lords over ancient battlefields and forgotten cemeteries, where it digests the spirits of the dead. It ensnares spirits with a haze of ectoplasmic ambrosia, clouding their minds with bliss while it slowly devours them. When beings with souls approach a phasmogorgia, it eagerly hunts them, using its soul-ensnaring hooks to hasten their demise, together with ghost bombs made from partially digested spirits.</p><h2>Too Hungry to Share</h2><p>The concentration of spiritual energy within a phasmogorgia grants the fungus malevolent intelligence. This buffet of souls attracts like-minded individuals, such as demons, daemons, and mortal necromancers. While some phasmogorgias cooperate with other soul hunters, they quickly attack allies who take more than they give.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Lore [Spirit], Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, death, energy resistant (vitality), force, spirit, strength, throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spiritsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The phasmogorgia senses the spirits of creatures, including living creatures, most non-mindless undead, and haunts within the listed range. As spiritsense detects spiritual essence, not physical bodies, it can detect spirits projected by spells such as project image or possessing otherwise soulless objects. It can't detect soulless bodies, constructs, or objects, and like most senses, it doesn't penetrate through solid objects.</p>"},{"n":"Ectoplasmic Ambrosia","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","death","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>40 feet. The phasmogorgia releases a constant haze of mystical spores that enraptures creatures, bringing them one step closer to death. When a creature with a soul starts its turn within is Doomed 1 unless it succeeds at a DC 30 Will save. A creature that succeeds at the save is temporarily immune to Ectoplasmic Ambrosia for 24 hours. Spirits that fail the Will save are also Fascinated by the phasmogorgia for 1 minute and can't willingly move away from it while they remain fascinated. If the phasmogorgia takes a hostile action against a spirit or any of its allies, the spirit can attempt a new saving throw to end the fascination at the start of its next turn. If a doomed creature dies within the aura, its soul is bound to the phasmogorgia, and it can't be resurrected until the phasmogorgia is slain.</p>"},{"n":"Phantasmorgasbord","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["healing","occult"],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The phasmogorgia digests captive spirits to rejuvenate itself, restoring 100 healing Hit Points. All creatures stunned by Soul Snatch and spirits Fascinated by Ectoplasmic Ambrosia take 5d6 force damage (DC 33 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Soul Snatch","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["incapacitation","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The phasmogorgia latches an ethereal hook to the soul of a creature within 30 feet. The target must attempt a DC 33 Will save. Creatures with no soul automatically critically succeed.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target is Stunned 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target's soul is shunted out of its body, stunning it for 1 round. While stunned in this way, the creature takes 2d8 force damage every time the phasmogorgia uses a ghost bomb Strike, as it includes the creature's soul in the projectile.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature is stunned for 3 rounds. At the end of each of its turns, it can attempt a new Will save to end the effect.</p>"},{"n":"Spirit Swarm","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["force","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A swarm of spirits surges out of the phasmogorgia, dealing 5d6 force damage to each creature in a 40-foot cone (DC 33 basic Fortitude save). A creature that fails the save is also Drained 1 and Frightened 1 (Drained 2 and Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/5LfG4HCgUwer4RuB":{"lang":["Common","Sakvroth","Venenan","Speak with animals"],"gear":["Arsenic","Graveroot","Putrid Eggs (4)"],"flavor":"<p>Pilatatries are the bane of farming communities and foodies worldwide. They sneak onto farms in the form of small animals and secretly corrupt the livestock for no purpose other than to see people who eat them become sick. It's quite tricky to detect the handiwork of a pilatatry other than a very specific and unusual aftertaste of the cooked food or the sight of an unknown farmhand walking the fields. But even when caught, they can fool most people with quick talking or barter their way out of trouble with trinkets and gossip.</p><p>Pilatatries tend not to stick around a place for too long, just long enough to spread their poison and ensure it takes effect. However, they do linger longer for chances to ruin festivals and fancy banquets. They love nothing more than watching people spit out food that took months to prepare. Pilatatries often keep ledgers with the dates of such feasts and their travel time, ensuring that they can ruin such events year after year.</p><p>Pilatatries' innate poison is quick to expire, so they often lace livestock products with an inert version and provide the necessary magic to activate it only at the key moment. To augment their toxic capabilities, pilatatries also learn to make stable mimicries of a variety of other toxins out of corrupted animal products. Hags are often well aware of any nearby pilatatries and tend to visit them to trade for poisons. Pilatatries are proud that powerful creatures value their services and occasionally grant themselves the title of hag lieutenant. When in this state, they seek out other gremlins to subjugate and present to the hag as new subjects. Most hags ignore the antics of these lower creatures, but a select few use these armies to deal with rivals or spread corruption in new and unusual ways, like luring adventurers into deadly traps.</p><h2>Purveyors of Royal Foodstuffs</h2><p>A pilatatry can be just as status-minded as any other creature, and the most snobbish among them take pride in corrupting the most prestigious events possible. Why sicken a mere farming village when you can ruin a royal banquet? For pilatatries, infiltrating high society is in some ways easier than sneaking into a commoners' festival. After all, everyone in an isolated village knows one another, while amongst nobility, no one pays attention to the hordes of servants underfoot. For a pilatatry at a grand event, the greatest risk is in running into a rival pilatatry out to steal their glory.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Deception, Nature, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Knock, Pest Form, Speak with Animals), wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Pilatatry Toxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 20 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d4 poison damage and Sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d4 poison damage and sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d4 poison damage and Sickened 2 (1 round).</p>"},{"n":"Strange Taste","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 10 feet of the pilatatry Interacts to consume food or drink, including consumable items</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The pilatatry quickly adds extra ingredients to the mix. The pilatatry attempts a Thievery check against the creature's Reflex DC. On a success, the target is exposed to pilatatry toxin.</p>"},{"n":"Corrupted Products","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The pilatatry spends 1 hour tending to livestock, corrupting their products in secret. For 24 hours, all products produced from those livestock carry a latent form of pilatatry toxin, which the pilatatry can activate using Twisted Snack. Those who consume these modified products can detect an odd, unpleasant aftertaste with a DC 24 Perception check. Additionally, if a pilatatry corrupts the same animals' products each day for 1 week, they can attempt to Craft a common alchemical poison of their choice of 4th level or lower, using those products as the only ingredients.</p>"},{"n":"Twisted Snack","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The pilatatry causes the toxins in corrupted food to suddenly flare up. Each creature within 30 feet that has eaten food laced with Corrupted Products is exposed to pilatatry toxin. The poison gains the virulent trait when applied from this ability.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/IZ1hu6iuIsOfoVvA":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Truespeech"],"gear":["Rapier","Trinket (3)"],"notes":{"speed":"mobility"},"flavor":"<p>Pilfermins are larcenous fey who blend into society to satisfy their insatiable desire to collect other people's belongings. Pilfermins rarely engage in open hostility, instead leveraging their mind-altering magic and manipulating the community. A pilfermin might \"reveal\" themselves to be the long-lost parent of the head of the constabulary just as investigators catch wind of their deeds.</p><p>If another thief draws a pilfermin's attention, the pilfermin might provide a gauntlet of challenges to the thief, culminating in a grand heist. Should the thief succeed, the pilfermin offers a boon, perhaps even the opportunity to be transformed into a pilfermin.</p><h2>Favored Thefts</h2><p>Most pilfermins aren't picky about what they steal, taking every opportunity to pinch or purloin anything that isn't nailed down. Like many fey, however, pilfermins sometimes have particular (and often peculiar) tastes. While one pilfermin might want to steal only jewelry, others might prefer weapons, spellbooks, or stranger objects such as pepper shakers, children's toys, or locks of hair. Still other pilfermins limit their thefts to a particular class of person, stealing only from the rich (who can afford the loss of goods) or the poor (who can't).</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Occultism, Society, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Occult: Daze, Disguise Magic, Illusory Disguise, Invisibility, Mind Reading, Rewrite Memory, Sanctuary, Subconscious Suggestion, Suggestion, Truespeech), wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fair Trade?","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An adjacent creature hits the pilfermin with a Strike while the pilfermin has a trinket in one of their hands</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The pilfermin offers the trinket in exchange for their life. The attacker must accept the trade unless they succeed at a DC 26 Will save. If the attacker accepts the trade, the Strike misses. The attacker can accept the trinket into a free hand to gain a +2 status bonus to AC and saving throws against the pilfermin for as long as they hold the trinket, otherwise, the trinket falls to the ground in the attacker's space.</p><p>Effect: Pilferman Trinket</p>"},{"n":"Knock Loose","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The pilfermin's last action was a successful rapier Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The pilfermin attempts to Disarm an object the creature they hit is holding. They gain a +4 status bonus on the Athletics check. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the pilfermin's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Mine Now","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The pilfermin can Steal or Palm an Object that's closely guarded, such as in a pocket, without taking the –5 penalty, and they can Steal objects of up to light Bulk. They can't steal objects that would be extremely noticeable or time consuming to remove (like worn shoes or armor or actively wielded objects). They can attempt to Steal from a creature in combat or otherwise on guard. When doing so, Stealing requires 2 actions instead of 1, and they take a –5 penalty to the Thievery check.</p>"},{"n":"What Else Do I Got?","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> There is at least one trinket in the pilfermin's bag or unattended in an adjacent square</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The pilfermin takes a trinket into their free hand.</p>"},{"n":"Don't You Remember Me?","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","incapacitation","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The pilfermin spends 10 minutes speaking to a humanoid target and then uses the details they've gleaned to implant false memories of a close relationship. The target must attempt at DC 28 Will save. Regardless of the results of the save, the target is temporarily immune to this ability for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected and knows the pilfermin tried to enchant it.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target is unaffected and thinks the pilfermin was talking to them normally.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is friendly with the pilfermin for 24 hours, or until the pilfermin takes a hostile action against the target or its allies.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the target is helpful to the pilfermin.</p>"},{"n":"Mobility","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the pilfermin Strides to move half their Speed or less, that movement does not trigger reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A pilfermin deals an additional 2d6 precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/uQWWgf2d7AS9cp4t":{"lang":["Sussuran","Talican","Voltan"],"notes":{"speed":"swiftness"},"flavor":"<p>Plasma lashers are born when a metallic core charged with primal energy naturally forms or is implanted within an air elemental. While many elementals are destroyed in this process, a handful remain intact, barely held together by magnetic forces. The elemental loses most of its previous shape and structure, but the remnants of its airy form provide an excellent medium for discharged electricity to run to the ground. The gases also provide protection for the core, acting as an inhospitable and whirling barrier against distant attacks.</p><p>This transformation often causes the elemental to lose their original sense of self. Plasma lashers are typically driven by two major impulses that often come into conflict. The first is a drive to explore far and wide, left over from their pasts, while the second is a desire to seek grounding. Plasma lashers are particularly intrigued by highly conductive metals. While silver is their favorite metal, they also enjoy copper and gold. Many plasma lashers collect a horde of precious metals for this purpose, which can make them targets for greedy adventurers. However, lashers value items based on their metal content rather than their artistic value, so canny traders can make quite a profit by exchanging large metal bars for smaller pieces of fine jewelry.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (light, medium, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, charisma, dexterity, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), metal, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dispersed Form","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Other creatures can occupy the same space as the plasma lasher. However, the gases in this space are not safe to breathe. A creature that ends its turn in the plasma lasher's space and does not hold its breath takes 2d6 poison plus 1d12 electricity damage and becomes Shocked.</p>"},{"n":"Metallic Core","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature occupying the same space as the plasma lasher can attempt to target their metallic core with a melee Strike or spell attack roll. The plasma lasher gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against such attacks, but they lose their physical resistance, instead gaining gains weakness 10 to bludgeoning damage.</p>"},{"n":"Sudden Discharge","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the plasma lasher's reach uses a move action, or ends its turn occupying the same space as the plasma lasher</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A jolt of plasma strikes the target creature, dealing 3d12 electricity damage (DC 32 basic Reflex save). On a failed saving throw, the creature is Shocked for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Conductive Connection","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A plasma arc forms between the plasma lasher's metallic core and a creature within 90 feet, dealing the creature 8d12 electricity damage (DC 32 basic Reflex save). On a failed saving throw, the electrical connection persists and the target creature becomes Shocked for 1 minute. The shocked condition automatically ends if the creature and the plasma lasher are ever more than 90 feet apart.</p>"},{"n":"Pulse Current","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The target creature is Shocked from the plasma lasher's Conductive Connection</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Current flow through the shocked creature, dealing 4d12 electricity damage (DC 32 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Swiftness","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The plasma lasher's movement doesn't trigger reactions.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/1TvvWS4tspelQjW8":{"lang":["Common","Cryan","Pyric","Sussuran"],"flavor":"<p>Plisayashas swim freely across the sky, using thermal energy to hunt and defend themselves from assailants of all sorts. While plisayashas are sociable among their own family groups, they typically attempt to avoid strangers unless someone directly threatens or provokes them. Groups of plisayashas who have been forced to contend with hunters in the past are quicker to respond violently to incursions on their territory. While plisayasha meat isn't particularly appetizing, birds and fish cut out of their stomachs have become a delicacy due to the precise pattern of freezing and cooking the food is subjected to as it passes through their bodies. Though befriending plisayashas can be difficult, they have a soft spot for druids.</p><p>Plisayashas trace their origins to a colony of mundane penguins. Their distant progenitors—under the guardianship of a circle of druids—fled their ancestral home to escape an elemental cataclysm and embarked on a long voyage through the various elemental planes. As they passed through bitter blizzards and endured raging flames, the primal energies imbued in the penguins' bodies mutated, granting them an unusual constellation of powers associated with the conflux of multiple elements.</p><h2>Always Going Home</h2><p>Plisayashas don't have a land of their own, either on the elemental planes or elsewhere; areas of pure ice are too cold and areas of unbound flame are too hot. The habitats they prefer, zones of mixed fire and ice, are almost paradoxically rare. Plisayashas are a far-flung species, having flown and swam through countless environments on their quest for the perfect home.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, burning barbs, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (cold, fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, ice, sensory (low-light vision), throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Anomalous Combustion","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>If a plisayasha takes fire damage after applying their resistance to fire, they lose their fire resistance and take 1d6 persistent fire damage. After the persistent damage ends, they can regain their resistance to fire by spending 10 minutes submerged in cold water.</p>"},{"n":"Flight or Swim Response","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The plisayasha takes damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The plisayasha attempts to escape. They Fly or Swim, up to half their Speed. This movement doesn't trigger reactions based on movement.</p>"},{"n":"Frigid Breath","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Inhaling an immense amount of air, the plisayasha inflates their belly enormously and then rapidly expels a powerful gust of glacial air in a 20-foot cone. Creatures within the cone take 4d8 cold damage and must attempt a DC 21 Fortitude save. The plisayasha can't use Frigid Breath again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is pushed 5 feet back from the plisayasha.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is pushed 10 feet back from the plisayasha. If Frigid Breath's cold damage ended the persistent fire damage from Thermic Compound, the creature is also Paralyzed for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Thermic Compound","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The plisayasha spits a scorching, viscous glob from their mouth, dealing 1d6 persistent fire damage in a 5-foot burst within 30 feet (DC 21 Reflex save negates). While a creature is taking this persistent fire damage, it also gains weakness 2 to cold. If the creature takes any cold damage while under the effects of Thermic Compound, the persistent fire damage from Thermic Compound immediately ends, after applying the cold weakness once. Unlike many other fire effects, Thermic Compound can be used underwater. The plisayasha can't use Thermic Compound again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/iNQzXZxN215iaoQC":{"lang":["Fey","Muan"],"notes":{"saves":"–2 status to all saves vs. air effects","speed":"forest passage, lightweight"},"flavor":"<p>Pollen brawlers are animated elemental spirits composed of millions of individual particles of pollen who help propagate various species of plants within their home regions. Stealthy and cautious, pollen brawlers are known for wrapping themselves around trees or concealing themselves within large bushes, only to erupt without warning when creatures antagonistic to them pass too close to their hiding place.</p><p>When left to their own devices, pollen brawlers wander the land peacefully, exchanging pollen with plants as they go. Druids often call upon pollen brawlers to help defend sacred groves or other significant locations from various threats. Conservationists and disaster recovery specialists also prize pollen brawlers for their ability to help rekindle ecosystems in the aftermath of a natural disaster. However, those who call upon a pollen brawler to rekindle local plants must take care, since pollen brawlers don't distinguish between invasive species and native ones. In fact, when invasive species proliferate at a seemingly impossible rate or foliage quickly overcomes an abandoned street, a pollen brawler might be responsible.</p><p>Pollen brawlers recognize that other creatures consume plants as a part of the natural cycle, and they don't interfere with anyone seeking to harvest plants from their groves. However, they react violently to attempts to eradicate even a single one of the species they propagate, a tendency that can lead to conflict with loggers seeking land development, individuals attempting to reclaim their lands from an invasion of poison oak, or farmers attempting to clear out a new field.</p><h2>Pollen Allergies</h2><p>Some people are severely allergic to pollen—a condition that can be both a detriment and a boon when battling pollen brawlers. Allergic creatures must attempt a Fortitude save against hay fever whenever they start their turn within 30 feet of a pollen brawler. While a tendency to go into a sneezing fit in the presence of a pollen brawler makes it difficult to adequately defend oneself, it's also a counter to the pollen brawler's skill at staying undetected.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision, scent), tangle shield, throwing, wild, winged, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"–2 Status to All Saves Vs. Air Effects","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Pollen Eruption","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The pollen brawler is critically hit by a Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A puff of pollen erupts from the pollen brawler's body. All creatures within 10 feet of the pollen brawler is exposed to hay fever, and must attempt a Fortitude save against the poison.</p>"},{"n":"Hay Fever","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 22 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Sickened 1</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> sickened 1 and Dazzled for 1 round</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> Sickened 2 and dazzled for 1 round</p>"},{"n":"Forest Passage","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The pollen brawler ignores any difficult terrain caused by plants, such as bushes, vines, and undergrowth. Even plants manipulated by magic don't impede their progress.</p>"},{"n":"Among the Leaves","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>On the first round of combat, If the pollen brawler rolls Stealth for initiative while within vegetation, creatures that haven't acted yet are Off-Guard to the pollen brawler.</p>"},{"n":"Lightweight","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>In still air or mildly windy conditions, the pollen brawler can fly at a Speed of 10 feet. However, stronger winds can easily push the pollen brawler around. In stronger winds, the pollen brawler can move up to the wind's speed, but they can only fly in the wind's direction and they take a –2 circumstance penalty to attack rolls for as long as they remain off the ground. A pollen brawler can spend 1 action to stabilize themself and negate this penalty until the end of their turn.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/cVmnNTnajHN8Ihz5":{"lang":["Common","Truespeech, communicates through writing only"],"lore":[["Postal Lore",26]],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>It is said that neither rain nor snow nor hail can keep the mail from being delivered. Sometimes, it turns out death itself can't stop the post.</p><p>Postgeists are formed when the employees of a postal service or other devoted messengers pass away in a time of great need. If the departed deliverers were sufficiently tenacious in their work, a part of their souls might fuse with psychic fragments of their raw determination, coalescing into a sentient being.</p><p>Postgeists see to the complete work of their departed forebears, using their telekinetic abilities to sort, file, and deliver letters with terrifying efficiency. The spirits never deviate from their appointed duties. Interlopers are booted aside with extreme prejudice, and a creature that dares flee from a delivery is chased to the ends of the world.</p><h2>Address Unknown</h2><p>Each postgeist has their own route, featuring favored postboxes, courier satchels, secret drop sites, and other mail repositories that they check unfailingly. Someone who knows a postgeist's route can use it to track down otherwise unfindable people. Simply address a letter to the quarry, wait for a postgeist to show up, and follow the postgeist—provided you can keep up with their unresting, all-weather travel pace.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Lore [Postal], Occultism, Society, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> constitution, death, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, sonic, void), force, intelligence, mirrored, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Environmental Endurance, Knock, Telekinetic Hand, Telekinetic Maneuver, Teleport, Truespeech), spirit, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"I Know Where You Live","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["detection","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A postgeist carrying the written name of a location always knows the fastest traversable path to that location. If they carry the written name of a creature that the writer was familiar with, they know the direct path to the location where the creature last made their daily preparations. If the location or creature is under any effect that would block detection, the postgeist attempts a counteract check against that effect (counteract modifier +22); if the counteract check fails, the postgeist can't attempt to counteract that same effect again.</p>"},{"n":"Swirling Letters","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet, 3d8 slashing, DC 27 basic Reflex. The postgeist can suppress or activate this aura as a single action with the concentrate trait.</p>"},{"n":"Return to Sender","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["concentrate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature targets the postgeist with an auditory or linguistic effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The postgeist attempts a counteract check against the effect (counteract modifier +22). On a success, the magical words or sounds from the effect are transcribed in a letter. The next time the postgeist targets that creature with a letter Strike, the letter flies to that creature and opens before it, targeting it with the counteracted effect. The effect uses the attack roll bonuses, DCs, and other statistics from the original source, not the postgeist's, and neither applies nor counts toward any creature's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Telekinetic Shove","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The postgeist's last action was a successful boot Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The postgeist attempts to Shove the creature, using their spell attack modifier instead of Athletics. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the postgeist's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Paper Weight","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The postgeist's last action was a successful letter Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The postgeist hampers the movements of the creature they hit with their Strike. The creature must succeed on a DC 30 Reflex save or become Off-Guard until the end of the postgeist's next turn and Slowed 1 (Slowed 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Swift Delivery","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The postgeist Flies up to their Speed. Each creature that is within 10 feet of the postgeist at any point during this movement takes 6d10 slashing damage (DC 30 basic Reflex save). The postgeist can't use Swift Delivery again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/1t0jhDC7zOtNrGpg":{"lang":["Astral","Chronan","Common","Telepathy 120 feet, Truespeech"],"flavor":"<p>When the lives of heroes, villains, tricksters, or other figures with great destinies are cut short under extraordinary circumstances, the unrealized potential can manifest as an echo of that lost future on the Astral Plane known as a pruned future. Whether cast into this state by a norn's shears, divine intervention, or the meddling of powerful aberrations, these beings are tormented by the dissonance between their failed reality and the grand potential they held in life. The last regrets of countless souls are drawn to their lamentations, empowering the pruned fate and inspiring them not to abandon the dreams of their old lives and the glory that could have been.</p><p>A pruned future that reaches the mortal realms and attempts to enact their destiny often draws the attention of entities that protect the integrity of time.</p><hr><p>Temporal paradoxes are a rare category of spirits that arise when strong-willed individuals are separated from their rightful place in the timeline. Though they retain strong memories and emotional connections to their pasts, it is seldom possible for them to go back to where they came from. Even if they were to manage the daunting task of locating their original reality and timeline, it would fundamentally reject their return. This rejection stems from a combination of their former realities editing them out in the interest of self-stabilization and the influence of their new realities causing their old homes to recognize them as incompatible invaders. Unfortunately for temporal paradoxes, their new realities don't fully accept them either, making them vulnerable to unwelcome twists of fate.</p><h2>Temporal Dissonance</h2><p>Just as a pruned future can sort through multiple futures and choose their favorite, they are simultaneously aware and unaware of their pruned state. They both believe in the grand accomplishment of their heroic destiny and are aware that the same destiny lies in ruins, their great deeds undone. From moment to moment, the pruned future chooses either to exemplify the swaggering demigod or the tragic spirit, often as the mood strikes them.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,250/2,800/5,150 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Truespeech), spiked, spirit, strength, throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Fortune Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A pruned future takes additional damage from attacks whose attack roll was altered by a fortune effect.</p>"},{"n":"Thoughtsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The pruned future senses all non-mindless creatures in the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Borrow Future","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":["occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>At the beginning of their turn, a pruned future can gain 10 temporary Hit Points. The pruned future can't use this action if they are currently under any effects with the misfortune trait.</p>"},{"n":"Split Future","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The pruned future branches their timeline temporarily and then decides between the two possible futures. The pruned future creates a temporal copy of themselves. Both they and their copy Stride up to their speed and roll a Strike against a different target. The pruned future then chooses which version of themselves is the true one. The true version's Strike occurs normally, while the copy disappears instead of harming their target. The copy has identical statistics to the original at the time the copying occurs, and any damage or conditions that the copy incurs do not affect the original.</p>"},{"n":"Discern Stratagem","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The pruned future peers into a creature's future to prepare a killing blow. The pruned future attempts an Occultism vs. Will DC check against the Will DC of a target they can sense, then makes a Strike against that target, automatically succeeding on any flat check due to the Concealed or hidden conditions. If the Occultism check was a success, the pruned future rolls twice and takes the better result on the Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Fatal Fortune","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A pruned future's melee and ranged Strikes gain the fatal d12 trait when benefiting from a fortune effect.</p>"},{"n":"Curse of Unraveled Fate","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["curse","misfortune","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature struck by the pruned future's pruning shears must attempt a DC 34 Fortitude save as the frayed ends of the pruned future's fate tug on those of the creature.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1 round, Doomed 1, and must roll twice and take the worse result on the next check it makes.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1 minute, Doomed 2, and must roll twice and take the worse result on every check it makes for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Pjr7QbGS4I9yUxZX":{"lang":["Fey","Thalassic"],"flavor":"<p>Puddle hoppers enjoy resting with their disproportionately long limbs hidden under the water's surface, pretending to be nothing more than typical giant frogs. Then, they wait for someone to pass by before emerging from the puddle to reveal their true height. Often, the shock causes travelers to flinch and flee, much to the delight of the puddle hopper. If the travelers drop something interesting while they run, so much the better.</p><p>Puddle hoppers are avid collectors, and they have no qualms about nabbing anything they find that strikes their interest. They usually keep to unattended objects left out in the open far from civilization. In addition to gathering the dropped belongings of the people they scare, puddle hoppers collect other objects that have been left behind in remote areas. In contrast to their normal behavior, they can become particularly bold in times of heavy rain as large puddles fill settlements; they use this opportunity to seek out even more intriguing things that other people own. Due to their aquatic nature, the majority of their thefts are things people left outside their homes, with wind chimes and birdhouses being favorites. However, a few unfortunate incidents have occurred in homes where puddle hoppers accidentally mistake full baths for some sort of small ponds.</p><p>They particularly enjoy looting waterlogged homes; families returning after evacuating from a flood might find a seemingly random collection of their belongings gone.</p><h2>Equivalent Exchange</h2><p>Though not particularly brilliant, puddle hoppers aren't unreasonable and are sometimes willing to trade their pilfered goods for items they regard to be of equal or greater value. What constitutes a fair trade, however, varies from one hopper to another. Whereas one puddle hopper might trade a precious gemstone for a bit of interestingly shaped crystal, another might regard a relatively worthless bauble as a priceless treasure and demand nothing less than a king's ransom for it!</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, strength, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Hop","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature successfully deals damage to the puddle hopper</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The puddle hopper Leaps up to 15 feet. They can instead use Water Hop if they are within range of a body of water that is large enough.</p>"},{"n":"Unnerving Leap","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":["concentrate","emotion","fear","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The puddle hopper rolls initiative while Hiding in water</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The puddle hopper jumps out of the water, stretching their limbs wide in an attempt to frighten nearby creatures. The puddle hopper Leaps and attempts to Demoralize all creatures within 10 feet of where they land. If they critically succeed to Demoralize a creature, that creature drops whatever it was holding.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Puddle Reach","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a puddle hopper is in contact with a body of water, they can target other creatures within 30 feet that are in the same body of water with their fist Strike. They can also attempt to Palm an Object off of them.</p>"},{"n":"Water Hop","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move","primal","teleportation","water"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The puddle hopper Leaps into a body of water within range and teleports to another body of water within 60 feet. This body of water must be large enough for the puddle hopper to occupy without Squeezing.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/AHBLTimP4FbtX7N2":{"lang":["Khaotan","Melodian"],"flavor":"<p>A quantum hummer is a colorful, musical bird whose wings beat fast enough to distort spatial probability. This causes the bird to appear in multiple places simultaneously as a swarm consisting only of itself. The creature uses this ability to evade predators and communicate with members of its kind through song. Direct, precise observation of a quantum hummer collapses its spatial probability, thwarting the bird's superposition and leaving it vulnerable.</p><p>Though their heads have dark spots that mimic eyes, quantum hummers are blind and rely on their swarming songs and keen hearing to communicate and navigate their environment. The exchange of songs between quantum hummers builds the foundation of their social structure, and they work diligently to perfect a song to elevate their status and attract mates.</p><p>Quantum hummers are drawn to pleasing sounds and melodies and enjoy collecting musical items like small wind chimes and bells and storing them in dangling nests to accompany their songs. Quality musical performances can easily draw the attention of a quantum hummer, who might follow the musician on their journey or attempt to trade an item from their collection for the musician's instrument.</p><h2>Quantum Tunneling</h2><p>Sages speculate that, since the act of observing a quantum hummer can change its state from a swarm to an individual, an unobserved quantum hummer is both a swarm and an individual. To test this theory, one enterprising scholar tried putting a quantum hummer in a sealed birdhouse to see whether it would eat enough birdseed for a single bird or an entire swarm. When they opened the house, they found a surprising result: no birdseed, no bird, and a hole to another dimension pecked through the back of the structure.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), music, space, space shield, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Echolocation 120 Feet","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A quantum hummer can use their hearing as a precise sense at the listed range.</p>"},{"n":"Superposition","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The quantum hummer is not Immobilized, and there are at least three unoccupied squares adjacent to the quantum hummer</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The quantum hummer moves rapidly to distort space and appear in multiple adjacent spaces, becoming Hidden. While they distort their position in this way, they become a Large swarm, with immunity to the Grabbed, Prone, and Restrained conditions as well as weakness 5 to area damage and splash damage. The bird remains in this state until they are immobilized or Unconscious, or until their precise location is observed, typically through an effect that negates invisibility or a critically successful Perception check to Seek them. A quantum hummer can Dismiss Superposition.</p>"},{"n":"Humming Resonance","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","auditory"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The quantum hummer hears a Swarming Song from another quantum hummer within 40 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The quantum hummer enters a Superposition. If the quantum hummers is already in a Superposition, they instead perform a Swarming Song.</p>"},{"n":"Swarming Song","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The quantum hummer exists in a Superposition</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Each enemy in the swarm's space takes 3d6 sonic damage (DC 24 basic Fortitude save). A creature that fails its save against Swarming Song becomes Deafened for 1 round. In addition to the typical DC 5 Flat to perform actions with the auditory trait while deafened, creatures that are deafened from Swarming Song must succeed at DC 5 Flat to perform concentrate actions.</p>"},{"n":"Quantum Body","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The quantum nature of the quantum hummer's body attack allows them to Strike from multiple places at once. If the quantum hummer is in a place where teleportation effects are suppressed, their reach decreases to 0 feet, the damage of their quantum beak is reduced to 2d6, the damage of their quantum wing is reduced to 2d4, and their Strikes lose the teleportation trait.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/bfIBIrft88Ghp2w0":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Draconic","Empyrean","Melodian","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>Quietuses arise from the souls of those who delight in oppressing art and speech, especially when it criticizes them or opposes their beliefs and goals. Their tyranny in life manifests in their new demonic forms as a zone that silences everyone else nearby. Though quietuses can technically communicate telepathically, they seldom choose to, as they enjoy forcing others to endure the grating sound of their voices. Whatever the topic of conversation, quietuses are utterly convinced that their perspective is correct, and they use a combination of threats, lies, magic, and sheer stubbornness always to have the last word.</p><p>When a quietus arrives in a settlement, it seeks out centers of expressive speech and performance, such as town halls, temples, amphitheaters, libraries, and museums, and utterly destroys them. While the demon's schemes often begin with cultural centers, they indiscriminately wreck buildings, commit murder, and cause havoc until little is left but silent rubble.</p><h2>Noise Demons</h2><p>On rare occasions, quietuses can arise from the souls of jealous and controlling musicians. Instead of casting a field of silence, these demons overpower all other sounds with their music, creating the same effect. Such quietuses are sometimes called noise demons, and they believe their music to be above all other forms of art.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Intimidation, Religion, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Command, Dispel Magic, Dissonance, Noise Blast, Silence, Steal the Show, Suggestion, Truespeech), unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Hush","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature affected by the quietus's silence spell successfully Strikes the quietus</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The quietus puts a finger to their lips and hisses loudly, assaulting the mind of whoever dared disturb their peace. The creature takes 4d6 mental damage (DC 32 basic Will save).</p>"},{"n":"Performance Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A quietus can't bear to be in the presence of people sharing mirth through music, dance, or other forms of art. When a creature Performs for an ally who can hear them and their Performance check is higher than quietus's Will DC, the quietus takes 3d6 mental damage.</p>"},{"n":"Steal Voices","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The quietus attempts to steal the voices of nearby creatures. All creatures within 30 feet take 5d8 mental damage and must attempt a DC 32 Will save, after which they are temporarily immune to Steal Voices for 1 hour.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage. The creature can't speak or use effects with the concentrate trait for one round, during which time the quietus can speak using the creature's voice.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, except the creature can't speak or use effects with the concentrate trait for 3 rounds. During this time, the quietus can cast spells using the creature as the origin point.</p>"},{"n":"Louden Spell","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","divine","spellshape"],"f":"","d":"<p>The three mouths of the quietus whine in discordant harmony, making sure they are heard above all others. If the next action the quietus uses is to Cast a Spell with the auditory or sonic trait, creatures that fail their saving throw against the spell's effects also become Deafened until the start of the quietus's next turn. While deafened, they can still hear the quietus's voice.</p>"},{"n":"Hypocritical Silencing","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A quietus's auditory abilities, speech, and spellcasting are unaffected by their own Silence spell, including when speaking using another's voice by means of Steal Voices.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/oGEzTB4EAiHzXH3O":{"lang":["Luxan","Petran"],"notes":{"per":"radiant resonance (precise)","speed":"earth glide"},"flavor":"<p>Prismites are a class of earth elementals composed partially or entirely of crystals and precious gemstones, making them highly sought after by the greedy, the curious, and the scholarly alike. Most prismites have enough experience with selfish treasure hunters to view anyone they come across with suspicion. However, some can be convinced to work for wealthy individuals in exchange for a steady diet of delicious crystals and an opportunity to pummel anyone who would dare assault their homes.</p><p>Refulgent prismites are a particularly brilliant variety of prismites, composed of clear or mostly transparent crystals like quartz, diamond, or sapphire. The primal energies of these powerful elementals manifest as light deep within their cores, which these elder elementals weaponize, refracting through their crystalline bodies to dazzle and burn those that would threaten them.</p><p>Prismites don't often interact with other elementals and keep amongst their own kind. More typical elementals often find prismites pretentious, insufferable, and overly concerned with appearances.</p><h2>Flawed Stone</h2><p>The precious stone composing a refulgent prismite is nowhere near as valuable as the prismite claims it is: the shards of a destroyed prismite reveal themselves to be flawed, worth only the value of the monster parts for a creature of their level. Letting this fact be known would dissuade refulgent prismite hunters, perhaps saving the prismites' lives, but refulgent prismites couldn't bear the disgrace of having their flawed nature known.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Intimidation, Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, earth, earth armor, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, light, strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Radiant Resonance","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The refulgent prismite's crystalline form is attuned to the light that they emit, allowing them to detect creatures that they have bathed in bright light as a precise sense. This ability typically allows them to see in the area of their radiant gleam aura, but its radius can be temporarily extended by the refulgent prismite's other abilities. They lack typical vision, as they can't detect light from other sources.</p>"},{"n":"Refract","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fire","light","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The refulgent prismite is within the light radius of another refulgent prismite's Brilliance</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The refulgent prismite enhances their ally's brightness. The radius of the fiery emanation increases by 10 feet and the radius of the bright light increases by 100 feet. If this would cause the light radius to include a new refulgent prismite, that prismite can then Refract.</p>"},{"n":"Radiant Gleam","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","light","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet. A refulgent prismite constantly sheds intense light, making it difficult to look directly at them. Creatures that end their turn within the aura must attempt a DC 27 Fortitude save. On a successful save, the creature is temporarily immune to radiant gleam for 1 minute.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Brilliance","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fire","incapacitation","light","manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The refulgent prismite concentrates their light so severely that it sears flesh, dealing 8d6 fire damage to all creatures in a 30-foot emanation (DC 29 Fortitude save). After using Brilliance, the refulgent prismite shine emits bright light in a 100-foot radius until the end of their next turn. It can't use Brilliance again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to being Blinded or Dazzled by a refulgent prismite for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is dazzled for 1 round and takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is blinded for 1 round and takes full damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is blinded for 1 minute and takes double damage.</p>"},{"n":"Earth Glide","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A refulgent prismite can Burrow through earthen matter, including rock. When they do so, they move at their full burrow Speed, leaving no tunnels or signs of their passing.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/YgDGRRkFDpEwDqbh":{"lang":["Common","Requian"],"flavor":"<p>Left behind by psychopomps as grisly warnings to pry no further, rictus masks are temporary beings who decay over time unless called upon to undo further rebels against death or guard a graveyard that has caught the interest of unsavory individuals.</p><p>Explorers who intrude upon lands where the dead slumber would do well to note any crumbling rooms in otherwise sound structures, and carefully avoid any suspicious areas of random decay hidden among otherwise healthy vegetation.</p><p>Rictus masks look like grimacing earthen death masks with closed eyes. The features of their face transform into a morbid facsimile of those of their most recent victim. When disturbed, a rictus mask opens their eyelids, revealing unsettlingly realistic eyeballs as dust rises and churns around them. Rictus masks relish opportunities to destroy undead. While they don't particularly enjoy killing living creatures, they don't hesitate to do so when living creatures threaten them or refuse to obey their orders to stay away from locations they protect.</p><h2>Woe, Destruction, Ruin, and Decay</h2><p>While the activities of some psychopomps are reasonably well-documented, reliable information concerning rictus masks is scarce, Those who escape a rictus mask's vengeance rarely speak of what they saw. Even fewer return to face the rictus mask again. No one wants to be confronted by their own dead face.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Intimidation, Nature, Occultism, Religion, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, death, dexterity, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (vitality, void), force, life, lifeward armor, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Boulder Crush, Detect Magic, Earthbind, Fear, Lock, Vision of Death, Vital Beacon, Vitality Lash, Void Warp), throwing, time, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Out, Out Brief Candle","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["divine","earth"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature damages the rictus mask while the rictus mask is within 10 feet of a statue they created with their Echoes of the Damned ability</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The rictus mask causes one of their statues from Echoes of the Damned within 10 feet to explode, reducing the amount of damage the rictus mask takes by 10. The remnants of the statue gather on the rictus mask, reshaping their facial features to match the creature who triggered this reaction. The creature depicted on the mask doesn't reduce the value of their frightened condition automatically at the end of their turns until the mask takes on another person's appearance or 1 minute passes, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Echoes of the Damned","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","earth"],"f":"","d":"<p>The rictus mask creates two Medium statues within 10 feet. Each of these statues is a dusty facsimile of a creature that has previously taken damage from the rictus mask. The statues are inanimate objects with object immunities, 10 Hit Points, and 20 AC; for all other defensive statistics, they use the rictus mask's bonuses. If the rictus mask uses Echoes of the Damned again, any statues they previously created turn to dust.</p>"},{"n":"Tomorrow, And Tomorrow, And Tomorrow","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The rictus mask accelerates time in a 10-foot burst within 120 feet until the end of their next turn. Within this area, flesh falters, plants shrivel, and even stone begins to crumble. Any creature that enters or begins its turn in the area of accelerated time takes 8d6 force damage (DC 25 basic Fortitude save) and is then temporarily immune to this ability for 1 day. The rictus mask can Sustain the effect up to twice on future turns to extend its duration; each time they do, the radius increases by 10 feet.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/UZ5kdaiW1kp78USz":{"lang":["Common","Spirituan","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"flavor":"<p>Folktales tell of unruly children and argumentative spouses disappearing into the night, never to be seen again. Some of these tales stem from the deeds of rommors—friendly spirits who help people trapped in disparaging and neglectful situations, whisking them away into their magical cloak of a sea of stars and traversing the world to find them a new, loving home.</p><p>Unfortunately, many well-intended adventurers have sought to \"rescue\" those taken by a rommor. When confronted by misguided individuals, a rommor usually attempts to flee and tries only to use violence when absolutely necessary, such as when rescuing someone or protecting one of their wards from harm.</p><h2>Witness Protection</h2><p>Sworn to magical secrecy, people once saved by a rommor can't reveal the existence of their supernatural saviors, even when they suspect that a rommor is behind some fresh disappearance. As adventurers are summoned to hunt for a lost person, all these people can do is hint that perhaps the tragic circumstances of the missing folks' lives merit as much investigation as the clues leading toward their discovery.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Diplomacy, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, darkness, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality, void), force, fortification, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Arcane: Calm, Darkness, Fly, Soothe, Spirit Blast, Telekinetic Hand, Telekinetic Projectile, Teleport, Translocate, Truespeech), spirit, sturdy, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Umbra Shield","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","darkness","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the rommor's void aura is targeted by an attack</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The rommor unleashes shadowy wisps to protect the targeted creature, granting a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against the triggering attack. If the attack misses, the targeted creature can immediately teleport to any unoccupied space within the void aura.</p><p>Effect: Umbra Shield</p>"},{"n":"Umbral Shroud","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["arcane","aura","darkness"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet. Shadows emanates from the rommor, as a 7th-rank Darkness spell. Creatures of the rommor's choice gain greater darkvision and a +1 status bonus to AC while they are within the aura.</p>"},{"n":"Spirit Nova","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","spirit"],"f":"","d":"<p>The rommor unleashes a wave of spiritual energy, dealing 14d6 spirit damage to creatures in a 20-foot emanation (DC 33 basic Fortitude save). The rommor can exclude any number of creatures in the area from the blast. Creatures that fail the save are knocked 5 feet away from the rommor (or 10 feet on a critical failure). The rommor can't use Spirit Nova again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Cloak of Stars","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","extradimensional","incapacitation","manipulate","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The rommor's cloak is not already holding 8 creatures</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The rommor shrouds an adjacent creature in their cloak, transporting the creature into an extradimensional sea of stars. An unwilling creature can attempt a DC 33 Will save, after which it becomes temporarily immune to Cloak of Stars for 10 minutes. A willing creature receives the results of a failure, except the rommor can allow the creature to remain in the extradimensional space until the creature wants to leave.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature blinks into the extradimensional space momentarily before reappearing in an unoccupied space of the rommor's choice within the void aura.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is sent into the extradimensional space until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature remains in the extradimensional space for up to 1 minute. The creature can attempt a new Will save to exit the cloak at the end of each of its turns. Each held creature appears as a constellation on the cloak. A rommor can eject a held creature as a free action. If the rommor is slain, all held creatures reappear in the nearest unoccupied space.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/HXxyZNQEUZEZKaho":{"lang":["Necril","Speak with wasps"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Rothives arise when flesh-eating wasps or bees infest a corpse suffused with void energy. Clerics of insect-related deities or wicked druids sometimes encourage the creation of rothives by collecting swarming insects and placing their queen in the mouth of a fresh corpse, then leaving it to slowly reanimate.</p><p>Rothives communicate through the hum of their insects, frequently singing about \"the Thrum\" and asserting their ability to carry out its commands. What the will of the Thrum entails remains a mystery, as most often the observable activities of rothives tend to be relegated to ambushing travelers in woodland areas or commanding swarms of wasps to descend on unsuspecting communities.</p><p>In the rare communities that deal with rothives regularly, beekeepers are respected for their ability to use their bee smokers in defense of their homes. There are stories of a beekeeper who sedated a rothive with smoke and spoke to them in the tongue of the dead, brokering a peaceful trading arrangement with the creature's Mother Hive. What is not often spoken of is the price the community has to pay to stay at peace or what terrible gifts the rothives bestow on them in return.</p><h2>Bees of a Feather</h2><p>Rothives are fascinated by mellifican hives and jealous of the psychic honey wasps' intelligence. Rothives that consume enough mellifican hives can eventually manifest new powers, granting them additional sonic damage or psychic abilities and increasing their overall power accordingly. These mellifican devourers are typically level 5 or 6, though rumors abound of ancient rothives who have grown to an even greater strength and intellect this way. These rothive masterminds hide in the shadows, manipulating other rothives for their own benefit.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, constitution, death, dexterity, energy resistant (void), mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, unholy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Speak with Wasps","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A rothive can communicate with wasps and use Diplomacy to Make an Impression on wasps and Request things from wasps.</p>"},{"n":"Consume Swarm","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["healing","olfactory","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> a rothive is reduced to 0 Hit Points and a wasp swarm is within 40 feet</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> the rothive releases a pheromone that calls the wasp swarm to reinforce their crumbling body. The wasp swarm uses its reaction to fly into the rothive, destroying itself and transferring all its remaining Hit Points to the rothive. Wasp swarms whose Intelligence modifier is greater than –5 can attempt a DC 18 Will save to resist the effect; when used against such swarms, the effect gains the incapacitation trait.</p>"},{"n":"Disorienting Drone","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["auditory","aura","emotion","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>10 feet. The hum of wings from within the rothive bends the minds of those who come too close. All creatures within the aura must attempt a DC 18 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is temporarily immune to Disorienting Drone for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Stupefied 1 until the end of its next turn.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Stupefied 2 until the end of its next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Smokesleep","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["air","olfactory"],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a rothive begins their turn or enters a space where there is enough smoke to grant the Concealed condition, the rothive must attempt a DC 25 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The rothive is Stunned 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The rothive is Stunned 2.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The rothive is Stunned 3 and becomes indifferent to all other creatures for 1 minute. During this time, the rothive's attitude can worsen toward a creature only if that creature takes a hostile action against them.</p>"},{"n":"Corpse Honey","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 18 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Sickened 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> sickened 2 and the creature can't reduce its sickened value below 1 (1 round).</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/rpHYBm2ldBqm8P4M":{"lang":["Arboreal","Fey","Can't speak any language"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. primal spells","per":"detect magic (primal only)"},"flavor":"<p>When an inattentive druid or other primal magic user attempts to awaken a dying tree that is infested with harmful insects and fungi, they can accidentally create a vengeful rotwood. Wracked with pain and filled with fury toward the one who bestowed them with consciousness, a rotwood's first act is typically to attempt to slay their creator. If the rotwood survives this battle, they then turn their attention to others flush with primal magic, such as fey and other plant creatures.</p><p>While primal magic users are their favorite targets, they don't hesitate to kill anyone else who would stand in the way of their destructive rampages. Rotwoods that eliminate all other primal magic sources in the nearby area continue to wander the woods in search of new targets. When they come across corrupted plants, such as husks, or other rotwoods, they might form alliances of convenience. However, these alliances are almost inevitably doomed to end in violence once the plants' mutual adversaries are no more.</p><h2>Dark Dreams</h2><p>When a rotwood has eliminated all their enemies—druids disposed of, fey banished, and villages abandoned and overgrown with weeds—they sink into a dreaming half-slumber in which they relive their acts of vengeance. Tainted by their dark dreams, forests grow twisted and rotten around them. The rotwood only awakens when new explorers dare to enter their woods.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, death, elongating, energy resistant (vitality, void), sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Detect Magic, Dispel Magic), spiked, strength, sturdy, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rotten Influence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","death","primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet. A creature who enters or begins its turn in the aura takes 1d8 void damage (DC 21 basic Fortitude save). Plant creatures take a –2 circumstance penalty to this saving throw. All non-creature plants in the area that are Small or smaller and are not magical wither, eliminating any difficult terrain, cover, or concealment they provided.</p>"},{"n":"Fungal Burst","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 15 feet hits the rotwood with an unarmed or melee weapon Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Fungi embedded in the rotwood's bark discharge a burst of spores toward the creature. Each creature in a 15-foot cone must attempt a DC 22 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Dazzled for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Blinded for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Withering Touch","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["primal","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The rotwood's last action was a successful rotting bough Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The rotwood channels corruption into the target of their Strike, dealing 3d8 void damage (DC 22 basic Fortitude save).</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is Enfeebled 1 for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage and is Enfeebled 2 for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Spew Insects","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The rotwood vomits forth a great swarm of termites and mosquitoes that bite and stinging all in their wake, dealing 7d6 piercing damage in a 30-foot cone (DC 25 basic Reflex save). Any creature that fails its save is also Sickened 1. The rotwood can't use Spew Insects again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Throw Rock","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>The monster picks up a rock within reach or retrieves a stowed rock and throws it, making a ranged Strike.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/7x4kMhvRpnl65hKL":{"lang":["Necril","Plus any one language they knew when they were alive"],"gear":["Sarcophagus Lid"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","ac":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Necromancers seeking resilient mummy guardians developed a ritual for creating mummies capable of using their own sarcophagi for defense, either as a shield to protect the mummy from damage or as an independent minion capable of fighting alongside the mummy. Either way, by fighting in concert with their sarcophagus, a sealed mummy is much more dangerous than a mere mummy guardian.</p><p>While many of the unfortunate individuals who become mummies are unwilling and arise with hatred for all living things, sealed mummies retain their memories and loyalties to those they once served. These mummies tend to be people who chose undeath in order to guard someone or something that inspired their eternal devotion, and they follow their liege or their ward beyond the grave.</p><h2>Unlidded</h2><p>A sealed mummy prizes their sarcophagus lid, which often bears a likeness of themselves as they were in life, above all else. When their sarcophagus lid is broken, a sealed mummy sometimes go berserk with rage, abandoning all other goals beyond seeking revenge on those who defaced their precious lid.</p>"},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/SkFYQTSgoKL6b0BM":{"lang":["Aetheran","Astral","Common","Diabolic","Draconic","Empyrean","Utopian"],"lore":[["Axis Lore",13]],"flavor":"<p>Scupas resemble mechanical peacocks with multi-colored tails. They dedicate their lives to bringing order and beauty to places that were once chaotic and destroyed, and they pride themselves on their ability to organize, create, and restore. Each scupa has different preferences for what they prefer to repair, ranging from buildings to everyday tools to artwork, and they can rearrange their tail feathers into different patterns.</p><p>When not occupied with work, scupas enjoy puzzles, especially sliding and twisting ones. They are especially fond of puzzles with multiple solutions or that look aesthetically pleasing even in an unsolved state. Scupas enjoy finding non-scupas to share their puzzles and delight in helping others solve their vicious brain teasers.</p><p>Scupas dislike violence, but they recognize that it is sometimes necessary to prevent greater destruction. If their efforts to calm the situation fail, they analyze their foes with the same precision they bring to their beloved crafts before dismantling them into parts.</p><h2>Birds of a Feather</h2><p>Although they look similar to one another, each scupa has a subtly unique tail pattern that they use to identify themselves in social gatherings. Social gatherings between scupas are frequent, as they often like to compare artworks and perform challenging puzzles together. Teams of scupas also come together to quickly repair cities following a disaster or even establish their own art communes in the wild. Although rare, some even join local governments, as scupas are often revered for their innate sense of distance and planning, which makes them excellent city planners.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Lore [Axis], Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), force, intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Daze, Dispel Magic, Mending, Telekinetic Hand, Telekinetic Maneuver, Telekinetic Projectile), throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The scupa deals 2d4 extra precision damage to Off-Guard creatures. If the creature became off-guard due to Analyze Armor, they instead deal 2d8 extra precision damage.</p>"},{"n":"Analyze Armor","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The scupa carefully surveys the defenses of a creature they can see within 60 feet, searching for weak points. If the creature is benefiting from an item bonus to AC, such as from armor, bands of force, or mystic armor, the scupa can attempt a Crafting check to find a weakness in that defense. The DC of the Crafting check is equal to the standard DC for an item of that level or spell of that rank. If the creature is not benefiting from an item bonus, the Crafting check automatically fails.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Off-Guard against the scupa's attacks for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> No effect.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The scupa can't attempt to Analyze Armor on the same target again for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Display Tailfeathers","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","mental","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The scupa fans out their tailfeathers, creating fascinating patterns. Each non-scupa creature within 30 feet must attempt a DC 19 Will save. The effects of Display Tailfeathers last for 1 round, but if the scupa uses this ability again on subsequent rounds, they extend the duration by 1 round for any creature already affected.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is immune to Display Tailfeathers for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Fascinated by the scupa.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, plus the creature is Dazzled.</p>"},{"n":"Invisible Hands","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a scupa casts Telekinetic Hand, they generate a pair of hands. This pair of hands has all of the typical properties of a single <em>telekinetic hand</em>, except that when the hands are in the scupa's square or an adjacent square, the scupa can use the hands to perform manipulate actions.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/g8YuMWTr9gu42UCs":{"lang":["Necril","Plus any one language they knew when they were alive"],"gear":["Sarcophagus Lid"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing","ac":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Necromancers seeking resilient mummy guardians developed a ritual for creating mummies capable of using their own sarcophagi for defense, either as a shield to protect the mummy from damage or as an independent minion capable of fighting alongside the mummy. Either way, by fighting in concert with their sarcophagus, a sealed mummy is much more dangerous than a mere mummy guardian.</p><p>While many of the unfortunate individuals who become mummies are unwilling and arise with hatred for all living things, sealed mummies retain their memories and loyalties to those they once served. These mummies tend to be people who chose undeath in order to guard someone or something that inspired their eternal devotion, and they follow their liege or their ward beyond the grave.</p><h2>Unlidded</h2><p>A sealed mummy prizes their sarcophagus lid, which often bears a likeness of themselves as they were in life, above all else. When their sarcophagus lid is broken, a sealed mummy sometimes go berserk with rage, abandoning all other goals beyond seeking revenge on those who defaced their precious lid.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> constitution, death, energy resistant (void), force, intelligence, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Alchemical Weakness","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sealed mummy's weakness to alchemical items not only applies to damage from alchemical items, but the sealed mummy also takes 10 untyped damage when splashed with non-damaging alchemical items or dosed with alchemical poisons, even if they're immune to their other effects.</p>"},{"n":"Raise Lid","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lid of the sealed mummy's sarcophagus is attached, and the lid isn't broken</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sealed mummy raises their sarcophagus's lid defensively, using it as a shield. This grants them a +2 circumstance bonus to AC and allows them to use the Shield Block reaction with their lid instead of with a shield. While the lid is raised, the mummy loses their alchemical weakness.</p>"},{"n":"Shield Block","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster has its shield raised and takes damage from a physical attack.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster snaps its shield into place to deflect a blow. The shield prevents the monster from taking an amount of damage up to the shield's Hardness. The monster and the shield each take any remaining damage, possibly breaking or destroying the shield.</p></p>"},{"n":"Telekinetic Lift","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sealed mummy attempts an Athletics check to Grapple a creature they can see within 30 feet. The sealed mummy does not require a free hand to attempt this check, but they can only have one creature grappled by Telekinetic Lift at a time. On a success, in addition to the normal effects of Grapple, the creature is lifted in the air up to 20 feet off the ground (this is forced movement), and when the condition ends, it falls. The effect automatically ends if the sealed mummy moves.</p>"},{"n":"Unseal","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult"],"f":"once per minute","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per minute</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The lid of the sealed mummy's sarcophagus is attached, and the lid isn't broken</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sealed mummy detaches their sarcophagus lid and animates the lid as a minion for up to 1 minute. The lid then takes its two actions for the turn. As a minion, the lid is a separate Medium creature with its own space and attacks, initially detaching from the sealed mummy in an adjacent square. The lid has a fly speed of 40 feet, AC 28, and its listed Hardness and Hit Points; this is typically Hardness 8, HP 64, and BT 32, but the lid might be missing Hit Points from the sealed mummy's previous Shield Blocks. The lid uses the mummy's saving throw values and has object immunities. It can make a lid bash Strike at +20 for 2d10 bludgeoning damage. If the lid ends a move action adjacent to the sealed mummy, the mummy can choose to Dismiss the effect as a free action, reattaching the lid. If the mummy does not attach the lid within 1 minute, it falls to the ground, and the sealed mummy can use an Interact action while adjacent to reattach it manually. While the lid is detached and acting as an independent minion, the sealed mummy can't use Raise Lid.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ey7HjiwkdozbK6Ih":{"lang":["Necril","Venenan"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Sepsis serpents are intelligent hunters who spend much of their time lurking inside small cracks or inside corpses, ready to pounce on the first creature that comes near them. When prey approaches, they strike fast, infesting the victim with their venom. If the victim seems particularly strong and healthy, a sepsis serpent might also inject a tiny clone of itself into the target's bloodstream as insurance against defeat. Once injected, the tiny clone remains inert, but it primes itself to quickly mature into a new sepsis serpent upon its host's death. Sepsis serpents do not tolerate competition for prey, and offspring that don't quickly retreat and cede their host's corpse are soon devoured by their parent.</p><p>As sepsis serpents age and feed, they steadily grow in size, with more potent toxins to match. The first sepsis serpents were said to have originated centuries ago from an awakened snake that was cursed to die from his own poison. According to legends, these first serpents are now large enough to swallow up an elephant in a single gulp.</p><h2>Persistence of Memory</h2><p>When a sepsis serpent dies and a new one coagulates from its venom, the new serpent retains the memories of the elder. It is aware of the elder's enmities and alliances, and it inherits its elder's treasures and long-term plans. Nevertheless, the serpent considers itself a new individual, and usually takes a new name. It generally doesn't hold a grudge against its elder's killers, and it might even reward them for allowing it to take its elder's place.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, death, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, void), fortification, intelligence, poison, poisonous armor, spiked, throwing, unholy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Liquid Body","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sepsis serpent can effortlessly reshape their body to easily flow through narrow crevices. They can move at their full Speed through any opening large enough to fit their head.</p>"},{"n":"Motion Sense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A sepsis serpent can sense nearby motion through vibration and air movement.</p>"},{"n":"Sepsis Serpent Venom","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 23 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d6 poison damage and Enfeebled 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 4d6 poison damage and enfeebled 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d6 poison damage, enfeebled 1 (1 round) and Drained 1</p>"},{"n":"Venomous Reproduction","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If a creature dies while under the effect of Septic Invasion, the creature's blood coagulates and forms a new sepsis serpent 1 minute after its death. This newborn serpent's size is Tiny and they can't use Septic Invasion. Additionally, the new serpent is Sickened 2 and can't reduce or remove the condition in any way for 1 week.</p>"},{"n":"Activate Invader","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sepsis serpent activates a clone from Septic Invasion that is residing inside a creature within 30 feet. The creature is exposed to sepsis serpent venom, after which it is temporarily immune to Activate Invader for 1 minute. On a successful save (but not a critical success), the creature takes 2d6 poison damage or the poison damage for their current stage of septic serpent venom, whichever is greater.</p>"},{"n":"Septic Invasion","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The sepsis serpent damages a creature with their fangs Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sepsis serpent releases a minuscule copy of themselves into the bloodstream of a living creature. The copy remains dormant inside the creature until sepsis serpent activates it. The copy becomes inert and dies if it is not activated for 24 hours. The host or an adjacent creature can spend 1 manipulate action and use a healer's toolkit to attempt a DC 24 Medicine check to remove the copy with the following results.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The septic invasion is removed without harm to the host.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The septic invasion is removed, and the host takes 1d6 slashing damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The removal fails and the host takes 1d6 slashing damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The removal fails miserably. The host takes 1d6 slashing damage and the copy activates, as if the sepsis serpent had used Activate Invader.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/vtgjgsv0aWUBGysN":{"lang":["Common","Diabolic","Draconic","Empyrean","Necril","Truespeech"],"notes":{"hp":"dualistic nature"},"flavor":"<p>Shadowblooded are vampiric fallen angels that change their nature depending on whether they are in darkness, shadow, or light. Their dual nature makes them a dangerous and unpredictable foe.</p><h2>Vampiric Angels</h2><p>Shadowblooded are a rare variety of fallen angels who develop vampiric powers. They can only be born from angels who willingly undertake or are otherwise exposed to rituals to transform into undead but who have too much innate spiritual power to be completely remade. Thus, shadowblooded are neither fully holy nor fully unholy. They are often most comfortable in the shadows, both physically and metaphorically. However, their dual nature makes them volatile, as they can engage in both acts of great mercy and benevolence, as well as deeds of utter depravity.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5,000/12,000/22,500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Intimidation, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, death, darkness, dexterity, energy resistant (void), life, lifeward armor, light, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), space, space shield, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Aura of Noon and Midnight","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","darkness","divine","light","shadow"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. The shadowblooded emits an aura that controls the light level around them. They can choose between bright light, dim light, or darkness. When they choose bright light, all creatures in the aura's light are Dazzled. When they choose darkness, even creatures with darkvision (but not greater darkvision) treat targets seen through the aura's darkness as Concealed. The shadowblooded can change the nature of their aura by spending an action with the concentrate trait.</p>"},{"n":"Dualistic Nature","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The body of a shadowblooded interacts in unusual ways with both vitality and void energy, shifting instantaneously depending upon the lighting conditions. In bright light, the shadowblooded is immune to vitality damage and can be healed by effects with the vitality trait. In darkness, the shadowblooded is immune to void damage and is healed by effects with the void trait. In dim light, the shadowblooded is immune to both vitality and void damage but can't be healed by effects with either the vitality or void trait.</p>"},{"n":"Spectral Strike","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>All of the shadowblooded's strikes have the benefits of the <em>ghost touch</em> property rune.</p>"},{"n":"Shadowlight","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The damage type that the shadowblooded's Strikes deal depends upon the lighting conditions: fire damage in bright light, cold damage in darkness, and half fire damage half cold damage in dim light.</p>"},{"n":"Dualistic Vampirism","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","manipulate","vitality","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The shadowblooded reaches draws in vitality energy, void energy, or both, affecting different creatures depending on the lighting conditions. All affected creatures within 60 feet take 16d6 untyped damage (DC 42 basic Fortitude). In bright light, creatures with void healing are affected, and the effect deals vitality damage. In darkness, living creatures are affected, and the effect deals void damage. In dim light, both living creatures and creatures with void healing are affected, with each taking whichever of vitality or void damage is worse for them. The shadowblooded then gains a number of temporary Hit Points equal to the amount of damage taken by the creature that took the most damage from this effect (or half this number of temporary Hit Points in dim light). The shadowblooded can't use Dualistic Vampirism for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Shadow Jump","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","manipulate","shadow","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The shadowblooded is in an area of dim light</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The shadowblooded steps into a shadow, reappearing out of another shadow that they can see within 300 feet. If they end this movement adjacent to a creature, they can make a melee Strike against that creature. The target is Off-Guard against this attack.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zMjTv37TMUZQ6dt3":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Shadowtongue"],"gear":["Bastard Sword"],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Shadowthieves are undead that arise from assassins and other figures who engaged in nefarious activities under cover of darkness. They feed upon shadows, infusing them into their being to power their magic and sustain their bodies and are continuously on the hunt for new shadows to feed upon.</p><p>If the shadow thief is sufficiently entertained by one of their victims, they might choose to spare their lives and instead offer them an extended contract for their shadow. Over time, these victims become a network of debtors they can call upon to assist in future bargains, with the shadowthief endlessly promising them back their shadow for loyal service.</p><h2>Chasing Shadows</h2><p>Though they can forcefully steal another creature's shadow, that process inevitably damages the shadow. Instead, they prefer to entice creatures to trade their shadows away. Though shadowthieves are willing to pay these deals out in coin, they much prefer deals that provide them with an exciting task to perform, such as robbery or murder. Those who barter away their shadows are left cloaked in a vaguely unsettling aura that inhibits their ability to interact with others, a permanent mark of the price they've paid.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> charisma, constitution, death, darkness, dexterity, energy resistant (void), sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Illusory Disguise, Umbral Journey), strength, unholy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Shadow Bargain","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The shadowthief makes a bargain with a willing creature, who agrees to give away their shadow in exchange for payment or a service. A creature who has lost their shadow has an unsettling presence. It takes a –2 penalty to Diplomacy checks, and the initial attitude of animals it encounters is one step worse than normal (for instance, unfriendly instead of indifferent). Sapient creatures who notice the missing shadow might mistake the creature for a vampire.</p>"},{"n":"Lashing Kick","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature adjacent to the shadowthief attempts to use a move action</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The shadowthief uses Stomp on Shadow against the triggering creature. This attack neither applies nor counts toward the shadowthief's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When the shadowthief is reduced to 0 Hit Points, they appear as the missing shadow of the nearest a creature that is currently cursed by Shadow Bargain, where they lie dormant. If the curse is not removed in the next 2d4 days, the shadowthief reforms out of the shadow, fully healed, and the Shadow Bargain automatically ends.</p>"},{"n":"Extend Shadow","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The shadowthief extends their shadow outward and links it with the shadow of another creature within 60 feet. While the link remains, the shadowthief can momentarily project their body through the linked shadows, allowing them to make melee Strikes on the linked creature. This effect ends when either the shadowthief or the creature moves, or when the shadowthief uses Extend Shadow again to link to a different creature's shadow.</p>"},{"n":"Stomp on Shadow","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The shadowthief makes a foot Strike. If the Strike hits, the shadowthief stomps on the target's shadow. The target is Immobilized and Off-Guard until the start of the shadowthief's next turn or until they are no longer adjacent to the shadowthief, whichever comes first.</p>"},{"n":"Void's Grasp","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a shadowthief wields a melee weapon, it temporarily becomes a <em>+1 striking weapon</em> that deals an additional 1d8 void damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/E0iKYyBMI2b9Bkmv":{"lang":["Fey","Muan","Thalassic"],"flavor":"<p>Shrubmerged are plants that live in swamps, serving as protectors of both the terrestrial and aquatic environments. These tree-like hedges can subsist on land and in water thanks to their lengthy roots, and the shelter these roots provide for smaller creatures accentuates their role as guardians.</p><p>Shrubmerged take pride in their ability to defend their habitats. They act quickly whenever they sense that someone has harmed the swamp, such as through overfishing or draining the water. Shrubmerged mete out vigilante justice, violently punishing intruders as they see fit. If their targets surrender or flee, shrubmerged typically let them off with a warning that they must never return to the swamp.</p><p>Shrubmerged are quick to form grudges and slow to let them go. If someone who formerly harmed the swamp returns, even if they are genuinely repentant, the shrubmerged will most likely conclude they weren't violent enough the first time.</p><h2>Fear of Fire</h2><p>Like many tree creatures, shrubmerged fear and hate creatures who use fire above all others. If a shrubmerged is burned, it does its best to flee, often submerging itself underwater. While the battle may be over, the war has just begun; once burned, a shrubmerged stealthily follows its tormentor, looking for an opportunity to snuff out its foe's life like a candle.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (light, medium), skill items (Athletics, Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, dexterity, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, sensory (low-light vision), strength, tangle shield, throwing, water, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Personal Swamp","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","earth","primal","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet. The shrubmerged's roots spread out and saturate the ground with water, creating thick mud that is difficult terrain. The area is also difficult terrain for swimming creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Muddy Water","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the shrubmerged damages a creature with a water jet Strike, mud particles collected in the water encumber them. The target must attempt a DC 24 Reflex save. On a failure, the target takes a –10-foot status penalty to its Speeds for 1 minute or until it Escapes; on a critical failure, the target is Immobilized for 1 round or until it Escapes and then takes –10-foot status penalty to its Speeds for 1 minute or until it Escapes.</p><p>Effect: Muddy Water</p>"},{"n":"Murky Camouflage","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Mud collected on the shrubmerged's roots makes the water around them murky. When fully submerged in water, the shrubmerged can Hide from other creatures even if they don't have cover against them and aren't Concealed from them.</p>"},{"n":"Water Stride","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The shrubmerged can stand and move on the surface of water without falling through. This ability allows them to ignore the difficult terrain from their Personal Swamp. They can go underwater if they wish, but they must Swim to do so.</p>"},{"n":"Wall of Shrubbery","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","manipulate","plant","primal","wood"],"f":"","d":"<p>The shrubmerged rouses nearby foliage and extends their roots into the soil, creating a thick wall of plant matter with the effects of a 4th-rank Wall of Thorns spell, but the shrubmerged can move through the wall unimpeded without taking any damage and each of the wall's segments has weakness 9 to fire damage. The shrubmerged can't use Wall of Shrubbery again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Entrapping Roots","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The shrubmerged's turn begins</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Roots attempt to drag one creature within the shrubmerged's personal swamp closer. The creature must attempt a DC 24 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and becomes temporarily immune to Entrapping Roots for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is pulled 5 feet closer to the shrubmerged. This is forced movement.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature is pulled 10 feet closer.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/v4UF2K2hmyvi7nD3":{"lang":["Aklo","Can't speak any language"],"flavor":"<p>Sirenspiders are deadly, otherworldly predators that lure in prey with haunting melodies that flow through their hollow, metallic bodies before paralyzing and devouring them.</p><p>Young sirenspiders hunt with their siblings. As they mature, the pitches of the spiders gradually diverge from each other, and when this dissonance becomes sufficiently discordant, the enraged spiders rip their siblings apart until only the strongest survive, nesting and making melodies together.</p><h2>Luring Song</h2><p>A sirenspider's musical arrangements exhibit remarkable sophistication for a barely sapient creature, and they sometimes spread far beyond the spider's lair. One enterprising composer even made a living scribing the siren's melodies and turning them into orchestral performances for rapt audiences, claiming that you can't plagiarize an unintelligent creature. To no one's surprise, he came to a bad end: caught in the spell of the music, he was devoured by his own muse.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light, heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), metal, music, sensory (darkvision), strength, sturdy, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Web Tether","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the sirenspider's web Strike is Restrained and tethered to the sirenspider, preventing it from moving farther away from the sirenspider. The sirenspider can have one creature tethered at a time. The DC to Escape or Force open the web is 36. The tether can be severed with a Strike (AC 20, Hardness 5, HP 30), but this doesn't free the restrained creature.</p>"},{"n":"Resonant Carapace","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The sirenspider is within the area of an auditory effect</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sirenspider's carapace resonates with the sound, amplifying it. The area of the triggering effect extends to include a 15-foot emanation around the sirenspider. In addition, if the effect requires a saving throw and the sirenspider rolls a success, they get a critical success instead.</p>"},{"n":"Drain Essence","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A Grabbed, Paralyzed, Restrained, or Unconscious creature is within the sirenspider's reach</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The sirenspider sinks their fangs into the creature to draw out its vital essence. The sirenspider attempts an Athletics vs. Fortitude DC check against the creature's Fortitude DC. On a success, the creature becomes Drained 1 and Stupefied 1, and the sirenspider gains 20 temporary Hit Points. Draining Essence from a creature that's already drained and stupefied increases the value of one of the conditions of the sirenspider's choice (to a maximum of 4). A victim's stupefied condition decreases by 1 per hour.</p>"},{"n":"Ensnaring Melody","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","incapacitation","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sirenspider emits an entrancing melody. Each non-sirenspider creature within a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 33 Will save. The effect lasts until the end of the sirenspider's next turn, but the sirenspider can Sustain it. A creature that succeeds at its save is temporarily immune for 24 hours.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Fascinated with the sirenspider for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is fascinated with the sirenspider and must spend each of its actions on its turn to move closer to the sirenspider as expediently as possible while avoiding obvious dangers. If a fascinated creature is adjacent to the sirenspider, it stays still and doesn't act. If the creature is attacked, the fascination ends.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the fascination doesn't automatically end if the creature is attacked. Instead, the creature can attempt a DC 33 Will save each time it is attacked to end the fascination.</p>"},{"n":"Paralyzing Tones","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","incapacitation","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The sirenspider's metallic carapace chimes with a melody pitched to immobilize its prey. Each non-sirenspider creature within 15 feet of the sirenspider must attempt a DC 33 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Stunned 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Paralyzed for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is paralyzed for 3 rounds. At the end of each of its turns, the creature can attempt a new Will save to reduce the remaining duration by 1 round, or end it entirely on a critical success.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/jPkF2iFr6yMXA9sf":{"lang":["Common","Pyric","Spirituan","Sussuran","Talican"],"gear":["Repair Toolkit (Superb)"],"notes":{"speed":"Seize Flight"},"flavor":"<p>For almost as long as these people have dreamed of soaring through the skies, inventors have sought ways to fuse air magic into solid objects, making devices to allow anyone to experience the wonders of flight. Skyhearts are spirits born from the most successful of these attempts. If their creators managed to fly briefly but failed to sustain that flight, skyhearts could be spirits of collective hope unrealized, able to go airborne temporarily through sheer willpower. Alternatively, they might be relics of a lost civilization left to rust away, able to fly in the ancient past but no longer.</p><p>The physical forms of skyhearts are shambling mechanical masses some 15 feet tall, magical marvels of air, fire, and metal. They want nothing more than to become capable of sustained flight. Some follow at the heels of mages who can briefly grant their wish through magic. Others repeatedly ascend and leap off cliffs and mountains. Still others strive to invent some way to stay aloft through their own ingenuity.</p><hr><p>Elemental spirits are spiritual beings who embody one or more elements, though those of composite elements tend to be more complex existences.</p><h2>Science Fantasy Settings</h2><p>In high-tech settings, skyhearts might be created through a hybrid of magic and technology or through technology alone. They might be powered by an intentionally created artificial intelligence or an emergent \"ghost in the machine.\" Visually, they might look more like airplanes or spacecraft than magical airships. For such advanced skyhearts, consider reflavoring their abilities to match the vessel's appearance (for example, replacing their fuel spittle with a rocket launcher or laser beam).</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 560/1,250/2,300 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), armor (heavy), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, burning barbs, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, metal, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spirit, strength, sturdy, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Seize Flight","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["air","arcane","concentrate","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The skyheart falls</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The skyheart is at least 10 feet above ground</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The skyheart attempts a DC 30 Will save.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The skyheart ceases falling, then gains a fly speed of 60 feet and Flies in place. This fly speed lasts for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> Treat the skyheart's fall as up to 240 feet shorter.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The skyheart falls normally.</p>"},{"n":"Turbine Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An adjacent creature can attempt to disrupt one of the skyheart's two magical turbines with Disable device. On a success, the skyheart is Stunned 1. On a critical failure, the skyheart immediately attempts a turbine Strike; this Strike neither applies nor counts toward the skyheart's multiple attack penalty. If both turbines are disabled, the skyheart can't use turbine Strikes until they spend 10 minutes Repairing themself.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Large, 3d6 slashing plus 3d6 fire, Rupture 25</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Gasp","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","arcane"],"f":"","d":"<p>The skyheart inhales sharply. Each creature in a 60-foot cone must succeed on a DC 34 Fortitude save or be moved 20 feet closer to the skyheart (40 feet on a critical failure); this is forced movement. Creatures that are already adjacent to the skyheart must instead succeed at DC 34 Reflex save or be knocked Prone.</p>"},{"n":"Reckless Launch","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire","move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The skyheart deals 10d6 fire damage in a 15-foot emanation (DC 31 basic Reflex save), then moves in a straight line up to 30 feet vertically and 120 feet horizontally. The skyheart can't use Reckless Launch again for 2 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Raised Spirits","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["air","arcane","emotion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>While the skyheart has a fly speed and is at least 10 feet above ground, they gain a +2 status bonus to saving throws and attack rolls. Additionally, each creature within 60 feet gains the same fly Speed for as long as it remains within range.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/VNp4r9ibhS8rs6e7":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Spirituan","Telepathy 120 feet"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>A soul collector is an evil spirit trapped in a prison demiplane, anchored to reality only by a mystical soul decanter. The soul decanter constantly draws in nearby souls, both to strengthen the prison and to foil anyone the spirit might reach out to for aid. Yet, after centuries of imprisonment, some soul collectors have wrested control over their prison. Rather than strengthening the decanter, these souls now propagate their magic and turn prisoners' souls into a loyal undead army.</p><p>Those who encounter a soul collector in the mortal realm first come across their soul decanter, which the spirit can move around telekinetically to find more victims. It's only when they're sucked into the prison demiplane that they come across the soul collector face to face and experience the creature's true power. The soul collector often uses their spells, which they can cast using the decanter as a medium, to entice victims closer so that they can pull them in where the soul collector can face them directly and trap their soul.</p><h2>Picturesque Prisons</h2><p>A soul collector's prison demiplane often bears some relationship to their soul decanter. For instance, inside a clay decanter painted with the figures of mythical warriors, a soul collector broods among animate illusions of those same warriors. Inside a silver urn speckled with jewels, a soul collector bestrides a tiny planet under a silver, star-speckled sky. A prison demiplane is often a place of heartwrenching beauty to outsiders, although hideous in the eyes of the soul collector.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 8,750/17,500/30,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, charisma, constitution, death, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, void), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Dispel Magic, Dominate, Shred Soul, Spirit Blast, Suggestion, Telekinetic Projectile, Void Warp, Wails of the Damned), spirit, strength, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Prison Demiplane","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The soul collector is trapped on a finite demiplane. Typically, both time and gravity function normally within the demiplane, but its other features have been gradually molded into a foreboding environment that suits the soul collector's tastes. When a creature dies within the demiplane or has its soul pulled in by soulcall aura, its soul is trapped. The soul collector can release souls from the demiplane with imbue soul, but souls can otherwise only be freed by wish or the direct intervention of a deity. If a soul collector is destroyed, the demiplane collapses, freeing all trapped within.</p>"},{"n":"Soul Decanter","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["occult","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>The bridge between a soul collector's prison demiplane and the Universe is a magical soul decanter. The soul collector's senses extend out in a 120-foot radius around the decanter. They can cast spells, telepathically communicate, and use abilities with the occult trait with the decanter as the origin point. They can also use Fly to telekinetically move the decanter up to their fly Speed. A soul collector is immune to most effects from creatures outside of the demiplane. However, if the decanter is destroyed (Hardness 15, HP 150, BT 75), all living creatures within the demiplane are ejected and the demiplane is temporarily disconnected from the Universe. After 24 hours, a new soul decanter forms.</p>"},{"n":"Soulcall Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","incapacitation","occult","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>60 feet (centered on the soul decanter). All creatures with a soul in the aura are drawn into the prison demiplane, arriving as close to the soul collector as they were to the soul decanter. Unwilling creatures can attempt a DC 42 Will save to resist. Souls of creatures that die in the aura are drawn in automatically. Living creatures that remain in the demiplane for 1 hour are expelled back to their original space and are temporarily immune to the aura for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Far-Reaching Control","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a soul collector casts Dominate or Suggestion, the range increases to 120 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Imbue Soul","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["occult","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a soul collector uses create undead to make a non-mindless undead, they can use a soul from their prison. If they do, they can see through the undead's eyes and communicate with them telepathically at any distance. Additionally, they can graft excess soul energy onto the undead to make it seem alive. These undead receive an automatic result of 40 on Deception checks to pass as living creatures.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ueZuFVYw8SWFebjB":{"lang":["Common","Melodian","Necril"],"lore":[["Music Lore",18]],"gear":["+1 Striking Ghost Touch Baton"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>Even in death, a spectral conductor desires simply to conduct performances in front of an audience, creating music to delight their audience from beyond the grave. On their own, spectral conductors are relatively harmless compared to other creatures with a similar amount of power. At worst, they might try to recruit passing travelers for their band or hold a concert or practice at an inopportune time and place, such as the middle of the night inside an unsuspecting person's bedchamber. While this can be inconvenient, when considering what else they might be capable of doing, these acts might seems like mere pranks or shenanigans in comparison.</p><p>Spectral conductors recruit anyone they can find who is willing to sing or play an instrument—even those who most people might consider to be talentless hacks... or even bloodthirsty monsters. As a result, their concerts are prone to becoming gatherings of dangerous creatures that could easily wreak havoc in the local area after the performance is over. It's easy to see how this can put authority figures and security personnel on edge, potentially causing them to engage in a preemptive attack against the perceived threat. However, this aggressive action can increase the danger, since attacking any member of the spectral conductor's band during a rehearsal or performance is sure to inspire the otherwise-peaceful spectral conductor to join the fray on behalf of their concertmates. This can quickly turn what was a peaceful, if bizarre, concert into a devastating life or death battle.</p><h2>Battle of the Bands</h2><p>Spectral conductors welcome all who wish to perform regardless of talent, finding beauty in any earnest expression. Their ethos puts them at odds with storm conductors, who brutally punish those who fail to measure up to their standards. Spectral conductors often attempt to upstage storm conductors by building more popular bands, and they are quick to lash out at their more cantankerous counterparts with a blast of discordant noise should they attempt to interfere with a performance.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Diplomacy, Lore [Music], Performance, Society)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, void), fortification, intelligence, music, sensory (darkvision), time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Lead the Band","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Through rehearsals, the spectral conductor helps improve future concerts. Creatures that spend at least 2 hours rehearsing with the spectral conductor gain a +2 circumstance bonus to Performance checks involving the music practiced during the rehearsal as well as to Performance checks rolled because of the spectral conductor's Take it from the Top for 1 week.</p><p>Effect: Lead the Band</p>"},{"n":"Take it from the Top","a":"f","c":"interaction","t":["auditory","occult","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> Initiative would be rolled</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> All creatures in a 120-foot emanation use a Performance check for their initiative roll. Unwilling creatures can resist this effect with a successful DC 26 Will save.</p>"},{"n":"Accelerando","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The spectral conductor quickens the tempo of their concert. The spectral conductor and all of their allies within a 60-foot emanation gain 10 temporary Hit Points and are Quickened for 1 round; they can use this extra action for only Stride or Strike actions. The spectral conductor can't use Accelerando again until after they have used Ritenuto.</p>"},{"n":"Ritenuto","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The spectral conductor reduces the tempo of their concert. The spectral conductor is Slowed 1 until the end of their next turn. All foes within a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 26 Will save. The spectral conductor can't use Ritenuto again until after they have used Accelerando.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d6 sonic damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 4d6 sonic damage and is slowed 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes 8d6 sonic damage and is slowed 1 for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/7CVaWes8wThgMad8":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Spirituan","Can't speak any language"],"notes":{"hp":"void healing"},"flavor":"<p>The spiritflame lantern is a symphony of spirits bound within a silver lantern. These lanterns are adorned with ornate figures that echo the appearances the spirits once held in life, which constantly morph and dance in tune with the restless fire. As the lantern moves, they emit an eerie, melodic hum reminiscent of distant chants and forgotten lullabies.</p><p>Spiritflame lanterns form when tattered souls are ensnared through a foul ritual that amalgamates them together with a concoction of silver, black onyx, and unholy fire. The imprisoned spirits seek out places stained with memories of death, hoping to collect fragments of lost souls to join them in their prison. Each time a new spirit is bound, the spirits within surge free, if only for a moment. This experience gives each spirit hope that someday, if they doom enough to their wretched fate, they will finally be able to escape to the afterlife.</p><p>When someone dies within the lantern's light, the spiritflame lantern tears off a tiny piece of their soul and holds it fast. While this loss does not prevent the deceased from being restored to life, it leaves them plagued by nightmares of confinement and flames. Only the lantern's destruction can release all bound within, but the spirits are seldom willing to contemplate this course, for they believe that shattering the lantern will mean their annihilation. Instead, they believe that if they doom enough souls, the lantern will be satisfied with their efforts and release them.</p><h2>Burning Hatred</h2><p>Spiritflame lanterns fear and despise vigil flames on an instinctual level. The intensity of this innate loathing has led many ritual scholars to speculate that the process for a spiritflame lantern's creation might have been designed as a perversion of the process that allows vigil flames to be born.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Occultism, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> burning barbs, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, void), fire, fire shield, sensory (darkvision), spirit, throwing, unholy, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Ethereal Illumination","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The spiritflame lantern emits a soft, ghostly radiance, casting dim light in a 10-foot emanation. This illumination is magical and can't be extinguished by non-magical means.</p>"},{"n":"Spiritfire Burst","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The spiritflame lantern unleashes a bright burst of ethereal flames in a 10-foot emanation. All creatures within the area take 1d8 fire plus 1d8 void damage (DC 21 basic Reflex save). Creatures who fail the saving throw are Dazzled for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Spiritflame Beam","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The spiritflame lantern focuses their spectral energy into a concentrated beam. The imprisoned spirits relish a brief taste of freedom as they surge forth, clinging to whomever they reach. Each creature in a 60-foot line must roll a DC 21 Reflex save. The spiritflame lantern can't use Spiritflame Beam again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 1d12 fire damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 1d12 fire plus 3 persistent fire plus 1d12 void damage. It is Frightened 1 and can't reduce or remove this condition until it removes the persistent fire damage.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes 2d12 fire plus 3 persistent fire plus 2d12 void plus 3 persistent void. It is frightened 1 and can't reduce or remove this condition until it removes the persistent fire and void damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/WvNvFibg8kaCDErD":{"lang":["Common","Shadowtongue"],"notes":{"speed":" (burrow in wooden floors only)"},"flavor":"<p>Old, abandoned houses are the favorite haunt of the insatiable splintertooth. Creaking floorboards—dry, cracking, and veiled in shadow—are their hunting ground. With one careless step of an explorer’s foot, the floorboards split, rising to form a maw of sharp splinters that swallows its victim in an instant.</p><p>Splinterteeth are corrupted creatures of wood and shadow that inhabit the border between the Universe and the Shadow Plane. Trapped between these two realities and unable to fully manifest in either, they briefly hunt in the Universe before slinking back into shadow. In addition to eating living creatures, they enjoy eating furniture. Instead of digesting these inorganic materials, they store them inside their bodies, where they can be ejected at future prey with enormous force.</p><p>It is difficult to spot a splintertooth, especially because they prefer to lie still in darkness. In the light of a lantern, they might appear as a darker patch of wood, knotted and worn. Even when they move, causing the floorboards to ripple, their true form is difficult to discern. Scholars believe that this is because their unique physiology is divided between planes so that only the creature’s face and teeth are phased into reality. If this is true, many of its victims have been ingested into the Shadow Plane, never to be seen again.</p><h2>Variant Splinterteeth</h2><p>Cobblestone, gravel, tile, and even wattle and daub varieties of splinterteeth exist, and all favor dark, abandoned houses.</p><p>These dwellings are often inhabited when the splintertooth first arrives. Once a splintertooth finds a home they like, they claim it as their own, devouring all of the building’s tenants. Nobody knows why they avoid natural areas, but they certainly enjoy tormenting people where they feel safest—in their own homes.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (medium), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, darkness, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spiked, strength, sturdy, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"All-Around Vision","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>This monster can see in all directions simultaneously and therefore can't be flanked.</p></p>"},{"n":"Shadow Invisibility","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The splintertooth is Invisible in areas of dim light or darkness. When they make a jaws Strike or use Splinter Spray, they become visible until the start of their next turn.</p>"},{"n":"Shadow Step","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["occult","shadow","teleportation"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The splintertooth that was in an area of dim light or darkness is exposed to bright light</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The splintertooth retreats toward dimmer areas, teleporting to an unoccupied square within 30 feet that is not in bright light.</p>"},{"n":"Swallow Whole","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["attack"],"f":"","d":"<p>Large, 2d8+4 bludgeoning, Rupture 20</p><hr><p><p>The monster attempts to swallow a creature of the listed size or smaller that it has grabbed or restrained in its jaws or mouth. If a swallowed creature is of the maximum size listed, the monster can't use Swallow Whole again. If the creature is smaller than the maximum, the monster can usually swallow more creatures; the GM determines the maximum. The monster attempts an Athletics vs. Reflex DC check opposed by the grabbed creature's Reflex DC. If it succeeds, it swallows the creature. The monster's mouth or jaws no longer grab a creature it has swallowed, so the monster is free to use them to Strike or Grab once again. The monster can't attack creatures it has swallowed.</p>\n<p>A swallowed creature is Grabbed, is Slowed 1, and has to hold its breath or start suffocating. The swallowed creature takes the listed amount of damage when first swallowed and at the end of each of its turns while it's swallowed. If the victim Escapes this ability's grabbed condition, it exits through the monster's mouth. This frees any other creature grabbed in the monster's mouth or jaws. A swallowed creature can attack the monster that has swallowed it, but only with unarmed attacks or with weapons of light Bulk or less. The swallowing creature is Off-Guard against the attack. If the monster takes piercing or slashing damage equaling or exceeding the listed Rupture value from a single attack or spell, the swallowed creature cuts itself free. A creature that gets free by either Escaping or cutting itself free can immediately breathe and exits the swallowing monster's space.</p>\n<p>Effect: Engulf and Swallow Whole</p>\n<p>If the monster dies, a swallowed creature can be freed by creatures adjacent to the corpse if they spend a combined total of 3 actions cutting the monster open with a weapon or unarmed attack that deals piercing or slashing damage.</p></p>"},{"n":"Improved Grab","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Improved Grab as an action and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Improved Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Splinter Spray","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult","wood"],"f":"","d":"<p>The splintertooth spits splinters of wood in a 30-foot cone that deals 8d4+8 piercing damage (DC 26 basic Reflex save). It can't use Splinter Spray again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Sudden Swallow","a":"r","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature enters the splintertooth's space</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The splintertooth is unnoticed by the triggering creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The splintertooth attempts a jaws Strike against the triggering creature. If the splintertooth successfully Grabs the triggering creature, it uses its Swallow Whole against it. The splintertooth is then Immobilized and can't use Shadow Step; these limitations persist until the creature Escapes or until the end of the splintertooth's next turn, whichever comes first.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/9ojfqM3d8OzCevsw":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Pyric","Sussuran","Venenan"],"notes":{"ac":"hateful reformation, void healing","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic","per":"smoke vision"},"flavor":"<p>Even the phoenix, the embodiment of radiant life conquering death, is not immune to the twisted magic of necromancy. A staccarix, or ash phoenix, is the antithesis of everything a living phoenix represents. They seek out libraries and repositories of lore only to scorch them all to cinders, and they gleefully spread suffering and death everywhere they go. The only characteristic that staccarixes share with living phoenixes is their capacity for self-resurrection, a terrifying thought for all who seek to destroy these monstrosities for good.</p><h2>Book Burner</h2><p>A staccarix hates knowledge and seems determined to sink the world into a state of ignorance from which it can never recover. It's theorized that some library somewhere in the world must contain the secret to permanently destroying a staccarix, and that staccarixes are determined to destroy this lost knowledge before some researcher happens upon it.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (light), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Nature, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, burning barbs, charisma, death, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (fire, void), fire, fire shield, ice, mirrored, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Dispel Magic, Execute, Falling Stars, Harm, Massacre, Pinpoint, See the Unseen), spiked, strength, sturdy, throwing, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Smoke Vision","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Smoke doesn't impair a staccarix's vision; they ignore the Concealed condition from smoke.</p>"},{"n":"Extinguish Knowledge","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the staccarix's reach uses a concentrate action</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The staccarix makes a beak Strike against the creature. On a hit, they disrupt the action. This Strike neither applies nor counts toward the staccarix's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Ash Storm Mantle","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","fire"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. All creatures in the aura are Concealed by swirling smoke and ash, and all creatures outside the smoke become concealed to creatures within it. Any creature that needs to breathe must hold its breath while in the aura or begin to suffocate. A creature that breathes in the ash takes 2d6 fire plus 2d6 poison damage.</p>"},{"n":"Hateful Reformation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a staccarix dies, their ashes are borne away by a sudden fell wind. Unless quickly gathered and kept within the confines of a holy temple, these ashes reconstitute the staccarix's body over the course of 1d6 days.</p>"},{"n":"Weakness to Scattering","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When the staccarix is hit by or fails a saving throw against an effect with the air trait, or when they start their turn in an area of wind that is strong enough to provide greater difficult terrain, they take 15 bludgeoning damage.</p>"},{"n":"Ashen Flight","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The staccarix discorporates into a cloud of superheated toxic ash and Flies twice. Their amorphous form allows them to pass through the squares of other creatures or into small cracks, and their Flight does not trigger reactions based on movement. Creatures within 20 feet of each square the staccarix moves through takes 4d6 fire plus 4d6 poison damage (DC 28 basic Fortitude save); creatures that fail the save are also exposed to choking embers.</p>"},{"n":"Choking Embers","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>With their Strikes and Ashen Flight, the staccarix's body releases painful embers that spew a sickening smoke. Affected creatures must succeed at a DC 36 Fortitude save or take 1d6 persistent fire plus 1d6 persistent poison damage and become Sickened 1. On a critical failure, they become Sickened 2 and can't reduce or remove the sickened condition until they end the persistent poison damage.</p>"},{"n":"Darkened Skies","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The staccarix doesn't need a secondary caster for their Control Weather ritual. In addition to the normal options, they can invoke an ashfall or volcanic tornado made of hot, poisonous gases.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/4cr0JWI1VjU8kfiK":{"lang":["Aetheran","Aklo","Empyrean","Khaotan","Protean","Truespeech"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic","speed":"unfettered movement"},"flavor":"<p>When the stars first appeared in the skies over the celestial realms, not all of them were content with remaining in place. Unable to ignore their wanderlust, the first starborns pulled themselves out of the tapestry of stars and began to explore. Ageless and seemingly incapable of maturing, starborns enjoy reality with a childlike wonder that many travelers would envy, as they seem never to grow tired of the unending cosmos.</p><p>A starborn is a being with terrifying gravitational might, which they use to prank others and entertain themselves. These lonely beings mean well, but they are completely oblivious to the nature of their own strength, a tendency that occasionally brings misfortune to their playmates. However, their greatest vulnerability is their overly trusting nature, which often leads them to be manipulated into helping their friend's wishes come true, for good or for ill. Because of this, malefactors who befriend a starborn gain a formidable pawn in service to their schemes, often one significantly more powerful than any of their other allies.</p><h2>Powerful Playmates</h2><p>Eternal children despite their cosmic power and vast intellects, starborn enjoy playing with other children and take care never to harm or displease them. The same can't be said for the annoying adults who ruin their best games. These inconvenient guardians might find themselves walled away in an invisible prison, banished to the ceiling to live out their lives with reversed gravity, or even permanently teleported to a faraway land.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 8,750/17,500/30,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Diplomacy, Occultism, Performance, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, charisma, death, dexterity, electricity, energy resistant (cold, fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, force, holy, ice, intelligence, life, lifeward armor, mirrored, poison, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Black Hole, Disappearance, Falling Stars, Heal, Implosion, Internal Distortion, Light, Manifestation, Spirit Blast, Telekinetic Haul, Telekinetic Maneuver, Telekinetic Projectile, Translocate, Truespeech, Unfettered Movement, Wall of Force), sturdy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"-20 Penalty to Sense Motive","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Voidwalker","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The starborn doesn't need to breathe, and it's also immune to the effects of pressure extremes from the environment, such as the effects of being deep undersea or in outer space.</p>"},{"n":"Gravity Well","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 100 feet casts a spell or makes a Strike that targets the starborn</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature must attempt a DC 42 Will save. On a failure, the starborn determine the targets, destination, and other effects of the spell or Strike, as though they were the caster or attacker.</p>"},{"n":"Collapsing Star","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["death","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a starborn dies, their body collapses into an event horizon. All creatures within 100 feet must succeed at a DC 42 Fortitude save or be pulled 10 feet toward the sphere (20 feet on a critical failure). A creature that makes contact with the event horizon takes 20d6 void damage; if this reduces them to 0 Hit Points, they are annihilated.</p>"},{"n":"Wandering Star","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>Due to their connection to gravitational magic and the space element, the starborn is under the effects of subjective gravity at all times, regardless of the true gravitational features of the plane they are on.</p>"},{"n":"Enforce Gravity","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The starborn enforces gravity in a 40-foot cube within 100 feet. First, they attempt to counteract all effects within the area that allow creatures to fly, hover, or otherwise avoid falling (counteract modifier +39). Then, they choose one of the cube's sides and set it as the new direction of gravity. All creatures in the area must succeed at a DC 47 Reflex save or be knocked Prone by the disorienting shift. Creatures that can't fly immediately fall in the new direction. If they collide with a solid surface, they take the typical bludgeoning damage from falling. The starborn can't use Enforce Gravity again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/rrQWASJf4HM9dxhh":{"lang":["Common","Melodian","Necril","Voltan"],"lore":[["Music Lore",19]],"notes":{"hp":"rejuvenation, void healing"},"flavor":"<p>A storm conductor forms when the ghost of a conductor who possessed a stormy temperament in life manages to grant their temper an insubstantial spiritual form, causing them to return to life as an unusual form of ghost. A storm conductor wander the land eternally unsatisfied, seeking nothing more than a choir or orchestra that can meet their exacting standards. Sadly, the performers who can do so are few and far between, and so storm conductors are cursed to an eternity of wandering and testing others, and punishing their inevitable failures.</p><p>Turbulent storm clouds swirl in the sky above a storm conductor, providing a constant beat of thunder that, coupled with the eerie orchestral music that surrounds the ghostly creature, makes them rather easy to notice from a significant distance. In combat, the conductor seeks to magically force those they encounter to sing or play along with their invisible orchestra. When one of the conductor's targets fails to meet their expectations, the ghost calls down a violent thunderbolt from the sky above to violently correct their musical inadequacies.</p><h2>Bringing Down the House</h2><p>The stormy weather that whips around a storm conductor prevents the performance of anything but the most bombastic music—a tragedy to those conductors who prefer more delicate, pianissimo works. As one storm conductor bemoaned at the climax of one of her raucous symphonies, \"An eternity of conducting Fanfare of the Storm Giants: a fate worse than Hell itself!\"</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> shield, perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Intimidation, Lore [Music], Occultism, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> charisma, death, dexterity, electricity, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, void), intelligence, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Illusory Scene, Lightning Bolt, Summon Instrument, Wave of Despair), sturdy, throwing, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Void Healing","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A creature with void healing draws health from void energy rather than vitality energy. It is damaged by vitality damage and is not healed by vitality healing effects. It does not take void damage, and it is healed by void effects that heal undead.</p></p>"},{"n":"Stormbound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A storm conductor can't enter buildings or underground areas. They must remain outside under an open sky.</p>"},{"n":"Conductor's Rebuke","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":["electricity","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet that the storm conductor can hear fails a Performance check</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> A bolt of lightning emerges from the sky and zaps the creature who offends the storm conductor's ears. The triggering creature takes 4d12 electricity damage (DC 28 basic Reflex save). On a failure, the creature is Deafened for one round.</p>"},{"n":"Rejuvenation","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>When a storm conductor is destroyed, they re-form after 2d4 days within 100 feet of where they were previously destroyed, fully healed. A storm conductor can be permanently destroyed only if they hear a truly inspiring and flawless concert.</p>"},{"n":"Tuneless Target","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>If the storm conductor takes sonic damage, they become consumed with rage directed at the source of the offensive discordance. Until the end of their next turn, all of the storm conductor's offensive actions must target the source of the damage. If the storm conductor took sonic damage from multiple sources, they can choose these targets from among them.</p>"},{"n":"Tuning","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","emotion","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The storm conductor attempts to prime their unwilling subjects for the coming performance. The conductor attempts a Performance vs. Will DC check against the Will DCs of each enemy within 30 feet.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Tuning for 1 minute.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature must Perform or use another action that requires a Performance check as it first action on its next turn.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, and the creature is also Stupefied 1 for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/goMX5Hkka6sqx98W":{"flavor":"<p>The stormadillo is a bulky, short-legged mammal with an armored leather shell and the ability to mark its territory with static electricity, allowing it to trap its prey and bulldoze over it at terrifying speeds. Despite their lack of vocalization, a chorus of electrical pops and snaps often heralds their arrival.</p><p>Stormadillos are omnivores, but they quickly strip the vegetation from their preferred rocky mountain environments. They are fiercely territorial and tend to challenge anything their size or smaller that encroaches on the significant territory they patrol. However, they are cautious fighters and only engage in combat with larger creatures when cornered or protecting their young. As a result, some giant communities have had success domesticating stormadillos.</p><p>A typical adult stormadillo is 13 feet long from head to tail and weighs roughly 400 pounds. The static electricity that infuses a stormadillo's body makes metal objects stick to it, forming an extra layer of protection around its body. Stormadillos have learned to deliberately cultivate these collections to enhance their shells' natural defenses, gathering an eclectic hodgepodge of everything from cooking pots and gardening tools to loose coins and small weapons.</p><h2>Bad Neighbors</h2><p>Stormadillos live in the same storm-wracked hills and mountains that volt rams inhabit, and the two species often come into conflict. In competition for the same scarce pasture lands and high peaks on which to catch the occasional lightning bolt, stormadillos and volt rams often engage in thunderous clashes, with the volt ram charging impetuously forward and the stormadillo rolling into the volt ram's horns like a bowling ball.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, slashing), armor (medium), perception item</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, electricity, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), sensory (scent), strength, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Curl","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The stormadillo coils into a ball to defend itself. It gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC. The stormadillo's speed is reduced to 10 feet, and it can't use its claw attacks or take other actions that require it to use its limbs while in its Curl. It can unfurl as a single action with the manipulate trait, ending the effects of Curl.</p>"},{"n":"Static Cling","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The stormadillo can cling to electrically charged terrain (such as terrain affected by Electrify Domain) and roll along it at great speeds. As long as the stormadillo is clinging to such a surface, it can't be knocked Prone or subject to forced movement, and it can move along walls and ceilings at its full Speed. The stormadillo's Speed increases to 40 feet, or 60 feet while using Curl. If a stormadillo using Static Cling moves out of electrified terrain at any point, its movement immediately ends.</p>"},{"n":"Trample","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Medium or smaller, shell, DC 24 basic Reflex. Trample deals an additional 1d8 electricity damage if the Stormadillo is using Static Cling.</p><hr><p><p>The monster Strides up to double its Speed and can move through the spaces of creatures of the listed size, Trampling each creature whose space it enters. The monster can attempt to Trample the same creature only once in a single use of Trample. The monster deals the damage of the listed Strike, but trampled creatures can attempt a basic Reflex save at the listed DC (no damage on a critical success, half damage on a success, double damage on a critical failure).</p></p>"},{"n":"Electrify Domain","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The stormadillo slams its claws on the ground, creating a 10-foot wide, 120-foot long line of electricity that travels along surfaces (including walls and ceilings), dealing 2d8 electricity damage to all creatures along the line and leaving a lingering band of electrified terrain that persists for 1 minute. Any creature in the line must attempt a DC 24 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes no damage.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage and takes a –10-foot penalty to its Speeds for 1 round. As normal, this penalty can't reduce a creature's Speed below 5 feet. Effect: Electrify Domain</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage and is Immobilized for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/sJOTFVO8q0cvqEtZ":{"lang":["Common","Diabolic","Shadowtongue","Venenan"],"lore":[["Synthecite Lore",15]],"notes":{"per":"painsight"},"flavor":"<p>Synthecite vivisectionists are a variety of fleshwarps, people whose bodies and minds have been twisted by various magical or alchemical processes. In this case, all synthecites, vivisectionists included, found their forms and intellects completely corrupted and reshaped into something new by a sentient poison called synthecite substrate. This poison is the prized creation of a velstrac demagogue who seeks to \"improve\" mortal life into a new superior form. Those who would perish from the poison are instead agonizingly reborn, becoming new synthecites with a religious devotion to the demagogue. Their doctrine compels them to spread their transformation to all mortals, whether by sneaking the substrate into small village wells, infecting crops to gradually corrupt the masses, or directly injecting mortals and removing their rebellious flesh for more expedient results.</p><p>Synthecite vivisectionists have two main features (besides their horrific appearance of course): a hollow bone spike on one arm that they use to inject a substance into their victims and two bone scythes over their shoulders that they skillfully use to make unwanted surgeries that speed up the transformation process.</p><p>Though the vast majority of people who are exposed to synthecite substrate turn into new synthecite vivisectionists, the bodies of particularly skilled individuals occasionally react differently, morphing into a stronger variety of synthecite that retains some of the abilities the person originally held in life, if not their morals, priorities, or outlook. Examples of some of these synthecite variants include stealthy horripolators, adaptable eugenicists, and zealous flense-priests.</p><h2>Half of the Time, It Works Every Time</h2><p>Synthecite vivisectionist doctrine holds that synthecite substrate never fails, inevitably transforming its subjects into new vivisectionists. On occasion, though, a would-be victim shakes off the poison, either through robust health or curative magic. When this happens, vivisectionists see the survivor as a personal insult and a threat to the natural order, targeting the heretical survivor for instant destruction.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (medium), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Intimidation, Lore [Synthecite], Medicine)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, energy resistant (vitality), poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision, greater darkvision), spiked, strength, unholy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Painsight","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>A synthecite automatically knows whether a creature they see has any of the Doomed, Dying, and Wounded conditions, as well as the value of those conditions.</p>"},{"n":"Radical Surgery","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The synthecite vivisectionist is adjacent to an Immobilized or Prone creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The synthecite vivisectionist makes two scythe arm Strikes at a –2 penalty against a prone or immobilized target. These attacks count toward the vivisectionist's multiple attack penalty, but the multiple attack penalty doesn't increase until after they make both attacks. If both attacks hit and the creature has been poisoned with synthecite substrate, the poison advances by 1 stage.</p>"},{"n":"Synthecite Substrate","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 23 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> Drained 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> Doomed 1 and Drained 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> doomed 1 Drained 3 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 4</strong> doomed 1 and Drained 4 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 5</strong> dead and transforms into a new vivisectionist (see Sentient Poison)</p>"},{"n":"Sentient Poison","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a creature dies from synthecite substrate, their body transforms into a new synthecite vivisectionist after 1d4 rounds. The transformation takes time to finalize; for the first 24 hours after being created, a synthecite vivisectionist is Slowed 1 and can't use synthecite substrate.</p>"},{"n":"Knockdown","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/wVA6f8rAb103Umyh":{"lang":["Common","Pyric","Spirituan"],"gear":["Hatchet (2)"],"flavor":"<p>Tabildas are unusual spirits found in places where frost is feared and life often hinges on the strength of fire. Living embodiments of this duality are tabildas, who are utterly dependent on the delicate flames that burn within their chests. If a tabilda's heartflame is ever snuffed out, its bearer soon perishes alongside it. Tabildas, whose flames have faded, become desperate to restore them as quickly as possible. This desperation psychically weighs upon nearby creatures as the spirit's terror of the cold becomes their own.</p><p>Tabildas wander across frozen landscapes in search of kindling, gathering loose firewood or felling trees with a few well-placed strikes from their hatchets. They gladly offer the warmth of their heartflames to wanderers needing rest, asking only for fuel in return. Groups trekking through the wilderness with flammable goods are sometimes shadowed by a tabilda, much like a stray dog harrying those carrying fresh meat.</p><p>Timid creatures by nature, tabildas prefer to flee from a fight if given the choice. However, tabildas do not take kindly to a disruption in their fuel sources. Settlements that engage in irresponsible deforestation might find a tabilda creeping in at night, cutting chunks out of buildings and burning precious treasures to feed their heartflames.</p><hr><p>Elemental spirits are spiritual beings who embody one or more elements, though those of composite elements tend to be more complex existences.</p><h2>Watch the World Burn</h2><p>While a tabilda with a well-functioning heartflame tends to be prudent with its resources, a tabilda on the verge of freezing to death becomes frenzied and uncontrollable. Its hunger for warmth becomes insatiable, and it indiscriminately burns anything that will take light, without any concern for saving fuel for tomorrow. Stands of trees, spellbooks, the tents of helpful allies—all become fuel for one great inferno.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, ice, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spirit, strength, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Chilling Presence","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","cold","emotion","fear","mental","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet, DC 18 Will.</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The tabilda's heartflame is extinguished</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> As Frightful Presence, except creatures that fail their Will save gain a weakness to cold damage equal to 1 + their frightened value.</p><p>Effect: Chilling Presence</p><p><p>A creature that first enters the area must attempt a Will save.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the result of the saving throw, the creature is temporarily immune to this monster's Frightful Presence for 1 minute.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected by the presence.</p>\n<p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Frightened 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is Frightened 4.</p></p>"},{"n":"Heartflame","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>An open fire called a heartflame burns in the tabilda's chest cavity. The heartflame is fragile and difficult to maintain. If the tabilda takes damage, they must succeed on a DC 11 Flat at the start of their next turn or the flame is extinguished. The flame is immediately extinguished if the tabilda takes damage from or fails a saving throw against an effect with the air, cold, or water traits. While the flame is extinguished, the tabilda takes persistent 1d6 cold damage. The flame reignites if the tabilda is exposed to fire or successfully uses Kindle.</p>"},{"n":"Soothing Fire","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet.</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The tabilda's heartflame is burning</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The heartflame emanates warmth and bright light. All creatures in the aura gain resistance 5 to cold. The temperature in the aura is two categories warmer than the ambient temperature, to a maximum of normal temperature (for example, extreme cold becomes mild cold).</p>"},{"n":"Elemental Infusion","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A tabilda's melee Strikes deal 1d6 additional damage; this damage is fire if their heartflame burns or cold if it is extinguished.</p>"},{"n":"Kindle","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","fire","manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tabilda attempts a DC 22 Survival check. On a success, they can either reignite their heartflame if it is extinguished or set an unattended, flammable object in an adjacent space alight.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/kVnAJHIGESI6fDHr":{"lang":["Common","Spirituan","Thalassic","Telepathy 100 feet"],"lore":[["Farming Lore",24]],"gear":["Kama (2)"],"notes":{"per":"rain sight","speed":" water walk"},"flavor":"<p>Tanbogamis are kami warriors who protect rice fields and work with farmers to help bring a bountiful harvest. Tanbogamis have an impressive command over the amount of rainfall a field receives, with floods and droughts being the signs of a tanbogami's displeasure. Though given their role as protectors and cultivators, tanbogami are more hospitable to mortals than most kami.</p><p>Farmers often perform ritual ceremonies in devotion to the tanbogami at the beginning of the year and during harvest time. They also pray to the tanbogami in case of disaster or a poor harvest. During these ceremonies, the local tanbogami often reveal themselves to bless the fields, purify crops, and manage the amount of rainfall. In the direst of circumstances, a tanbogami can create vast yields of rice for starving villages.</p><h2>Limits of the Domain</h2><p>A tanbogami can wander far afield from its chosen community, patrolling the wilderness and even visiting neighboring settlements. However, they can't set foot in a rice field that belongs to a different settlement than their own. An evildoer or an evil spirit that flees to a neighboring rice field can use it as a sanctuary, remaining safe from the tanbogami for as long as they remain there.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Lore [Farming], Nature, Religion, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (fire, vitality), holy, ice, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Cleanse Cuisine, Control Water, Create Food, Downpour, Water Walk), spiked, spirit, strength, throwing, water, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Rain Sight","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tanbogami ignores concealment from fog, mist, and rain, even if they are magically created.</p>"},{"n":"Ward","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tanbogami is bound to a rice field. They can merge with or emerge from their ward as a single action with the concentrate trait. While merged, they are Immobilized, but they recover Hit Points each minute as if they spent an entire day resting.</p>"},{"n":"Aura of Rain","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet. The tanbogami is surrounded by a downpour of rain, which extinguishes nonmagical flames in the aura. Creatures in the aura are Concealed and gain fire resistance 10.</p>"},{"n":"Rend (Kama)","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A Rend entry lists a Strike the monster has.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster hit the same enemy with two consecutive Strikes of the listed type in the same round.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster automatically deals that Strike's damage again to the enemy.</p></p>"},{"n":"Holy Raindrop Dive","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","holy","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tanbogami Flies 40 feet straight up into the air, then Flies in a straight line to an empty space of their choice on the ground within 80 feet, crashing down in a crushing deluge of rain. The rain deals 2d8 bludgeoning plus 3d6 spirit damage to all creatures in a 15-foot emanation (DC 30 basic Reflex save). Creatures with the unholy trait take an additional 3d6 spirit damage.</p>"},{"n":"Holy Droplets","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","holy"],"f":"","d":"<p>A tanbogami's melee Strikes deal an additional 1d8 spirit damage, or 2d8 spirit damage vs. unholy targets.</p>"},{"n":"Dual Onslaught","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The tanbogami makes two kama Strikes against the same target, one with each of their kamas. The tanbogami combines the damage of any attacks that hit and applies resistances and weaknesses only once. Both attacks count toward the tanbogami's multiple attack penalty, but the penalty increases only after both attacks. If the tanbogami misses with both kama Strikes, they still deal the damage from one kama to the target, though they still miss completely if both attack rolls were critical failures.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Knockdown","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"}],"strikeNotes":{"Holy Rain Spray":"<p>(8 spirit splash vs. unholy creatures)</p>"}},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/U4q3wiF5enw2wjCo":{"lang":["Aklo","Fey","Sakvroth"],"flavor":"<p>The constructs that would come to be known as cranklejacks were built long ago by a conclave of nymphs to protect the beautiful places of the world. Unfortunately, gremlins love to tinker, break, and corrupt, and over the centuries, they managed to reimagine these once-noble protectors in their own image. Dubbed cranklejacks, the resulting constructs are unreliable, mercurial, and random, interfering with the use of magic around them while barely able to predict the results of their own attacks.</p><p>Still, cranklejacks don't have the full malevolence of a true gremlin, and, as they are intelligent, it can be possible to appeal to their reason. Even so, they have no desire to fulfill responsibilities as guardians any longer, and even those who don't follow the gremlins' path of destruction are grateful to gremlins for freeing them from that yoke.</p><h2>Tanglejacks</h2><p>Either through a quirk in their creation or a conscious effort to return to their roots, some cranklejacks possess a stronger connection to the natural world than the rest of their kin. These so-called \"tanglejacks\" replace their hammer Strike with a sickle that deals 2d4 + 11 slashing damage and has the agile and trip traits and their impaling spike Strike with a vine that deals 2d8 + 11 bludgeoning damage and has a reach of 15 feet. Some tanglejacks might also substitute one or more of their innate spells with spells of equal or lower rank chosen from the primal spell list.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Spelltwisting Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>20 feet, creatures who Cast a Spell in the aura, including the cranklejack, must succeed at a DC 23 Will save or the spell becomes a surge of wild magic. Either roll on the Wild Magic table from Battlezoo Dragons: Misfit Dragons or on any other wild magic table you prefer.</p>"},{"n":"Chaotic Gizmos","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The cranklejack unleashes an unpredictable flurry of attacks that even the cranklejack themself can't predict. Roll 1d6 twice. For each roll of 1 or 2, nothing happens. For each roll of 3, the cranklejack makes a hammer Strike, for each roll of 4, the cranklejack make an impaling spike Strike, for each roll of 5, the cranklejack makes a saw Strike, and for each roll of 6, the cranklejack makes a volt fist Strike. If the cranklejack makes two Strikes, they can choose which order to make them.</p>"},{"n":"Twice for Bad Luck","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["misfortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The cranklejack hits the same foe with a second Strike on the same turn</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Once is a coincidence, but twice is just bad luck. The foe becomes extremely unlucky (DC 26 Will save; the foe must roll this Will save twice and take the worse result). On a failure, the creature must roll twice and take the worse result on all checks for 2 rounds.</p><p>Effect: Twice for Bad Luck</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/2dKNLlms0fpynzXf":{"lang":["Envisioning"],"notes":{"hp":"regeneration 15 (deactivated by spirit)","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic"},"flavor":"<p>Tempestuans are a perfect encapsulation of the contradictory elements in nature that create weather and define the seasons. They carry with them the bitterest polar cold and the heat that rises from planetary cores in equal measure and through these extremes they dedicate themselves to maintaining the weather systems of entire worlds.</p><p>Physically, a tempestuan appears like a violent storm brought to life because that is what they are. Though tempestuans typically confine these storms into a narrow sphere that surrounds their bodies, they unleash the storms' full wrath on anyone who threatens their crucial work.</p><h2>The Greater Good</h2><p>When a tempestuan determines that a world's weather systems require correction, they'll inflict whatever natural disasters they deem necessary to restore equilibrium. These corrections are performed with little regard to the life on the planet itself, as to a tempestuan, the overall stability of an entire ecosystem is more important than a single life... or even millions of lives if necessary.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 1,875/4,300/8,000 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Diplomacy, Nature, Occultism, Religion, Society, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, body, bane, burning barbs, charisma, dexterity, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (cold, electricity, fire, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, ice, mind, mind shield, mirrored, music, sensory (darkvision), spells (Divine: Control Weather, Control Weather, Fireball, Frostbite, Ignition, Lightning Bolt, Punishing Winds, Rushing Gust, Wrathful Storm), strength, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Eye of the Storm","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["air","aura","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>25 feet. The tempestuan is always surrounded by an aura of breathable air. In the absence of magical weather effects, the air within 20 feet of the tempestuan is still, while the air between 20 and 25 feet away from them is constantly experiencing hurricane-force winds. The winds repel creatures and objects, making physical ranged attacks with typical arrows impossible and imposing a –4 circumstance penalty to ranged attacks with siege weapons and other similarly large projectiles. Moving through the winds to exit or enter the eye of the storm requires a successful DC 35 Athletics check. Creatures that enter or exit the aura due to the tempestuan's movement do not need to attempt an Athletics check. The tempestuan can choose to exclude any number of creatures within this aura from their spells and abilities.</p>"},{"n":"Quiet Wind","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet that the tempestuan can see uses a spell or other effect with the air trait</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> the tempestuan attempts to counteract the triggering spell or effect (counteract modifier +35). If the counteract check succeeds, the caster takes 3d10 persistent sonic damage. If the check fails, the caster is temporarily immune to Quiet Wind for 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Unleash Storm","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","divine","electricity"],"f":"","d":"<p>The tempestuan unleashes the pent-up energy in the hurricane that surrounds them. All creatures in a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 40 Reflex save as they are struck by lightning and battered by wind. The wall of hurricane-force wind disappears from Eye of the Storm for 1d4 rounds, and the tempestuan can't use Unleash Storm again until the wall returns.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 4d12 electricity damage and is pushed 25 feet away from the tempestuan.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes 4d12 electricity damage and 10d6 bludgeoning damage and is pushed 50 feet away from the tempestuan.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double the amount of electricity and bludgeoning damage from a failure and is then pushed 100 feet away from the tempestuan.</p>"},{"n":"Master of Storms","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The tempestuan can cast the Control Weather ritual in one hour and doesn't require a secondary caster.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Push 30 Feet","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Push in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Shove the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Improved Push lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pushed on a success to that distance.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/LDWiSl3QVxJjuisU":{"flavor":"<p>Terrordactyls are unusual cousins to pterosaurs, with shark-like hammerheads and tough black hides that absorb light and make them difficult to see in shadowy places. Their sturdy, toothy beaks are well-adapted to crushing hard-shelled prey, whether it be insects or armored travelers.</p><p>Terrordactyls roost upside down in caves or on rocky cliffs, where they prepare to ambush potential prey passing below. Against large groups, they often prefer to swoop down and take a bite out of each creature, testing which is tastiest. Once they pick their favorite morsel, a terrordactyl tears it apart with their sharp talons.</p><p>Due to their voracious appetites, terrordactyls are often solitary hunters that zealously guard their favored territories. They bury their eggs in concealed rocky crevices, often in caves or cliff faces. Terrordactyls who are nesting or who are raising young are particularly fierce, as they remove any potential threats to their offspring and gather additional prey to feed them.</p><h2>Territories of Terror</h2><p>In addition to natural rocky features, terrordactyls occasionally make their homes in abandoned ruins. These frightening creatures have been responsible for more than one tale of unearthly screaming emanating from an abandoned castle. For such arrangements to persist long-term, however, the ruin needs to provide sufficient food. When wandering wildlife fails to suffice, adventurers coming to investigate rumors of hauntings might provide supplemental nutrition. In time, the victims of terrordactyl attacks might just make those rumors about lingering ghosts a reality.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), armor (medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Intimidation, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, darkness, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (low-light vision), wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Startling Appearance","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The terrordactyl's unsettling appearance transcends language. It does not take a penalty on its checks to Demoralize for not sharing a language with its target.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Pursue Prey","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> An enemy within the terrordactyl's reach attempts to move away from the terrordactyl</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The terrordactyl keeps pace with the retreating foe. The terrordactyl Flies up to its fly Speed, following the enemy and keeping it in reach throughout its movement until the enemy stops moving or the terrordactyl has moved its full fly Speed.</p>"},{"n":"Drop From Above","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The terrordactyl is Hiding while hanging upside down from a surface that is at least 15 feet off the ground, such as a cave ceiling or a rocky cliff face</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The terrordactyl swoops down, Flying up to twice its Speed and then it makes a jaws or talon Strike against a creature in its reach.</p>"},{"n":"Swooping Bite","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The terrordactyl Flies, making jaws Strikes against any number of creatures that are adjacent to its path. Its multiple attack penalty applies to these Strikes normally.</p>"},{"n":"Blend with Shadows","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Terrordactyls are particularly adept at Hiding in shadowy areas. They have the Concealed condition in dim light against creatures with low-light vision (but not creatures with darkvision), and in darkness against creatures with darkvision (but not creatures with greater darkvision).</p>"},{"n":"Terrifying Ambush","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The sudden appearance of a terrordactyl terrifies its prey. When a terrordactyl hits a creature that is Off-Guard against it with a Strike, that creature takes 1d6 additional precision damage and must succeed at a DC 24 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ki4PBlk1PDQVXEG1":{"lang":["Chthonian","Common","Diabolic","Empyrean","Fey","Melodian","Necril","Requian"],"gear":["Soul Feather (10)"],"flavor":"<p>A thananycti cloak themselves in the trappings of psychopomps, calling out to lost souls with a soft, sorrowful melody, beckoning them to rest. But this is merely a ruse, utilizing their masterful lies and fey magic to conceal their true intentions—trapping the soul and fusing them into the thananycti itself.</p><h2>Soul Games</h2><p>Thananycti enjoy building elaborate charades, earning acclaim and veneration from settlements for removing restless and dangerous spirits. But as the souls of both the dead and the living begin to disappear, questions often arise, and those asking those questions often disappear as well. Eventually, they lose interest in the charade, and they shift their strategies, launching a campaign of terror and bloodshed that culminates only when no survivors remain to share the story.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5,000/12,000/22,500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Deception, Diplomacy, Occultism, Performance, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, death, energy resistant (vitality, void), life, lifeward armor, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), spells (Occult: Disappearance, Dissonance, Figment, Shred Soul, Truesight, Wails of the Damned, Wave of Despair), strength, unholy, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"False Guide","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The thananycti's magic repels efforts to pierce their disguise. They automatically attempt a counteract check (counteract modifier +35) against effects or abilities that would determine their traits. On a success, the effect seems to work but falsely claims that they are a monitor without the unholy trait.</p>"},{"n":"Take Under Wing","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","occult","unholy"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> At least one of the thananycti's soul feathers doesn't contain a soul</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The thananycti traps the soul of a creature of up to 18th level in an empty soul feather on one of their wings. This functions as seize soul, using a feather in place of a valuable item, except the target must be either a creature who died within the last minute while drained from Sorrowful Melody or an incorporeal undead that is drained from Sorrowful Melody. When a living creature's soul trapped, its body swiftly turns to dust. The feathers generally can't be destroyed while attached to a living thananycti, but an adjacent creature can pluck a soul feather by spending two actions and succeeding on a DC 40 Thievery check.</p>"},{"n":"Spirit Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A thananycti's Strikes have the benefit of a <em>ghost touch</em> property rune and deal an additional 3d6 void damage to living creatures or 3d6 vitality damage to undead.</p>"},{"n":"Sorrowful Melody","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","incapacitation","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The thananycti sings an eerie song. Each living or undead creature within a 60-foot emanation must attempt a DC 38 Will save. Despite being a mental effect, this ability affects mindless undead.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is temporarily immune to Sorrowful Melody for 24 hours.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is Stupefied 1 for one round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is stupefied 1 for one minute and Drained 1. If the target was already drained, the value of the condition increases by 1 (to a maximum of Drained 4). Additionally, the creature is entranced by the thananycti and must spend the first action on its next turn to move closer to the thananycti as expediently as possible while avoiding obvious dangers. If it is already adjacent to the thananycti, it instead spends its first action standing still.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the creature must spend all of its actions on its next turn to move toward the thananycti, standing still once it is adjacent.</p>"},{"n":"Devour Soul","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["occult","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> A soul has been trapped in a soul feather for 24 hours</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The thananycti consumes the soul. The soul is utterly destroyed, and the thananycti regains HP equal to three times the creature's level.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/TjKN1EdH5UAHUUz8":{"flavor":"<p>Time warps are an innate defense mechanism of the timestream that can arise in the aftermath of excessive use of time magic or intrusion into the Dimension of Time. Lacking complex minds of their own, time warps instinctively attempt to stabilize the timestream to prevent any further damage or degradation. While time warps are actively aggressive to the perpetrators of the instability, excising these instabilities from the timeline, they can be equally dangerous to bystanders who've become swept up in the unstable region of time. Anyone caught within the maelstrom of temporal energies that a time warp produces is subjected to mental and physical distress as their minds struggle to handle the quick fluctuations in temporal perception and the entropic forces of accelerated aging and decay.</p><h2>Temporal Tornadoes</h2><p>Despite the fact that they are technically alive, time warps are natural forces, and you can no more reason with them than you can with tornadoes. Atemporal creatures, they can linger indefinitely in the eddies of uncontrolled time magic, and they're just as likely to sweep up long-future innocent travelers—or well-meaning chronomancers trying to put the world to rights—as they are to punish the authors of the original calamity.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 125/275/500 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> perception item, skill items (Acrobatics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality, void), force, fortification, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), throwing, time, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Time Magic Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a time warp is targeted by or in the area of a spell that has the time trait or that grants the Quickened or Slowed conditions, it takes 5d8 force damage instead of the spell's normal effects. If the spell requires an attack roll, this vulnerability only applies if the attack roll hits.</p>"},{"n":"Decelerate Time","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","mental","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The time warp decelerates time in area around it, slowing nearby creatures and causing them mental distress. Creatures in a 30-foot emanation take 6d6 mental damage and experience additional effects based on the results of a DC 29 Will save. The time warp can't use this ability again until it uses Accelerate Time.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage, and they are also Slowed 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage, and they are also slowed 1 and Stupefied 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage, and they are also Slowed 2 and Stupefied 2 for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Accelerate Time","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["force","manipulate","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p>The time warp accelerates time in the area around it, quickening nearby creatures and subjecting them to entropic forces. Creatures in a 30-foot emanation take 6d6 force damage and experience additional effects based on the results of a DC 29 Fortitude save. Creatures that are Quickened from can only use the extra action to Stride or Strike. The time warp can't use this ability again until it uses Decelerate Time.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes no damage and is quickened for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage and is quickened for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage and is quickened and Enfeebled 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double damage and is Enfeebled 2 for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Disrupt Chronomancy","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["mental","occult","time"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The time warp hits a target with a time lash Strike, and the target is affected by a magical effect that has the time trait or that grants the Quickened or Slowed condition</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The time warp attempts to remove the effect from that creature, rolling a counteract check against the effect (counteract modifier +21). If successful, the effect ends on the creature (but not any other affected creatures), and the creature takes 4d8 mental damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/SgOQYk15XW8eDCaV":{"lang":["Common","Can't speak any language"],"flavor":"<p>Originating from the dreams of children, a toyblox appears to the untrained eye to be a mundane toy box that can mysteriously produce any toy. Toybloxes feed on proximity to children's imaginative thoughts. Most toybloxes form a bond with a specific child, whom they entertain, inspire, and protect from danger.</p><h2>No Time to Play</h2><p>As children develop more responsibilities and have less time for play, toybloxes do what they can to encourage them to retain their sense of wonder and imagination. Still, most toybloxes depart for new opportunities at some point in the child's teenage years, though those who look back fondly on their experiences might choose to entertain that child's younger relatives or even their future children.</p><p>Toyblox that remain bonded to a child for several years sometimes develops additional abilities to manipulate their surroundings to fit that child's desires. If not handled carefully, this growth can lead to towns becoming unstable playgrounds of oozing nonsense.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Occultism, Society, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), fortification, intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), space, space shield, spells (Occult: Mirage), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Plush Defense","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature targets the toyblox with an attack and the toyblox can see the attacker</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The toyblox manifests a plush toy between itself and the incoming blow, gaining a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against the triggering attack.</p>"},{"n":"Toy Chest","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The toyblox has a hollow interior, which it can use to store up to 25 Bulk worth of items without being encumbered. It can release any number of objects held within by spending a manipulate action. It can also allow other creatures to take what it is holding. When the toyblox is within a creature's reach, a creature can Interact with the toyblox to stow or retrieve items as if it were a mundane box. If the toyblox is not willing to have the object stowed or retrieved, the creature must first succeed at a DC 25 Athletics check.</p>"},{"n":"Temporary Toy","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>1 minute. The toyblox conjures forth a temporary toy, which must have a total size of 1 cubic foot or smaller and lasts for 1 hour. The toy can include multiple pieces, such as a jigsaw puzzle, and it can include moving parts. The toy is sturdy enough to survive typical use, though it isn't usually suitable for use as an improvised weapon. It can take the form of a common level 0 piece of adventuring gear with a value of 1 gp or less, but it can't typically replicate more complex or expensive items.</p>"},{"n":"Tidy Up","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The toyblox tidies up its environment. All unattended objects within 30 feet fly into the toyblox's internal toy chest. If the items would exceed the toy chest's bulk limit, the toyblox chooses which items to leave behind. Non-magical dust, dirt, and grime disappear from all objects and surfaces within the range. The toyblox attempts a counteract check (counteract modifier +12) against magical sources of mess in the area, such as the grease spell. If the counteract check fails, it can't attempt to Tidy Up that same effect a second time.</p>"},{"n":"Put That Away!","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The toyblox last action was a successful slamming lid Strike against a creature of size Large or smaller</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The toyblox disapproves of the use of dangerous objects like weapons. The toyblox attempts to Disarm the creature, ignoring the typical requirement of having a free hand. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the toyblox's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Conjure Guardian","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["occult","summon"],"f":"","d":"<p>The toyblox conjures a giant toy soldier, which uses the statistics of animated armor. It can Sustain the toy soldier for up to 1 minute. The soldier is metaphysically linked to the toyblox's life force. If the toy soldier is reduced to 0 Hit Points, the toyblox takes 10 untyped damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/jAaTydbGggb8hUgN":{"lang":["Aetheran","Astral","Common","Plus all languages spoken by its creator"],"lore":[["Creator Lore",13]],"gear":["Ring of Sigils"],"flavor":"<p>Practitioners of magic often rely on the convenience of <em>telekinetic hand</em> in place of their own hands to interact with faraway or unpleasant objects. In rare cases, this spell can take on a life of its own. Over time, this new consciousness becomes aware of the drudgery of its existence but is unable to communicate or control its movements. But if enough resentment builds up within the sapient spell, the hand becomes material, a humanoid head and neck sprout from its wrist, and a treacherous telekinetic hand is born.</p><p>Born from misery, this unfortunate creature's greatest desire is to make its creator's life miserable from the shadows before inflicting a slow and cruel demise. Typically, the hand begins by haunting its creator with recurring nightmares of its fingers strangling them. The hand will go to any length to achieve its aim, from hiring mercenaries to plotting elaborate deathtraps to making pacts with fiends.</p><p>While a typical hand wears a Ring of Sigils, hands adore jewelry and can be bribed into temporary service with more valuable rings or distracted from their quest by an opportunity to strangle someone for their jewelry.</p><p>Once a treacherous telekinetic hand's creator has died, the hand might take over its home and settle into a slothful life, relying on their own <em>telekinetic hand</em> spell to handle the labor they are so sick of performing themself. Alternatively, the hand might expand its ring collection and hunt for additional accouterments. However, if the hand was not the one to snuff out its creator's life, its jealousy toward whoever was is almost guaranteed to turn violent.</p><h2>Hand to Hand</h2><p>Tales tell of a treacherous telekinetic hand whose own <em>telekinetic hand</em> spell gained sentience and turned against its creator. Far from sympathizing with its creation's plight, the original hand strove to destroy its rebellious servant, just as the servant battled the master. Legend has it that the two hands still seek each other's lives, engaged in a plane-spanning thumb war with no end.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Intimidation, Occultism, Stealth, Survival, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (force, spirit, vitality, void), force, intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Occult: Message, Nightmare, Phantom Pain, Telekinetic Hand, Telekinetic Projectile), strength, wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Creator's Memories","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hand gains a +2 circumstance bonus to Perception checks when it Seeks its creator, to Survival checks when it Tracks its creator, and to damage rolls when it Strikes its creator.</p>"},{"n":"Scramble","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The treacherous telekinetic hand is targeted by a melee attack by an attacker it can see</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The treacherous telekinetic hand gains a +2 circumstance bonus to AC against the triggering attack. After the attack resolves, the hand Strides, Climbs, or Flies up to 10 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Choke. Choke. CHOKE!","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The hand deals 2d6 bludgeoning damage to a creature that it has Grabbed or Restrained (DC 20 basic Fortitude save). If the creature is restrained, the treacherous telekinetic hand also attempts a check to Demoralize it.</p>"},{"n":"Squeeze Throat","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that is Grabbed by the treacherous telekinetic hand has difficulty speaking or swallowing and must spend an extra action to perform any action that requires speaking, such as casting spells.</p>"},{"n":"Tenacious Strangler","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A treacherous telekinetic hand can grapple creatures up to size Medium, and it can grapple its creator no matter how large they are. However, the hand's grip is particularly easy to dislodge with assistance. If a creature is Grabbed or Restrained by the hand and another creature succeeds at a check to Aid their Escape, the grabbed or restrained creature can immediately attempt to Escape as a reaction.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/aNpCv8zW8bJGTH47":{"lang":["Thalassic"],"lore":[["Deep Sea Lore",28]],"notes":{"per":"heatsense (imprecise) 120 feet"},"flavor":"<p>In the deepest reaches of the ocean, where the pressure would crush most creatures and light is an alien concept, elementals known as trench lords reign above their lesser kin.</p><p>Trench lords typically live around hydrothermal vents, with boiling temperatures, toxic chemicals, and immense pressure found in the deepest waters, making their homes inhospitable to nearly all others. They often go centuries without encountering another sapient being, peacefully enjoying the ocean currents. When they do encounter creatures from the surface, they stealthily observe them and take violent action at the slightest hint that these strange beings might endanger the environmental balance of their oceans.</p><p>The few trench lords that don't dwell in the deep sea live in environs with similar conditions, such as active volcanoes becoming fledgling islands, hot springs, and pressurized vats used in mass alchemical production. Such trench lords are often more varied in behavior; one in a hot spring might enjoy the company of humanoids, while one newly formed from volcanic activity might be aggressive toward all life.</p><h2>It Takes Two</h2><p>While a single trench lord tries to preserve the status quo in the oceans and is a force for stability, two trench lords in close proximity can breed wholesale destruction. When these two usually solitary creatures discover the joys of communication—or the anguish of forced contact with an enemy—they often begin to engineer the birth of other trench lords by causing underwater eruptions, either to create new friends or to recruit allies in their battle with their rival. Several volcanic archipelagos began as the joint project of two trench lords.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 560/1,250/2,300 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Lore [Deep Sea], Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, burning barbs, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, poison, poisonous armor, strength, throwing, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Heatsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Trench lords sense all nearby sources of heat, such as hydrothermal vents and living creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Waterbound","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When not touching water, the trench lord is Slowed 1 and can't use reactions.</p>"},{"n":"Benthic Compression","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["primal","water"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature spends an action to move into a square within 15 feet trench lord while underwater</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The trench lord momentarily pressurizes the water around the creature, exposing its body to rapid compression and decompression. The creature takes 2d10 bludgeoning damage (DC 34 Fortitude save). On a failed save, it becomes Drained 1 (or Drained 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Sulfide Cloud","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet. A creature that starts its turn in the aura takes 2d6+7 poison damage (DC 31 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Improved Push 15 feet","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Push in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Shove the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Improved Push lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pushed on a success to that distance.</p></p>"},{"n":"Improved Pull 15 Feet","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The trench lord hits with their surge Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The trench lord uses Pull.</p><hr><p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a success with a Strike that lists Pull in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Reposition the creature, moving it closer to the monster. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Pull lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pulled on a success to that distance.</p></p>"},{"n":"Boiling Assault","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>Fire damage dealt by the trench lord results from volatile chemical reactions. It ignores half of the target's resistance to fire.</p>"},{"n":"Riptide Rush","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The trench lord moves up to 500 feet in a straight line through the water without triggering reactions. Creatures within 15 feet of a square that the trench lord passes through during this movement must succeed at a DC 34 Reflex save or be Off-Guard for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Torrent","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","primal","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>The trench lord vastly increases their internal pressure before releasing it in a concentrated blast of boiling water. All creatures in a 90-foot line take 4d12 bludgeoning plus 2d12 fire damage (DC 34 basic Reflex save). A creature that fails this save is pushed 30 feet away from the trench lord, or 60 feet on a critical failure.</p>"},{"n":"Vent","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fire","poison","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The trench lord's body expels the superheated chemicals that churn within. All creatures in a 30-foot emanation take 6d6 poison plus 6d6 fire damage (DC 34 basic Fortitude save). A creature that fails this save is also Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure). The trench lord can't use Vent again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/OwOUJlqRJuIcWzlK":{"lang":["Aklo","Common","Pyric","Spirituan"],"flavor":"<p>Ugadalus, also known as root walkers, are sadistic creatures comprised of gnarled wood with large masks for faces. According to legend, the first ugadalu was a warrior whose soul was bound to his mask after a grisly ritual, though how they've since grown into an entire species of creature is unclear. Did the first ugadalu perform the same ritual on others? Or did the spell, once performed, plough a furrow in the world's magic, making it easier for similar creatures to occur naturally?</p><p>Ugadalus subjugate small settlements and rule through terror. They respond violently to anything that would dare to threaten their dominion. An ugadalu doesn't care for the identity of this intruder. Instead, they treat all invaders equally, whether they are dangerous monsters on the prowl or heroes seeking to free the people from tyranny. When not actively issuing commands to those they bully and intimidate, ugadalus spend much of their time in the form of wooden statues, preparing to ambush and frighten whoever is unfortunate enough to walk by. In battle, ugadalus unleash a continuous stream of flowing fire from their open mouths, which they can flare into a powerful burst of flame and spew at their foes.</p><p>Though they enjoy inflicting fear upon others, ironically, ugadalus can't bear the experience of being afraid themselves. Not only that, what they fear more than anything else lies buried deep within their own natures. As such, they are rattled by the sight of others of their kind, and even their own reflections are intolerable.</p><h2>No Self-Reflection</h2><p>Ugadalus relish their roles as rulers of their small domains. They issue sweeping decrees, both for the joy of watching others do their bidding and for the thrill of punishing those who stubbornly—or unwittingly—disobey. Some of the most common ugadalu laws are the banning of reflective surfaces: mirrors must be shattered, silver melted, and clear ponds muddied or emptied. Some go further and order the destruction of all writing implements, on the grounds that they could be used to draw a representation of an ugadalu.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Crafting, Intimidation, Nature, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, burning barbs, charisma, constitution, death, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), fire, fire shield, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Fear, Vision of Death), spirit, strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Fear Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>An ugadalu is their own worst nightmare. When an ugadalu starts their turn frightened, they take 1d8 mental damage. If an ugadalu that was not already frightened sees an ugadalu within 30 feet, whether it be another ugadalu, a creature wearing an ugadalu's mask, or a clear reflection of their own face, the ugadalu becomes Frightened 1. While within 30 feet of this fearful sight, the ugadalu can't reduce the value of their frightened condition below 1.</p>"},{"n":"Jump Scare","a":"1","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature moves within 30 feet of the ugadalu while the ugadalu is in Statue Form</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> Initiative has not yet been rolled</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The ugadalu attempts an Intimidation check to Demoralize the triggering creature, using their Intimidation result for their initiative.</p>"},{"n":"Feast on Fear","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugadalu's jaws Strike deals an additional 2d6 precision damage to frightened creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Spew Flame","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugadalu concentrates their stream of fire and emits a burst of flames that deals 8d6 fire damage in a 30-foot cone (DC 25 basic Reflex save). They can't use Spew Flame again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Statue Form","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","polymorph","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The ugadalu transforms, taking the form of a wooden statue of a warrior. Perception checks don't typically reveal the ugadalu's true nature, but a creature who examines the statue attempts a secret DC 25 Crafting check; on a success, they are able to determine that the statue is alive. While in statue form, an ugadalu can observe everything around them, but they can't receive a result better than a failure on Reflex save, so if they roll a success or critical success, they get a failure instead. They can't act other to Dismiss this effect or use Jump Scare.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/nlYfiMrcHJbJ1vbp":{"lang":["Arboreal","Common","Fey","Speak with plants"],"lore":[["Forest Lore",30]],"flavor":"<p>Deep within old-growth forests, ancient arboreals can spend countless cycles meditating on the changes each season brings, eventually absorbing the essence of this natural cycle into their bodies. Unlike arboreal archives, who actively focus on accumulating knowledge, unbound arboreals prefer to hone their understanding of the world through self-reflection, a difference in philosophy that leads to impassioned debates that can last for decades.</p><h2>Seasonal Variation</h2><p>Arboreals living in areas without four seasons might have different abilities than those described here. For example, an unbound arboreal in an equatorial region might have powers like Spring's Blossoming and Summer's Tangle during the rainy season. In the dry season, they could have a power similar to Autumn's Abscission and the ability to dry out nearby creatures.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Diplomacy, Lore [Forest], Nature, Occultism, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), ice, life, lifeward armor, mirrored, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Control Weather, Speak with Plants), strength, sturdy, tangle shield, time, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Improved Pull 50 Feet","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The unbound arboreal hits with their boundless root Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal uses Pull.</p><hr><p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a success with a Strike that lists Pull in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Reposition the creature, moving it closer to the monster. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Pull lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pulled on a success to that distance.</p></p>"},{"n":"False Felling","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"once per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per day</p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The unbound arboreal is reduced to 0 Hit Points, and their wounded value is 2 or less</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal's trunk splits, crashing down on their foes before rewinding time to remain standing. All creatures in a 20-foot line take 6d8 bludgeoning damage (DC 33 basic Reflex save). The arboreal avoids being knocked Unconscious and remains at 1 Hit Point, but their wounded value increases by 1.</p>"},{"n":"Stumbling Roots","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts to uses an action with the move trait to exit a square within 15 feet of the unbound arboreal</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal raises the base of one of their roots out of the ground, attempting to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the unbound arboreal's multiple attack penalty.</p>"},{"n":"Autumn's Abscission","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The unbound arboreal is influenced by autumn</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal unleashes a shower of razor-sharp leaves, dealing 8d6 slashing damage to all creatures in a 15-foot emanation (DC 36 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Spring's Blossoming","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["healing","primal","vitality"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The unbound arboreal is influenced by spring</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal draws on spring's new growth, shedding healing petals. The unbound arboreal and all living creatures of the arboreal's choice in a 15-foot emanation recover 6d8 healing Hit Points, and all undead creatures within 15 feet take 6d8 vitality damage (DC 36 basic Fortitude save).</p>"},{"n":"Summer's Tangle","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["plant","primal"],"f":"once per round","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per round</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The unbound arboreal is influenced by summer</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal spreads summer's wild growth, causing all squares in a 15-foot emanation to become greater difficult terrain until the start of their next turn. The unbound arboreal is not impeded by this growth. In addition, a creature that ends its turn in an affected space must succeed at a DC 33 Reflex save or become Grabbed by the unbound arboreal until the end of that creature's next turn (Escape).</p>"},{"n":"Winter's Withering","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["cold","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The unbound arboreal is influenced by winter</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal invokes the withering chill of winter, enervating those in their vicinity. Each creature in a 15-foot emanation takes 10d6 cold damage (DC 36 basic Fortitude save); creatures that fail additionally become Slowed 1 for 1 round, or for 1 minute on a critical failure.</p>"},{"n":"Confluence of Seasons","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The unbound arboreal's turn begins</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The unbound arboreal can control their passage through the cycle of seasons if they don't want to remain in their current season. The arboreal becomes influenced by either the previous season in the cycle or the next. The influencing season determines which abilities they can use. An unbound arboreal typically begins an encounter influenced by the current season.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ggwd8gqyyYCtdb5f":{"flavor":"<p>This animated bowl of crab, rice, and egg soup is the unfortunate result of an arcane cooking experiment on a massive serving dish. It can conjure vestiges of the crabs that were cooked within it, usually in the form of pincers made out of scalding hot broth. It gathers this same broth into a wave beneath it, traveling overland on the wave's crest.</p><p>Occasionally, one can spot a vengeful crab bowl roaming the countryside, leading people to speculate that it seeks out its reckless creator to exact revenge. Whatever the case may be, the bowl has an instinctive hatred for humanoids, attacking them on sight.</p><p>The vengeful crab bowl is not the only creature to arise from a failed cooking experiment; similar soup bowls and other culinary creatures made from the same or different ingredients can be found across the world. Rumors tell of a crafty group of adventurers who defeated a bowl of vengeful chicken soup by simply spilling the bowl over, and another tale tells of an animated meat skewer that impales its foes.</p><h2>Kitchen Battles</h2><p>The appearance of multiple soup-based dishes gaining hateful sentience at once has struck some observers as being too coincidental to be believed. Investigating their common causes, they found that many of the vengeful bowls were said to have originated from cooking contests—and that the same contestant participated in each of these contests, never winning the prize. It's speculated that this contestant might be a mediocre chef but an excellent spellcaster, determined to wreak vengeance on anyone who defeats them in culinary contests, or perhaps a new type of gremlin distantly related to the pilatatry.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 30/65/125 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, spirit, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, sensory (low-light vision), strength, throwing, water, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Bite Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>Due to being made out of food, the vengeful crab bowl takes 5 additional damage from jaws Strikes, fangs Strikes, or other similar Strikes with a creature's mouth. When it is Grabbed or Restrained by a creature's jaws, fangs, or mouth, the DC to Escape is increased by 2.</p>"},{"n":"Spill Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a vengeful crab bowl is knocked Prone, some of its broth spills out onto the ground. It becomes Drained 1; this is cumulative with other drained conditions from Spill Vulnerability, to a maximum of Drained 4. Additionally, all creatures within a 10-foot emanation take 2d6 fire damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Goopy Egg","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature hit by the vengeful crab bowl's egg drop Strike is covered in sticky egg goop, taking a –10 circumstance penalty to their Speeds for 1 minute or until they move a total of 40 feet.</p><p>Effect: Goopy Egg</p>"},{"n":"Boil Over","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vengeful crab bowl flies into a boiling rage, superheating its broth. For the next minute, it takes a –1 circumstance penalty to its AC, but deals an additional 1d6 fire damage to creatures it hits with a Strike. Additionally, any creature that hits the crab soup with an unarmed Strike or otherwise touches it during this time takes 3d6 fire damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Rice Ball","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","fire","water"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vengeful crab bowl launches a glob of steaming hot rice with a range of 60 feet. When the glob hits the ground, it explodes in a 20-foot burst of hot steam that persists for 2 rounds. Creatures that start their turn in the area take 5d6 fire damage (DC 24 basic Reflex save). All creatures within the steam become Concealed, and all creatures outside the steam become concealed to creatures within it. The vengeful crab bowl can't use Rice Ball for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/FEGVklTE5yEyBocO":{"lang":["Common","Can't speak any language"],"notes":{"speed":"mudstrider"},"flavor":"<p>Animated by their sheer hatred of being trodden on every day, these doormats have had <em>enough</em> of being wiped with dirty feet. They retaliate the only way they know how: by subjecting their targets to mud, filth, and endless stomping. The magic that gives these carpets a spark of life also increases their size, allowing them to wrap fully around and crush their original owners. Only legless creatures like snakes and worms are spared from the vengeful doormats' wrath.</p><p>Vengeful doormats typically arise in rural towns, where muddy boots are commonplace. Such doormats are a nuisance, but locals can band together to deal with them. However, gigantic doormats found in mansions and castles can be a truly fearsome force, requiring trained combatants and cleaning crews to slay them. Some truly cruel rug makers purposefully spawn these creatures, selling their corpses to nobles looking for a status symbol and still-animated doormats to sadists wanting to leave a nasty surprise at a front door.</p><p>Vengeful doormats have the amazing capability of attracting animated brooms, which then continuously dust the doormats off. Some arcane scholars theorize that animated brooms might be the key to a more ethical way to manage the vengeance of the doormats, as properly dusted doormats are markedly less aggressive.</p><p>In some cases, vengeful doormats turn back into normal doormats after achieving their retribution. This transformation typically occurs after the vengeful doormat has killed or severely maimed its original owner, but the doormat is occasionally satisfied with simply covering the owner's body and everything they treasure in mud.</p><h2>Welcome Message</h2><p>For the unwary, there aren't a lot of clues that a vengeful doormat is anything out of the ordinary. A frequent tell-tale sign of a feral doormat is that it displays an embroidered message in which it vents its rage, from a pithy \"GO AWAY!\" to a more specific message, such as \"BILL WITHERS, IF YOU WALK ACROSS THIS THRESHOLD WITH THOSE MUDDY HOBNAILED BOOTS AGAIN, SO HELP ME, I WON'T BE HELD RESPONSIBLE!\" For creatures of rather limited intellect, vengeful doormats are surprisingly good spellers.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3.5/7/13 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, dexterity, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (spirit, vitality, void), sensory (darkvision), strength, throwing, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dirty Retribution","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["arcane","earth"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature succeeds at a melee Strike against the vengeful doormat</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The doormat angrily summons forth and flings dirt at the offending creature, attempting to disgust it. The creature must succeed at a DC 15 Fortitude save or be Sickened 1.</p>"},{"n":"Foot Vulnerability","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A vengeful doormat takes 2 additional damage from Strikes made with feet. A creature can Grapple a vengeful doormat without a free hand by entering the doormat's square and stepping on it.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Mud Spray","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once per hour</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The vengeful doormat shakes itself off, spraying massive quantities of mud out in a 15-foot cone. The muddy area is difficult terrain. The mud persists for 1 hour before reappearing on the doormat's body.</p>"},{"n":"Unwelcome Mat","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vengeful doormat flattens and folds its body down to the ground. Until the next time it acts, the vengeful doormat appears to be a mundane doormat. It has an automatic result of 27 on Deception checks and DCs to pass as a doormat.</p>"},{"n":"Mudstrider","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vengeful doormat moves at full speed through difficult terrain caused by mud.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/NMIvy0ScC2YkbEUI":{"lang":["Aklo","Common","Venenan"],"notes":{"saves":"+1 circumstance vs. poison","per":"emotionsense (imprecise) 60 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Venopaths, so named for their ability to telepathically inject venomous thoughts into the minds of others, are dangerous cavern-dwellers found in small communities deep underground. Their bodies are mostly hollow and humanoid-shaped, with tough, sinewy strands forming a cage-like structure around several dark, alien organs. Atop their bodies, their dark skulls barely contain their bulging brains.</p><p>Venopath communities tend to be relatively small, often only 6 to 8 members, since high concentrations of their corrosive thoughts indiscriminately kill most nearby creatures. Though venopaths have no regard for the lives of other species, they have no desire to waste potential food.</p><p>While they are cooperative within their small circles, venopaths reflexively despise outsiders. This hatred allows them to be prepared to defend their homes with deadly toxins at a moment's notice. Individual venopaths rarely step out of line, even in non-venopath organizations, as doing so in a community of their own people could result in group disapproval literally eating their brain from within.</p><p>Young venopaths must mature quickly, as venopath parents rarely allow their offspring to remain a part of their group for more than a few years. Though parents might not overtly contribute to their children's lives after this point, underground dwellers who attempt to prey on the newly exiled rarely survive the experience.</p><h2>Public Menace</h2><p>Confident in their ability to destroy any sentient creatures who cross them, venopaths don't make particular efforts to hide their presence from nearby prey. A venopath on an errand might walk calmly right through a nearby settlement, seemingly ignoring the consternation it causes—but marking particularly fearful or hostile onlookers as future dinners.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Deception, Diplomacy, Intimidation, Occultism)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (darkvision), throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"+1 Circumstance to All Saves vs. Poison","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Venomous Mind","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["mental","occult","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature uses a mental effect on the venopath, attempts to communicate telepathically with the venopath, or attempts to read the venopath's mind</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The venopath deals 5d6 poison damage to the triggering creature with a DC 26 basic Will save. The damage increases to 8d6 poison if the creature attempted to read the venopath's mind.</p>"},{"n":"Emotive Toxin","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["emotion","mental","occult","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Will</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 6 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 1d6 mental plus 1d6 poison damage, and Stupefied 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 2d6 mental plus 2d6 poison damage and Stupefied 2 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 3d6 mental plus 3d6 poison damage, and Stupefied 3 (1 round).</p>"},{"n":"Toxic Intimidation","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","emotion","fear","mental","occult","poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>The venopath chooses a creature within 30 feet and fills its thoughts with toxic imagery. The venopath attempts an Intimidation vs. Will DC check against the target's Will DC.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target takes 6d8 poison damage and becomes Frightened 2.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes 3d8 poison damage and becomes Frightened 1.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target is unaffected and is temporarily immune to Toxic Intimidation for 1 hour.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zGsgfo7RsM8Pebx9":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Melodian","Sussuran","Voltan"],"notes":{"per":"stormsight"},"flavor":"<p>Vernals are the tempestuous, fickle maestros of the four seasonal fey. Riding the storm winds and volatile weather fronts common in spring, vernals embody nature's potential for sudden and chaotic change, whether whimsical or tempestuous. Unlike other seasonals, vernals don't share the spotlight gladly with others of their own kind; when vernals happen to meet on the capricious winds of a storm, they engage in deafening sonic sky-duels to claim \"ownership\" rights over the spring squall. Vernals are quick to respond to challenges to their authority over the storms with a little bit of intimidation or violence, yet they just as quickly grow bored of opponents who lack the determination and skill to keep up with them. An attacking vernal's goal is to display their dominance and leave their opponent cowed and in awe, or at least to enjoy an exciting bout with a worthy adversary, rather than to annihilate an unworthy foe who wastes their thunder.</p><p>A vernal's music can seem discordant and cacophonous to the untrained ear, but remarkable artistry hides within the walls of sound torn loose from their crackling instruments. It is said that a vernal never plays the same song twice, always improvising and duetting with the lightning, thunder, hail-clatter, and sleet-noise of their accompanying storms. Humanoids of various ancestries have claimed the violent clash of a vernal's music resonates and gives voice to their own turbulent emotions, and some of these \"stormchasers\" rush to follow a vernal's tempest as it tours across the land and sea, in a dangerous attempt to find themselves.</p><hr><p>Seasonals are a cycle of four kinds of fey, each tied to one of the four seasons: vernals who embody spring's renewal and storming showers, aestivals who embody summer's lazy heat and burning passion, autumnals who embody the melancholy culling and reaping harvest of autumn, and hibernals who embody winter's quiet cheer.</p><p>Whatever the current season in the mortal world, seasonals wrap themselves in a metaphysical cloak of their own season's magic and carry the songs full of that season's surging emotions with them wherever they go in the mortal world or across the realm of Faerie. Whenever seasonals meet, shy hibernals typically prefer to amuse themselves by watching the antics of the other three kinds of fey, while vernals impatiently call for energetic contests, aestivals languidly nap until roused to fiery bursts of activity, and autumnals attempt to imbue a sense of stark responsibility in the rest of their compatriots. Exceptions exist, with those seasonals interacting with the others in ways unexpected based upon the stereotypes of their season.</p><h2>Storm Songs</h2><p>Vernals consider themselves the premier conductors of the elements, and meetings between them and other atmospheric musicians are sure to strike sparks. They have a particular rivalry with storm conductors. When the two meet, their twin cloud formations often remain fixed in place over some unfortunate locale, unleashing increasingly violent storms as the spirits engage in a battle for supremacy.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Intimidation, Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, dexterity, electricity, energy resistant (electricity, sonic, vitality), music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Lightning Bolt, Electric Arc, Know the Way, Lightning Bolt, Noise Blast, Rushing Gust, Shatter, Thunderstrike, Wall of Wind), wild, winged</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Booming Cloak","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p>Attempts to hinder the vernal's passage are met with a punishing crack of thunder. A creature that successfully Strikes the vernal with a reaction triggered by movement takes 4d6 sonic damage (DC 25 basic Fortitude save). On a failure, they are also Deafened for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Drown Out","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within 60 feet uses an ability with the auditory or sonic traits</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> With overwhelming sound, the vernal attempts to counteract the effect, with a counteract modifier of +17 and a counteract rank of 4.</p>"},{"n":"Thunderous Retreat","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["air","primal","sonic"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The vernal is adjacent to at least one enemy</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Letting loose a sonic boom, the vernal Strides or Flies twice to a space not adjacent to any enemies. All enemies adjacent to the vernal at any point take 6d6 sonic damage (DC 25 Fortitude save). Any creature that fails its save is pushed back 5 feet from the movement path (or 10 feet on a critical failure). The vernal can't use Thunderous Retreat again for 1d4 rounds.</p>"},{"n":"Lightning Improviser","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A vernal can cast their Electric Arc cantrip with a single action to target only one creature, or they can instead use three actions to target three creatures and increase the damage dice to d6s.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/zQSl56dEyy20YFhb":{"lang":["Requian","Can't speak any language, Manifest empathy"],"flavor":"<p>Ideally, graveyards are places of memory and repose, where the physical bodies of the dead can rest while their souls migrate to the afterlife. However, soul energy often pools and gathers in these places, and this excess soul energy is at risk of forming into incorporeal undead and restless spirits if left to its own devices. To help this energy pass on, cultures that respect the gods of life and death often place lanterns in graveyards to stand a silent vigil over the souls of the dead.</p><p>When enough soul energy gathers around one of these vigil lanterns, it can manifest a new consciousness devoted to the cause of shepherding lost souls. And when these consciousnesses bond with the energy of Purgatory, they can form a nascent psychopomp called a vigil flame, one of the few psychopomps to complete the process of creation in the mortal realm instead of Purgatory. These vigil flames lend their aid to both the living and the dead, but they despise undead as the ultimate insult to the natural order. Despite their benevolent demeanor, they are true servants of the cycle of life and death, and they don't hesitate to usher hesitant lost, restless, or malevolent souls into the afterlife by force.</p><p>Of all the undead that roam the world, none inspire vigil flames to fight as fiercely as spiritflame lanterns, which represent a perversion of their form and purpose.</p><h2>Grave Lights</h2><p>While a vigil flame's first duty is to the dead, they are often found leading the living as well. They sometimes pause their eternal war with undeath to escort a hapless traveler safely through undead-haunted wastes—or to beckon competent adventurers further into those wastes, in order to team up against an undead threat that the vigil flame can't defeat alone.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 18/45/80 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Diplomacy, Medicine, Occultism, Religion)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, burning barbs, charisma, death, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality, void), fire, fire shield, fortification, life, lifeward armor, light, spells (Divine: Heal, Holy Light, Revealing Light), throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Living Flame","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vigil flame is a literal flame contained in a lantern, and so it sheds bright light in a 60-foot radius and dim light in the next 60 feet.</p>"},{"n":"Manifest Empathy","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vigil flame can communicate on a rudimentary level with all intelligent creatures by moving their flame and changing their color. This communication is limited to basic concepts and emotions.</p>"},{"n":"Snuff the Flames","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["divine","fire","vitality","void"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The vigil flame is reduced to 0 Hit Points</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> In a last, bright flash, the vigil flame consumes themselves completely. All allies within 20 feet of the vigil flame regain 2d6 healing Hit Points, and all foes within 30 feet take 2d6 damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save); this damage is 2d6 void to living creatures or 2d6 vitality to undead.</p>"},{"n":"Stoke the Flames","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","fire","vitality","void"],"f":"","d":"<p>The vigil flame's flame burns brighter and hotter, both aiding those in need and punishing their foes. Living allies within a 20-foot burst gain a +1 status bonus to attack rolls, damage rolls, and saves against fear effects for 1 round. Living foes in the radius take 3d6 fire plus 3d6 void damage, and undead foes in the radius take 3d6 fire plus 3d6 vitality damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save). The vigil flame can't use Stoke the Flames again for 1d4 rounds.</p><p>Effect: Stoke the Flames</p>"},{"n":"Shepherd's Touch","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A vigil flame's Strikes always have the benefit of a <em>ghost touch</em> property rune, and they deal an additional 2d6 void damage to living creatures or an additional 2d6 vitality damage to undead.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Tz3SiS6dq1TVzzkz":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Jyoti","Spirituan"],"notes":{"per":"plantsense 60 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Vitatellas are nature spirits brimming with vital energy who dwell anywhere plants can grow. They are most common in forests, jungles, and marshes, but they can also be found underwater in kelp forests and traveling between oases in deserts. Vitatellas are drawn to areas where a lack of vital energy causes plant life to suffer, particularly in places where void energy has corrupted the natural environment. Once they take root, energy slowly seeps out of them and into their surroundings, causing foliage to sprout rapidly around them.</p><p>The foliage trails they leave in their wake start off green and luscious, but after a few days without the vitatella's assistance, plants that are not well adapted to their surroundings wither away. Though vitatellas can fly and pass through the earth, they feel unnerved when they aren't in contact with the ground's surface. They seldom choose to leave the surface unless they are in dire peril.</p><p>Because vitatellas are perpetually leaking life energy, they eventually run out and return to the earth. Vitatellas, near the end of their lifespan, become increasingly incorporeal before dissipating completely. For communities that have come to rely on a vitatella for their harvests, the spirit's disappearance is a devastating outcome. A few such communities have gone to terrible lengths to gather and deliver vital energy to their local vitatella, only to discover that the vitatella responds to the infusion with violent rage. To a vitatella, this is often seen as an insult and perversion to the natural order of life, and death.</p><h2>Unchecked Growth</h2><p>Vitatellas cause seedlings of all kinds to quicken and sprout, making no distinction between crops and weeds. While they are a blessing to starving communities, vitatellas can be a bane to well-tended farmlands and to rich folks' ornamental flower gardens. What's more, they look unkindly on those who don't appreciate their gifts; while they won't attack a farmer for weeding their unruly bounty, they look coldly on requests that they kindly leave.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Medicine, Nature, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (acid, cold, electricity, fire, sonic, vitality), fortification, life, lifeward armor, poison, poisonous armor, space, space shield, spells (Primal: Autumn's Howl, Create Food, Entangling Flora, Heal, Nature's Pathway, Wall of Thorns), spirit, tangle shield, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Plantsense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vitatella is aware of all plants within 60 feet as a precise sense. They can sense anything in contact with these plants as an imprecise sense.</p>"},{"n":"Growth Aura","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","plant","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>30 feet. Plants in the aura sprout and flourish. The area is difficult terrain for all creatures that are not vitatella. Each square of difficult terrain can be removed by spells or abilities that deal fire damage to an area. The vitatella can use a single concentrate action to regrow all destroyed plants.</p>"},{"n":"Pollen Cloud","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The vitatella causes large flowers to sprout and release pollen. The pollen floats in the vitatella's growth aura for 1 round. All creatures within the mist become Concealed, and all creatures outside the mist become concealed to creatures within it; the vitatella can see clearly through their own pollen. Each creature that enters the area or that starts its turn in the area must attempt a DC 26 Fortitude save. The vitatella can't use Pollen Cloud again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes 2d6 poison damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature becomes afflicted with Lung Clot Pollen at stage 1.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature becomes afflicted with Lung Clot Pollen at stage 2.</p>"},{"n":"Lung Clot Pollen","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["poison"],"f":"","d":"<p>While a creature who is afflicted by this poison attempts a verbal action, it must succeed at a DC 5 Flat or the action is lost</p><p><strong>Saving Throw</strong> DC 26 Fortitude</p><p><strong>Maximum Duration</strong> 5 rounds</p><p><strong>Stage 1</strong> 2d6 poison damage and Sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 2</strong> 3d6 poison damage and sickened 1 (1 round)</p><p><strong>Stage 3</strong> 4d6 poison damage and Sickened 2 (1 round)</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/6pFKNWj3Bfl9R50V":{"lang":["Common","Necril","Petran","Pyric"],"gear":["Magma Shield"],"flavor":"<p>Calamity knights are undead incarnations of various natural disasters. Some calamity knights see themselves as avatars of nature's vengeance. Others act as indiscriminate bringers of death and destruction. Whatever their motivations, calamity knights usually arise from individuals with deep ties to primal magic who die in the natural disaster that they will come to embody. The calamity knight detailed below has the power of a volcanic eruption. However, calamity knights can embody a broad range of other disasters, including earthquakes, famines, hurricanes, plagues, and tsunamis.</p><h2>Calamity Dragonrider</h2><p>Calamity knights sometimes form bonds with destructive dragons whose nature matches their own. For example, a calamity knight tied to volcanic eruptions might forge a connection with a crag linnorm or a volcanic primal dragon, with whom they collaborate to devastate an entire region. However, these alliances are often volatile, surviving only as long as both partners agree on their general objectives as well as the distribution of the spoils of their rampages. When a calamity knight and their draconic partner clash, the resulting battles leave scars on the landscape for generations to come.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), armor (heavy), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Intimidation, Nature, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> burning barbs, constitution, death, earth, earth armor, energy resistant (fire, void), fire, fire shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Volcanic Eruption), strength, throwing, unholy, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Eruptive Shield","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity knight carries a magma shield. In the knight's hands, the shield has Hardness 15, HP 120, and BT 60. The knight can spend 10 minutes to create a new Eruptive Shield; doing so destroys any existing shield.</p>"},{"n":"Overwhelming Heat","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>15 feet, 8d6 fire, DC 33 basic Fortitude</p>"},{"n":"Reactive Strike (Special)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity knight can use Reactive Strike when a creature within their reach takes persistent fire damage, in addition to its normal trigger. If the triggering creature already had persistent fire damage, the calamity knight triggers a secondary eruption. The triggering creature and all creatures in a 5-foot emanation take 2d10 fire damage (DC 36 basic Reflex).</p><hr><p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature within the monster's reach uses a manipulate action or a move action, makes a ranged attack, or leaves a square during a move action it's using.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts a melee Strike against the triggering creature. If the attack is a critical hit and the trigger was a manipulate action, the monster disrupts that action. This Strike doesn't count toward the monster's multiple attack penalty, and its multiple attack penalty doesn't apply to this Strike.</p></p>"},{"n":"Shield Block (Special)","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity knight gains 1 extra reaction each round, which they can use only to Shield Block.</p><hr><p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster has its shield raised and takes damage from a physical attack.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster snaps its shield into place to deflect a blow. The shield prevents the monster from taking an amount of damage up to the shield's Hardness. The monster and the shield each take any remaining damage, possibly breaking or destroying the shield.</p></p>"},{"n":"Volcanic Defense","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The Calamity knight Raises a Shield. All creatures in a 5-foot emanation take 2d10 fire damage and 2d10 persistent fire damage (DC 36 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Layering Lava","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As the calamity knight continually piles magma on a target, it becomes harder to remove. Whenever the calamity knight would deal persistent fire damage to a creature that already has persistent fire damage, the DC of the flat check to remove the persistent fire damage increases by 1 (to a maximum DC of 20).</p><p>Effect: Layering Lava</p>"},{"n":"Magma Prison","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["earth","fire","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity knight cools the magma on one of its foes into a rocky prison by transferring the heat into the foe's body. The knight chooses a creature within 60 feet who is taking persistent fire damage from one of their abilities. The creature takes 4d10 fire damage, becomes Clumsy 1, and takes a –10 status penalty to its Speeds until it Escapes. The persistent fire damage automatically ends.</p><p>Effect: Magma Prison</p>"},{"n":"Pyroclastic Rush","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["move"],"f":"","d":"<p>The calamity knight transforms into a pyroclastic flow. They travel up to 200 feet in a straight line, passing freely though creature's squares (though this movement can still trigger reactions). All creatures in their path take 7d6 fire plus 7d6 bludgeoning damage (DC 38 basic Reflex), and debris left behind makes the path difficult terrain. The calamity knight can't use Pyroclastic Rush for 1d4 rounds.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/e0Q5qWbdR1rIRm6y":{"flavor":"<p>At first glance, volt rams look like sheep. However, the branching scorch marks on their horns and their particularly fluffy wool are the first signs of the electric charge that dances across their bodies. Those who approach closer find their skin tingling and their hair standing on end.</p><p>The unique structure of a volt ram's wool allows them to generate and store static electricity with their movements and use it in various ways. As the charge grows, the volt ram's wool gathers into pointed spikes. These spikes are a sure sign that the electric charge has grown to dangerous levels. While a volt ram's fleece is dangerous if improperly handled, it is a valuable crafting component for magic items.</p><p>Beings with a natural resistance to electricity prize volt rams as livestock in order to harvest their fleece. Even those without such protections occasionally accept the risks inherent in keeping a flock. Reports from these eccentric shepherds state that volt rams are relatively easy to care for, all things considered, as the creatures' lighting is more than enough to scare off any would-be predators. A tool for safely releasing the ram's stored charge is an essential part of these shepherd's toolkits.</p><p>In the wild, volt rams travel in large flocks. They sometimes choose to accompany runebranded aurochs (Battlezoo Bestiary, page 79). Runebranded aurochs that live among volt rams often develop enhanced powers over electricity.</p><p>Both male and female volt rams have horns. Male volt rams are more direct and aggressive with potential threats to the flock, while female volt rams are more likely to store their charge as a threat display. Juvenile volt rams, sometimes called spark lambs, gradually develop their ability to hold a charge over their first year of life. Newborn lambs can't generate more than an unpleasant tingling sensation.</p><h2>Spring Showers</h2><p>In springtime, when young volt rams battle each other for dominance, wise shepherds know to wear their sturdiest rain gear. When a pair of volt rams charges each other, the impact of their horns produces a crash of thunder that can be heard for miles. These battles are usually accompanied by a violent, if short-lived, cloudburst.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, constitution, electricity, energy resistant (electricity, vitality), sensory (darkvision), strength, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Reactive Zap","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature hits the volt ram with a melee attack roll using either an unarmed attack or a metal weapon</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The volt ram has Stored Charge</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> Some of the electricity stored in the volt ram's fleece surges into the target, dealing 1d6 electricity damage (DC 18 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Store Charge","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A volt ram's wool is exceptionally capable of storing electricity. Whenever a volt ram is targeted by or in the area of an effect that deals electricity damage, or when it uses Running Charge, its wool captures and stores the electric charge.</p>"},{"n":"Discharge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The volt ram releases stored static electricity in a blast of crackling lightning. All creatures in a 10-foot emanation around the volt ram take 2d6 electricity damage (DC 16 basic Reflex save). A creature that critically fails the saving throw is also Stunned 1. The volt ram can't use Discharge again until it Stores Charge.</p>"},{"n":"Running Charge","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The volt ram runs and charges up the static electricity as it does. It Strides twice and Stores Charge.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/toJlCvwy0iVqOeI5":{"lang":["Aklo","Common","Voskan"],"gear":["Scroll of Heal (Rank 4)","Leather Armor","Shortsword","Thieves' Toolkit","Repair Toolkit (Superb)","Electrosphere"],"flavor":"<p>Voskas are a colorful, moth-winged ancestry native to the Ethereal Plane. Most voska cultures celebrate curiosity, playfulness, and innovation. Voska inventors blend magical breakthroughs that originate from several different planes of reality with novel feats of mystical engineering. These designs are rarely usable by members of other ancestries without significant adjustment.</p><p>Voskas who leave the Ethereal Plane might be driven by a desire for intellectual exchange, but their methods aren't always peaceful. Some research groups employ bounty hunters to acquire materials that they can't easily gain through legitimate means or to handle other threats to their enterprises.</p><p>This voska bounty hunter wields an electrosphere, which is a relatively popular device among voskas whose work is likely to require violence but who would rather avoid killing their targets outright.</p><h2>Spacefaring Voskas</h2><p>By default, voskas are ethereal travelers with a penchant for tinkering with magical technology. With slight adjustments, they can be reimagined as creatures from a faraway planet who readily adapt technology from numerous worlds. Such voskas might replace ethereal flight with an ability that helps them fly in low-gravity environments.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Crafting, Deception, Diplomacy, Lore [Ethereal Plane], Occultism, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, dexterity, electricity, energy resistant (cold, vitality), intelligence, sensory (darkvision), throwing, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Ethereal Flight","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A voska's wings are optimized for flight in areas with subjective gravity, such as the Ethereal Plane. A voska's fly speed in areas of subjective gravity is 60 feet. With exposure and diligent training, some voskas, such as the voska bounty hunter, develop the ability to fly in normal gravity.</p>"},{"n":"Zap","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A creature that takes damage from a voska bounty hunter's electrosphere is Shocked for 1 round. A shocked creature is Off-Guard against attacks that deal electricity damage (even if it's only additional electricity damage, such as a shock weapon) and takes –2 circumstance penalty on Fortitude and Reflex saving throws against electricity effects. The shocked condition ends early if the creature takes any amount of electricity damage from a source other than persistent electricity damage, or if they spend an Interact action to discharge the electricity harmlessly.</p>"},{"n":"Electrosphere","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The voska bounty hunter carries a jury-rigged magic item called an <em>electrosphere</em>. While holding the <em>electrosphere</em> in one hand, they can make electrosphere pulse Strikes and Electrosnares. The <em>electrosphere</em> also causes any nonmagical metal weapon held in their other hand to deal an additional 1d12 electricity damage.</p>"},{"n":"Trick Magic Item","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["manipulate"],"f":"","d":"<p>As the Trick Magic item skill feat, except the voska bounty hunter can roll a Crafting check in place of Arcana, Nature, Occultism, or Religion. When required, their spell DC is 25 and their spell attack modifier is +17.</p>"},{"n":"Electrosnare","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["arcane","electricity","incapacitation","nonlethal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The voska bounty hunter is holding their electrosphere and a creature within 60 feet has the shocked condition</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The voska bounty hunter activates their electrosphere to supercharge the electricity cracking through the body of a shocked target within 60 feet, causing its muscles to lock up. The creature takes 1d12 electricity damage and might have its movement impaired based on the results of a Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target takes half damage and is Stunned 1 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target takes full damage is Stunned 2 for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target takes double electricity damage is Paralyzed for 1 round.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/8xbTrq6MiN8tPh5z":{"lang":["Chthonian","Diabolic","Empyrean","Requian","Telepathy 100 feet, Truespeech"],"flavor":"<p>Wagabagos prey on people's fear of change. They taunt their victims with illusions that subvert their hopes for the future, making their pasts seem insurmountable and their dreams impossible to attain. Just before their prey succumbs entirely to despair, they switch tactics, inflating those dreams with unrealistic optimism. They repeat this cycle of despair and hope until their targets become paralyzed by their pasts and terrified at the prospect of the slightest change to their lives.</p><p>Three ever-shifting masks adorn a wagabago's black billowy cloak, which hides their many-eyed limbs. When a wagabago peers into a person's memories, their masks project the faces and voices that are most effective at tormenting their victims.</p><h2>No Future</h2><p>Wagabagos choose as victims people with the greatest abilities to enact change: rulers and commanders in positions of power and young adventurers with glorious destinies before them. By neutralizing these potential difference-makers, they seek to stave off future golden ages and keep the world mired in a stagnant present. Some of those unfortunate people stunted by a wagabago's trickery become pruned futures and other temporal paradoxes.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 375/840/1,560 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Performance, Religion, Society, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Daze, Fear, Figment, Mask of Terror, Message, Truesight, Truespeech, Ventriloquism), throwing, unholy, wild</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Phantasmal Tormentor","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"3 times per day","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per day for each mask</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The wagabago activates one of their three masks, causing it to fly off their face and spin an illusory body that mimics a Medium creature. This functions as a 6th-rank Illusory Creature, except that creatures who disbelieve the illusion do not recover any damage taken and continue to take full damage from the illusion. Additionally, the illusion can use a special ability based on its mask.</p><ul><li><strong>Trauma</strong> The illusion takes on the form of a long-buried memory.</li><li><strong>Persecution</strong> The illusion takes on the form of an authority figure that abuses their power.</li><li><strong>Temptation</strong> The illusion takes on the form of a person who promises hope, redemption, power, or pleasure.</li></ul>"},{"n":"Walk the Ethereal Line","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["divine","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The wagabago slides off the Ethereal Plane to walk the thin line between the Ethereal Plane and the Universe, existing on both planes simultaneously. They can spend one action to exit this boundary; if they do, they only exist on the Ethereal Plane.</p>"},{"n":"Lingering Terror","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>Creatures hit by a fear spear Strike do not reduce their frightened condition automatically at the end of their turns.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/GbB8QdaHBaGKzDlG":{"flavor":"<p>Wagabagos prey on people's fear of change. They taunt their victims with illusions that subvert their hopes for the future, making their pasts seem insurmountable and their dreams impossible to attain. Just before their prey succumbs entirely to despair, they switch tactics, inflating those dreams with unrealistic optimism. They repeat this cycle of despair and hope until their targets become paralyzed by their pasts and terrified at the prospect of the slightest change to their lives.</p><p>Three ever-shifting masks adorn a wagabago's black billowy cloak, which hides their many-eyed limbs. When a wagabago peers into a person's memories, their masks project the faces and voices that are most effective at tormenting their victims.</p><h2>No Future</h2><p>Wagabagos choose as victims people with the greatest abilities to enact change: rulers and commanders in positions of power and young adventurers with glorious destinies before them. By neutralizing these potential difference-makers, they seek to stave off future golden ages and keep the world mired in a stagnant present. Some of those unfortunate people stunted by a wagabago's trickery become pruned futures and other temporal paradoxes.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Twist the Knife","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The illusion's last action was a successful Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The illusion aggravates the wound it just inflicted, dealing 3d6 persistent bleed damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/bKAlt8e7QE8jSUh6":{"flavor":"<p>Wagabagos prey on people's fear of change. They taunt their victims with illusions that subvert their hopes for the future, making their pasts seem insurmountable and their dreams impossible to attain. Just before their prey succumbs entirely to despair, they switch tactics, inflating those dreams with unrealistic optimism. They repeat this cycle of despair and hope until their targets become paralyzed by their pasts and terrified at the prospect of the slightest change to their lives.</p><p>Three ever-shifting masks adorn a wagabago's black billowy cloak, which hides their many-eyed limbs. When a wagabago peers into a person's memories, their masks project the faces and voices that are most effective at tormenting their victims.</p><h2>No Future</h2><p>Wagabagos choose as victims people with the greatest abilities to enact change: rulers and commanders in positions of power and young adventurers with glorious destinies before them. By neutralizing these potential difference-makers, they seek to stave off future golden ages and keep the world mired in a stagnant present. Some of those unfortunate people stunted by a wagabago's trickery become pruned futures and other temporal paradoxes.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Beautiful Illusion","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","illusion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>All non-fiend creatures within 20 feet must succeed a DC 30 Will save or become Dazzled for 1 round (or 3 rounds on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/bvk3bmx17qyD1Gll":{"flavor":"<p>Wagabagos prey on people's fear of change. They taunt their victims with illusions that subvert their hopes for the future, making their pasts seem insurmountable and their dreams impossible to attain. Just before their prey succumbs entirely to despair, they switch tactics, inflating those dreams with unrealistic optimism. They repeat this cycle of despair and hope until their targets become paralyzed by their pasts and terrified at the prospect of the slightest change to their lives.</p><p>Three ever-shifting masks adorn a wagabago's black billowy cloak, which hides their many-eyed limbs. When a wagabago peers into a person's memories, their masks project the faces and voices that are most effective at tormenting their victims.</p><h2>No Future</h2><p>Wagabagos choose as victims people with the greatest abilities to enact change: rulers and commanders in positions of power and young adventurers with glorious destinies before them. By neutralizing these potential difference-makers, they seek to stave off future golden ages and keep the world mired in a stagnant present. Some of those unfortunate people stunted by a wagabago's trickery become pruned futures and other temporal paradoxes.</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Traumatic Memories","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","illusion","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>All non-fiend creatures within 20 feet must succeed at a DC 30 Will save or become Sickened 1 (Sickened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/Lp7wnIL4pwfdfCVm":{"lang":["Arboreal","Common","Spirituan","Can't speak any language"],"gear":["Musical Instrument (Virtuoso handheld) (4)"],"flavor":"<p>Willow trees are symbols of both loss and rebirth. On rare occasions, willows that watch over a settlement's festivals and rites can absorb a portion of the settlement's collective consciousness. By gathering fragments of communal song, these trees develop minds of their own and the ability to manifest a quartet of spirits. Though the spirits spend much of their time caught up in their performances, they experience occasional moments of clarity. During these times, the spirits can speak. However, their guidance is often cryptic and poetic in nature, blending the past and present freely into a tangled tale.</p><h2>Playing Favorites</h2><p>Waltzing willows who oversee a village usually have a few favorites: kind souls who dedicate their lives to the community, or loners who confide their troubles under the willow's eaves. When these favorites are threatened, even by other villagers, the willows take action, though often in plausibly deniable ways. Many a bully has mysteriously broken a limb while climbing a willow tree, with none the wiser as to the cause—although sharp-eyed observers might notice that the willow's branches seem to have changed configuration.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 250/560/1,030 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), shield, skill items (Athletics, Nature, Occultism, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, light, mind, mind shield, music, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Occult: Soothe), spirit, strength, sturdy, tangle shield, time, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Death of Music","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["curse","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a creature kills a waltzing willow, it must succeed at a DC 29 Will save or become cursed. While cursed in this way, the creature can no longer hear music at all. The creature is Deafened only to instruments and sung voices and the creature can no longer benefit from composition spells.</p>"},{"n":"Improved Knockdown","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature as a free action. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"},{"n":"Spirit Lights","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":["light","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The waltzing willow is surrounded by the glowing spirits of four musicians, which each shed light in a 60-foot radius. Once per round, the willow can spend one action with the concentrate trait to move each spirit up to 60 feet. At the end of each spirit's movement, the willow can attach it to an adjacent creature, who must attempt a DC 32 Reflex save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The spirit attaches itself to the creature for 1 round. While the spirit is attached, the creature is Dazzled. If it was invisible, it becomes Concealed, and if it was already concealed for any other reason, it is no longer concealed.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> As success, except the spirit remains attached for 3 rounds.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As success, except the spirit remains attached for 1 minute.</p>"},{"n":"Spotlight","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","emotion","light","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>The waltzing willow fills a 30-foot burst within 120 feet with light as their spirit's performance swells to a crescendo. All creatures in the spotlight must attempt a DC 32 Reflex save or a DC 28 Performance check as they are swept into a dance. Waltzing willows are not immune to Spotlight, but they receive the results of a critical success if they roll a success. The Waltzing willow can't use Spotlight again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target dances exceedingly well. If they rolled a Performance check, they are Quickened from the momentum until the end of its next turn; they can use this extra action only to Stride.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target stumbles over its feet, becoming Off-Guard until the end of the waltzing willow's next turn or until it Steps.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target's dance places horrific strain on its body as it fails to keep up with the music. It takes 12d6 bludgeoning damage and falls Prone.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> As failure, but the target takes double damage.</p>"},{"n":"Two Step Waltz","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The waltzing willow Steps twice and can cast a cantrip.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/kuFQolHcEUddmS9y":{"lang":["Sussuran","Voltan"],"notes":{"speed":"grounded"},"flavor":"<p>Weather vanes are useful ways of telling direction with the wind. Occasionally a stray bolt of lightning connects with one, and even rarer, the weather vane absorbs not just the electricity, but a storm spirit as well. Once the storm spirit realizes they have become trapped in a metal prison, they lash out at anyone with the misfortune to come nearby. With deadly bolts of electricity and powerful gales, they attempt to keep others from approaching their newly immobile form.</p><p>Unfortunately for weatherbanes, unless someone manages to push through their storms and destroy their metal bodies, they are doomed to remain trapped. If someone does manage this deed, the spirit bears no grudge toward them and might even offer them a minor boon before taking to the skies once more.</p><h2>Songs of the Storm</h2><p>When a weatherbane is topped by the image of a creature such as a rooster, the weather spirit embodies that creature, and its sound can be heard echoing from rooftops for miles around. Weatherbane-haunted villages ring with the crows of roosters, the clop of horses' hooves, or the roars of dragons. In seaside towns, weatherbanes might instead emit songs similar to those of whales or sirens.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 7/18/30 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning), perception item, skill items (Intimidation, Nature)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> air, bane, charisma, dexterity, electricity, elongating, energy resistant (electricity, sonic, spirit, vitality, void), intelligence, sensory (darkvision), throwing, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Deflecting Gust","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["air","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The weatherbane is the target of a physical ranged attack</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The weatherbane is aware of the attack and not Off-Guard</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The weatherbane gains a +4 circumstance bonus to AC against the triggering attack.</p>"},{"n":"Electroblast","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["electricity","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>With a smell of ozone, the weatherbane calls for the might of the storm. They attempt an electrobolt Strike. If the Strike hits, the target and any creatures within a 15-foot emanation around the target take 1d6 electricity damage (DC 20 basic Fortitude save). Any creature that fails its save also takes a –10-foot status penalty to its Speeds for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Blowback","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A Large or smaller creature takes damage from the weatherbane's gust Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The weatherbane attempts to blow the creature away from them, rolling a Nature check against the creature's Fortitude DC. The creature is blown back 10 feet on a success, or 20 feet on a critical success.</p>"},{"n":"Wind Blast","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["air","manipulate","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The weatherbane changes the direction of the wind in a 60-foot emanation until the start of their next turn. They can choose to make the wind in the entire area point in a specific direction, to draw the wind toward themselves, or to push the wind away from themselves. All Large or smaller creatures in the area must attempt a DC 18 Fortitude save. Large or smaller creatures that later enter the area while the winds persist must also attempt this save. The weatherbane can't use Wind Blast again for 1d4 rounds.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature can't move against the wind.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is pushed 30 feet in the wind's direction and knocked Prone. If it was flying, it experiences the effects of critical failure instead.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is pushed 60 feet in the wind's direction, knocked prone, and takes 2d6 bludgeoning damage.</p>"},{"n":"Grounded","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>As an enraged spirit in a metal prison, a weatherbane can only spin in place. They can't move or be moved, except through teleportation or similar magical means. If a weatherbane is moved, the grounding connection is severed, freeing the spirit within and destroying the weatherbane.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/wVvrX49kTdaZPMSL":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Thalassic"],"notes":{"per":"lucksense (imprecise) 330 feet"},"flavor":"<p>Wellwishers are an unusual composite creature who forms as the result of a ritual in which a trio of fey embodying fortune merge together into a single whole. Wellwishers require fresh water and the fortune of mortals for nourishment, which makes wells their most common habitat and leads to their name. However, sometimes they live in pristine woodland pools and other locations with legends for granting wishes. Though a wellwisher's blessings sometimes bring good luck, they are more likely to cause misfortune at a critical moment.</p><p>Wellwishers harbor a distinct obsession with the number three. Areas near their homes often demonstrate a bizarre number of threes, both subtle and impossibly obscure, from triangles and groups of three stones to waymarkers placed exactly three paces apart, measured from the average stride of the person who dug the well. An esoteric way to detect the presence of a wellwisher is to simply pour water from a well that they are suspected to dwell within onto a flat surface. Given a few minutes, the water will flow into a distinctly triangular shape.</p><h2>Wellwisher's Rhyme</h2><p>A simple communion with the wellwisher is all it takes for them to exert their power, made all too appealing by an old piece of folklore:</p><p><em>Three coins tossed, wishes cast,</em></p><p><em>Drink deep well's water, 'til thirst has passed.</em></p><p><em>Rest and dream of water's flow.</em></p><p><em>Your wishes granted, hopes bestowed.</em></p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 90/200/360 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), shield, perception item, skill items (Athletics, Deception, Diplomacy, Nature, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, mind, mind shield, sensory (darkvision), spells (Primal: Sure Strike), strength, sturdy, throwing, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Lucksense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>A wellwisher senses the threads of fate that dance around creatures affected by fortune or misfortune effects.</p>"},{"n":"Wish Twist","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["fortune","misfortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature who previously made a wish in the well would attempt a check in pursuit of fulfilling their wish, or a creature who took damage from one of the wellwisher's Strikes in the last minute would attempt a check</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The creature must immediately attempt a DC 13 Flat. On a success, the creature rolls twice and takes the better result on the triggering check; on a failure, it rolls twice and take the worse result. After using Wish Twist on a creature, the wellwisher can't use Wish Twist on that creature again until that creature makes another wish or they hit the creature with another Strike.</p>"},{"n":"Trinary Luck","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The wellwisher rolls a 3 or 13 on a check</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The wellwisher can choose to resolve the check as if they had rolled a 20 instead. If they do, the next time they roll a 20 on a check against an opponent of at least level 5; they treat this 20 as if they had rolled a 3 instead. This mandatory conversion is not a fortune or misfortune effect and happens after all fortune or misfortune effects are applied. After turning a 3 or 13 into a 20, they can't do so again until after a 20 has been turned into a 3.</p>"},{"n":"Knockdown","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Knockdown in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Trip the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty.</p></p>"},{"n":"Improved Push","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Trigger</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Improved Push in its damage entry</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The monster attempts to Shove the creature. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the monster's multiple attack penalty. If Improved Push lists a distance, change the distance the creature is pushed on a success to that distance.</p></p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/dHbYxUPOegI4rAS3":{"lang":["Fey","Thalassic"],"lore":[["Ocean Depths Lore",24]],"gear":["Bone Harpoon"],"notes":{"per":"wavesense (varies) 1 mile"},"flavor":"<p>In the depths of the ocean, the fall of the carcass of a massive creature like a whale or kraken is a momentous occasion. A single corpse can birth and sustain a thriving ecosystem. Like any area of natural splendor, these ecosystems can attract nymphs as guardians and caretakers.</p><p>And what becomes of these nymphs when the corpse finally gives all it can? Some become nymph wanderers, but others who can't let go of their attachments instead transform into whalefellers. These aquatic predators dedicate their lives to slaying the largest creatures they can find and guiding their corpses to the ocean floor. The death of their wards strips whalefellers of many of the natural powers of a nymph, but in exchange, it imbues them with a desperate and vicious bloodthirst.</p><p>When hunting animals proves insufficient, whalefellers have been known to hunt and sink entire ships full of sailors or slaughter villages of sea-dwelling creatures in the hopes that enough individual corpses might help kickstart new growth.</p><h2>That Was My White Whale</h2><p>Whalefellers have a selfish streak—if a leviathan of the deep is to be slain, they want to be the one who strikes the killing blow. There's nothing they hate more than a ship full of adventurous fools who battle sea monsters on their own. If denied a kill by the crew of a ship, a whalefeller might follow the ship for leagues, picking off crewmembers one by one as part of their twisted revenge.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 175/390/720 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Diplomacy, Lore [Ocean Depths], Nature, Stealth, Survival)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, death, dexterity, energy resistant (fire, vitality), intelligence, spells (Primal: Control Water, Create Water, Hydraulic Torrent), spiked, strength, water, wild, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Aquatic Animal Empathy","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The whalefeller can ask questions of, receive answers from, and use the Diplomacy skill with aquatic animals.</p>"},{"n":"Aquatic Tracker","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The whalefeller can follow trails through the water via careful surveillance of currents, disturbed fauna, and other telltale signs obvious only to a dweller in the deep. They can use Survival to Track even in aquatic environments.</p>"},{"n":"Wavesense","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The whalefeller's wavesense is precise out to a range of 120 feet, imprecise out to 300 feet, and vague out to 1 mile.</p>"},{"n":"Bone Harpoon","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A whalefeller's bone harpoon is a part of their body. Unlike a typical harpoon, it doesn't have a visible rope or chain; instead, the whalefeller is magically connected to their harpoon and can recall it with Reclaim Harpoon. If another creature within 1 mile is holding the bone harpoon, the whalefeller can attempt to Disarm it, no matter how large the creature is. If the harpoon is ever destroyed, the whalefeller must succeed at a DC 30 Fortitude save or be Sickened 2.</p>"},{"n":"Colossus Hunter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The whalefeller deals 4 additional damage with their Strikes against creatures that are Large or larger.</p>"},{"n":"Dart Away","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The whalefeller is targeted with a melee or ranged attack by an attacker they can see that is currently taking persistent damage from Tear Free</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The whalefeller gains a +2 status bonus to AC against the triggering attack. If the attack misses, the triggering creature takes 2d6 piercing damage from fragments of bone left in the wound.</p>"},{"n":"Reclaim Harpoon","a":"f","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The whalefeller's harpoon is within 1 mile and is not being held by any creature</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The whalefeller's bone harpoon returns to their hand.</p>"},{"n":"Tear Free","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The whalefeller's last action was a successful harpoon Strike</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The whalefeller sprouts additional barbs from the harpoon before violently recalling it with Reclaim Harpoon. The target takes 2d6 persistent bleed damage and takes a –10-foot status penalty to their swim Speed for as long as they are taking this persistent damage. Even once healed, the wound makes the target easier to track; the whalefeller gains a +2 circumstance bonus to Survival checks to Track the target for 24 hours.</p><p>Effect: Tear Free</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/wy8gy9ZrQ7dh9HHj":{"flavor":"<p>A whittler's serpent is the inevitable manifestation of bardic magic combined with bardic instruments. Made up of the wood shavings that accumulate in the workshops of luthiers and reedmakers, the whittler's serpent is slowly animated by the power of music and song. As notes resonate through fallen whorls of wood, these remnants weave together into sinuous patterns of scales. A whittler's serpent starts off small, with a soft and breathy voice and only the knowledge of the tunes that animated it to life. As the years pass, it builds up a library of music and learns to improvise and accompany other performers. With no need to breathe, eat, or sleep, whittler's serpents are free to spend their every moment experiencing the sounds around them and seeking out new sources of music.</p><p>Many music shops and concert halls keep a whittler's serpent as a kind of mascot or pest control. These serpents are also favored companions of wandering bards and even spies, who value the clever creature's skill at reproducing conversations. However, those who enjoy a serpent's company must take care never to allow another whittler's serpent to approach. When two whitter's serpents meet, the result is often deadly. The larger serpent typically kills and consumes the smaller one, absorbing its victims' wood shavings and auditory memories alike. If this process happens enough times, the resulting serpent grows in size and intelligence, developing its own innate occult magic. Engineering this transformation is risky, however, as these stronger serpents tend to be cunning and bloodthirsty.</p><h2>Musical Companion</h2><p>Made from the wood shavings planed from many a lute and pipe, whittler's serpents have an instinctive affinity for the instruments from which they sprang. A whittler's serpent will sometimes develop a particular attachment to one of their sister instruments, following its owner whether the serpent is wanted or not. Sometimes a whittler's serpent will even stow away in order to stay with a beloved instrument, although finding one is easy: just strike up a tune and they can't resist singing along.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 3.5/7/13 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing), perception item, skill items (Athletics, Intimidation, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (sonic, vitality), mind, mind shield, music, sensory (darkvision), tangle shield, throwing, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Minstrel's Mimic","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["occult"],"f":"","d":"<p>A whittler's serpent does not understand languages, but it can emulate speech, poetry, and music that it has heard, even producing instrumental sounds such as maracas, drums, or pipes. It instinctively accompanies nearby music. If it rolls a failure or critical failure on a check to Aid a Performance, it gets a success instead.</p>"},{"n":"Warning Rattle","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["auditory","emotion","fear","mental","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature attempts a melee attack roll against the whittler's serpent</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The whittler's serpent rattles its body in a sound reminiscent of a rattlesnake or a maraca, in an attempt to divert the attack and discourage further violence. The creature must succeed at a DC 17 Will save or become Frightened 1 (Frightened 2 on a critical failure).</p>"},{"n":"Enigma's Refrain","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["fortune","occult"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> Once every 1d4 rounds</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The inspiration that drives bards and other entertainers is left as a remnant in the whittler's serpent. The whittler's serpent makes a bite Strike, rolling the attack twice and using the better result. The attack ignores circumstance penalties to the attack roll and any flat check required due to the target being Concealed or hidden.</p>"},{"n":"Whistling Wind","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>A whittler's serpent can Feint using Performance instead of Deception. The Feint gains the auditory trait and loses the mental trait.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/ZuKdYLhfCh2zLSOc":{"lang":["Fey","Muan"],"flavor":"<p>Common woodplucks are small, plump fey, and they don't look like much of a threat, though thanks to the power of their imaginations, they can be more dangerous than they look to fledgling adventurers who decide to pick a fight with them.</p><p>Woodplucks tend to make their homes in large communities in forests. They subtly alter the color and texture of their bodies to match the trees they live around. Teamwork is prized in woodpluck culture. Singing is also a cultural touchstone, as it is one of the best ways to align the thoughts of a group together. The sprouts of a group of singing woodplucks dance in unison, portraying the various events and emotions their voices describe.</p><p>Despite the difficulty woodplucks have with hiding their thoughts, they love pulling pranks and practical jokes. It's considered a sign of respect among woodplucks to allow tricks to play out even when you see them ahead of time. It's easy to see a joke coming when the comedian has a giggling flower on their head, but outsiders are well advised to play along to avoid causing offense.</p><hr><p>Woodplucks are plump fey creatures with a single arm protruding from their torsos. Their eager imaginations manifest in physical form through a small plant sprout embedded in each individual's head, which grows and shapes itself into whatever the host woodpluck is thinking about at the time. When a woodpluck sleeps, their sprout's roots dig into their mind and grow out into the most vivid scenes of their dreams.</p><p>Woodplucks can gather in choruses to work together, and when their imagination grows, they can become powerful elders.</p><h2>Take This, It's Dangerous to go Alone!</h2><p>Woodplucks are generous to a fault, always eager to share the fruits of their imaginations with outsiders. In fact, some woodplucks consider it impolite to leave an interaction with a non-woodpluck without offering them some sort of gift, such as a weapon or useful tool. Unfortunately, woodplucks don't seem fully aware that their gifts only last as long as their attention does. When the woodpluck cultivates a new item, the recipient of their \"gift\" might find that it dissolves in their hand at the most inconvenient time.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Deception, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), tangle shield, throwing, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Tree Merge","a":"2","c":"interaction","t":["concentrate","plant","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck merges their body into an adjacent tree. While merged, they can't take any actions that require their body (including Cultivate Imagination), but they can still use their mind and senses as normal while merged. They can exit the tree into any space adjacent to the tree as a two-action activity, ending the effect.</p>"},{"n":"Splinter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a woodpluck wields a melee weapon they created with Cultivate Imagination, the weapon produces splinters on impact, dealing an additional 1d4 piercing damage.</p>"},{"n":"Cultivated Performance","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["auditory","concentrate","incapacitation","mental","primal","visual"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck sings a powerful song, and their sprout dances along with them. The woodpluck chooses up to four creatures within 30 feet, which must each attempt a DC 18 Will save.</p><hr><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature is Fascinated for 1 round.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature is fascinated and stunned for 1 round.</p>"},{"n":"Cultivate Imagination","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","plant","primal","wood"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck imagines a common level 0 non-magical weapon, shield, piece of adventuring gear, or simple tool of no more than 1 Bulk, which then grows on their head. The cultivated item is made entirely from wood and other plant matter, but otherwise functions as the base item. The woodpluck can then Interact to grab the item. The most common weapons imagined by woodplucks are listed in their Strikes above. Cultivating an item in this way causes any items previously created by the woodpluck's imagination to break down into ordinary plant matter.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/z4hdFCI8xXXLIVJJ":{"lang":["Fey","Muan"],"flavor":"<p>When woodplucks need to work together, they gather into large groups called choruses. They typically form choruses to share ideas and collaborate on large projects, such as those inspired by a woodpluck elder's boundless creativity. They also form choruses to defend themselves against threats. Together, they can create wooden devices to climb, swim, or even fly, defend each other from harm, and launch coordinated attacks.</p><p>Choruses are loud and vibrant groups. Instead of taking turns when speaking, members of a chorus share their ideas as soon as they come to mind. By varying their pitch and volume, woodplucks make their voices stand out from the wall of sound. While woodplucks have no trouble understanding multiple ideas being spoken at once, most other ancestries have considerable difficulty making sense of a chorus' speech.</p><hr><p>Woodplucks are plump fey creatures with a single arm protruding from their torsos. Their eager imaginations manifest in physical form through a small plant sprout embedded in each individual's head, which grows and shapes itself into whatever the host woodpluck is thinking about at the time. When a woodpluck sleeps, their sprout's roots dig into their mind and grow out into the most vivid scenes of their dreams.</p><p>Woodplucks can gather in choruses to work together, and when their imagination grows, they can become powerful elders.</p><h2>Gadgets and Gizmos</h2><p>To outsiders, the inventions of a woodpluck chorus seem to accomplish their goals in the most complex way possible. Since every member of the chorus is eager to add their own twist to the project, woodpluck devices usually include scores of seemingly useless widgets and thingamajigs. Nevertheless, these gadgets work—although any effort to replicate a woodpluck invention by non-woodplucks usually results in failure.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 45/100/180 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Diplomacy, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> body, bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), tangle shield, throwing, wild, winged, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Dense Swarm","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The individual creatures in a woodpluck chorus are Small. Unlike most swarms, they can't share their space with other creatures, and they aren't immune to precision damage or resistant to physical damage.</p>"},{"n":"Cultivated Volley","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodplucks cultivate javelins and then throw them as a volley. This volley is a 10-foot burst within 30 feet that deals 4d6 piercing damage (DC 22 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Group Imagination","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","plant","primal","stance"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck chorus collectively imagines components of a large project they then quickly assemble as needed, granting one of the following benefits of their choice. This benefit lasts for 1 minute or until the woodpluck chorus uses this ability again.</p><ul><li>A climb, swim, or fly Speed of 20 feet.</li><li>Wooden Flurry and Cultivated Volley also deal 1d6 persistent bleed damage.</li><li>Access to the Cultivated Party action.</li></ul>"},{"n":"Wooden Flurry","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodplucks lash out with cultivated weapons at each foe within 5 feet, dealing 1d6+3 piercing or 1d6+3 slashing damage (DC 24 basic Reflex save). The chorus can independently choose whether the damage is piercing or slashing for each foe.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/BdaAoJecY11FBMbD":{"lang":["Common","Fey","Muan"],"flavor":"<p>On rare occasions, a woodpluck develops a particularly powerful imagination, significantly strengthening their power to cultivate and manifest whatever they can dream up. These woodpluck elders often become leaders in their communities. They can cultivate large and intricate constructions from their minds, such as rideable beasts, and to a woodpluck, the iconic image of an elder is atop the back of a majestic imagined creature. When woodpluck communities are threatened, elders lead choruses into battle to defend their homes.</p><p>Though elders are respected for their creativity and prowess alike, few use that influence to rule outside of emergency situations. After all, spending considerable time on the responsibilities of leadership would interfere with their ability to engage with the joy of creation. Woodpluck elders often seek inspiration beyond the boundaries of their home villages, wandering to settlements that are home to a variety of ancestries and eagerly trading crafting ideas with anyone who catches their eye.</p><hr><p>Woodplucks are plump fey creatures with a single arm protruding from their torsos. Their eager imaginations manifest in physical form through a small plant sprout embedded in each individual's head, which grows and shapes itself into whatever the host woodpluck is thinking about at the time. When a woodpluck sleeps, their sprout's roots dig into their mind and grow out into the most vivid scenes of their dreams.</p><p>Woodplucks can gather in choruses to work together, and when their imagination grows, they can become powerful elders.</p><h2>Junior Elders</h2><p>Despite their title, woodpluck elders aren't necessarily the longest-tenured members of their community. Many so-called woodpluck elders are young adults or even children whose outsized imaginations make them the envy of their seniors. No matter their age, the inventions of woodpluck elders often incorporate many childlike elements: hobby horses and cuddly dragons are common.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 64/140/250 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (medium), skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Deception, Diplomacy, Nature, Performance)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality), light, mind, mind shield, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: Enthrall, Laughing Fit, Revealing Light, Wall of Thorns, Weapon Storm), strength, tangle shield, throwing, wild, wisdom, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Tree Merge","a":"2","c":"interaction","t":["concentrate","plant","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck merges their body into an adjacent tree. While merged, they can't take any actions that require their body (including Cultivate Imagination), but they can still use their mind and senses as normal while merged. They can exit the tree into any space adjacent to the tree as a two-action activity, ending the effect.</p>"},{"n":"Splinter","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>When a woodpluck wields a melee weapon they created with Cultivate Imagination, the weapon produces splinters on impact, dealing an additional 1d8 piercing damage.</p>"},{"n":"Cultivate Imagination","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","plant","primal","wood"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck imagines a common non-magical weapon, shield, piece of adventuring gear, or simple tool of up to level 3 and no more than 3 Bulk, which then grows on their head. Any weapon created with this ability is a <em>+1 striking</em> weapon and gains the effects of a <em>returning</em> rune if it has the thrown trait. A woodpluck elder can wield a weapon that requires two hands in its one hand. The cultivated item is made entirely from wood and other plant matter, but otherwise functions as the base item. The woodpluck can then Interact to grab the item. The most common weapons imagined by woodplucks are listed in their Strikes above. Cultivating an item in this way causes any items previously created by the woodpluck's imagination to break down into ordinary plant matter.</p>"},{"n":"Cultivate Mount","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","plant","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The woodpluck imagines a Medium or Large creature or simple vehicle, which then grows from a secondary sprout to form a wooden mount underneath it. The elder's size increases to that of the imagined creature, and the elder chooses to either increase its Speed to 40 feet or gain a fly Speed of 25 feet. The mount is an extension of the elder, not a separate creature. The elder can Dismiss this effect.</p>"},{"n":"Stampeding Mount","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Requirements</strong> The woodpluck elder has a Cultivated Mount</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The woodpluck elder jumps off their mount and sends it charging forward, dealing 8d6 bludgeoning damage to creatures and unattended objects in an 80-foot line. Creatures in the line must attempt a DC 26 Reflex save. If the mount collides with an object or creature of a size larger than it, the line stops early unless the object or creature is reduced to 0 Hit Points, After the charge, the mount breaks down into ordinary plant matter.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The creature is unaffected.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The creature takes half damage.</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The creature takes full damage and is knocked Prone.</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The creature takes double damage, is knocked prone, and becomes Clumsy 1 for 1 minute.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/H7hsdULvwcUSTeK2":{"lang":["Common","Fey"],"flavor":"<p>Wripies are mischievous fey that delight in pulling pranks. They believe that levity is the key to enjoying life and find boredom the most intolerable emotion of all. Unfortunately, wripies are quickly bored by repetitive tasks, leading them to believe that disrupting other people engaged in such activities is doing them a favor. However, the same people who greet their pranks with merry laughter at a party are far less likely to be good-natured after a wripie's hijinks have ruined a day's work. The more praise their pranks receive, the more they are encouraged to scale up their mayhem. But if a wripie's pranks are met with anger or hostility, they might conclude that violent pranks are the best way to resonate with their audience. Wripies recognize that the lives of others are fragile, and they take care to ensure that even their most painful schemes don't cause lethal harm.</p><p>Wripies' flashiest trick is to create and puppet duplicates of their own bodies. While wripies certainly don't enjoy pain, they appear to have no fear of death. When a wripie is mortally wounded, their body vanishes in a cloud of green smoke. Whether this vanishing act is a great escape, an unusual death, or even an indication that wripies are puppets of a yet greater fey mastermind is a question no wripie would dare to answer.</p><h2>Spring Fools</h2><p>Wripies hold the first day of each season as sacred—a day where work should be set aside and merriment must reign. They try to help others remember this fact by ruining any work that is undertaken, scattering harvests, spilling ink on manuscripts, and using their whips to snatch tools from an artisan's hands. The best way to rid oneself of a wripie on a first-of-the-season pranking spree is to play a trick in return: with a salute of admiration, a pranked wripie will disappear in a puff of green smoke.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 5/12/22 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), armor (light), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Athletics, Deception, Medicine, Nature, Performance, Stealth, Survival, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, charisma, constitution, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), sensory (low-light vision), space, space shield, spiked, tangle shield, wild, wood</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Sneak Attack","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The wripie deals 1d6 extra precision damage to Off-Guard creatures.</p>"},{"n":"Grab","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The monster's last action was a successful Strike that lists Grab in its damage entry, or the monster has a creature Grabbed or Restrained</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> If used after a Strike, the monster attempts to Grapple the creature using the body part it attacked with. This attempt neither applies nor counts toward the creature's multiple attack penalty.</p><p>The monster can instead use Grab and choose one creature it's grabbing or restraining with an appendage that has Grab to automatically extend that condition to the end of the monster's next turn.</p></p>"},{"n":"Not That One!","a":"r","c":"defensive","t":["primal","teleportation"],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> A creature targets the wripie with an attack and they can see the attacker, and the wripie is with 30 feet of at least one of their copies</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The wripie swap places with a copy within 30 feet. If the attack hits the copy, the copy explodes, spraying razor-sharp flower petals in a 5-foot emanation. All non-wripie creatures in the area take 2d6 slashing damage (DC 16 basic Reflex save).</p>"},{"n":"Create Copy","a":"1","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","illusion","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>The wripie creates a duplicate of themselves within 30 feet. This copy has the same statistics as the wripie, but disappears if it takes any damage. The wripie and all of their copies share their actions and multiple attack penalty. A creature that Seeks can determine of a wripie is real or a copy with a successful DC 18 Perception check. A copy lasts for 1 minute. A wripie can maintain up three copies of themselves at a time; if they create a new copy while three copies are active, the oldest copy disappears.</p>"},{"n":"Petal Dancing","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["move","primal"],"f":"","d":"<p>With an explosion of flower petals, the wripies burst into motion. The wripie and all of their copies Stride, gaining the Concealed condition during this movement.</p>"},{"n":"Final Prank","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Trigger</strong> The wripie is reduced to 0 Hit Points</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The wripie decides if their foes have been good sports or poor sports when dealing with their pranks before disappearing, leaving behind a 20-foot emanation of green smoke.</p><p><strong>Good Sports</strong> The emanation fills with soft flower petals and soothing smells. Each creature in the area heals 2d8 healing Hit Points.</p><p><strong>Poor Sports</strong> The emanation fills with thorned vines, dealing 2d6 slashing damage (DC 16 basic Reflex save). The area becomes difficult terrain. If the terrain is not a viable environment for the vines, they wither after 24 hours.</p>"},{"n":"Puckish Prankster","a":null,"c":"offensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>All damage that a wripie deals is nonlethal unless they consciously decide to deal lethal damage.</p>"}]},"battlezoo-bestiary-es-pf2e.sf2e-bestiary/QA66yZfnpV7G3Zsb":{"lang":["Envisioning"],"lore":[["Mathematics Lore",33]],"notes":{"hp":"half life","saves":"+1 status to all saves vs. magic","per":"temporospatial reckoning"},"flavor":"<p>A xenarian is an aeon of mathematical paradoxes, embodying those paradoxes' resolutions as they repair the space-time continuum. Much like fractrixes, xenarians are entwined with the fundamental concept of infinity and its finite manifestations within the confines of reality. But instead of being tasked with repairing spatial anomalies, xenarians concern themselves with anomalies that affect both space and time. They call upon fractrixes for aid in resolving complicated problems, including contending with anyone who would interfere with their vital work. They act with a cool, logical precision, their every action unfolding across multiple timelines in harmony. To witness a xenarian in motion is to observe the unraveling and reweaving of causality itself, as they prune divergent futures and stabilize fractured histories.</p><hr><p>Aeons are caretakers of order and balance, who watch over reality and guard it from the excesses that would throw it astray. Those who seek to cause discord and chaos will find themselves at odds with aeons' goals.</p><h2>Work is Never Done</h2><p>Like most aeons, xenarians strive to maintain order and balance throughout the multiverse. They are frequently frustrated, however, as no matter how hard they toil, their work is only ever half finished. When the actions of meddlesome mortals result in further setbacks, xenarians often choose to simply eradicate the offenders rather than risk additional delays.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 810/1,850/3,400 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (piercing, slashing), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Arcana, Athletics, Crafting, Deception, Lore [Mathematics], Nature, Occultism, Religion, Stealth)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, energy resistant (vitality, void), intelligence, mind, mind shield, mirrored, sensory (darkvision, scent, true seeing), space, space shield, spells (Divine: Teleport, Interplanar Teleport, Truesight, Unfettered Movement), throwing, time, wild, winged, wisdom</p>","abilities":[{"n":"Envisioning","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":["aura","divine","mental"],"f":"","d":"<p>100 feet. A xenarian can communicate mentally with any creatures in the aura using wordless psychic projections. They don't need to share a language, though the aeon's meaning to non-aeons can be vague and is often mysterious. An aeon can use this ability to communicate flawlessly with any other aeon on the same plane as themselves.</p>"},{"n":"+1 Status to All Saves vs. Magic","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":""},{"n":"Fractions of Life","a":"f","c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><strong>Frequency</strong> once per each creature's turn</p><p><strong>Requirements</strong> The xenarian is at 1 Hit Point and would take damage</p><hr><p><strong>Effect</strong> The xenarian falls back on tiny fractions of their remaining Hit Point to remain conscious. They do not take damage.</p>"},{"n":"Half Again","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["aura","divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>160 feet. A creature that approaches the xenarian with a move action can't move more than half the remaining distance toward the xenarian. As a reminder, adjacent creatures are 5 feet apart.</p>"},{"n":"Half Life","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine"],"f":"","d":"<p>Whenever a xenarian takes damage and is not at a damage threshold, the damage can't reduce them below their next damage threshold. When they regain Hit Points and are not at a damage threshold, they can't increase their Hit Points above their next damage threshold. Their damage thresholds are 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, and 1. Even if a damaging effect deals damage of multiple types, such as a flaming weapon, or fires multiple attacks, such as a force barrage, all damage is first added together before the xenarian's Hit Points are reduced. If a xenarian has multiple types of persistent damage, they are also all added together first.</p>"},{"n":"Inexorable","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p>The xenarian recovers from the Paralyzed, slowed, and stunned conditions at the end of their turn. They're also immune to penalties to their Speeds and the Immobilized condition, and they ignore difficult terrain and greater difficult terrain.</p>"},{"n":"Temporospatial Reckoning","a":null,"c":"defensive","t":["divine","revelation"],"f":"","d":"<p>A xenarian automatically detects and identifies space-altering magic (including teleportation effects) and time-altering magic and is only affected by the magic if they choose to be.</p>"},{"n":"Constant Spells","a":null,"c":"interaction","t":[],"f":"","d":"<p><p>A constant spell affects the monster without the monster needing to cast it, and its duration is unlimited. If a constant spell gets counteracted, the monster can reactivate it by spending the normal spellcasting actions the spell requires.</p></p>"},{"n":"Bisector","a":"2","c":"offensive","t":["divine","incapacitation"],"f":"","d":"<p>The xenarian reaches into the wells of essence that animate each creature in a 20-foot burst within 500 feet and attempts to cut them in half. Creatures in the area must attempt a DC 36 Fortitude save.</p><hr><p><strong>Critical Success</strong> The target is unaffected and becomes temporarily immune to Bisector for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Success</strong> The target's Hit Points are reduced to three-quarters of their current Hit Points (rounded up).</p><p><strong>Failure</strong> The target's Hit Points are reduced to half of their current Hit Points (rounded up).</p><p><strong>Critical Failure</strong> The target's Hit Points are reduced to one-quarter of their current Hit Points (rounded up).</p>"},{"n":"Summon Fractrix","a":"3","c":"offensive","t":["concentrate","divine","manipulate","summon"],"f":"","d":"<p>The xenarian summons a fractrix to fight on their side assist in other tasks. They can Sustain the summon for up to 1 minute.</p>"}]}});
